/* Machine translations of Logik Blomberg (AA XXIV), keyed by block id.
   Not part of the Academy Edition text. Source: data/logik-blomberg-24.js */
const EN_BLOMBERG = {
"blomberg-s01-001": "All our cognitions are acquired",
"blomberg-s01-002": "1): through experience",
"blomberg-s01-003": "2): through our own exercises and 3): through instruction",
"blomberg-s01-004": "Through learning the spirit of imitation arises. This learning takes place in childhood. There is indeed some skill that one brings about through imitation; but he who imitates shows the lowest degree of capacity.",
"blomberg-s01-005": "The first origin of our knowledge is empirical. Many things arise through imitation and many cognitions through imitation (imitatio).",
"blomberg-s01-006": "We see what others have shown us how to do, and we do it after them.",
"blomberg-s01-007": "The second kind is the exercise of our acquired skill. Through frequent exercises of our insights and talents we arrive at a greater cognition and at new insights, and these can be enlarged still further through our own experience.",
"blomberg-s01-008": "The last is to arrive at a cognition through general rules; these are derived from reason.",
"blomberg-s01-009": "The first method of arriving at cognitions must fall away immediately, as soon as the faculties have obtained the capacity to cultivate themselves (excoliren). One must seek to think for oneself and to judge for oneself.",
"blomberg-s01-010": "This kind of exercise of the faculties attains the same degree even if one thinks contrary to what others have thought.",
"blomberg-s01-011": "The natural understanding has its own laws, according to which alone it can proceed. These are called natural laws.",
"blomberg-s01-012": "Besides the natural laws there are also precepts which are for the artificial understanding. The first is to proceed entirely without laws, and to make use neither of the natural [ ... ] laws nor of the precepts. If one wants to cultivate (excoliren) one's own reason in this way, one first makes use of the natural laws of reason. This happens when one thinks often, or through exercises and frequent attempts, when one considers an object according to particular as well as general laws.",
"blomberg-s01-013": "The second use of the understanding takes place according to artificial rules, or according to precepts, and from this the artificial understanding arises.",
"blomberg-s01-014": "One learns to walk first by being led; so too one learns to think by first of all imitating, and then one begins to use one's feet by oneself and alone. Thus we too begin to judge for ourselves, and to use our understanding for judging by ourselves through frequent and self-repeated attempts.",
"blomberg-s01-015": "The merely natural laws are cultivated (excoliret) through experience, but for the artificial use I must have other rules. First one must imitate, then exercise oneself, and last one receives precepts.",
"blomberg-s01-016": "Through imitation we can acquire handicrafts, but through precepts a science. Versification can, as it were, be compared with handicrafts.",
"blomberg-s01-017": "We learn to judge beautifully through rules, but to compose poetry beautifully through frequent exercises.",
"blomberg-s01-018": "All cognition takes place according to rules: these are either merely natural or artificial.",
"blomberg-s01-019": "The natural rules concern the common and sound understanding and sound reason.",
"blomberg-s01-020": "The artificial rules are the rules of learnedness. The use of the former we acquire not through imitation, but through the frequent use of our reason, whereby the common understanding is cultivated (excoliret).",
"blomberg-s01-021": "The use of the common understanding is that which concerns objects of experience.",
"blomberg-s01-022": "The artificial is that which proceeds according to precepts and rules.",
"blomberg-s01-023": "Nowadays people talk much of sound understanding; it is praised too highly. Some have believed that sound reason is distinguished from the sciences merely in degree. But this gives no distinct boundary where the one stops and the other begins. Sound reason and learnedness are distinguished not merely in degree but also in species (Specie); there are two distinct sources, one for each kind.",
"blomberg-s01-024": "A learned person is one whose learnedness is not grounded on common experience. All understanding is called, according to common usage of speech, a common understanding, insofar as it is found in the majority in equal degree.",
"blomberg-s01-025": "But it is a sound understanding when it is correct. It is not sound according to degrees, but rather the correctness is that understanding and reason judge according to such laws as agree with experience. But if there are no other principles (Principia) than experiences, then a sound reason remains nonetheless a common reason. There is also an instructed understanding, which could not have been acquired through common experiences; and this is a science. There is a logic (Logica) of the common understanding and of sound reason, and a logic of learnedness. It serves to enrich the common and sound understanding, and to cultivate (excoliren) this sound reason up to learnedness.",
"blomberg-s01-026": "The logic of sound reason will have one's own experience as its principle (Principio), and will thus be empirical; it will not contain the rules how one ought to think, but will indicate the rules according to which one commonly thinks. There are two kinds of rules: A:) the rules according to which one proceeds; these are laws of appearance, and subjective. These subjective laws are the laws of reason according to which it commonly proceeds in judging and thinking. B:) Objective laws, according to which the understanding ought to proceed. These are called dogmata.",
"blomberg-s01-027": "The subjective laws set forth the errors. One extends sound reason by attending to the rules according to which the understanding ought to proceed.",
"blomberg-s01-028": "The objective laws we extend through doctrines; but in order to cultivate (excoliren) sound reason, one must give no general rules and precepts; learned reason is not grounded on empirical principles (Principia).",
"blomberg-s01-029": "The common understanding is the understanding to judge according to laws of experience; the common reason, however, is the reason to infer according to laws of experience. One calls this reason common because it is the lowest degree, in all objects that thus come before us. On occasion of experience we always have a yardstick with which we compare magnitudes with one another. Thus we proceed when we want to find the mean magnitude; namely, we take that of which we want to find the mean magnitude, e.g. man: we count each man's height by the yardstick, add them together, and divide by the number of men whom we have never seen in our life, and then the mean magnitude comes out, which is appropriate to every man. So too the mean magnitude of understanding is brought out, from the understanding that one knows through common experience. The common understanding is the measure. The common and sound understanding is a small but correct understanding, and its cognition has great usefulness. In a stretch of country of peasants far fewer errors are found than in a district of the learned of equal size. For the peasant always goes by the guiding thread of experience: he judges about little, but about that little correctly. The exercises of common and sound reason are also easy; all cognition from experience has more life and more of the intuitive than that which has arisen from general concepts. The first advantage of common reason is simplicity. The second is correctness. The third usefulness. The fourth ease and the fifth clarity and liveliness. For the cognitions arise with the light with which the representations of experience are endowed. Sound reason will accordingly be sufficient for the beautiful sciences.",
"blomberg-s01-030": "Taste actually requires no learnedness at all in order to be quite perfect. The learned taste is in fact already a false, a corrupted taste.",
"blomberg-s01-031": "What is beautiful must please universally and everyone. For the judging of beauty experience is required, and the judgment of the beautiful and the ugly is made in accordance with common and sound reason. In all learnedness common and sound reason lies at the ground; it becomes ridiculous if it is not grafted onto this stock. In history one needs no sound understanding; the same holds of many mathematical propositions themselves. But in the philosophical and all other sciences, where not merely an imitation of another's understanding suffices, but the spirit of genius itself is necessary, the common understanding is indispensable.",
"blomberg-s01-032": "The use of all cognitions of the learned and the application of them is possible only through sound reason. To be sure, someone can also be learned without a sound understanding, but that is a ridiculous pedant. All learnedness is either historical or rational. Historical is the cognition that is borrowed, as to form, from common reason, but as to matter from common experiences. Historical learnedness contains history, geography, etc.; a historically learned person can also make reflections, but these already belong to rational learnedness. The matter in rational learnedness can also be grasped through common reason and experience. The form, however, is wholly different. Human beings find in the capacity to be in a position to create some pleasure for themselves more amusement than in the pleasure itself. When I abstract the particular from the general, I need understanding; but when I infer the general from particular cases, then I need another method. The understanding cognizes everything a priori, sound reason a posteriori. We have cognitions that are empirical, where we ascend from below upward, and in rational ones we descend from above. Logic, which is an organon of rational learnedness, does not concern historical but rational cognitions. Logic, which is an organon, can prescribe rules for a learnedness already at hand, and then it is called Logica Critica, or else rules by which one can attain learnedness, and then it is called Logica Dogmatica; all logics are not dogmatic but critical. Philosophy requires more genius than imitation.",
"blomberg-s01-033": "All of nature moves according to rules; thus water moves according to hydraulic rules; nature works according to rules; even unsteady weather has its certain rules, though we do not notice them. Animals move according to rules of which they are often not even conscious. Man acts according to rules, and makes use of the understanding in particular according to certain propositions and rules. How often do human beings also act according to rules without being conscious of them? E.g. they speak their mother tongue. Often an exercise of our power takes place without our being conscious of it, and indeed first (1mo) because this already lies in our nature. Second, it often happens through imitation, by means of an example which we imitate, in order gradually to use the understanding as we see that others use it.",
"blomberg-s01-034": "Third: one's own use. One's own exercise brings us to the skill of using the understanding without being conscious of its rules.",
"blomberg-s01-035": "As man learns to walk, so he also learns to think. This understanding so formed is sens commun (common sense). When it is correct, it is called the sound understanding, because the skill of using it is subject to a common use. The frequent use of our understanding on objects of experience finally makes us fit to use it correctly. It can therefore become correct through mere exercise and use. The sound understanding is supposed to be common; the understanding, therefore, that goes further than its senses teach it in experience is not common; that would be the learned use of the understanding. The sound understanding therefore concerns experience; it must proceed according to rules of which it is itself conscious. It is therefore not undisciplined; only it does not cognize these rules in abstracto but in concreto. Thus a sound, alert understanding belongs to many trades.",
"blomberg-s01-036": "The causes of things do not fall so much under the senses as their effects do. One must therefore also know the rules according to which one can use the sound understanding. In many cases one cannot rely on one's own exercise; but the sole teacher of the sound understanding is experience. The sound understanding and sound reason are therefore, first, of great necessity, in order to lay them at the ground of all abstract higher cognitions. One commonly opposes sound reason to affected learnedness, to proud school wisdom. But this will not do, for learnedness always presupposes the sound understanding; art presupposes nature. They are indeed distinct but not opposed. But it has become fashionable to praise the sound understanding to the detriment of the honor of the sciences. To be sure, it is no reproach if one denies someone science; but if someone has no sound understanding, he is a natural minor, a child. From everyone, therefore, we demand at least sound understanding.",
"blomberg-s01-037": "There are certain middle kinds of perfections; the beauty of a woman, e.g., is as it were a merit, ugliness displeases, but a tolerable appearance is as it were the middle thing that one demands of a person. Beauty and ugliness distinguish people especially. So it is too with the height of people. Our eyes form a certain idea (Ideam) of the mean measure, and what is peculiar, people for the most part agree in it. Thus, e.g., one speaks of a person's means. The mean measure between wealth and poverty we call being well-off; we therefore always make a mean measure the yardstick in all our concepts. So too the sound understanding is the mean measure among the capacities of human beings. Sound reason is not to go one step further than experience leads it. The sound understanding cognizes and judges not in abstracto but in concreto; it draws its judgments from experiences. Nevertheless it can also cognize much of what experience teaches us universally a priori and in abstracto. The sound understanding is therefore the faculty to judge correctly in concreto. Often very reasonable people cannot judge in abstracto at all, though they think in concreto just as often; so it is in moral cases, e.g. through an example in an occurrence one cognizes things in concreto. The understanding that can judge in abstracto is called the finer scientific understanding. The natural faculty for judging is the understanding, the faculty for inferring is reason. Through experience one becomes prudent, but not learned. Prudence does not consist in science; one can be and become prudent without any learnedness. The sound understanding and reason show their utility especially in practice (Praxi). A certain lack of the sound understanding and of its application in concreto no science can in the least replace or compensate, although it can otherwise replace much. Our general judgments alone cannot help us. With all rules I must know whether they can be applied to the case",
"blomberg-s01-038": "Second: how I am to apply them here; thus there can be very learned people without any sound understanding. There is therefore, as it were, in the use of the understanding something that nature has reserved for genius, and which no art can replace.",
"blomberg-s01-039": "Through all too great a fullness of learnedness people often become the more insipid and wholly unfit to judge in concreto. To the sound understanding, which is small but correct, belongs: 1. Simplicity. It holds to the ground of experience, and does not love chimerical ideals. This very simplicity makes the understanding more correct, more certain, and more reliable than science. An academy of sciences at Paris hatches a thousand times more errors than a village full of peasants. 2. Its cognitions are intuitive through examples and instances. It represents the objects (Objecta) to us in nature, whereas the finer understanding shows only the shadow of things. The sound understanding is the controller of learnedness. The common sound understanding grows without instruction. Thus the common living languages are distinguished from the learned dead languages.",
"blomberg-s01-040": "To give general rules by which one could judge the infallibility of a witness requires more than mere common and sound understanding; it requires art and learnedness. Languages existed before grammars of them, orators before rhetoric, poets before poetics (Poesia).",
"blomberg-s01-041": "When I have general cognitions of the understanding, and also its general rules, I must nonetheless in addition have understanding in particular, in order to judge whether this or that falls under this rule or not. All doctrine in the whole world is only a general sum of the rules for using the powers of the understanding; but there must nevertheless be an understanding present, which nature alone can give. Persons who possess learnedness but lack a sound understanding are far more insipid than wholly ignorant ones. Pedantry arises from this. A stubborn blockhead cannot be called a pedant. Learnedness must always be present, but the capacity to apply the cognized general rules in concreto to particular cases must be lacking. Although, to be sure, a pedant with all his science always retains a kind of stupidity. So it is, e.g., with all the contributions of witty ideas in company, with compliment-making even according to general prescription. The Chinese have books of compliments; general rules always testify to the weakness of our understanding. As a lexicon of a language finds in a beginner much regularity without consciousness of the rules, so it is with the sound understanding. The use of the understanding according to rules of which one is conscious is called science. There are cases where one person without science can go further than another with science, but also where the greatest sound understanding cannot go as far as science alone. Thus in mathematics and surveying one cannot get on through mere understanding; some science must always be present. So it is also with medicine. For the judging of morality, of right and wrong, on the other hand, no science and learnedness is needed.",
"blomberg-s01-042": "The human race would otherwise be very imperfect. Here the sound understanding is the judge of science.",
"blomberg-s01-043": "In all sciences a precept serves well for the development of our capacities for all occurrences. But in every instruction in art it is nevertheless always necessarily left to the understanding of the subject whether, and in what degree, these general precepts are applied in individual cases. Thus one cannot possibly become prudent according to universally valid rules and precepts, for prudence consists precisely in this, that one can subsume individual cases in common life under the general rules of it.",
"blomberg-s01-044": "The discipline that contains the rules of the good use of the understanding and of reason in general is called logic (Logica). It determines no individual cases; in all other sciences logic is applied. Logic stands to the whole use of the understanding exactly as grammar (Gramatica) does to a language. Grammar has a source of the correctness of the rules, but the proof lies in experience.",
"blomberg-s01-045": "Logic shows the rules of the use of the understanding and reason which can themselves be cognized a priori and without experience, since they do not depend on it. The understanding here has insight into its own rules, and makes of them a discipline, an instruction, which can however be cognized a priori, and hence it is called a doctrine.",
"blomberg-s01-046": "In logic reason is not merely applied; rather reason is its object, the object of its consideration in use (in usu).",
"blomberg-s01-047": "There are many cognitions whose form is not rational, and then it is no doctrine of reason. So, for example, it is with the science of taste.",
"blomberg-s01-048": "Philosophy is in general the rational science (Vernunft-Wißenschaft) of the qualities of things. By the former it is distinguished from the historical, by the other, however, from the mathematical sciences. But it is called a science of reason because it presents cognitions in abstracto.",
"blomberg-s01-049": "For the establishment of the first science, e.g. such as logic is, nothing else is needed than the common sound understanding.",
"blomberg-s01-050": "Logic is called, with a particular name properly belonging to it, the science of reason (Vernunft-Wißenschaft), because reason is its object. The many other rational sciences (Scientias rationales) should be called sciences of reason.",
"blomberg-s01-051": "Logic is called a science because its rules can be proved a priori by themselves without any use. For that reason neither grammar nor aesthetics is a science. And there is really no science of the beautiful at all, properly speaking, but what we ever know of the beautiful is nothing but a critique (Critica).",
"blomberg-s01-052": "Logic (Logica) will therefore have no other grounds and source than the nature of the human understanding. There are always, of every cognition, and thus also of logic, 1. .. a) objective and b) subjective grounds. In every science one studies by rights the object, what belongs to its greatest perfection, but also ... 2. the subject, i.e. the means, the substrata, to attain to the object. And so I must also in the sciences of the understanding first of all study man; but since this happens seldom, or by few, there arise from it, e.g., the many faults especially in morals, which most moralists, who can indeed speak excellently of virtue, praise, recommend and describe it, are unable to instill in their listeners and to produce in them the desire and love to practice it, because they have not studied man.",
"blomberg-s01-053": "Logic (Logica) is a good philosophy about the good use of reason and its rules. It considers, therefore, not only the nature of human reason alone but also in particular its precepts.",
"blomberg-s01-054": "The nature of the human understanding, developed from experience, is treated by psychology (Psychologia). It will therefore also have its sources, i.e. we shall have to learn from psychology and experience many subjective laws of the use of reason.",
"blomberg-s01-055": "We can therefore, first (1mo), make the subjective laws of our understanding, according to which we actually use the understanding, the object of our consideration.",
"blomberg-s01-056": "That science which examines the subjective laws, as distinct from the rules, of our use of understanding and reason is called psychology (Psychologia).",
"blomberg-s01-057": "Logic does not properly contain the rules according to which man actually thinks, but the rules how man ought to think. For man often uses his understanding and thinks otherwise than he ought to think and to use his understanding.",
"blomberg-s01-058": "Logic therefore contains in itself the objective laws of the understanding and of reason. So, often the portrayal of a good republic is so opposed to an actually existing republic that it accurately contains the opposite of it.",
"blomberg-s01-059": "We have therefore at bottom only two objective doctrines, for there are only two objective powers of the soul: the power of cognition and the faculty of desire. Logic, then, as well as morals, have as their object a praxis (Praxin), an exercise, the use of our understanding, the application of our free power of choice. For that reason, however, they are not yet called practical sciences.",
"blomberg-s01-060": "We can have a theoretical as well as a practical logic and morals. Disciplines are practical when their effects are practical.",
"blomberg-s01-061": "Logic is not a mere critique; it is a real doctrine that can be proved. Its rules are taken and proved in abstracto. But every doctrine is not yet always practical; it can be a pure theory. Now if logic is a mere theory of the conditions under which a cognition is perfect, according to laws of the understanding and of reason, then it is no means of [31a] execution; it would be a theory but no organon.",
"blomberg-s01-062": "There is a logic that is called a scientia propaedeutica, an introductory science.",
"blomberg-s01-063": "and so it will serve us as a means of critique or as an organon; but that it is not; it only sharpens the understanding in judging about cognitions",
"blomberg-s01-064": "Logic therefore contains the rules for the use of our understanding and our reason. Everything else therefore stands under it. It paves the way for all the other sciences.",
"blomberg-s02-001": "There are many rules of motion, and of the understanding; every ship, every fish orients itself by the rules of motion. We ourselves are subject to these laws. We observe them in walking and in other movements. Nature has implanted these laws in human beings and animals. But in regard to them we do nothing more than fulfil them, ignorant like the animals.",
"blomberg-s02-002": "It would be unnecessary if one wanted to spy out these laws of motion, and to take no step without deliberation. As the fish in its swimming observes these rules most exactly through practice, so we too can acquire a skill in observing them only through practice. How quickly do we know how to help ourselves when we are about to fall? But this skill would be lacking to us if we wanted to deliberate beforehand what posture we should assume.",
"blomberg-s02-003": "Borellus wrote a book on regular motion.",
"blomberg-s02-004": "Besides the laws and rules of motion, however, there are also rules and laws of the understanding. All our judgments we arrange according to these laws. As a child in walking learns to observe the laws of motion, so it also seeks in judging to form itself according to the laws of the understanding. But it does all this unknowingly, and attains the skill merely through practice.",
"blomberg-s02-005": "For common cognition it is not necessary that we be conscious of these rules and deliberate upon them. We would lose very much if we wanted to do that. But if our understanding wants to have ascended to learned cognition, it must be conscious of its rules and use them after deliberation, because common practice is not enough for it here. These rules, which the understanding has to observe in learned observation or cognition, are prescribed to us by logic, which we now want to present.",
"blomberg-s02-006": "The author defines it quite rightly: it is a science that treats of the rules of learned cognition and of learned presentation. A shorter definition would be: Logic is a science that teaches us the use of the understanding in learned cognition.",
"blomberg-s02-007": "It is also called philosophia instrumentalis (instrumental philosophy), because it can be regarded as, so to speak, an instrument (instrumentum) of all the other philosophical sciences.",
"blomberg-s02-008": "Through frequent observations the rules of the understanding have been spied out. Aristotle already laid down some; but these were nothing other than signposts to wrong paths. It has already cost much effort to forget such false propositions, to give the understanding its natural perfection again and to investigate its true rules.",
"blomberg-s02-009": "A correct logic is like a straight line, so that one must deviate from it neither to the right nor to the left. It were to be wished that logic had already been brought to such correctness and perfection.",
"blomberg-s02-010": "The author indicates to us in this paragraph from what we must derive its rules if we want to make it so perfect.",
"blomberg-s02-011": "Namely, the following can serve for this",
"blomberg-s02-012": "1. the experiences of the effects of human reason. Bacon of Verulam (see his life in Formey's History of Philosophy, p. 293 et seq., and in Gentzken's Historia Philosophica, pp. 156, 157) was the first to show the world that all philosophy consists of chimeras if it does not rest on experiences.",
"blomberg-s02-013": "It would be just as necessary to write an experimental logic as a physics, in which one should investigate how man can err through prejudices and haste, and also in other ways, so that rules can be prescribed to him how he is to guard against them.",
"blomberg-s02-014": "One should furthermore make observations how one can judge most correctly, and also prescribe rules for this in logic (Logica).",
"blomberg-s02-015": "2. we must look to the nature of human reason, so that the rules can be established in conformity with it.",
"blomberg-s02-016": "It would be foolish to arrange logic for rational beings in general, or even for angels, and to think that it could also be of use to us. That would be just as if I wanted to give rules and precepts to a lame man who had to walk on crutches, how he should walk and run like a healthy one. 3. our rules must conform to the general fundamental truths of human cognition, with which ontology (Ontologia) deals. These fundamental truths are the principles (Principia) of all sciences, and consequently of logic (Logica) too. We must remark here that at the beginning of philosophy we are not yet in a position to deliver a perfect logic (Logicam); insights are required for that, which can be used only with such listeners as are acquainted with the whole of worldly wisdom. We shall here strive only to give preliminary rules for learned cognition, whereby one will make the learning of the other sciences easier.",
"blomberg-s02-017": "The aim of the doctrine of reason can be divided into the immediate and the mediate. The immediate is the improvement of learned cognition, the mediate that of common cognition. The latter can happen when one seeks in daily intercourse to improve people's concepts, whereby, however, one must not come with scholastic terms (Terminis) if one does not want to be called a pedant.",
"blomberg-s02-018": "As regards the immediate aim of logic, namely the perfection of learned cognition, this concerns the learned by profession as well as others. Everyone can improve his learned cognition through it as much as he wants. The learned by profession are distinguished from others by certain technical terms (Terminos Technicos), and also by the fact that learnedness is their foremost occupation, whereas by the others, e.g. by kings, it is for the most part pursued only as a side-instrument.",
"blomberg-s02-019": "It would be fair, since the multitude of sciences is now so great, that each person be assigned his particular field, since it is impossible to make oneself perfect in all sciences.",
"blomberg-s02-020": "Among the persons who are not learned by profession one could also count women, who can also occupy themselves with learnedness in accordance with their capacities.",
"blomberg-s02-021": "There are sciences for which a keen sense, much reflection, and profundity are required. These are for the male sex. On the other hand there are also such sciences for which wit and a kind of feeling are required, and these are suitable for women.",
"blomberg-s02-022": "Here worldly wisdom (philosophy) is spoken of. Its object is the general qualities and constitutions of things. But it is well to note that philosophy does not look to quotiens aliquid positum sit? (how many times something is posited) but only to quod positum sit? (that it is posited); it considers only the qualities, it weighs only what is posited. Mathematics (Mathese), on the other hand, investigates how many times the thing is posited, and investigates the quantities (Quantitates) of things, how great they are. Philosophy furthermore does not consider the whole of the qualities, not the whole constitution of things, and all of them, but only those which are found in many things, and are therefore called general.",
"blomberg-s02-023": "Philosophy is a science, but one must not therefore think that in philosophy one must always demonstrate and define. Mathematics has this about it.",
"blomberg-s02-024": "The definition of our author which he gives of philosophy (Philosophia) is the following: worldly wisdom, he says, is a science of the general constitutions of things, insofar as they are cognized without faith. One is also accustomed otherwise to say: Philosophia est Scientia, quae circa rerum rationes versatur (philosophy is the science that is concerned with the reasons of things). But since worldly wisdom occupies itself not merely with the grounds of things but also often with their constitutions simply, one easily sees that this definition is incorrect. A higher use of reason belongs to philosophy; but it is difficult to determine where philosophy begins and where it ends; just as the boundaries between poverty and wealth, e.g., are to be determined. A man is rich, one says, e.g. if he has 50000 or 20000 Rthlr., but at what sum of money does he cease to be rich, and when does he become rich? – Just as difficult are the boundaries between avarice and thrift to be determined. At one point philosophy must separate itself from the common use of reason, poverty from wealth, stinginess from thrift; but where this point is, is difficult to say.",
"blomberg-s02-025": "Now we want to consider the limitations of the author; he says: the constitutions of things must be cognized in philosophy without faith.",
"blomberg-s02-026": "The kind of our cognition is threefold. We cognize something",
"blomberg-s02-027": "1. through experience, which takes place with events, and in general with many objects of history.",
"blomberg-s02-028": "2. through reason, which abstracts the general cognitions from individual things, and",
"blomberg-s02-029": "3. through faith in another's testimony. In this last way we know that Paris, London, etc. are in the world [43a], although we have seen none of them. We also do not doubt it at all, since we rely on the testimony of others who have been there. In philosophy faith has no place; here no one must rely on the testimony of others; even if someone wanted to swear to us the immortality of the soul, we would nevertheless not believe or trust him without proofs, because in philosophy faith has no place. How, then, does the name of a philosopher come to him who is a blind follower of another? Pythagoras's pupils accepted for only 5 years everything their teacher said to them, and these supposed philosophers do it their whole life long, and always say αὐτος ἔφα (he himself said it).",
"blomberg-s02-030": "If simply nothing but faith were in philosophy, it could not be possible that now this one, now that one were set on the throne of the sciences, and such changes of fashion occurred in the learned horizon. As great lords keep people who are learned for them, who conduct the sciences, give counsels, have a good heart for them, etc. etc., while they themselves occupy themselves merely with digestion. So Wolff (Wolffius), and other true philosophers, have done preparatory work for some supposed philosophers, and laid the foundation, and these have need of nothing more than to make themselves acquainted with it.",
"blomberg-s02-031": "If we want to divide philosophy (Philosophiam), we must presuppose that the actions of our soul consist of cognition, feeling and desire. The science that treats of the use of the understanding is",
"blomberg-s02-032": "Logic (Logica), which speaks of the general objects of the understanding, is",
"blomberg-s02-033": "Metaphysics (Metaphysica), which deals with the corporeal objects, is",
"blomberg-s02-034": "Physics (Physica), which deals with feeling, is",
"blomberg-s02-035": "Aesthetics (Aesthetica), and the science that has to do with our actions and desires is called",
"blomberg-s02-036": "Morals or practical philosophy (Philosophia practica).",
"blomberg-s02-037": "The first philosophy (Philosophia) began without doubt where the objects could not fall under the senses, and this kind of objects were without doubt the objects of religion.",
"blomberg-s02-038": "Up to xxiv 35: Suppl. excerpt from Formey, Kurzgefassete Histoire der Philosophie (1763), cf. pp. 33-40, 78-71, 174-181.",
"blomberg-s02-039": "As regards the history of philosophy, the following is to be noted: the Chaldeans had a philosopher in Zoroaster, and among the Persians a Zoroaster is likewise known, who however is wholly different from the Chaldean. The philosophy (Philosophia) of the Chaldeans was very obscure and uncertain. (Theology therefore first extended worldly wisdom, ad pag. 44) It ascends to the highest antiquity, and what can be brought out about it stems from very suspect reports. One must therefore be very cautious if one does not want to mix the doctrines of the ancients with the fictions spread in later times. The religion of these peoples was a mixture of the most absurd superstition, a crude and abominable idolatry. The priests deceived the stupid people by all sorts of tricks. Soothsayings, enchantments, interpretations of dreams, childish and extravagant ceremonies made up the whole. If something good lay under this crude rind, it is impossible now to pick it out. This worldly wisdom therefore has the appearance of the most perfect folly, but it must also have been a dangerous folly; for from the earliest times of the Roman monarchy the arts of the Chaldeans were banished as suspect and godless. But notwithstanding such obscurity of the Chaldean philosophy, it was nevertheless very famous in all antiquity. Its adherents divided themselves into many sects, such as: the Hipparenians, Babylonians, Orchenians, Marsyperians, Borsippenians, etc. Zoroaster is given out as the author of magic, i.e. as the father of the crudest of all deceptions and of the most shameful superstition. It is said: he was killed by fire from heaven. Belus is said to have instructed the priests in astronomy (Astronomia) and physics (Physica). Berosus, Marmaridius, Zabrates, and Teucrus are known to us merely by their names alone.",
"blomberg-s02-040": "All philosophies of the oldest peoples were to be found among the priests. The Egyptians and Persians were probably the first whose understanding overstepped its limit and began to make speculations (speculationes). Astrology (Astrologia) and cosmology (Cosmologia) arose earlier than the physical sciences. The very first object of investigation was the origin of things, for these are without doubt the most important questions, and ones which must have occurred to human beings at once, after they had first provided for the necessities of their bodies. However, to be sure, this first inquiry will have contained more superstition than worldly wisdom. This much is certain: before philosophy (Philosophia) in a nation had wholly and entirely separated itself from the power of the government and the rule of the clergy, no worldly wisdom could be rightly constituted.",
"blomberg-s02-041": "The originator of the Persian philosophy (Philosophiae) was Zerdusch or Zoroaster. He lived at the time of the Babylonian captivity; he was the head of the religion. He collected his doctrine in a work called Zendavesta. His oracle is a book falsely ascribed to him by the Neoplatonists. The priests of the Persians were called Magi, and their head Archimagus. The doctrines of their philosophy (Philosophiae) were the following. The supreme God is the intellectual fire: from this, in order to produce the world, two mutually opposed fundamental beings or principles (Principia) proceeded. The foremost being, from which all others proceed, on which the law of emanations also depends, is called Mithra. The two fundamental beings are Oromasdes, a very pure, active and spiritual light, and Arimanius, the passive and material darkness, which was born from the limitation of the light, and is bound up with it as a necessary consequence. From the mixture of these two fundamental beings all things beneath the moon have proceeded, and thus one must explain the origin of evil on earth. But a time will come when the darkness will be overcome by the light and annihilated. The soul, in their opinion, came from the gods, and was, like them, immortal. Their moral teachings recommended chastity, uprightness, fairness, flight from pleasures. They expressed this thus: You must follow the light, and guard yourselves against the plague of darkness or the generation of matter.",
"blomberg-s02-042": "Among the Chinese we note 3 great philosophers: Confucius, Keilau and Janzu, whom they worshipped under a three-headed idol image. They held the opinion of Pythagoras about the transmigration of souls, worship sun, moon and stars, indeed not seldom the devil, so that he may not harm them. They therefore set his image on the front part of their ships, and wear frightful heads on their clothes. Among the Chinese all philosophy (Philosophia) is nothing but morals, some astronomy and mathematics, and a kind of political science and art of government. Confucius was a Solon of the Chinese.",
"blomberg-s02-043": "Among the Jews we note, before the destruction of Jerusalem, some sects, such as 1. the Sadducees, who sprang from Antigonus Sochaeus. Out of vexation at the doctrine of good works, by which one can earn nothing with God, he fell into the complete opposite, and said: one must serve God without any hope of future reward. Zadok and Boethos misunderstood this doctrine; they therefore denied the resurrection of the bodies, and all rewards after this life.",
"blomberg-s02-044": "2: the Karaites, Karaites or scribes, who rejected all allegorical interpretations of scripture but otherwise accepted all Sadducean propositions. They adhered to the school of Chamai.",
"blomberg-s02-045": "3: the sect of the Pharisees; it has its name from the Hebrew word parash, which means separated people. For they believed they were very different from the people, partly through their science, which however consisted only in false explanations of the law, partly through meritorious works, which however were nothing more than observances of external ceremonies and superstitious acts. Their doctrine was chiefly supported by the school of Hillel.",
"blomberg-s02-046": "4: the sect of the Essenes. These departed most from the Jewish customs. The persecutions of the Syrian kings, or rather Cambyses' invasion, gave them their origin. Their way of life was very similar to the monastic orders. They served God in a very purely spiritual manner; they had set hours in which they performed certain duties.",
"blomberg-s02-047": "5. the sect of the Therapeutae. They flourished in Egypt, and were wholly monk-like.",
"blomberg-s02-048": "Of the times after the destruction of Jerusalem and of the Temple we note the rabbis: Gamaliel and Judah, called the Holy, the foremost originators of the Talmudic doctrine. The Cabbalistic doctrine is in the mysterious books Happlijah, Ha-Bahir, and Sohar. The rabbis Akiba and Simeon, son of Sohar, propagated it, but as a secret doctrine. Finally the philosophy of Aristotle mixed itself in, although all the rabbis set themselves against it. Proofs of this are found in the work Cuzzeri. The famous rabbi Moses Maimonides was very practised in it. This mixed doctrine has very much similarity with positive and moral theology (Theologia), which was founded on the explanation of the divine law. Rabbi Saudius brought the former into a system of doctrine. Moses Maimonides gave it a more philosophical turn, and made 13 articles of faith out of it, which he called roots. Finally the Jews in Egypt adopted the allegorical method, and the very philosophy of that country, namely the concept of emanations, which consisted of the mixture of the opinions of Zoroaster, Pythagoras, and others. All this they sought, as well as possible, to combine with their own opinions. Among the Therapeutae this doctrine was already in vogue, and Philo followed it. The fear of the Jews in the 2nd century of being dispersed moved them to set this doctrine down in writing, and so they propagated these errors ever further. See more of this in Formey's History of Philosophy, pp. 72, 184.",
"blomberg-s02-049": "The philosophy of the ancients has this fault about it, that they did not make observations at all as is done now. So it went with the mathematicians (Mathematicis), whose ancient writings are nevertheless so priceless, except that mathematics was not combined with observations of nature, which afterwards became the cause of so many discoveries.",
"blomberg-s02-050": "The ancients had no geography at all, or only a very imperfect one.",
"blomberg-s02-051": "It was among the Greeks that the philosophers were instructed in all the propositions and doctrines and in the manner and aim of government of their people, and where they did something toward its perfection. As crude as worldly wisdom came to the Greeks, so cultivated (excolirt) was it handed on again by them to other peoples. The merit of the Greeks in all sciences of the understanding and of taste is therefore the greatest. The government of the Greeks was formed through the schools of the philosophers. But among them we note in particular the so-called 7 Sages. They are as follows:",
"blomberg-s02-052": "1st, Thales, who among all deserves to be called a sage on account of his mathematics",
"blomberg-s02-053": "2nd, Solon, a fine mind, good poet, skilful general, and excellent lawgiver. His motto was: Consider the end.",
"blomberg-s02-054": "3rd, Chilon became visible and respected among the Lacedaemonians through his justice and patience. His motto was: do not learn too much, but know yourself.",
"blomberg-s02-055": "4. Pittacus of Mytilene, a courageous and good soldier; his motto was: know the time.",
"blomberg-s02-056": "5. Bias of Priene in Ionia, an upright, magnanimous, virtuous, and prudent man. His symbolum was: Love as if you could hate.",
"blomberg-s02-057": "6. Cleobulus, a native of Rhodes. He learned wisdom among the Egyptians. His daughter Cleobolina was the heir of his virtues and sciences. Nothing, he said, is better than moderation.",
"blomberg-s02-058": "7. Periander, a prince of the Corinthians.",
"blomberg-s02-059": "Democritus deserves to be called the first philosopher. He was the teacher of the great and renowned Epicurus, who among the ancients is what Cartesius represents among the moderns, and who improved the previous method of philosophizing. In Lucretius one finds propositions of the Epicurean philosophy. But since Lucretius, as is well known, was at times insane, he may well have distorted the propositions.",
"blomberg-s02-060": "Pyrrho was a man of great insight. He had the saying: non liquet (it is not clear), which he constantly called out to the bold sophists to dampen their pride. He was the founder of the Sceptics, who also called themselves Zetetici. But this sect finally drove doubting so far, and strayed so far, that it finally began to doubt everything, even the mathematical propositions.",
"blomberg-s02-061": "Socrates devoted himself to practical philosophy, which he demonstrated especially through his conduct.",
"blomberg-s02-062": "Pythagoras set great store by numbers; he held the number 10 to be the most perfect, others however the 4th, and thought that for this reason God had made 4 elementa. He wanted to think out everything through numbers. Hence he said among other things: Animal est Numerus se ipsum movens (an animal is a number moving itself).",
"blomberg-s02-063": "Plato was very rhetorical and obscure, so that he often did not understand himself.",
"blomberg-s02-064": "Aristotle won a blind trust for himself, and did more harm than good to philosophia.",
"blomberg-s02-065": "Zeno was the founder of the Stoic sect.",
"blomberg-s02-066": "The Romans, finally, produced no special principal sects, but followed the Greeks.",
"blomberg-s02-067": "The Arabs brought forth the doctrine of Aristotle again, which the Scholastici also followed. In some respects the more recent times have advantages over the ancients; these lacked cognitions of experience. Thus, for example, the eventus of nature, of the ebb and the flow of the tide, which was discovered in more recent times, was unknown to Aristotle and to all the Greeks. But in those days the means that are now available for attaining it were also not there. For example, to travel so quickly; and through this lack the extension of cognition of experience was especially held back; and yet these are the foremost sources from which reason is to draw.",
"blomberg-s02-068": "On the whole, the ancient philosophi were all either 1. Sceptical philosophers, and these were misologists or haters of reason, or Dogmatici, and all of these had their names from their principia. Moral philosophy, natural science and knowledge, however, were poor and false among them.",
"blomberg-s02-069": "Bacon de Verulam, who pointed out the importance of the endeavour to bring the observations of nature under certain propositions. (And even up to now the external, but not the internal, observations and phenomena of nature have been sufficiently extended.)",
"blomberg-s02-070": "Cartesius, Malebranche, Leibnitz and Wolffius, the last of whom through his industriousness brought about a systema of philosophy, were in the more recent times the improvers and proper fathers of philosophy.",
"blomberg-s02-071": "All endeavours of our philosophy are",
"blomberg-s02-072": "1 Dogmatic",
"blomberg-s02-073": "2 Critical.",
"blomberg-s02-074": "Among the critical philosophers Locke deserves the preference. Wolff, however, and the Germans generally, have a methodical philosophy.",
"blomberg-s02-075": "Finally Crusius became famous, who however, for a little that is good, has very much that is incorrect, and errs especially in that he wants to prove very many propositions merely from the nature of the understanding.",
"blomberg-s02-076": "As far as logica is concerned, Aristotle was the first who presented it, and who also invented the 4 syllogistic figures.",
"blomberg-s02-077": "The logica of the Scholastics consisted of sheer subtleties.",
"blomberg-s02-078": "Locke's book de intellectu humano (on the human understanding) is the ground of all true logica.",
"blomberg-s02-079": "At the time of the Reformation Petrus Ramus wrote a logic in France. He taught his countrymen the French to pronounce Quin, Quisquis, Quanquam not according to the French but the Latin dialect. Over this a great dispute arose between him and the teachers of the Sorbonne, so that many were dismissed merely on account of the perverse pronunciation, when this matter came to the government. After him Wolff, and also Crusius, wrote a logica, the latter of which, however, cannot make the learning of the sciences easier, but is rather so obscure that yet another logic is required to understand it.",
"blomberg-s02-080": "Now at last critical philosophy is reviving most of all, and the English have the greatest merit in it.",
"blomberg-s02-081": "For the most part the dogmatic method has almost universally fallen away in all sciences; even morals is no longer presented dogmatically, but more often critically.",
"blomberg-s02-082": "Logic deals either with the rules of certain, or of probable, learned cognition; the latter is called logica probabilium. In common life we act more according to probability than according to certainty, for which reason the logica probabilium would be very useful.",
"blomberg-s02-083": "Bernoulli did write one, but it is nothing other than a mathematics applied to games of chance. He shows how, according to the rules of probability, one could, for example, throw 8 times.",
"blomberg-s02-084": "The Logica Probabilium has merely examples, and has its use in widows' funds. But the one we mean here is supposed to extend to the experience of all human beings, and such a one does not yet exist.",
"blomberg-s02-085": "Crusius's Logica of the probable is held by many to be good; but it contains merely general reflections, which have come into repute through the learned tone.",
"blomberg-s02-086": "Just as a circle is easier to make than an ellipse, and the rules for virtue are easier to give than those for inconstancy, so too the rules for certain cognition can be found more easily than those for the probable. The grounds for certainty are determinate, those for probability however are not. But it is now to be shown how great they must be if something is to be probable, which is hard to determine.",
"blomberg-s02-087": "Logica can be divided",
"blomberg-s02-088": "1st into the theoretical",
"blomberg-s02-089": "2nd into the practical Logica",
"blomberg-s02-090": "Theoretical Logica shows us only the rules of learned cognition, the practical, however, applies these rules to particular cases.",
"blomberg-s02-091": "In this paragraph the author speaks of the use of the doctrine of reason. It promotes 1st the learning of the sciences in general. But this is to be understood only of logic in the objective sense, namely of that which is actually constituted as it ought fairly to be. A Logica, however, that is as obscure and excessively refined as that of Crusius cannot have this use.",
"blomberg-s02-092": "It improves 2nd the understanding and the reason, just as one who understands a language according to the rules cannot err as easily as one who has learned it only ex usu (by practice). Hence the latter is also not as certain of his matter as the former, and can hardly help himself in what has not yet come before him. Whereas, on the other hand, another can arrange everything according to the rules that the language prescribes to him, so too the one who has before his eyes the rules according to which he is to form his understanding, and applies them properly, will not err so easily as one who knows nothing of these rules at all.",
"blomberg-s02-093": "Logic promotes, for example, virtue. In doubtful matters one can in fact proceed according to the rules of logic and judge whether the thing is actually good or not. It has this use directly for virtue; indirectly it promotes virtue, like all speculative sciences and cognitions, in that, once a person has acquired a taste for it, it draws him away from sensible charms and at the same time instils in him a kind of decorum. If a person wants to occupy himself with speculations, he must be calm, decorous, and content with the things outside him, which can finally become habit for him with the help of logic.",
"blomberg-s03-001": "The first section. On learned cognition in general",
"blomberg-s03-002": "In this paragraph the author wanted to give a definition of representation; but since he was not able to, he helped himself out with a rhetorical flourish, as commonly happens when one cannot say anything certain. To that end he says that a representation is related like a picture which the painterly skill of the soul draws in its interior. What representation is cannot properly be explained at all. It is one of the simple concepts that we necessarily must have. Every human being knows immediately what representation is. Cognitions and representations are the same. Concepts, however, differ somewhat from them, as we shall see in what follows. Cognition and representation are taken in logic as the same. Every representation is something in us, which however refers to something else, which is the object. Certain things represent something, but we represent things to ourselves. Logic does not teach us at all how we are to represent something to ourselves through consciousness, but rather presupposes the consciousness of something already, as a matter of psychology.",
"blomberg-s03-003": "In every cognition we find something material. But also something formal.",
"blomberg-s03-004": "The object that we represent to ourselves is the object; the manner of representation, however, is called the formal. If, for example, I represent virtue to someone, I can look partly at what I represent to myself, partly at how I represent it to myself; this is the formal, that however the material, of the repraesentation. Logic, however, has for the most part to do with the formal of cognition. Cognitions, however, can have different forms in one materia. There are whole sciences whose philosophy differs from common understanding not in matter but only in form, in respect of distinctness; such is the case, for example, with morals.",
"blomberg-s03-005": "When we distinguish a representation, and its object to which it goes, from other representations, then we are conscious of the representation. Consciousness accompanies each of our states; it is as it were the intuiting of ourselves.",
"blomberg-s03-006": "Yet we are not conscious of the most, the mightiest and most powerful representations. Who would imagine that? Only to the representations of which we are conscious can we prescribe rules. Consciousness cannot be discussed at greater length here. The doctrine of it belongs properly in the Metaphysica, and to have a correct concept of it, much from the Metaphysica is laid at the ground.",
"blomberg-s03-007": "Here confused and distinct representations are discussed. If in a composite representation I distinguish from one another the parts of which it consists, that is a distinct representation. But if I do not distinguish its partial representations from one another, it is a confused representation. For example, when I see a cheese mite, my representation of it is at first confused. But if I take a microscope in hand and become aware in it of a maw full of teeth, 2 rows of feet, 2 black eyes, then I had represented all of that to myself before as well. But I could not distinguish these partial representations of the individual limbs from the whole body. Through the microscope, however, my cognition becomes distinct, in that I thereby represent to myself the individual parts of a cheese mite and distinguish them from its whole body. Likewise, when I look at the Milky Way with the naked eye, I distinguish nothing in it from anything else. I am conscious of no parts in the representation. But if I look at the Milky Way through a telescope, I see that it consists of fixed stars. These fixed stars I represent to myself as parts of the Milky Way and distinguish the representation of one fixed star from the representation of another, and hence have a distinct representation, because for such a one it is required that I be able to distinguish the partial representations from one another, and the partial representations of the fixed stars are partial representations of the Milky Way.",
"blomberg-s03-008": "A simple representation, and simple cognitions, can be neither confused nor distinct, and become so. Confusedness and distinctness therefore take place only in composite cognitions.",
"blomberg-s03-009": "A chief property of logic will therefore be to set a confused cognition apart and make it distinct.",
"blomberg-s03-010": "Indistinctness is opposed to distinctness, but confusion to order. Something can be confused although I am conscious of the partial representations and it does have distinctness. A distinct representation is not yet always orderly. The more we reduce our representations to simple ones, the more we remove the confusion.",
"blomberg-s03-011": "In the distinct representation the material is just the same as in the confused. In the one case we represent to ourselves just as much as in the other. In the confused representation we have the partial representations just as much as in the distinct. For if we did not have them, we would not have the total either.",
"blomberg-s03-012": "In the confused, however, we do not distinguish the partial representations. A cognition can be distinct only insofar as it is a total representation. Likewise only a total representation can be confused. A simple representation is neither confused nor distinct. For in it I cannot distinguish any partial representation from another. The distinct as well as the confused takes place only in total and composite representations.",
"blomberg-s03-013": "Distinctness can occur just",
"blomberg-s03-014": "1st in intuiting, when we can distinguish well the marks of what we intuit. That is distinctness in intuition.",
"blomberg-s03-015": "2. in thinking, when we connect clear concepts and representations with the intuiting. Often one can intuit something distinctly without thinking anything distinctly in doing so.",
"blomberg-s03-016": "We arrive at distinctness of intuition through greater attentiveness per Synthesin; to distinctness of concepts, however, belongs the dissection of what I think, of what I already actually conceive in thought, i.e. distinctness of the understanding per Analysin. All of morals has sheer concepts of the understanding. Empirical distinctness is acquired a posteriori through attentiveness to the objects of experience. Distinctness of reason, however, is acquired a priori through attentiveness to the actions of my understanding.",
"blomberg-s03-017": "A ground is that from which something can be cognized, and a consequence is what can be cognized from the ground. With this definition we can make do here. In the Metaphysica, however, we shall not be satisfied with it. A ground from which everything in the consequence can be understood, to which therefore nothing is lacking, is a sufficient ground, and one where only something is cognized is an insufficient ground. For example, if we say: that the moon has inhabitants, because there are mountains and valleys in it, this is an insufficient ground. One sees from it only that it is possible and probable that there are inhabitants in the moon. But if, for example, a merchant has a fortune of 100000 Rthl., and one says: 50000 Rthl. he acquired through trade, and 50000 Rthl. he inherited, this is a sufficient ground. The relation of the ground and the consequences is the connection; for example, the tree is connected with its fruits, the tree is the ground, and the fruits are the consequence.",
"blomberg-s03-018": "Every truth has its ground, i.e. that from which one can distinguish it from the false and hold it to be true. This is here in the Logica. Sufficient grounds are properly spoken of in the Metaphysica.",
"blomberg-s03-019": "If I [have?] merely the ground of a consequence, the cognition is for that reason not yet rational. For example, if one gives saltpetre as the ground of the thunderstorm. But if one sees distinctly how the consequence arises from the ground, for example how the storm comes from the saltpetre, then our cognition is rational. The author already speaks here of rational cognition without having first spoken of the distinct. He ought to have called it a cognition of reason, which itself arises from reason.",
"blomberg-s03-020": "All perfections of cognition are",
"blomberg-s03-021": "1st Aesthetic, and consist in agreement with the subjective laws and conditions.",
"blomberg-s03-022": "2nd Logical, and consist in agreement with the objective laws and conditions.",
"blomberg-s03-023": "All requisita of these perfections of cognition in general, however, are:",
"blomberg-s03-024": "1st Sensation, how I am affected by the presence of the object.",
"blomberg-s03-025": "2. Power of judgment.",
"blomberg-s03-026": "3. Spirit.",
"blomberg-s03-027": "4. Taste.",
"blomberg-s03-028": "A cognition agrees with the subject when it gives us much to think and sets our faculty into play. And to this belongs especially also ease, intuition; to this belong similia, examples, exempla. Sensation, however, has only the 2nd rank, intuition the 1st. For through sensation one cannot judge, but one can through intuition, and for that very reason the former has the lowest rank among what makes up aesthetic perfection, the latter however the highest.",
"blomberg-s03-029": "In cognition there are 2 kinds of perfections: 1. that it agrees with the constitution of the thing.",
"blomberg-s03-030": "2nd that it has an effect on our feeling and our taste. The former is a logical, the latter an aesthetic perfection, but both a formal perfection. We have a logic that makes, and can make, our cognitions logically perfect, and another that makes our cognition aesthetically perfect. The former teaches us to make representations that are in accordance with the constitution of the thing, which is what the logic does that we now want to treat. The latter, however, which contains aesthetics, must deal with the representations that have an effect on our feeling. To logical perfection belongs the distinctness of the representation, and also that it must be rational. The latter was treated in § 14.",
"blomberg-s03-031": "We can say that the cognition or representation is rational in which reason is applied, in which I cognize the grounds; but truth especially belongs to the logical perfection of a representation. Another means to objective perfection is distinctness. Thus, for example, a demonstration is logically perfect when the proofs adduced are secure, distinct, certain, and agree with the properties of the thing. A proof can also be easy, although this property does not properly refer to the object, but rather to the subject. Hence one and the same object can be hard to grasp for one subject and in turn easy for another.",
"blomberg-s03-032": "One must ordinarily sacrifice something of logical perfection if one wants to attain an aesthetic one, and expend something of the aesthetic if one wants to attain a logical perfection. Something can never be quite distinct without being somewhat difficult, and never quite lively without being somewhat indistinct, et vice versa.",
"blomberg-s03-033": "No one has ever yet been able to connect, determine, and find out accurately the right measure of aesthetic perfection with the logical. That requires much delicacy. What favours our life, i.e. what as it were sets our activity into play, that pleases. Something becomes easy for us when it is in order. Order is therefore a means to the agreement of our cognition with the object to which it goes. Furthermore, the new especially pleases us, and no less the incomprehensible, precisely because it is incomprehensible, if only it is nevertheless true. Altogether everything wondrous. Astonishment always has something agreeable for sensibility, but also something displeasing for the understanding. Hence one so gladly hears the most wondrous stories. An aesthetic perfection is a perfection according to laws of sensibility. One makes something sensible when one makes the object arouse a sensation and move, and when I make something capable of intuition. The greater art of taste consists in this, that I now make sensible what I first presented dryly, clothe it in objects of sensibility, but in such a way that the understanding loses nothing thereby. A perfection receives its worth from the fact that it can be communicated. In everything that concerns taste, sociability lies at the ground, and through this it is greatly elevated; he who chooses only what pleases himself but no one else has no taste at all. Taste can therefore not possibly be separately peculiar. The judgment about taste is therefore never a private judgment; from this we therefore see",
"blomberg-s03-034": "1st Taste must refer to the judgment of all. Taste is a judgment of the most general approval.",
"blomberg-s03-035": "2nd Taste carries with it something sociable, belonging to society.",
"blomberg-s03-036": "Sociability, however, gives life a certain taste which it otherwise lacks, and this taste itself is social. If what pleases me sensibly must directly please others too, then one says: I have taste. There are some people who have nothing social about them, although they can otherwise be honest people.",
"blomberg-s03-037": "Solitarii, eccentrics, all have no taste. In the human soul there is a certain principium that deserved to be studied much, namely that our mind is communicable and sympathetic, so that the human being both gladly communicates and lets himself be communicated to. Hence human beings so very gladly communicate with one another, and the human being rarely feels a proper pleasure if he cannot communicate himself, and his thoughts or inclinations, to someone. Our knowing is nothing if others do not know that we know it. Hence it comes that the human being tests his judgment against the judgment of others, and it pleases him at once if it agrees with his own. It is therefore quite perverse to say: taste is a certain private judgment of a human being about what delights him, what pleases him. Such a human being has quite and altogether no taste. Taste also has generally valid laws, but can these laws be cognized in abstracto a priori? No, but in concreto; because the laws of taste are properly not laws of the understanding but general laws of sensibility. Taste itself is an object about which we can reason subtly in many ways. This subtle reasoning, however, does not constitute taste, but only increases it. The judgment of taste is: One judges of this or that thing through and according to the look of it.",
"blomberg-s03-038": "If a human being has taste, he sees what he chooses. That pleases almost everyone, and throughout.",
"blomberg-s03-039": "The norm, the guideline (how something ought to be) of taste, this is not contained in the general rules of reason, but is found nowhere else than in the actual works of taste. There must therefore be lasting models of taste, otherwise taste will soon cease. So too there must likewise be lasting models of the manner of writing in a dead (learned) language, for these alone are unchangeable, i.e. the Latin and Greek languages. A priori much can be explained and cognized, and yet afterwards not be practised at all.",
"blomberg-s03-040": "The human being is polished, rubbed smooth, not through instruction but merely through social intercourse, and especially through intercourse with well-mannered and well-bred women.",
"blomberg-s03-041": "Genius and taste are to be distinguished from one another very much. Genius works, so to speak, in the rough, yet on excellent things. It would be desirable that school instruction proceed so:",
"blomberg-s03-042": "1.) to work in the rough, but on what is sublime",
"blomberg-s03-043": "2.) and then to polish.",
"blomberg-s03-044": "But there everything must be and become polished at once, and more attention is paid to trifles and side matters than to the chief matters, and moreover compulsion is even used. Genius, on the other hand, is seldom cultivated. The harm, however, which such school methods cause thereby is very great. For example, in elegant Latin. One never looks at the thoughts, but always only at the expression, at the words.",
"blomberg-s03-045": "Only through social intercourse and society is the human being polished. Models, models worthy of imitation, are necessary in order to form and maintain taste. The archetype of all perfections, however, is always only in thought. It is an Archetypon, and merely an ideal. True beauty must as it were be sketched through a sensibility. Morals contains the Archetypon of moral perfection.",
"blomberg-s03-046": "But if reason is not particularly employed in a representation, then that representation is not a rational cognition, but for that reason it is also not an irrational one. For example, when I know into what lands Europe is divided, I use for this not reason but only memory, and therefore this is not a rational cognition. But what is not rational is not yet irrational, rather this holds only of what is against reason. For example, if I have made myself acquainted with the motion of the planets without using reason, then the cognition I have of it is not a rational one, but also not an irrational one.",
"blomberg-s03-047": "A cognition that is not rational, where I use not reason but only memory, where I do not see how something comes from its ground, is a historical cognition.",
"blomberg-s03-048": "A perfect historical cognition, says the author in the 19th §, is a Cognitio pulchra sive aesthetica (beautiful or aesthetic cognition). But it is false that he takes beautiful and aesthetic for the same, for to the aesthetic belongs not only the beautiful but also the sublime. A historical cognition is one that is not rational. If therefore the author speaks of a perfect historical cognition and takes it for an aesthetic one, he can understand by it nothing other than a cognition which, although it is not rational",
"blomberg-s03-049": "can nevertheless still have a perfection. But he has explained himself very badly thereby. For who can see from this what sort of perfection this is supposed to be. This is just as much as if I wanted to say: the wig is that which, although it is neither a hat nor a cap, can nevertheless just as well be set on the head.",
"blomberg-s03-050": "We have already spoken first of all of the aesthetic perfection of a cognition, and said that it consists in the effect on our feeling. From this we can easily gather what an aesthetic cognition is, namely one that works on our feeling (through pleasure or displeasure). A cognition that is not rational is the historical. For example, if someone has great knowledge of natural history and does not cognize it in connection with its grounds, then he has no rational but only a historical cognition. Otherwise a historical cognition means one that goes only to individual things, and is opposed to the dogmatic. In this sense, however, it is not taken here. The author holds every perfect historical cognition to be a beautiful one. But a cognition can be historical without being beautiful. We can know everything mythological from Homer, also all the voyages of Ulysses, etc., and more, so that we have a quite perfect historical cognition, and yet it will not be beautiful if it has no effect on our feeling, if we look at everything coldly and feel nothing in doing so. Such a cognition, as we have already said above, is only beautiful and aesthetic if it has an influence on our feeling of pleasure or displeasure. The beautiful in a cognition is diminished when the rational and distinct is promoted in it, just as the beauty of a woman vanishes when one looks at her through a microscope. The best expressions, which move us most, lose their force when one dissects them and cognizes them more distinctly through the understanding. Our understanding often rejects something, for example various kinds of clothes, that is nevertheless to our taste and moves our feeling. Hungarian clothes are much more suited to the understanding than the French; they would be better, more comfortable etc. Likewise it is more suited to the understanding that the door not stand in the middle of the house and thereby make small rooms: but our feeling requires necessarily that everything be symmetrical, and that the door, for example, not stand at the corner of the house. Feeling is moved by confused cognition, for which reason it is very hard to observe, so that a science of it, i.e. Aesthetica, has very many difficulties. Baumgarten was the first to make a science of it.",
"blomberg-s03-051": "Rational cognition has advantages over the historical, in that it requires understanding and reason. But at times the historical has advantages over the rational. Historical cognition must lie at the ground of all rational cognition. The brooder who shuts himself up in his room and wants to attain many rational cognitions will not get far if he has not first brought about a historical cognition through experience from books, or also from social intercourse, from society with other people. Historical cognition is given to me a posteriori; it already contains in itself everything that belongs to the rational. It shows how something is, the rational however shows how something ought to be (so it is, for example, in morals); it only makes distinct what the historical indicates. It changes only the formal. Without historical cognition reason has no materialia which it is to make distinct. In our education it is a great fault that we occupy ourselves too early with rational cognition without yet having a sufficient historical cognition. Rational cognition, however, is not in all relations, not in all cases more perfect than the historical. For example, if someone is to be moved, rational cognition will do little or no effect thereby.",
"blomberg-s03-052": "With regard to common life historical cognition likewise has more use, just as the peasants in a state are far more useful than all mathematici and astronomers. –",
"blomberg-s03-053": "There is no human being who would not have metaphysical judgments. A science in general will consist of the cognitions that make up a whole.",
"blomberg-s03-054": "Philosophical cognition has the same objects that the historical and rational have. Only the form is different. It is undetermined where philosophical cognition begins and the rational leaves off.",
"blomberg-s03-055": "Just so, for example, the degree in the affects is undetermined. A greater degree of the sensible desires is an affect. But how great this degree must be is uncertain.",
"blomberg-s03-056": "In philosophical cognition one now seeks to see, from their constitution in things, their connection of grounds and consequences. Some think they have philosophy when they actually lack it, and others actually have it without thinking so. Those who learn by heart the definitiones from Wolff and other philosophers think they have philosophy. They have only a mere historical cognition, and actually cannot philosophize at all, nor think and judge about objects themselves. The skill of judging philosophically about a matter is at least lacking in them.",
"blomberg-s03-057": "In this paragraph the author speaks of the perfections of cognition. It is also explained by him what perfection is. The concept of perfection, however, belongs properly in the Metaphysica, and is introduced here in the wrong place. All perfection, however, is",
"blomberg-s03-058": "1st practical",
"blomberg-s03-059": "2nd Theoretical",
"blomberg-s03-060": "Theoretical perfection goes properly to the quantitas of it, as for example the extensiveness, fruitfulness. Practical fruitfulness, however, is on the contrary more a perfection according to quality, as for example the importance and the beauty of it, and hence it is in turn again",
"blomberg-s03-061": "1st Logical",
"blomberg-s03-062": "2 Aesthetic",
"blomberg-s03-063": "Beauty is properly a matter of art. Art, however, presupposes practice.",
"blomberg-s03-064": "In all perfection it always depends on having before one's eyes an aim, a model, an archetype and prototype. In regard to this one can properly judge and say whether, and which, thing is more perfect than another. So it is already in general with the nature of the human being. Everyone who has seen many makes for himself an archetype, a model of the size, of the beauty of a human being, which holds the mean in all parts and kinds of human form. For example, among many faulty copies of a human being the original for the most part makes and holds the mean.",
"blomberg-s03-065": "But from the composition of many partial beauties a perfect beautiful cannot possibly be produced. Many beautiful different clothes sewn together would look bad. (Nulla dies sine Linea Apelles — no day without a line, Apelles.) The artist, the painter etc., for example, cannot possibly compose an archetype of a painting for himself; rather he must create it for himself. Logical perfections are those that are perceived only when one looks at the thing distinctly. Aesthetic perfections, however, are those that are felt through confused concepts. The beauty of all good poetic representations, for example of Paradise Lost, has an aesthetic perfection. It is felt only with confused concepts, and loses its worth at once as soon as the concept is made distinct.",
"blomberg-s03-066": "But there are 1st beautiful objects,",
"blomberg-s03-067": "2nd beautiful representations of objects.",
"blomberg-s03-068": "The ugliest woman can be beautifully painted, i.e. excellently hit off, and the most beautiful woman can be ugly painted, i.e. badly hit off and disfigured. Beautiful objects can only take place in objects. Of a beautiful cognition, however, no judgment can properly be made at all. For this we do have examples, but those are not archetypes. Models, however, are examples that come closest to the archetype.",
"blomberg-s03-069": "In mathematics the propositions and theses have logical perfections; the concepts are distinct there. When the poet has made me the best, most moving, and liveliest representations of spring, when he has spoken of bleating, grazing herds, gently murmuring zephyrs, fragrant meadows etc. etc., then my cognition is aesthetically perfect. It is beautiful, but not distinct. I have thereby gained no distinct insight into the ground and the causes of spring. This, however, the astronomus can and must explain to me from the course of the sun. The French for the most part observe only aesthetic perfection. When they investigate grounds of something and want to have distinct concepts of a thing, they let themselves in for the beautiful, the moving, and the pleasing, and thereby they do great harm to logical perfection. There are learned men who are properly nothing but artificers of reason, and such imitating philosophers are commonly very obstinate in retaining their opinions once formed. Other learned men, on the other hand, are as it were, so to speak, the masters and lawgivers of reason.",
"blomberg-s03-070": "Learning that is historical in form but rational in matter is called a historical cognition. There are very many Ciceroni of philosophy, indeed many who do not even know what difference there is between philosophy and the science of philosophizing.",
"blomberg-s03-071": "The imperfections of cognition are again either logical or aesthetic. If the imperfections are found in confused concepts, they are aesthetic. But if they are found in distinct concepts, they are logical imperfections. For example, a Swiss poet says that, just as the Rhone runs through Lake Geneva and yet remains clear, so too the truly virtuous are to pass unharmed and unpolluted through the midst of the crowd of the vicious. Here is a logical imperfection. On closer investigation one finds that the Rhone runs muddy through Lake Geneva, so that it becomes clear and pure through it.",
"blomberg-s03-072": "A cognition that makes up beauty in aesthetic perfection is dry, for example in mathematical considerations. But if a cognition is to be beautiful and this aim is nevertheless not attained, then it is ugly. This, however, takes place with all such writers who want to be witty and yet are not, and cannot be.",
"blomberg-s03-073": "Aesthetic imperfections we notice far sooner than the logical. For those are at once repugnant to our feeling. –",
"blomberg-s03-074": "A historical cognition is historical either",
"blomberg-s03-075": "1 Materialiter",
"blomberg-s03-076": "2. Formaliter",
"blomberg-s03-077": "Furthermore, to imitate, to copy another's science is so called. There are sciences that can be learned which are historical in form but rational in matter. So are some sciences of reason. So it is, for example, with mathematics. Thus sciences can be acquired, and even communicated in the degree of perfection that accords with their nature. There are sciences of imitation, of learning, but also other sciences of genius, which cannot be learned.",
"blomberg-s03-078": "Philosophy, and the art of philosophizing, cannot possibly be learned, but mathematics can. So I can teach no human being new thoughts, wit, spirit, naivety in expression, any more than when I teach him to write or to calculate. A philosophy could indeed be learned in a certain time if I acquired it by copying it out, as it was given to me; then one would do nothing more than philosophize, as much as one has learned by heart. But beyond this what has been learned one would certainly not be in a position to philosophize in the least.",
"blomberg-s03-079": "The principles of mathematics, on the other hand, are for the most part intuitive, and their application likewise takes place according to a certain intuitus; but it is impossible so with philosophy.",
"blomberg-s03-080": "The character of a true philosopher is that he does nothing but exercise the natural powers and abilities, and indeed through the investigating inquiry of critique.",
"blomberg-s03-081": "Philosophy can never be learned in half a year, as is commonly believed. Philosophy belongs properly as a study for men who hold offices and have experience, although we think philosophy is far easier than jurisprudence and medicine.",
"blomberg-s03-082": "Philosophy cultivates the general faculties of nature through reason, and surpasses all effects of these faculties. The investigation that surpasses all cognition searches out the sources of the concepts of the understanding and their first origin. And this searching out is one of the most important, and it follows from it necessarily that one does not learn to philosophize in the least if one copies out the philosophy of others and learns it quite exactly. Rather one could far sooner profit somewhat if one accustoms oneself, and learns the science, to be able to criticize and judge about the philosophy of others. My philosophy must be grounded in myself, and not in the understanding of others. It must not bind me to any original model. The method of teaching philosophy is therefore twofold:",
"blomberg-s03-083": "1st That which cultivates the philosophical spirit, and can be neither learned nor imitated.",
"blomberg-s03-084": "2nd or that which faithfully follows a certain author, explains him, and in philosophy wants to take memory to its aid.",
"blomberg-s03-085": "This will do for mathematics, but not for philosophy.",
"blomberg-s03-086": "A cognition can be merely learned, or learned and beautiful at once. In the former one has merely distinctness in view, and this is dry. That cognition, however, which not only instructs the understanding but also stirs and allures the feeling, is an at once beautiful and learned cognition.",
"blomberg-s03-087": "The latter cognition, however, is the hardest, and far harder to obtain than the former. For if one goes only a little too far in beauty, one at once does harm to logical perfection. If on the other hand one wants to promote logical perfection properly, one becomes dry and loses the beautiful.",
"blomberg-s03-088": "To observe the learned and beautiful cognition at once in an oral, and still more in a written, cognition can therefore actually be called the true philosopher's stone of the learned. But who possesses it?",
"blomberg-s03-089": "A perfect historical cognition, if it is to be really beautiful, is perfect not in regard to the material but rather the formal. For example, an extensive science of geography is in itself historically very useful and perfect, but for that reason not yet beautiful. Rather, in the beautiful it always depends on the formal, on the manner of cognition. If, therefore, for example, Herr von Voltaire presents just the same historical cognition as Hübner, then the material of this cognition will be one and the same in these two presentations, but the formal very different; it will become quite different in the former than in the latter, namely it will be beautiful and move the feeling.",
"blomberg-s03-090": "The material, as for example the distinctness and correctness of cognition, can be constituted in one just as in the other. The formal, however, the moving, delighting, and what flatters our feeling, is different. Distinctness and correctness therefore concern the material, and consequently belong also to logical perfection as a property of it. Emotion and delight, however, which pleases all at once, concern the formal, and consequently belong also as a property to aesthetic perfection.",
"blomberg-s03-091": "In every aesthetic perfection I cognize the things confusedly. Much works on me all at once, and sets me thus in affect. For example, when on the theatrum a hero appears all at once, terrible to the point of terror, and with a great retinue, then the moving storms our soul, whereas the understanding lets go much that seems superfluous to it. It seeks rather only to make its cognition as distinct as ever possible, and for that very reason it passes over for the most part all taste, all flattering ornaments, all rouge drops away there, and what appears aesthetically very beautiful, we nevertheless regard as very wretched when we look at it again through the understanding alone. The French nation has this peculiarity, that it looks more and most upon the aesthetic, upon outward adornment. The English nation, on the other hand, cultivates rather the logical perfection. Hence it also comes that commonly our understanding learns not much or little from French books (yet some of them are excepted). One knows well afterwards, when one has read them, how to recall that one delighted in them as in a music; but one also knows at the same time that the understanding has learned nothing from them, which does not often happen with English books.",
"blomberg-s03-092": "These two paragraphs contain perfections of cognition. Extensiveness, among others, through which many objects are cognized, is certainly a perfection; for example, he who has made progress in true geography, who knows many cities, countries, rivers etc. by name, and their locations, may have the so-called name-geography perfectly in his command.",
"blomberg-s03-093": "In the 26th § the author speaks of the magnitude of cognition as likewise a perfection. In every magnitude there is also always a multitude, but in every multitude there is not yet a magnitude. In every magnitude I can consider the extensive and the intensive. In the first case, when I consider the extensive of a magnitude, I look at the multitude of the units, at their number, how many there are. In the other case, however, when I consider the intensive magnitude, I look especially at the magnitude of each unit. And so it is also with cognition. If I consider only the multitude of objects that I cognize, I look merely at the extensive magnitude of cognition. But if I cognize a single object wholly and quite perfectly, then the cognition is certainly intensive. In the latter it properly depends on the degree to which I cognize each unit.",
"blomberg-s03-094": "Truth is a perfection that belongs both to the logical and to the aesthetic perfection of cognition. It is a chief logical perfection, on which all the others depend. If I cognize the thing as it actually is, then my cognition is true. Truth is also required for aesthetic perfection. Hence Milton is censured for having represented death and sin as persons, as it were, since this does not accord with their constitution. Yet in the aesthetically perfect such great truth is not required as in the logical. In the aesthetic, something need only be tolerably true. In this way it is aesthetically true that Milton represents the angels in Paradise Lost as fighting and engaged in combat, for who knows whether this cannot take place? A good fable must always contain an aesthetic truth within itself. Thus, for example, in Aesop’s fable of the wolf there is such truth when he says, of the sheep and the wolf at the river, that the patient lamb excused itself quite meekly against the wolf’s malicious accusation and strove to appease his anger, but that the bloodthirsty and unjust wolf answered thereupon, or at least signified by his worthless answer: In short, now I am hungry, I must devour you –.",
"blomberg-s03-095": "Distinctness, further, is also a perfection of cognition, and it has this peculiarity, that it belongs as a necessary property both to logical and to aesthetic perfection. An extensive distinctness, such as one encounters in the beautiful understanding, is aesthetically pure; but intensive distinctness, which one finds in the profound understanding, belongs to logical perfection. The poet describes spring to me with extensive distinctness. He heaps many marks upon one another. The philosopher, however, describes it to me with intensive distinctness; that is, he does not look to the multitude of marks, but rather seeks only a very few, indeed, if nothing else, to represent even a single mark quite clearly and distinctly.",
"blomberg-s03-096": "The beauty of the understanding rests on one’s having many marks of a thing. The profundity of the understanding, however, requires only that a few marks be cognized clearly, and also at the same time be distinct and easy to see into.",
"blomberg-s03-097": "The author here explains certainty as: “the consciousness of the truth of a cognition.” For the present this explanation of it is enough and sufficient. We shall speak of it at greater length later. Certainty belongs to the logically and aesthetically perfect. The uncertain, the wavering, always displeases. For aesthetic certainty authority is often sufficient enough, that is, it is enough if one appeals in its support to the standing of great men who, for example, have believed in the immortality of the soul or in a future world; one will thereby already be sufficiently convinced aesthetically, although this does not yet constitute logical certainty. What great men, highly esteemed by the learned world, say is therefore a ground for aesthetic certainty, but never for logical certainty.",
"blomberg-s03-098": "If a cognition is to be aesthetically true, then very much must be heaped upon one another in this cognition, and a multitude of representations must be introduced at once. If, for example, I want to prove the infamy of this or that deed, I shall not point out and demonstrate the infamous according to morals, but rather digress, so to speak; I show, for example, how the rocks, trees, etc. etc. tremble before this deed, how everything quakes, how the heaven wraps itself in gloomy clouds so as not to be a witness of this deed, which is so abominable. Whoever, then, attains this and a similar cognition must also be blinded by its confusedness. Hence it also comes about that the judgments of other people are already sufficient for its certainty.",
"blomberg-s03-099": "A practical cognition we call one that has an influence on our will and, per consequentiam (by consequence), also on our actions (which are to be governed by the will).",
"blomberg-s03-100": "From the consideration of all the perfections of cognitions we see that the chief faculties of our cognition are properly the following: 1. understanding, 2. feeling, and 3. desire.",
"blomberg-s03-101": "If, then, 1. I make my cognition perfect with regard to my understanding, it is logically perfect. If 2dly I make my cognition perfect with regard to my feeling, it is aesthetically perfect",
"blomberg-s03-102": "If finally 3tio I make my cognition perfect with regard to my desires, it is practically perfect, or also morally.",
"blomberg-s03-103": "Moral perfection rests on logical and aesthetic perfection taken together. But the following 3 items belong to logical perfection:",
"blomberg-s03-104": "1mo Distinctness",
"blomberg-s03-105": "2 Truth and",
"blomberg-s03-106": "3tio Certainty.",
"blomberg-s03-107": "Distinctness, however, is the first and foremost property, on which a logical perfection particularly depends.",
"blomberg-s03-108": "Truth as well as certainty help little if they are not distinct.",
"blomberg-s03-109": "The 3 chief characters (Caractères) of an aesthetic perfection, however, are",
"blomberg-s03-110": "1mo Truth",
"blomberg-s03-111": "2do Certainty and especially 3tio Copiousness",
"blomberg-s03-112": "For in an aesthetic perfection the cognition is not distinct but confused. Yet truth, because of the confusedness and heaping up in the cognition, can of course not be found in a high degree in aesthetic perfection. For it does not properly occupy my will, but rather only my taste, and so it is likewise with certainty. Not every sensible certainty is already aesthetically perfect, but properly only that which touches our feeling and flatters it. One can, for example, have the greatest knowledge of geography, etc., which, although it is perfect, is nevertheless not aesthetically perfect, namely because it does not touch our feeling.",
"blomberg-s03-113": "The author here draws everything together and says that we ought to make our cognition at once logically and aesthetically perfect. The whole of our cognition that we possess ought to have all possible kinds of perfections. But who can accomplish this (prästiren)? In every part of our cognition this very often cannot take place at all. If, for example, I want to make a book logically perfect, I must not, and cannot, introduce the aesthetic and the practical everywhere at the same time. And if, on the other hand, I want to make a book aesthetically or practically perfect, I cannot think everywhere of introducing the logically perfect. –",
"blomberg-s03-114": "Many theological propositions do not bind our will straightaway. We can therefore leave out much of the logical and aesthetic perfection without the human being suffering any harm from it. Just as a wise teacher conceals much beauty that he knows, and divests himself of it, when he knows that his hearers’ minds are so constituted that they are accustomed to speculations and might thereby be turned away from the practical, so likewise one must in certain cases often abstract from the practical in this or that matter. Many things can be true and yet harmful to the human being. Not all truth is useful. Meanwhile it is also certain that all harmfulness that a truth may ever have is always only contingent. Thus there are certainly recommendations of certain cognitions and judgments that may finally turn out to be bribes, and again disparagements, contempt, censure of other cognitions or judgments, without examining what these cognitions are like. In order to avoid these faults on both sides, then, in every examination of a cognition one should take it entirely by itself, separated from all foreign questions (Quaestionen) of utility or harm, and especially when this utility or harm itself interests us; for otherwise partiality at once pronounces the judgment and stupefies all cool deliberations of the understanding.",
"blomberg-s03-115": "Opining has always been the fruitful source of all errors of the human understanding.",
"blomberg-s03-116": "All actions of human beings occur from the drive to perfection. This, however, is attained when our feeling is touched by pleasure and displeasure. If the feeling presupposes a rational cognition, it is a rational pleasure and feeling.",
"blomberg-s03-117": "But if it follows only from a confused cognition, it is a sensible pleasure and feeling. We must make our cognition perfect according to both kinds of feeling.",
"blomberg-s03-118": "What touches our rational feeling is logically perfect, e.g. when I take delight in thorough instruction. But what touches our sensible feeling is aesthetically perfect.",
"blomberg-s03-119": "On the whole the horizon of our cognition determined practically is far narrower than the horizon of cognition determined logically.",
"blomberg-s03-120": "Here the author shows how logical perfections can be combined with the aesthetic. E.g. logical perfections are found in Wolff and other writings, but no aesthetic ones. In the belles-lettres, however, one finds in turn fewer logical and, on the other hand, far more aesthetic perfections. The authors of such books, however, have the lowest rank. Everything that charms and touches us serves to the detriment of our power of judgment.",
"blomberg-s03-121": "Aesthetic perfection, especially emotions and charms, is in many respects opposed to the logical, in that the former concerns sensibility, the latter, by contrast, the abstract. In many respects, however, it is required after logical perfection, namely for what belongs to sensible aesthetic intuition.",
"blomberg-s03-122": "Whoever wants to convince himself truly and lastingly of the truth of his propositions must abstain from all sensation, impression, charm, and sought-after beauty, so that the reader does not believe that he wants merely to persuade him, deceive him, and flatter him, so that he gives assent to his propositions because he is not in a position to prove them to him.",
"blomberg-s03-123": "The whole cognition that we possess ought to be logical and aesthetic. If we therefore want to enlarge the logical perfections, we must not annihilate the aesthetic. Further, if we want to promote the beautiful in cognition, we must not wholly neglect the logical. Hence, if one wants to cultivate one’s understanding together with one’s taste, one should not read merely comedies, novels, gallant histories, etc. and other such books in which, to be sure, there are beautiful and aesthetic cognitions, but where no logical perfections at all are found. To be sure, feeling is thereby touched and taste refined, but it is also certain that the understanding suffers harm from it; it is thereby made duller and unusable. With children one should begin from the sensible. Geography could be presented to them in such a way that they always feel and are touched in the process. Thus, instead of having them learn the names by rote (verbosus), one could rather relate the remarkable features of the sea, the peculiar customs of foreign nations and peoples, etc., and not, as commonly happens, merely show them on the map, without remarking anything about it: that is Paris, that is London, etc. From history one could in turn present to them the events that have occurred in the most lively and touching manner, so that their feeling and desires would thereby be improved. With riper years one could also begin various exercises of the understanding with them. One could let them read skilful, select poems and good speeches themselves, and reflect upon them.",
"blomberg-s03-124": "But one must not, as commonly happens in school, before they can often yet think, force them to an oratory which afterwards avails them nothing at all, except that they forget it; for what one has had to learn unwillingly, or even through means of compulsion, one forgets very soon, and of itself. Dry cognitions, which are so very much in favor, such as reciting the names of places, emperors, the year-numbers etc., ruin youth in the worst way. The child learns them with vexation, and does not retain them but only seeks, from fear of punishment, to have its lesson ready to recite, etc. That what it has once learned should always remain in its memory is not its intention at all. Even if one tells it that otherwise it cannot be and become happy, it pays no heed to that; it is still wholly disinterested, and content only with its present pleasure, and so abhors everything that would diminish this for it. It will therefore naturally retain, and seek to retain, only what pleases it and touches its feeling. If everything is now presented in such a manner, it would also be able to retain everything gladly and with pleasure.",
"blomberg-s03-125": "To be truthful is the greatest virtue in the world, on which all the rest are grounded, and all the remaining virtues are in fact nothing but dissimulations without truthfulness. This virtue, then, should be presented to children first, and at the same time accustomed to them everywhere, where not yet by words, then at least by mien.",
"blomberg-s03-126": "Above all one should strive to implant in children a strong disgust at all and every untruth, under whatever semblance one might excuse it, just as, e.g., at a caterpillar or other vermin. They will afterwards gradually accustom themselves to lying as little in their lives as to touching a caterpillar. They will always be honest, and hate all possible dissimulation.",
"blomberg-s03-127": "Every human being, further, has a natural drive to benevolence, innate in him, as it were. Just as we are glad when we can make another laugh with our witticisms, so we are still more glad when we have made others happy through us, or merely set them in a joy, pleasure, etc. (only a few malicious minds, who wish their fellow human beings’ misfortune, or even promote it, are excepted from this). One would therefore have to know how to preserve this in a child as well. One should, e.g., give a child something to give to other children who are poorer, but not only then, when these ask for it as beggars, since they are in misery. For thereby one accustoms them merely to compassion, which is still very different from true mercy; rather one ought to let the child share something with others, merely in order to give, because it is fine and praiseworthy to share what one has to spare; because those others deserve it, even if they do not ask for it, or do not have the heart.",
"blomberg-s03-128": "If one has now instilled so much in it through sensation, this will certainly at once improve its moral character, and it will take pleasure in moral narratives.",
"blomberg-s03-129": "Once one has attained this, one can teach it to read; then it will itself see what the skill of being able to read helps it. It will itself get a liking for it, it will strive to attain a skill in it as soon as possible, since this reading puts it in a position to read letters, poems, moral occurrences, and other fine books itself, and henceforth not to need to ask others to tell it something. When this has now happened, one should seek to promote its historical cognition, because this must lie at the ground of the rational. One should by no means burden it with an immense multitude of names; from this it naturally sees no use at all, and consequently has no liking for it.",
"blomberg-s03-130": "One should grasp the histories morally; then it will take pleasure in them, and will regard the names, to be sure, only as side matters, but will nevertheless retain them.",
"blomberg-s03-131": "Finally one should also seek to extend and promote its rational cognition. One should tell it the grounds when it asks the cause of something, but one should not burden it with remote grounds. If it asks why no grain grows on this or that field, one should tell it: because it is sandy, etc., but give it no lengthy explanation of fertility; this serves for nothing but only to confuse its understanding.",
"blomberg-s03-132": "In this § the author says that one must let small perfections go when greater ones are thereby hindered. Greater logical perfections must be preferred to small aesthetic perfections, and greater aesthetic ones again to smaller logical perfections.",
"blomberg-s03-133": "If one follows this rule, one will be able to write logically and also aesthetically beautifully, and to express oneself in a presentation.",
"blomberg-s03-134": "The opposite of any perfection is always twofold, namely either the contradictory opposite, when the perfection is merely lacking, or secondly the real opposite, which wholly cancels the perfection that might otherwise be there on another ground. An example of the first would be, e.g., such medicaments as help nothing at all, but also harm nothing, such as may often be prescribed; this is aptly compared with the contradictory opposite of healing; but those medicines which not only do no good but even do harm have the real opposite of healing. And it is just so with cognitions.",
"blomberg-s03-135": "The imperfection which is the contradictory opposite is called the lack (Mangel), and the imperfection which is the real opposite of the perfect is called the fault (Fehler). Thus, e.g., ignorance of the immortality of the human soul is the contradictory opposite, but error, in which one even believes that the human soul is not immortal, is the real opposite of the cognition of the immortality of the soul. For ignorance only grounds are lacking; it can therefore easily be remedied. In error, however, there are actual grounds which are opposed, as counter-grounds, to the true cognition.",
"blomberg-s03-136": "Faults: vitia, must therefore be avoided far more than lacks, defectus. It is far worse if I cancel what has already been actually proved on other grounds than if I still know nothing; for in the latter case I can be instructed sooner than in the former. E.g. he who defines something not at all can manage with his common cognition; but if he makes false definitions and afterwards applies them, a far greater and more important harm arises from it, which could not have arisen from a lack. In the case of the vitia, however, cognition is not only not increased but rather diminished, and even what is already there is actually cancelled.",
"blomberg-s03-137": "Common and historical cognition can be much more perfect than the learned. They are to be regarded as heterogenea (heterogeneous things), but can nevertheless also be considered against a focus perfectionis (focal point of perfection), e.g. whether they are lively, fruitful, and so on etc. By historical cognition we understand not the aesthetic but the merely dry cognition. This dry cognition in a human being, e.g., of his circumstances of life and events, is however much more fruitful and extensive than the learned.",
"blomberg-s03-138": "A mathematician who otherwise possesses much cognition will nevertheless in praxi (practice) not get as far with his engineering science as another who has not studied the theory but has acquired a merely historical cognition of the practice itself.",
"blomberg-s03-139": "Machiavelli, e.g., had a great learned cognition of drawing up a whole army in battle order, but when he was actually once charged with commanding an army, he, instead of ordering it, threw the whole army into disorder, because he had no historical cognition of the incidental circumstances.",
"blomberg-s03-140": "In many respects the learned man with his solid cognition accomplishes in fact far less than another whose cognition is historical, because the former is not so universally useful as the latter.",
"blomberg-s03-141": "Rousseau holds that the sciences have brought more harm than benefit. We have faculties far greater than is necessary for this life. Our theoretical faculties are stronger in us than the practical. The former we can improve more than the latter: whence a disproportion, a monstrosity, in which the head is too large en régard (with regard) to the other members of the body. If we had no other life to expect, learnedness would certainly be more harmful than useful to us. For here we often have, for the trouble we give ourselves, far from enough advantages. Meanwhile learnedness has also already procured us many advantages here. Navigation, the art of government, etc. would certainly have remained very obscure and imperfect without it. What a great benefit it is that through it alone, as through a light, the darkness of superstition has been dispelled and so happily eradicated! Many old women can now grow older with honor and end their lives in peace, whereas before, they very often came under suspicion and indeed had to lose their lives miserably.",
"blomberg-s03-142": "Learned cognition considers the universal, and therefore gives occasion to inventions and improvements.",
"blomberg-s03-143": "If learned cognition is at the same time beautiful, then it is the most useful and serviceable, especially in praxi. One knows the cognition of the Scholastics, these dry philosophers bound to a very forced method, how they have, as it were, annihilated the moral properties and faculties of human beings and degraded them down to superstition. An irreplaceable loss! All this, however, could not have been otherwise, and came about merely because their cognitions, and consequently also their presentations of morals, etc., were merely learned, and not at the same time beautiful, but rather quite dry. So harmful, then, is a beautiful but not learned, and a learned but dry cognition.",
"blomberg-s04-001": "The copiousness of learned cognition is opposed to its poverty. This concerns either the material or the formal. If someone lacks historical cognition, he is in complete ignorance. Thus, e.g., the ancients were in complete ignorance of the existence of the New World; they had no historical cognition of it. The common man is still completely ignorant with regard to many things. But one can also be in ignorance with regard to the grounds of a thing: 1. in that it is wholly unknown to us, so that one has no historical cognition of it. E.g. if one does not know at all whence thunderstorms, rain, snow, etc. arise.",
"blomberg-s04-002": "2do If one does not at all see into the nexus (connection) of the thing with the ground, although the grounds are indeed known to one. E.g. if I know, to be sure, that the moon is the cause of ebb and flood, but do not yet understand and see how it comes about that ebb and flood proceed from the attraction of the moon.",
"blomberg-s04-003": "As regards ignorance, it can be divided",
"blomberg-s04-004": "1: into a necessary, and on the other hand",
"blomberg-s04-005": "2: into a voluntary ignorance.",
"blomberg-s04-006": "Where no data are given us for settling something, ignorance is necessary. E.g. to settle where the human soul will be after the death of the body. Item (likewise) concerning the generative power of animals and human beings.",
"blomberg-s04-007": "Thirdly, the place that we have in the world makes ignorance necessary for us. But if, on the contrary, from certain motives we voluntarily renounce some things, so that we deliberately do not want to know them, when we see that something is too difficult for us or without use, this is a logical ignorance.",
"blomberg-s04-008": "Logic is more a doctrine of skill, which belongs to the youth; the doctrine of wisdom, however, belongs to the man.",
"blomberg-s04-009": "That circle in which we can see things is called our horizon. The sum total of all things that the human being can cognize in a learned way without detriment to his other perfections is the horizon of learned cognition. What is above the horizon can be seen in the geographical sense. Here, however, it is to signify such things as one cannot cognize even if one wanted to, the ignorance of which is necessary. Of many things we even have an ignorance in historical cognition, e.g. of the joys in heaven, of the mysteries, because perhaps they cannot be expressed by our words. Of some things we are often in ignorance because of our weak faculties, but of others in regard to circumstances, of time, and of place.",
"blomberg-s04-010": "We determine the horizon of cognition",
"blomberg-s04-011": "1. logically, merely through the capacity, through the measure of our powers",
"blomberg-s04-012": "2. practically, according to ends.",
"blomberg-s04-013": "One believes it is always good to know more than one may, and that it is better to know too much than too little. But all effort in a greater degree toward one aim is superfluous and badly applied. It is therefore necessary that all our efforts be suited to the ends we have once set ourselves; otherwise the human being squanders the capital of his powers, which is very limited; we say: something goes beyond our horizon when it goes beyond the faculties of our cognition.",
"blomberg-s04-014": "Many more things are above the learned than above the historical horizon. The former is therefore far smaller than the latter. Much that I cognize historically I cannot always cognize in a learned and beautiful way. E.g. of the commercio animae et corporis (commerce of soul and body) I have indeed a historical but not a learned cognition. We must therefore not engage ourselves with all objects, but always bear in mind that we have a horizon in cognition. Hence it is also not necessary to engage in inquiry about the state of the soul after death, which is above our horizon.",
"blomberg-s04-015": "It is very difficult to determine what is properly above our horizon. If something often seems to me as though it were above my horizon, so that I even hold it to be impossible, it can nevertheless in time, through inquiry and diligence, come under my horizon. It is the true philosophy of the lazy to maintain of all things that they are above our horizon.",
"blomberg-s04-016": "But if someone says: I can indeed cognize something, but I do not need it, it is superfluous to me, or even it is harmful to me, I ought not to cognize it, that means just as much as: it is above my horizon. According to taste one determines the horizon",
"blomberg-s04-017": "1. According to the taste of the human being.",
"blomberg-s04-018": "2. According to the taste of the age.",
"blomberg-s04-019": "But if it is true that most human beings draw the horizon of their cognition only according to taste, then it is only a superficial cognition, in order to seem learned in everything, to be able to judge learnedly of everything. This, however, is as it were only the froth of cognition, for which a certain boldness itself is required.",
"blomberg-s04-020": "Our age is nearly such a one. But the harm from it is also unavoidable, namely that in the end all sciences are treated only superficially, on the surface.",
"blomberg-s04-021": "The more a science is restricted to a single aim, the greater a degree of perfection it can attain. In our times one would almost think everything had become refined and learned. Women, instead of doing coarse housework as formerly, now read fine writings. This is called wide reading, in which they seek a distinction. But precisely this great extension causes the science to lose its value. The logical horizon with regard to our faculties is 1.) historical, which is, and must be, the most extensive.",
"blomberg-s04-022": "2.) Rational, however, it is very narrow.",
"blomberg-s04-023": "The historical horizon is to be cultivated especially in our youth, yet the aim according to which I acquire the historical cognition must also be known.",
"blomberg-s04-024": "The polyhistor is he who has all historical cognition of all possible sciences.",
"blomberg-s04-025": "Philosophy is the field of all historical cognition, of the theologian, the physicus",
"blomberg-s04-026": "A polyhistor brings together the materials for a science; his head is a true bibliotheca (library); but to polyhistory there belongs especially also philology, the science of the instruments of learnedness.",
"blomberg-s04-027": "The more perfect a science is to become in its kind, the narrower it becomes. Those who have in their possession all historical and all rational cognition, these are the great, universal heads, but of these there are only very few.",
"blomberg-s04-028": "Everyone must think that he has, according to his faculties, be they great or small, a determinate horizon suited to them. E.g. he who knows something of geometry, suppose it is not yet much, must and can well think that finding the quadrature of the circle is raised above his horizon.",
"blomberg-s04-029": "Our horizon of cognition changes with time. What is now above it will in time, if I acquire more faculties and cultivate them properly and better (excolire), well come below it.",
"blomberg-s04-030": "Commonly a beginner thinks that nothing is above, but everything below his horizon, that he is perfectly able to cognize and settle everything. In time, however, one sees through the delusion and learns to restrict one’s horizon, or else falls on the contrary into the thought that everything is hard for us, or above our horizon.",
"blomberg-s04-031": "How many there are, too, among the so-called and merely nominal philosophers, who are vain of their outward glitter and quite taken with it, indeed even think they are in a position to be teachers of philosophy, though this is very far beyond their horizon.",
"blomberg-s04-032": "Beneath the horizon of learned cognition things are degraded when they are unworthy of our learned cognition. Some absurd opinions of the ancient philosophers are not even worthy of our historical cognition, so that we should make ourselves acquainted with them.",
"blomberg-s04-033": "In and on nature, however, not the least thing is unworthy of our historical cognition, unless one wished to devote oneself so much to it that one neglects more important things on that account.",
"blomberg-s04-034": "Delusion and methods have also brought very much beneath the horizon of our cognition. E.g. the examination of urine is now, because of people’s delusion, held almost indecent, although it serves very much for distinguishing diseases.",
"blomberg-s04-035": "Things are outside the horizon of our learned cognition that are excluded because they make us neglect more important things on their account. For an unrestricted understanding nothing can be outside the horizon; this occurs only with a restricted one.",
"blomberg-s04-036": "We must deal sparingly with our faculties, so that we can also apply them to important things.",
"blomberg-s04-037": "What therefore does not belong to that science which we have properly chosen to cultivate thoroughly (excoliren) is already outside our horizon, be it now above or below it.",
"blomberg-s04-038": "To occupy oneself chiefly with foreign matters, and with neglect of more important ones, is properly to overstep one’s horizon. The historical horizon can be made as far as is possible. Very seldom is a thing outside the historical horizon, but sooner outside the learned.",
"blomberg-s04-039": "According as the understanding grows, ever more and more objects come under our horizon, and it widens bit by bit.",
"blomberg-s04-040": "Rousseau says quite rightly that all who have written on education, and so also most parents, want to make perfect men out of children, whereas they should first form a perfect child out of them. They bring under the child’s horizon what properly belongs under the man’s horizon.",
"blomberg-s04-041": "Within our horizon is that which is neither below, nor above, nor outside it.",
"blomberg-s04-042": "The determination of the learned horizon is too difficult to be made by a youth.",
"blomberg-s04-043": "People of insight who have themselves erred in their own way can best determine the horizon. They will show the learner what is necessary and what unnecessary, and how one can very soon go astray here.",
"blomberg-s04-044": "It is better if one first seeks to see into very many things than if one wants to learn only a few, and these quite thoroughly, because this is much easier and more attractive for a youth. One should seek to form a chief concept of many sciences; afterwards one should take up one science after another in order. This would have the benefit that, if one had already resolved to treat this or that single science quite thoroughly, one could see the nexus with other sciences, and in each bring in an elucidation from the other. It is therefore very perverse when parents want to determine their children’s horizon and manner of life already in the cradle, when they want to infer, e.g., from the fact that it makes a serious face and can cry and scold lustily, that it will suit the pulpit well, and, because another in its youth likes to examine, dissect, and take apart all sorts of things exactly, to conjecture from this that he must one day become a skilful anatomist.",
"blomberg-s04-045": "Ignorance is always an imperfection, and therefore certainly can never be praiseworthy; but it can well be blameless, namely when one renounces certain objects in order not to neglect more important cognitions on their account.",
"blomberg-s04-046": "Leibniz could have had a blameless ignorance in many things. E.g. in chemistry, in antiquities, and in general in very many other matters that overburden a great genius too much.",
"blomberg-s04-047": "One can divide ignorance into the common and the learned. For a learned man to see into it properly that he knows nothing, great learnedness is required.",
"blomberg-s04-048": "Quantum est quod nescimus! (How much there is that we do not know!) But no one is wiser in his own thoughts than a beginner. For he does not yet understand at all what it is to know something, whether little or much or nothing. He does not even know himself whether he knows something or not. In time, however, one learns to appraise one’s insights and to see that they still have very great lacks and imperfections.",
"blomberg-s04-049": "Socrates first held it to be his greatest learnedness that he saw that he knew nothing. – A beginner believes he can define and demonstrate everything, but does not see at all what a labyrinth so many matters are for him; this, however, he learns to recognize when he already possesses more learnedness. The learned man is in many things just as ignorant as the common man, but he is nevertheless in a position to philosophize about this ignorance itself, and to be able to indicate its ground and its degree.",
"blomberg-s04-050": "When one has already made many investigations, learned much, researched much, then one first learns to recognize one’s ignorance. Who imagines, indeed, that of substances we still know only a mere outer determination and no other at all? Only he who has already sufficiently wandered through the fields of learnedness knows this, and is in a position to see how small, how deficient, and imperfect all his cognitions are.",
"blomberg-s04-051": "A cognition that is extensively great, or that comprehends very many objects within itself, the author calls a polyhistory. Those who study from gallantry are commonly wont to devote themselves to this polyhistory. Their cognition resembles a great country which contains many blank places within itself, and can by far not be as fruitful as that country which is indeed smaller but better cultivated.",
"blomberg-s04-052": "A cognition becomes detailed and complete when it is sufficient for a certain end. It is exact (genau) when it contains no more within itself than is required for the end which this cognition has before it.",
"blomberg-s04-053": "For the office of schoolmaster, e.g., many a preacher can have cognition enough, although not for the office of preacher. He would certainly have become an excellent schoolteacher, but now he is a wretched preacher.",
"blomberg-s04-054": "On the other hand, many a schoolteacher who can now administer his office only poorly would have become a better preacher than he is now a schoolmaster.",
"blomberg-s04-055": "He who merely runs through learnedness, who makes himself acquainted historically with everything merely so that he may know how to speak of everything in company, has only a meagre learnedness, and, as it were, nothing but a skeleton of it",
"blomberg-s04-056": "Such a one is contemptible in the same degree as one who makes great show with clothes, but in himself, because of a shrivelled stomach or other diseases and wretched circumstances, feels the greatest torment.",
"blomberg-s04-057": "He who possesses a meagre cognition can never satisfy himself, and sees well that it is only a delusion. By himself alone he cannot be content and pleased. By himself alone he is not in a position to amuse himself. He must seek others, go among others, and takes pleasure in being held by others, perhaps ignorant ones, at least to be learned.",
"blomberg-s04-058": "One must always be concerned to extend one’s learned cognition. If we do not come forward, we shall certainly always come backward.",
"blomberg-s04-059": "One must have an immediate inclination to learnedness if one wants to enlarge it constantly and never let it come into decline. But one commonly acquires thereby only as much as one needs in order to have bread and to be able to live.",
"blomberg-s04-060": "Certainly, we would never overload our head with learnedness and inflict harm on ourselves if we observed this properly, and took the easy before the difficult.",
"blomberg-s04-061": "It would be better if one presented all sciences at first quite easily, so that one would not need much beyond the common use of the understanding for them. Once one had presented the sciences in this way, one could then present them in their whole perfection and acuteness, which commonly happens at the beginning,",
"blomberg-s04-062": "although this perfection is nevertheless not necessary for all, and much harm also often arises from it.",
"blomberg-s04-063": "Many would be content with a preliminary presentation of the sciences at the beginning, e.g. according to Gesner’s Isagoge, rather than with a more extensive presentation of them, which thereby becomes obscure and difficult",
"blomberg-s04-064": "Hippocrates says wisely: Ars longa, vita brevis (art is long, life is short). Negligent people commonly complain about the length of the time in which they have yet to live. But that time which they have already spent wholly idle and dreaming seems to them to have been very fleeting, and appears to them very short, because they did nothing in it and consequently cannot remember anything important that went on in it.",
"blomberg-s04-065": "Many sciences are of such a kind that with the passage of time the human faculties are exceeded by their extent.",
"blomberg-s04-066": "Thus, e.g., history is already now very extensive, and with time and its length still more happens. More and more events keep occurring in the world. These all come to history, and this science will finally become so extensive, and grow so, that in the end our memory will by far not be great enough, since it is already so difficult now.",
"blomberg-s04-067": "With mathematics it will perhaps go just so. For what it now contains is already great enough for our slight faculties. Perhaps with the length of time it will become so great that our descendants will no longer dare at all to venture upon it.",
"blomberg-s04-068": "Perhaps in time the mathematical writings will stand like the Egyptian pyramids, which no one imitates, but of which people only remember those who lived in former times.",
"blomberg-s04-069": "But although learnedness is so very extensive, our life is nevertheless long enough to look about us in it properly, if we wanted to devote all our time to it.",
"blomberg-s04-070": "To earn bread is an ignoble, an unworthy end for learnedness. Great insights are not at all serviceable for earning bread. A slight measure of learnedness, boldness (Hardiesse),",
"blomberg-s04-071": "the art of disputation, shamelessness in exalting oneself and belittling other great minds, are nowadays the most suited to earning bread: to offices anyone can come, the worthy alone excepted",
"blomberg-s04-072": "To prevent forgetting, says the author, one must learn much, so that one still knows something even if in time one has already forgotten something, so that one is not suddenly stripped of all cognition.",
"blomberg-s04-073": "But it is best, when one has once acquired the skill to be able to philosophize about the objects themselves and to cultivate (excoliren) the sciences. He who has this is like a musician who has indeed already forgotten his learned pieces, but can play off everything put before him, and can also compose himself.",
"blomberg-s04-074": "A learned man who brings his science rather a good deal into conversation one calls a pedant. But in order to be this, someone must actually possess a skill, which he, however, seeks to bring in everywhere, even if it is often not fitting. He must not be wholly dull and ignorant, however. There can be pedants in all estates, apart from the learned. Thus there are, e.g., among noblemen hunting-pedants who talk of nothing but their hunt, dogs, foxes that have played tricks on them, hares, roes, stags, etc.",
"blomberg-s04-075": "Thus there are finery-pedants who cannot be vexed enough, and know no end of complaining, and are able to find fault when a cuff, or a curl, or a ribbon is spoiled or tangled. In company one calls someone a pedant when in his conversations he brings in unnecessary investigations and distinctiones, which would be far sooner and better suited to the lecture chair than to a pleasure party.",
"blomberg-s04-076": "In learnedness one becomes a pedant when one always wants to define and to raise unnecessary scruples. The methods one nowadays uses to instruct women are ways of making pedants of them.",
"blomberg-s04-077": "To charlatanry belongs this, that one thinks oneself great with one’s small insight, puts on airs, and even exalts oneself above others.",
"blomberg-s04-078": "The mathematical method, with which in former times one made a great show and wanted to be thorough, was nothing but a kind of charlatanry. To play or to brag with one’s demonstrations, or with one’s wit, is also charlatanry.",
"blomberg-s04-079": "To avoid these properly, one should acquire a thorough learnedness. Once this has been done, one will at once become aware and see how much we lack, and how little cause we have to make a great show with our plain sciences and knowledge, but rather how great cause we have to exclaim with humility: Quantum est, quod nescimus (how much there is that we do not know).",
"blomberg-s05-001": "In the preceding second section we spoke of the copiousness of learned cognition, but this belongs properly to the magnitude of cognition. The author has therefore done wrong in treating of its copiousness as well as of its magnitude in separate sections.",
"blomberg-s05-002": "In the preceding section we considered properly the extensive aspect of cognition, and now its intensive aspect still remains to be weighed. The author, however, makes here a really wholly unnecessary distinction.",
"blomberg-s05-003": "The degree of dignity (Dignitaet) with which we see into things depends especially, and first, on our choosing the right things. For this a correct taste and a good feeling are required, by virtue of which one is able to choose important objects which have either many or few, but yet important, consequences.",
"blomberg-s05-004": "The concepts of politeness and inconstancy have, to be sure, not great but yet many consequences in common life, and are therefore of very great importance.",
"blomberg-s05-005": "The multitude of consequences often makes up very well for what a single consequence, or a few, lack in importance.",
"blomberg-s05-006": "The correction of error, of the conscience, on the other hand, has single but great consequences.",
"blomberg-s05-007": "Further, it belongs to the dignity of our insights that we not apply too much effort to one thing where it is not really so necessary, and on the other hand withdraw our diligence from other objects. A particularly good feeling and true correctness of the imagination belong to the art of appraising the importance of cognitions rightly. The consequences I can easily show to another; but to feel the magnitude of these consequences cannot be taught him at all.",
"blomberg-s05-008": "Of metaphysics I can tell him that he will get enlightened concepts of his soul, of providence, of the highest being, of the world. But if he is insensible to this great utility, no description will help him.",
"blomberg-s05-009": "A cognition is great insofar as it has a universal use; but all these cognitions are so disadvantageously constituted that their use is indeed great, and one cognizes many things thereby, but that of these many things themselves one cognizes the less.",
"blomberg-s05-010": "I think little about many objects.",
"blomberg-s05-011": "But there is yet another cognition, through which one cognizes much in few things. The previous fault arises for the most part from this, that one always thinks in abstracto, and from this many others arise. Baumgarten says: beauty is a Perfectio Phaenomenon, perfection in appearance. But can one, from this explanation, really notice beauty in objects, in cognitions? Through the fact that a cognition comprehends much under itself, it comprehends all the less in itself. The lack in a cognition is not always blameworthy, provided it does not contradict a purpose that has been set. But as soon as it does, an error arises from this lack. From this it follows of itself that those who promise to give a definition of a thing entangle themselves in the danger that even the slightest missing mark is at once imputed to them, which would not have been done to them if they had indicated that they did not actually mean to develop the thing completely, but only to explain it in accordance with a certain consideration. It is just so with demonstrationes and proofs.",
"blomberg-s05-012": "The limits that we set to our desire for knowledge are so necessary that without them we can hardly cognize any other science properly. In order to arrive at being able to cognize a cognition completely, we must therefore voluntarily impose upon ourselves, as it were, an arbitrary lack with respect to others, and to the cognition opposed to this cognition, that is, a kind of ignorance of it, so that we may apply all diligence to the former and be able to have a fully perfect insight into it.",
"blomberg-s05-013": "The lacks (Defectus) are nothing other than the limits of a thing that is to begin. They are therefore the termini, its barriers, and the lack subsists quite well with the perfection of a beginning.",
"blomberg-s05-014": "To the estimation of cognitions there belongs not acuteness at all, but only feeling. Mathematicians can be very acute and yet have no taste for metaphysica, or for any other logical or moral investigation.",
"blomberg-s05-015": "Cognitions often seem to us dry and of no use. Afterwards, when we first wander through them and penetrate into their interior, their advantages are soon discovered. Thus, for example, electricity was nothing but a fine plaything; afterwards, however, its usefulness has shown itself in such a way that it may now serve for the healing of many a sickness. Perhaps with the help of electricity one will in time even be able to drive away thunderstorms.",
"blomberg-s05-016": "Euclid discovered many properties of the circle with it, and only now, in time, has one seen the far-reaching advantages of it. Many things have been despised, and become contemptible, only through misuse. E.g. the Wolffians talked so long and so much about the monads that they were mocked by merry poets.",
"blomberg-s05-017": "The expressions concerning the monads, the best world, the sufficient and insufficient ground have been so dishonored by the learned rabble that one now really already hesitates to make use of them. Much that is in fact possible and good can also very quickly be made ridiculous and represented as absurd.",
"blomberg-s05-018": "Thus, for example, Voltaire made ridiculous the thought of Maupertuis, when the latter is of the opinion that the Egyptians, in order to make themselves an immortal name, ought rather to have buried books in the earth instead of pyramids, so that he becomes quite ridiculous, though he was perhaps one of the greatest men of his time.",
"blomberg-s05-019": "Meanwhile, let no one apply his learnedness to useless objects, nor bring it along into common company, as happened, e.g., with the doctrine of the best world, which was finally applied to everything possible.",
"blomberg-s05-020": "Finally it is to be noted that many a thing is passed off as useless brooding because one is too weak for it and not in a position to be able to have insight into it and to cognize it.",
"blomberg-s05-021": "“As many a man often despises a beautiful woman because he cannot obtain her as he pleases, and as the fox decries the grapes as unripe when he cannot snatch them. –”",
"blomberg-s05-022": "One can never, or at least very rarely, have insight into, and know in advance, all the use of this or that cognition.",
"blomberg-s05-023": "Where, when he discovered his propositions, did Euclid ever think that from his propositions one would find out the distance of the heavenly bodies?",
"blomberg-s05-024": "The author says: that the intermediate truths must not be neglected, since they are often very necessary alongside the principal truths. Because a cognition is difficult, it is for that reason alone not yet to be highly esteemed.",
"blomberg-s05-025": "Because something is imposing, it sometimes pleases, even if it is of no use. To this belong, e.g., the sophistical questions of the Scholastics, the fallacies, the 4 figures of inferences. One of the Scholastic learned men is said to have studied the sophistical proposition that they called the Liar so much that he grew so lean that he finally had to wear leaden soles, so that he would at least have some weight and a strong wind would not blow him off the bridge and throw him down.",
"blomberg-s05-026": "If many trifles come together, they finally make up a great cognition.",
"blomberg-s05-027": "A cognition can be great on account of its many consequences, even if these consequences are not very great and correct. So it is, e.g., with the art of reckoning.",
"blomberg-s05-028": "What is great for one is small for another. The Latin language, as also metaphysical questions and investigations, are important and great to learned men.",
"blomberg-s05-029": "For women, however, these are unnecessary. So too for persons of rank, princes etc., not all cognitions are necessary, but they can rely on the learned in many sciences and in many matters; just as someone who does not understand this or that particular trade relies on the craftsman when he commissions him to make something. For such lords as princes etc. it would be unnecessary to engage deeply in metaphysica and its investigations.",
"blomberg-s05-030": "On the other hand, one should instil in them the true learnedness that is necessary for them; that is, one should teach them preeminently and first of all justice, the worth of man, of their subjects, hatred of flatterers and of flattery, true care for the citizens of the land, and to distinguish the true advantages of the state from the false, and impress upon them the knowledge of all this thoroughly; then many a now ill-disposed monarch will be full of noble magnanimity and of exalted sentiments worthy of his station. We shall have happy princes, subjects, and lands. A great spirit occupies itself, according to its feeling, not with trifles; it sees what is important, and chooses it as its object.",
"blomberg-s06-001": "The general, supreme question of one who wants to learn logica is surely a natural one: What is truth?",
"blomberg-s06-002": "To answer this question, which is so appropriate to the understanding of man, and especially of a beginner, will cause more difficulties than one would think, and there have already been people who have clearly seen that one is actually not in a position to give a distinct, complete, precise concept of it.",
"blomberg-s06-003": "It has always gone so; none of the ancient Roman jurists, e.g., could actually say what is right and not right (quid sit jus), and nevertheless the concept one has of it is complete for use and good enough.",
"blomberg-s06-004": "But in order to see the difficulties that can occur with the concept of truth, we note the following: First, if a mark (criterium) of truth is to be given, a judgment, a rule, will have to be given of it. The supreme criterium therefore cannot be given.",
"blomberg-s06-005": "If a cognition does not agree with the constitution of the thing that we represent to ourselves and want to cognize, then it is false, since it cannot subsist with truth. If, on the other hand, a cognition is in conformity with the constitution of that thing which we represent to ourselves, then it is true.",
"blomberg-s06-006": "The skeptic, however, said on the contrary: Something is true if it agrees with the object. But I cannot have insight into this without first considering and cognizing the object. It is therefore nothing other than that truth will consist in this, that the cognition of the object agrees with the cognition of the object. But this is indeed identitas, idem per idem, nothing, said the skeptics, is a circle in the explanation of truth. This argument was called the Diallele, and as the skeptics thus inferred, they finally derived from it the main conclusion that all distinction of the true and the false was nothing.",
"blomberg-s06-007": "In false cognition I do not cognize and grasp the thing itself that I want to represent to myself, but another. E.g. if I represent the polyp to myself as a worm, then I have a false cognition of a polyp.",
"blomberg-s06-008": "False learned cognitions are those which are supposed to be learned, or also seem to be learned, but in fact are not. This, however, always happens when one clothes the indistinct common cognitions in termini occulti, whereby one nevertheless really obtains no correct concept of the thing, but is merely explained idem per idem. E.g. if one asked after the cause of the propagation and increase of the human and animal understanding, and wanted to have a distinct concept of how this came about, the ancients would say with an affected learned air: the cause of the propagation of men and animals is the Vis plastica, propagating force.",
"blomberg-s06-009": "One then imagines that one has given a real ground of the thing, and yet says nothing more than what one actually asked and wants to know, only in other words.",
"blomberg-s06-010": "One clothes the concept of such a thing somewhat differently, puts, so to speak, a plume of feathers on it, and then esteems it exceedingly highly.",
"blomberg-s06-011": "In the writings of the ancient philosophers one finds such qualitates occultas very frequently, and in the writings of Crusius they are as frequent as they ever could have been among the ancients. He says, e.g.: something is true because no one can regard it as anything other than true, and hold it so.",
"blomberg-s06-012": "The one means just as much as the other; this is therefore a qualitas occulta.",
"blomberg-s06-013": "If one now will not let oneself be asked further about the cause of this or that, one often states a ground that is none. E.g. if I ask why one man yawns and looks at another, the ancients said gravely: the ground of it is sympathy. But this word indicates nothing other than a suffering-with. They thus said nothing other than that it came about because the one must have compassion with the other. But how is this applicable to yawning? Thereby they explain nothing.",
"blomberg-s06-014": "In the truth of learned cognition the formal aspect also matters. The cognition itself must really be not a common but rather a learned cognition.",
"blomberg-s06-015": "The material of a cognition can very often be true; the cognition can conform to the constitution of the thing, and thus as a common cognition have truth.",
"blomberg-s06-016": "But if the same cognition is supposed to be learned without really being so, it thereby becomes a false learned cognition.",
"blomberg-s06-017": "To spread qualitates occultas is the grave of philosophy. One then believes one possesses a real learned cognition, though it is nothing other than a mere common one.",
"blomberg-s06-018": "What Crusius says in his writings about probability pleases very many quite well. But it is in fact nothing other than what one already knows through mere common cognition, only that it is presented with a certain pomp that is always very characteristic of Crusius.",
"blomberg-s06-019": "From this it follows naturally that it carries with it indeed the semblance, but not the essence, of a learnedness and of a learned cognition.",
"blomberg-s06-020": "The first in every matter, cognition, science, art etc. causes great difficulty.",
"blomberg-s06-021": "Truth and error never lie in concepts, but merely in judgments. Whoever therefore never judges will also never err, but he will also never speak a truth.",
"blomberg-s06-022": "The concept is always the datum about which I am to judge. Concepts are the materials for judging.",
"blomberg-s06-023": "There are methods for avoiding all error, but these are so simple that for that very reason one does not heed them. They consist, briefly, in this: do not judge so much, do not decide often, indeed very rarely, except in certain cases where it is also necessary. Do not always, and very seldom, determine whether something is true or whether it is false.",
"blomberg-s06-024": "There is therefore, and it is necessary in order to avoid error, a certain moderation in thinking and in judging.",
"blomberg-s06-025": "But how many philosophers of our times like always to decide.",
"blomberg-s06-026": "To this bold assertion of opinions once adopted or hatched by oneself, to these decisive judgments about propositions of reason, already customary in ancient times, Pyrrho called out in opposition: “Non liquet”. – Many authors entangle themselves in such doubtful circumstances through decisive judgments and side-thoughts, which are often captured (capirt) as errors.",
"blomberg-s06-027": "A household remedy against errors is: to distinguish nothing, to judge little; hence it comes about that one finds a village full of peasants has by far not so many errors as an academy of sciences; thoughtlessness protects them from errors.",
"blomberg-s06-028": "Yet, in order to want to avoid all errors, we shall hardly be permitted to resolve to leave many things unconsidered.",
"blomberg-s06-029": "This tells us, and merely indicates, what the limits of our judgments are, and it is precisely about these limits that one must judge. But if one had exceedingly important purposes, so that every error, even the smallest, would be dangerous, then it is indeed better to stay in one place and judge only about what concerns our affairs, as, e.g., in morals.",
"blomberg-s06-030": "Ignorance is an empty space in our head and for that reason does not yet hinder any true knowledge, so long as it does not spread it. Error, on the other hand, is a hindrance of truth.",
"blomberg-s06-031": "So long as our cognitions are only speculations, we can well still venture to err; but as soon as an error becomes detrimental, and this happens as soon as it bears upon the practical, it is very dangerous to retain it.",
"blomberg-s06-032": "Judgments are actions [207a] of the understanding and of reason.",
"blomberg-s06-033": "The senses, however, do not judge. Not all concepts arise from the understanding, but some from the senses; all judgments, however, come merely from the understanding.",
"blomberg-s06-034": "Men often hold a certain judgment of their understanding to be a sensation and representation of the senses, when it is anything but that.",
"blomberg-s06-035": "About a sensation there is nothing to dispute. It is a datum, and not yet an error. But often such a sensation is actually the judgment of the understanding on the occasion of such sensations.",
"blomberg-s06-036": "Appeals to such feelings and sensations are very much in fashion nowadays; some often want to feel and sense right and wrong masterfully, and indeed through an inner moral feeling peculiar to them alone. Ridiculous. In this the understanding must necessarily be in play.",
"blomberg-s06-037": "Truth is nothing other than the agreement of a cognition with the laws of the understanding and of reason, and conversely the conflict of a cognition with the laws of the understanding and of reason is nothing other than falsity.",
"blomberg-s06-038": "No force in nature deviates in its actions from its laws, or from the conditions under which alone it can act; the understanding taken alone therefore cannot possibly err.",
"blomberg-s06-039": "All judgments agree with the laws of the understanding; all judgments of the understanding are therefore true. Even in our erroneous judgments the understanding must always have done something, and so there can never be everything false in them, but there must always still be something true.",
"blomberg-s06-040": "It is quite impossible that a man should err wholly when he judges.",
"blomberg-s06-041": "In error there is therefore still truth. But we judge there in a mixed fashion, and intermingle the effects of the remaining powers of the mind, and from this arises the erroneous, which is so much contrary to the understanding, and of which we nevertheless falsely think, and often believe quite certainly, that it is an effect of the understanding. Whereas it is in fact nothing other than, as it were, a bastard of sensibility and of the understanding.",
"blomberg-s06-042": "Since now, as we have already shown, every judgment takes place not without an ingredient of truth, it follows necessarily that we shall have to moderate our judgment very much concerning the criticism of truth and concerning the judgments or also errors of others. For it is a certain principium: One can never come to truth if one is always quarreling, if one always contradicts the other.",
"blomberg-s06-043": "Every discovery of an error that is in fact gross and manifest is very sad. One would therefore rather not make such sad discoveries, but help one another in common and in friendship, support one another, and not always agitate (agiren) one against the other.",
"blomberg-s06-044": "Instead, then, of refuting a thing, one will have merely to investigate it, whether there is not really a truth in it, what is missing to supplement it, and so to act in common everywhere, and then to make it comprehensible to the one in error in the least biting, but loving manner, that it is not at all to be wondered at, since he could very easily and gladly have erred.",
"blomberg-s06-045": "This moderate way of judging, this so well applied kindliness of dispositions, is just as necessary to the attainment of upright cognition as it may ever be in common life.",
"blomberg-s06-046": "Naturally, therefore, there is no total error. Otherwise the understanding would have to contradict itself and its prescriptions, and act against them.",
"blomberg-s06-047": "Errors, however great and important they may seem, are nevertheless all only particular.",
"blomberg-s06-048": "One can instruct no one except through the remainder of understanding that is still in him. One can improve no one himself except through the remainder of the good that is still in him.",
"blomberg-s06-049": "In the instruction of the understanding and improvement of the will I must always presuppose something true and something good.",
"blomberg-s06-050": "The judgment of every other man is always the judgment of one of those men, the judgment of all of whom taken together is my judge, and the greatest assessor of all my products of the understanding.",
"blomberg-s06-051": "A contradiction is indeed nothing other than an occurrence in which the one says: yes, the other, however, on the contrary: No.",
"blomberg-s06-052": "It follows from this that it must naturally disquiet every reasonable person to a certain extent. But it is a general duty of a philosopher, in such a case, always to take up the cause of humanity in general, and to think affably thus: these opinions, which seem so bizarre and absurd, are perhaps not so badly thought, not so absurd, as they may seem.",
"blomberg-s06-053": "Everything that makes men united and agreeable contributes really very much to promoting the perfection of the whole human race. Conflict, however, produces nothing. It holds everywhere, and so also in the whole world among the learned:",
"blomberg-s06-054": "Concordia res parvae crescunt",
"blomberg-s06-055": "Discordia dilabuntur",
"blomberg-s06-056": "Further, the barriers, i.e. the narrow limits by which human understanding is surrounded, are not in the least to be regarded as sources of errors, as so many supposed philosophers hold them to be; they are indeed in fact causes of man's ignorance in many respects, but not of error; but as soon as conceit, a presumption, a learned arrogance, to judge more than one knows and is in a position to judge, combines with this ignorance, error can arise from it.",
"blomberg-s06-057": "The sole cause of error is therefore the ill-applied presumption to overstep the barriers and limits of one's own understanding.",
"blomberg-s06-058": "The understanding alone does not err. The senses alone also do not err; they are passive and do not judge at all.",
"blomberg-s06-059": "The human understanding alone judges.",
"blomberg-s06-060": "There must therefore also be certain materialia, stuffs, for judging.",
"blomberg-s06-061": "The understanding must necessarily combine with sensibility in order to be able to judge, and this does not depend in the least on choice.",
"blomberg-s06-062": "There are therefore naturally also subjective conditions of our understanding and thinking, and these we reckon all to sensibility.",
"blomberg-s06-063": "Sometimes the understanding is, as it were, in a certain way perplexed. Its horizon is quite befogged; but since concepts must always be furnished to the understanding and laid before it for choice, we see well enough that sensibility must always assist the understanding. Only this often does not happen sufficiently. [217a]",
"blomberg-s06-064": "For every distinction of the true from the false the cognition of inner sense belongs, i.e. I must be conscious, and become conscious, of what actually lies in my concept and what I think.",
"blomberg-s06-065": "Inner sense is often dull, and its horizon beclouded, and it does not give us help enough.",
"blomberg-s06-066": "But since inner sense also belongs to sensibility, and without it the understanding cannot judge, the understanding must judge by the help of sensibility, and precisely this combination and intermixture of the understanding with sensibility is the source of all errors, namely when the effects of the understanding are taken for effects of sensibility.",
"blomberg-s06-067": "As we thus see, the understanding and sensibility alone cannot err. So we see how an error is possible.",
"blomberg-s06-068": "All errors are, so to speak, curved lines, which we determine in that we are driven from one side by the understanding, from the other side, however, by error.",
"blomberg-s06-069": "We see before us the play of the things of the world, but for that reason we have as yet no complete concept of their motion.",
"blomberg-s06-070": "The senses do not judge. Inner sense, which alone teaches us how we become aware whether we have all the requisita of cognition or not, does not always do us the right service, and from this arises the appearance that something is lacking in the objects themselves for their cognition.",
"blomberg-s06-071": "One can, e.g., have correct rules for reckoning and nevertheless go wrong in carrying them out. There it is commonly a fault of inner sense; this happens through overlooking.",
"blomberg-s06-072": "The sole means of avoiding error will therefore be that I find out certain criteria by which, in every judgment, I can properly distinguish what proceeds from the understanding from what outer or inner senses supply (suppeditiren) to us.",
"blomberg-s06-073": "The understanding, however, when abandoned by inner sense, judges at once, come what may, when inner sense offers it either something wrong or nothing at all.",
"blomberg-s06-074": "All criteria of truth, however, are either",
"blomberg-s06-075": "A: inner or B. outer",
"blomberg-s06-076": "The former are objective, and contain the ground why something is actually true or false. The latter, however, are subjective, and contain certain circumstances by which one is in a position to presume the truth or the falsity in a thing.",
"blomberg-s06-077": "That by which true cognitions are distinguished from false ones is the mark of truth.",
"blomberg-s06-078": "The proposition of identity is the formal mark of truth in affirmative judgments, and the principium contradictionis in negative judgments.",
"blomberg-s06-079": "Material marks are the notae intermediae, by which I prove a judgment, the principia materialia.",
"blomberg-s06-080": "In a simple and wholly simple idea there is naturally neither truth nor falsity to be found; rather, these occur only in comparison.",
"blomberg-s06-081": "If I attribute to the thing a mark that either contradicts it, or is even wholly contrary to it, or deny in it a mark that is nevertheless identical in it.",
"blomberg-s06-082": "That, however, by which I cognize that the comparison with a thing is right or not is called the nota intermedia (principium materiale); e.g. if I want to prove that a body is divisible, I assume a nota intermedia, that it is composed, and infer in the following way: everything that is composed is divisible, atqui every body is composed, ergo every body is divisible.",
"blomberg-s06-083": "Some propositions are so constituted that they have no material mark of truth at all in themselves, but carry only the formal one with them. These are properly the immediate judgments that we have of a thing, and can prove and derive from nothing further. E.g. that a body is composed.",
"blomberg-s06-084": "Simple concepts have the mark in themselves that they are always true. E.g. if I represent the moon to myself as round, this is a true concept because I actually think it so.",
"blomberg-s06-085": "But if I compare this representation of the moon with the representation of other planets, and doubt whether the moon is round or oval, I can very easily err in this, and my cognition can be as well true as false.",
"blomberg-s06-086": "Among all marks of truth the first inner characteristic, or chief mark of it, is inner possibility.",
"blomberg-s06-087": "Through experience we are instructed about the possibility of many things which one would otherwise really not have held to be possible.",
"blomberg-s06-088": "Possibility is nevertheless still far from a sufficient ground of truth: because something is possible, and we can represent it to ourselves well and think it through our reason, it is not yet really true.",
"blomberg-s06-089": "Yet possibility is at least a ground of probability; if something is possible and can be thought by us, we at least believe and hope that it could be true, supposing that we are not even in a position to assert it with certainty.",
"blomberg-s06-090": "Impossibility, however, is on the contrary an always sufficient and sufficient ground for the infallible proof of the untruth or falsity of a thing.",
"blomberg-s06-091": "If we have insight that something is wholly impossible, we commonly also at once hold it to be false.",
"blomberg-s06-092": "Yet it is to be noted here that we can err in both cases. We very often hold something to be useful, and per consequentiam also true and correct, which, when we examine it more closely, is really wholly impossible and false. And on the contrary we again hold many a thing to be impossible and false which is really possible and true at the same time, or if not true, at least useful.",
"blomberg-s06-093": "If the consequences of a cognition are true, then the cognitions themselves are also true. E.g. It is true, and settled for all, that there are fire-spewing mountains, warm baths etc. As this is now true, so it can also very well be true that there is a central fire in the earth.",
"blomberg-s06-094": "With some ground we can indeed always infer from this to the central fire of the earth, for these fire-spewing mountains, warm baths etc. can, with much conjecture, indeed even probability, be regarded as consequences of such a central fire, and from the consequences one can very often infer correctly to the ground.",
"blomberg-s06-095": "If all consequences of every cognition without exception are true, then the cognition is also certainly true. For if there were found even any falsity at all in the cognition, falsity would already have to lie in the consequences. But if this is not so, nothing in the cognition can be false.",
"blomberg-s06-096": "If, e.g., a fortunate engagement is announced to us in the newspapers, and the victory of a hero is made known; if its publication even takes place from the pulpit, if solemn arrangements are made and thanksgiving festivals are held on account of it, in short, if all consequences of it are true, then the engagement itself must also be true.",
"blomberg-s06-097": "Sometimes, however, we cannot cognize all the consequences of a thing, and then some false ones may also sometimes have been intermingled among them and be present, although we hold the cognition itself to be true. E.g. if we assume the central fire to be found in the earth, it will in turn follow that the strata of the earth that were not very far from this central fire would be wholly parched and spoiled by excessive and violent heat, and finally would even have to fall in upon one another.",
"blomberg-s06-098": "But since, as one readily sees, this consequence is false, it is also quite easy to notice that the central fire cannot be held to be wholly beyond doubt certain, and asserted reliably.",
"blomberg-s06-099": "If a cognition is to be possible in connection, it must have correct grounds and important and also true consequences.",
"blomberg-s06-100": "The cognition of the possibility of a thing arises from experience; reason cannot instruct us about it. E.g. that the magnet attracts iron.",
"blomberg-s06-101": "The possibility of composite bodies we can have insight into through reason, if we are convinced of the possibility of simple effects.",
"blomberg-s06-102": "But the first possibility of causes and effects becomes evident only through the senses from experience. Hence reason also gives no objection against the impossibility of a thing whose possibility, or actual existence, we clearly see through experience. For experience instructs us, through the actuality of a thing, naturally also of its possibility.",
"blomberg-s06-103": "Reason, however, can derive from these examples of experience certain laws of the possibility of this or that thing; but the first principia data and materialia of possibility must necessarily be given to us from experience and arise from it.",
"blomberg-s06-104": "But we must not hold something to be impossible because we cannot think it or represent it through the senses: e.g. one born blind cannot make for himself through reason any representation of colors, because experience denies him the data for it; nevertheless he can and may not at all infer to their impossibility. If we assumed that, all philosophy would be abolished.",
"blomberg-s06-105": "There are logical truths. These are those which relate merely to understanding and reason.",
"blomberg-s06-106": "On the other side, however, we can also think an aesthetic and a practical truth. The former relates to the relation of taste, the latter, on the contrary, to the rules of the free will.",
"blomberg-s06-107": "Logical truth, however, is not always aesthetically true, nor does it always carry practical perfection with it.",
"blomberg-s06-108": "Every truth is always in itself a logical perfection, or a perfect cognition, according to the rules of the understanding and of reason.",
"blomberg-s06-109": "Much can be aesthetically true without being logically true; it need only charm and please. E.g. so it is with novels.",
"blomberg-s06-110": "Even practical truth need not always be grounded on understanding and on reason. E.g. if I slander someone to another, I can thereby arouse his affects, and in such a way his will, although this slander is not logically true.",
"blomberg-s06-111": "Logical truth is distinguished from the aesthetic in that, when something delights and moves me, I can relax somewhat of its truth. Logical truth can therefore very often yield to the aesthetic.",
"blomberg-s06-112": "Truth often does not please us most, but rather even the lie. We are far happier in fables than in logical truths. Whoever, e.g., takes nature as his ground in painting does not always agree with delight, charm, and pleasure. Fiction often moves much more.",
"blomberg-s06-113": "Our wishes often belong among the fictiones. They often aim at something better than nature actually offers us. Thus, e.g., enthusiasm in friendship pleases us; we are moved by the earthly pastoral life. It is indeed true that also in aesthetic truth there must always be a certain logical truth; but the more we look to the aesthetically true, the less logical truth can be found in the subject that we want to represent to ourselves. Thus, e.g., it is aesthetically true that man, when he is dead, will not rise again. Although this runs directly counter to logical (not to mention moral) truth.",
"blomberg-s06-114": "What all men say is true according to the rules of taste, and therefore aesthetically true. For a general consensus is sufficient for this. Meanwhile, what is true according to the rules of taste is very often false according to the rules of reason.",
"blomberg-s06-115": "Men often think what they hope for, or at least wish for, as possible and actual, because they would like to have it.",
"blomberg-s06-116": "All fables contain an aesthetic, but seldom a logical truth. E.g. the speaking of animals. Here indeed something true must really be present: but aesthetic truth has precedence over the logically true. One represents to oneself something as possible in it. It also involves no absurdity that animals should not also be able to speak.",
"blomberg-s06-117": "Under a certain hypothesis, which may be invented, a certain degree of logical truth can also prevail even in a fable.",
"blomberg-s06-118": "The degree of truth, however, that must be found in it, no learned man has up to now been able to determine exactly, and the greatest aestheticians have not yet succeeded in indicating it. The objective marks would often not suffice for us. One can here only appeal either to the agreement of our cognitions among themselves, or with the cognitions of others, as happens, e.g., in reckoning.",
"blomberg-s06-119": "We always test a proposition against the reason of others, or against our own, at different times, and moods, and in different situations of our reason. But very often we also rely on the semblance, i.e. on the way it first appears to us. We merely want to obtain the approval of others.",
"blomberg-s06-120": "We gladly publish our judgments when we thereby have, or believe we can obtain, a merit.",
"blomberg-s06-121": "Since the human understanding has a natural law to extend its cognitions as much as ever possible, the means must not be forbidden to it by which alone it is able to distinguish the true from the false, and not only to enrich its cognitions but also to correct them.",
"blomberg-s06-122": "One must therefore set no hindrances in the way of the making known of judgments, that is, of their exposition, to the insights of all. That is indeed the general right of every man, and the only sure way to arrive at truth.",
"blomberg-s06-123": "Even mathematics borrows the proposition, and the triumphant and invincible certainty in its apodictic proofs and convictions, not so much from the objective as rather from the subjective criteria of truth, because in this science there is something that agrees with every reason.",
"blomberg-s06-124": "But where cognitions raise themselves very high, yet soon are cast down all the deeper, there the suspicion is very great that the general approval which was bestowed on them at the beginning and all at once has been grounded only on particular heads, and therefore cannot be lasting.",
"blomberg-s06-125": "A cognition is either wholly true, or either partly true, or partly false.",
"blomberg-s06-126": "But a cognition can never be wholly and entirely false, although it can often be wholly true.",
"blomberg-s06-127": "Every judgment arises from the understanding, hence it must also to some extent agree with the laws of the understanding and of reason.",
"blomberg-s06-128": "It is therefore impossible that a man should pass a wholly false judgment and even accept it as wholly true.",
"blomberg-s06-129": "For otherwise the judgment would have to contradict the laws of the understanding.",
"blomberg-s06-130": "A cognition, therefore, which is even passed off throughout as false will nevertheless always be only partly false, and always contain a certain degree of truth. Indeed, even in the judgments of a madman (however peculiar this remark may seem) a partial truth at least will always be found upon closer examination.",
"blomberg-s06-131": "If we were to assume that some judgments could be wholly and entirely false, it would have to be possible that the understanding deviate from all laws, and in this way, under this supposition, the whole authority of our doctrine of reason would fall and vanish; we would on the contrary be able to trust it very little.",
"blomberg-s06-132": "If another were in a position to judge wholly falsely, it would then be just as possible in the same way for us as for him. We would therefore be in the whole use of our understanding and our reason nothing but very insecure.",
"blomberg-s06-133": "If we therefore believe we find falsity in the judgment of another, we must by that very fact be convinced that some partial truth must prevail in it, however hidden it may be in it.",
"blomberg-s06-134": "In this basic proposition, however, there really prevails much fairness. Such a way of thinking is moral and sympathetic, so that one does not at once deny others all truth in their cognitions.",
"blomberg-s06-135": "But just as exclusive self-love lets only its own welfare be promoted, without taking the least care for the welfare of others and contributing anything to it, so also the exclusive judgment, in which one ascribes to oneself alone the possession of all true cognitions, but denies them wholly to others, and invents nothing but false ones, is nothing but a pernicious conceit, in which one esteems oneself alone highly and despises all others beside oneself.",
"blomberg-s06-136": "Such heads, possessed by this conceit, imagine in their understanding alone a Goshen, while everything else is shrouded in Egyptian darkness.",
"blomberg-s06-137": "Many judgments and concepts are true with regard to their essential parts, but false with regard to their accidental parts.",
"blomberg-s06-138": "In theory we can often judge falsely. Notwithstanding this, however, our judgments can nevertheless be true with regard to practice.",
"blomberg-s06-139": "Even in natural theology there can really be many erroneous ideas and speculationes which are nevertheless true in praxi. E.g. with the omnipresence of God one can represent and think it as well as nothing as also as an extension through the whole of space.",
"blomberg-s06-140": "This concept is indeed false, but notwithstanding this it is nevertheless true with regard to practice. Let God be omnipresent however and in whatever way he will; enough, he really is so, and we must therefore stand in awe of his omnipresence, let it consist in whatever it may.",
"blomberg-s06-141": "If a speculation is indeed erroneous, but this error nevertheless has no influence on praxis, i.e. on the morality of conduct, then it is indifferent with regard to conduct, and not so abominable as if it also corrupted conduct.",
"blomberg-s06-142": "If therefore our judgments are in conflict with the judgments of others, then in order to be able to compare these judgments with and against one another we must make use of the following rules: A mark of truth is: when our judgments agree with the judgments of others who can have knowledge of the matter, or, which is the same, when they are universally valid.",
"blomberg-s06-143": "If therefore something is to be found in my judgment which is contrary to the judgments of others, then I am not very sure as to the truth of my judgment, and I must therefore first be concerned to seek out some settled agreement of my judgment with the judgment of others, even if hidden and far-fetched, and this agreement will really, at least in most cases, be very easy to find. For in our agreeing judgments there is more agreement present than we suppose.",
"blomberg-s06-144": "It is only necessary that we understand the judgment of others and do not misinterpret it.",
"blomberg-s06-145": "There is also no systema of general truths of reason that would be wholly and entirely true, except mathematics, just as no cognition can be wholly false.",
"blomberg-s06-146": "A cognition can be partialiter true in that it is indeed wholly true, but does not extend to the whole object, but only to a part of it. Nonetheless such cognitions are always certain.",
"blomberg-s06-147": "Whoever therefore confidently gives the approval of truth to a whole philosophical systema of another at once, all at one time, and without thinking much about it, of him one can always rightly infer that he does not yet understand it and is not in a position to distinguish the true from the false properly. For it is certain: no systema can be wholly true; there is always a falsity set in it, even if it does not concern the essential of the system. In philosophical truths the errors and faults are always so intermingled with the truth that one always finds something in them to be improved.",
"blomberg-s06-148": "A cognition is exact (genau) which has enough truth in itself as is required only for the attainment of the chief purpose, but gross (grob) on the other hand if one cannot attain the chief purpose with it. (Exactness is a cognition, but cannot properly be set in opposition to its grossness; rather its grossness must be opposed to its unity.)",
"blomberg-s06-149": "Precision (Praecision), punctiliousness, is: when I also do not indicate more of what is true in a cognition than is necessary to have insight into it. The transgression of this rule becomes a great fault. That in a cognition is called gross which requires only a small attention, that is called subtle to whose clarity a greater attention is required.",
"blomberg-s06-150": "Very often it is necessary to be subtle, because from the omission of subtlety such great confusions arise, although subtlety too, since it requires an all too great attention, is often censured.",
"blomberg-s06-151": "With regard to practical cognitions one can appeal very much to sound understanding and rely on it.",
"blomberg-s06-152": "A rule for testing correctly oneself and one's science of any cognition is this: if I myself understand and have insight into a thing perfectly, I must be able to impart and represent it to another man who has only a sound understanding so clearly and so distinctly that he has insight into it just as perfectly as I. But if I cannot do this, it is a sure sign that I myself do not yet rightly understand it.",
"blomberg-s06-153": "Every cognition is subject to a partial untruth; but this untruth concerns merely the more or less exact, and it can nevertheless still always be exact enough to attain its purpose. E.g. if I measure a mountain, I shall never be able to measure so exactly and infallibly that I would not miss at least, supposing it were only a few inches; notwithstanding this, however, the quantum arrived at through my measurement is sufficient and exact enough to determine the whole height of the mountain. Many of our judgments of reason are made only by eye-measure, and can therefore indeed not be exact enough according to the finer reason and learnedness; notwithstanding this they are quite exact for their purpose.",
"blomberg-s06-154": "The highest and most laborious exactness, however, which must be attained with much accuracy and learnedness but is yet of no use with regard to the chief purpose, is called pedantry. In mathematics, however, it is quite otherwise. There an exactness must always take place. Nothing must be lacking in it.",
"blomberg-s06-155": "If a cognition is correct considered historically, it can nevertheless be incorrect considered formaliter. E.g. the soul is immortal; this proposition is materialiter true and correct. The form, however, when one represents this to oneself, can often be quite false.",
"blomberg-s06-156": "A cognition, however, is materialiter true when we have a material science of it. Formaliter true, however, when the grounds for proving and deriving it are correct.",
"blomberg-s06-157": "Much that is undoubtedly certain with regard to praxis is false with regard to reason. E.g. a part is smaller than the whole, or: the world must have a beginning.",
"blomberg-s06-158": "A cognition can be materialiter true and formaliter false, but not conversely; it is then a formal error. This occurs more frequently in philosophy than one believes.",
"blomberg-s06-159": "So too there lies no material but a formal error in the qualitates occultae which one gives as a ground for the cause of a thing, and answers the given question with the question itself.",
"blomberg-s06-160": "All definitions that explain idem per idem are formaliter erroneous.",
"blomberg-s06-161": "These formal errors, where one gives the philosopher the proof as a gift in a cognition, in that one is already taken in by it anticipando, and holds the proof to be wholly dispensable.",
"blomberg-s06-162": "Where my sound understanding already precedes the cognition of the truth, there I am not in the least scrupulous, nor may I be, about examining the rigor of the proof. This rigor, which is applied to certain propositions, also often comes from the fact that they must be regarded not only as very true but also as very necessary.",
"blomberg-s06-163": "So it is with the two articles of faith of natural religion",
"blomberg-s06-164": "1: there is a God.",
"blomberg-s06-165": "2: there is another world.",
"blomberg-s06-166": "Those who want to examine such shallow proofs of such necessary propositions, and to make some fine distinctiones, immediately fall into bad repute, because people believe they may well be enemies of the propositions themselves.",
"blomberg-s06-167": "These are the piae fraudes (pious frauds), which are so suited to man, so very much his own.",
"blomberg-s06-168": "One thinks, and believes one is quite right, when one believes that one does not deceive someone if, under all sorts of pretext, dissimulation, etc., one brings something good to him; indeed one often seeks merit in it; but, without looking to the moral side of this deception, which nevertheless always remains, it is already reprehensible in itself; the recommendation of virtue must be virtuous. –",
"blomberg-s06-169": "Much is often recognized as true without any marks. Such truths, unshakable in themselves, are called indemonstrable, i.e. because one cannot doubt them. Otherwise it is not necessary to prove them specially.",
"blomberg-s06-170": "A cognition all of whose consequences are true is also true. But if only one consequence is false, then the cognition is also false.",
"blomberg-s06-171": "From a false cognition nothing true can arise. For a false cognition is the true nothing. But the consequences of a cognition that is partly true, partly false, can be true.",
"blomberg-s06-172": "Hence in the examination of a cognition one will be obliged to look not so much to its true consequences as to its false ones.",
"blomberg-s06-173": "In general it is indeed true that the so-called apagogic proofs, or the demonstrationes ad absurdum contrarium, in which, namely, in order to maintain and strengthen one's opinion, one makes it clear and shows how ridiculous and wholly absurd it would be if one were to assume the opposite of it, that these proofs are very easy; but they do not give me enough light with regard to the sources of truth.",
"blomberg-s06-174": "The connection of cognitions with their grounds is therefore probably the surest and best mark of the truth of a cognition.",
"blomberg-s06-175": "Historical cognitions are distinguished from rational ones according to the rational form of the cognition",
"blomberg-s06-176": "A cognition is historical if it does not agree with the form of reason, rational if it agrees with it, and indeed without regard to the object. Here, however, we must also reflect on the object, if we want to fix the distinction between dogmatic and historical cognition.",
"blomberg-s06-177": "1stly The dogmatic is a universal cognition that arises a priori from reason",
"blomberg-s06-178": "2ndly The historical, however, is not always universal, and rests on reports of things that have happened, on the report of others; it thus arises a posteriori.",
"blomberg-s06-179": "All morals is dogmatic; one teaches, censures, and points out there not what is commonly abhorred by human beings, but what ought to be censured.",
"blomberg-s06-180": "Physics, however, is not dogmatic. One part of it would be pure physics, where we derive the properties of bodies from universal rules and concepts.",
"blomberg-s06-181": "The weight of bodies, however, already belongs to experience and is a historical cognition a posteriori.",
"blomberg-s06-182": "All dogmata should properly be grounded first on reason, but they can also often be historical.",
"blomberg-s06-183": "The object of dogmata is not individual things but universal properties and constitutions of things. The object of history, however, is individual things.",
"blomberg-s06-184": "Although dogmata have universal properties of things as their object, they can nevertheless often be cognized historically, although by their nature they are rational.",
"blomberg-s06-185": "All dogmata are by their nature objectively rational, although they can be subjectively historical.",
"blomberg-s06-186": "We must now also speak of systems (Lehr-Gebäude, doctrinal edifices). The author restricts the meaning of this word far too much, in that he applies it merely and solely to dogmatic truth.",
"blomberg-s06-187": "A better definition of a system, then, will be the following:",
"blomberg-s06-188": "A system is a multitude or manifold of many simple and connected cognitions and truths, such that, taken together, they make up a whole. Every system must therefore carry a unity with it, but this unity can have as its ground either coordination, as with historical cognitions, or subordination.",
"blomberg-s06-189": "A systema, however, is",
"blomberg-s06-190": "A. historical",
"blomberg-s06-191": "B. rational",
"blomberg-s06-192": "The form of a system is in most cases regarded as arbitrary.",
"blomberg-s06-193": "Human beings have different understandings; he who likes to go to the parts is subtle. He who goes to the whole is the great one.",
"blomberg-s06-194": "A genuine system must be built by only a single person. It cannot possibly be a patchwork to which one adds this and another that.",
"blomberg-s06-195": "This capacity to form a systema is so excellent because it is so rare, and at the same time has such great utility.",
"blomberg-s06-196": "One must in any case have some kind of systema, for each of our cognitions must have a logical place.",
"blomberg-s06-197": "The earliest systemata are naturally astonishingly deficient. Gradually they become larger and more complete.",
"blomberg-s06-198": "We have already above fixed the distinction between logical and aesthetic truths.",
"blomberg-s06-199": "Much can be logically true without therefore having an aesthetic truth in itself, and much, on the other hand, can be aesthetically true without therefore having logical truth in itself.",
"blomberg-s06-200": "To this belong all fictiones.",
"blomberg-s06-201": "To this belong all aesthetic truths, which are true according to the rules of taste.",
"blomberg-s06-202": "In these cognitions there really reigns a kind of fashion. To this belong, e.g., proverbs, which are really subject to a kind of fashion, although they are really not even always such propositions as are according to taste.",
"blomberg-s06-203": "Aesthetically true is everything that accords with the rules of taste and agrees with the rules of appearance.",
"blomberg-s06-204": "Poets who want to write merely aesthetically true things are surely not tested by the gold-scales of logic; one does them great wrong thereby. They are poets, and not moralists and logicians, and always remain in their worth.",
"blomberg-s06-205": "In order to be aesthetically true, a degree of untruth can often also take place, which, however, must nevertheless not be wholly contrary to the truth. E.g. when a philosopher once exclaimed: My dear friends, there are no friends, this is aesthetically true, and a good thought, because good friends are very rare, and a complete agreement of minds is very rare.",
"blomberg-s06-206": "We now come to a very important doctrine of the whole of logic, namely to the doctrine of the origin of error. Does error arise from a lack of reason?",
"blomberg-s06-207": "Answer: No. From a lack of reason nothing arises but ignorance. Ignorance, however, is very much distinguished from error.",
"blomberg-s06-208": "He who knows little can nevertheless judge quite correctly about this little.",
"blomberg-s06-209": "The other question is:",
"blomberg-s06-210": "Does error also not arise from a lack of cognition, combined with the desire to judge?",
"blomberg-s06-211": "Answer: He who knows for certain that he is ignorant will certainly not venture to judge about something he does not understand.",
"blomberg-s06-212": "But suppose he did not feel himself incapable either, and thought he knew much, and therefore wanted to judge about much, nothing more would arise from this; he would apply his effort to judge in vain.",
"blomberg-s06-213": "But that it still seems very hard to see how the understanding in judging can deviate from its laws, how can that happen?",
"blomberg-s06-214": "No power can by itself conflict with its own laws. E.g. when a body is left to its weight, it falls downward according to the laws of gravity, and one is really in a position to calculate the speed of its fall.",
"blomberg-s06-215": "If, therefore, in our judgments through the understanding no power were active, no power intermingled, we would really never err, but only often be ignorant.",
"blomberg-s06-216": "No error therefore arises properly from the understanding, but another power must at the same time be active in the understanding if an error is to arise. All powers of nature deviate from their laws only when foreign laws mingle among the given laws.",
"blomberg-s06-217": "If, therefore, we had nothing but understanding, we would indeed judge only little, but all of this little we would judge truly.",
"blomberg-s06-218": "If we furthermore had a [274a] wholly pure will, then, supposing we did only very little good, we could nevertheless never sin.",
"blomberg-s06-219": "The deviation from the rules of the pure will constitutes moral evil, and this arises only then, and thereby, because other effects of other powers mix with the otherwise pure laws of the will. E.g.: the inclinations and affects. In just the same way, indeed, when foreign powers mingle with the correct laws of the understanding, a mixed effect arises, and error arises from the conflict of our judgments with the laws of the understanding and of reason.",
"blomberg-s06-220": "Every error is a phaenomenon, a riddle with regard to the concept of its possibility.",
"blomberg-s06-221": "It is, as it were, a wholly unusual, unnatural appearance, which contradicts the laws of the understanding.",
"blomberg-s06-222": "Everything that is contrary to the laws of nature is for us no object of investigation, but certainly that which occurs rarely and is contrary to the laws of nature.",
"blomberg-s06-223": "Error does indeed occur often, but it conflicts with the laws of the understanding and of reason, and so it is worthy of being an object of our investigation.",
"blomberg-s06-224": "Error proceeds merely from the understanding and reason, because foreign powers intermingle in them, but never from the senses and the imagination.",
"blomberg-s06-225": "Since our understanding and reason judge objectively, there are nevertheless other subjective grounds of our judgment, which do not agree with the understanding and reason.",
"blomberg-s06-226": "All error arises when we take subjective grounds of our judgments for objective ones. [276a]",
"blomberg-s06-227": "The whole history of ghosts is grounded not on the judgment of the understanding but on the imagination, which, in observing an object, adds other similar objects to it, although they do not exist.",
"blomberg-s06-228": "The understanding connects concepts with a thing, in accordance with its laws and with the thing, the object itself.",
"blomberg-s06-229": "The imagination, however, adds other images, which do not relate to the object but to the subject.",
"blomberg-s06-230": "From this mixing of the imagination with the understanding arise such effects [276b] as do not wholly agree with the rules of the understanding and of reason.",
"blomberg-s06-231": "Many errors arise from habit, others merely because a change has occurred in the subject, e.g. when we make a spot on the wall.",
"blomberg-s06-232": "Many errors arise because one constantly renews in the mind the images once had, and therefore regards everything similar as the thing itself. E.g. when a youth has been treated harshly by his teacher, so that he hates him on that account, and he afterwards sees a man who resembles him, he is very often inclined to hate him too merely [276c] on account of the resemblance to his disciplinarian.",
"blomberg-s06-233": "One can often reject something through the understanding, and through the effect of another power accept the same again; all this arises merely from the mixing of the powers, which, even if they are not contrary to one another, are nevertheless of different kinds.",
"blomberg-s06-234": "We cognize the size of things in no other way than through judgment with regard to distances, e.g. the difference between a fly and an elephant.",
"blomberg-s06-235": "We notice a fly well when it is near, an elephant etc. also at a distance, and therefore we [276d] infer only that the latter must be larger than the former.",
"blomberg-s06-236": "The imagination, however, can greatly alter these judgments, and bring about in our soul other impressions, other representations of the thing than we ought to form of it when we see its true properties.",
"blomberg-s06-237": "Let one therefore remove from man the imagination, sensible wit, and the judgment of the understanding will always be true.",
"blomberg-s06-238": "The understanding mixed with other powers errs, and we must in turn see this error through the understanding itself. From this [276e] it is evident that we must see and recognize through the same power whose faults we want to notice.",
"blomberg-s06-239": "No error can arise from the understanding and from reason alone, for no power can act contrary to its own laws.",
"blomberg-s06-240": "That ignorance in itself cannot be in the least a source of an error has already been shown at greater length in the previous paragraph.",
"blomberg-s06-241": "Error is either avoidable or unavoidable.",
"blomberg-s06-242": "If one does not judge at all, then one can of course avoid all errors; he who does not judge also does not err. An error can also be prevented through ignorance. This ignorance, however, does not consist in the ignorance of lack, but in the ignorance of resolve, and this latter is really a kind of wise ignorance. Many speculationes are nothing other than a path to the greatest errors. Many errors, however, can be avoided if one has the resolve to settle nothing, to decide nothing. But many things are of such a kind that they cannot possibly remain undecided; e.g. when a judge is to settle disputes, suppose, then, that he errs in these matters and cases too, the error is unavoidable with regard to him",
"blomberg-s06-243": "One can hold an opinion about something, and indeed with reason without erring; but if one passes off the same mere opinion and deficient conjecture as a certain truth, then one often errs.",
"blomberg-s06-244": "The propensity to decide is therefore the surest path to error, and a certain dogmatic pride (more common among some so-called philosophers); but if one does not yet trust the marks of truth that one has so far recognized, and therefore does not yet pass a judgment, but often withholds it, then one really avoids many errors.",
"blomberg-s06-245": "A cognition is",
"blomberg-s06-246": "1. manifestly or 2. covertly false.",
"blomberg-s06-247": "This division of false cognition is relative.",
"blomberg-s06-248": "We have a kind of middle understanding, which is abstracted from the understanding of all human beings, and is, as it were, a measuring-rod, i.e. an ideal.",
"blomberg-s06-249": "If we now take an error that can subsist with this middle degree of understanding, then the error is still tolerable; but if it can no longer subsist with it, then it is no longer to be tolerated, and then it is an absurdity (Ungereimtheit).",
"blomberg-s06-250": "Absurdity, namely, presupposes not only a false cognition but also that the common understanding (le sens commun) can see it to be false.",
"blomberg-s06-251": "One can make every covertly false cognition manifestly false, and then one brings the cognition ad absurdum, so that he who has hitherto held that cognition is indeed not absolutely absurd, but would be manifestly absurd and would act contrary to all reason if, in view of all the grounds that have been laid before him for the falsity of his cognition, he wished to hold it true.",
"blomberg-s06-252": "It is foolish, therefore, when learned men call themselves absurd and yet afterwards want to dispute with one another whether what they have written is true or false. For by calling one another absurd, they deny one another all true cognition at once, and if I suppose that 282] someone possesses no cognition whatever, and is able to possess none, how can he then see my grounds for the falsity of his cognition, or even boast of a true cognition and believe he possesses it? But how am I then in a position to dispute?",
"blomberg-s06-253": "In the 114th paragraph the author deals with the necessary and the contingent, and contingent truths, on which we have nothing further to remark.",
"blomberg-s07-001": "All grounds of cognition are either internal or external. The former are determinations in the thing itself, through which it can already be cognized without comparison with other things. Through the latter, however, I obtain a cognition of a thing only insofar as I compare it with other things. And these external grounds of cognition are called marks (Merckmahle) or tokens (Kennzeichen).",
"blomberg-s07-002": "Internal cognitions, however, cannot properly even be called marks or tokens of a thing.",
"blomberg-s07-003": "In the comparison of one thing with another we can always represent to ourselves two things: either sameness, identitas, or difference, diversitas. E.g. marks of sameness in human beings and animals are that both have a perishable body. Marks of difference, however, are that the former are endowed with a rational soul, the latter, however, have no reason at all.",
"blomberg-s07-004": "In most cases one uses the marks of identity, but in many also the marks of diversity.",
"blomberg-s07-005": "All genera and species, e.g., arise from the marks of identity, not of difference.",
"blomberg-s07-006": "But if one wants, e.g., to distinguish metals from one another, then one must on the contrary have notae diversitatis, e.g. that gold is heavier than all metals. For this is the chief distinguishing mark of gold from silver, copper etc. and the remaining metals.",
"blomberg-s07-007": "A mark is therefore not merely a ratio disjunctionis (ground of separation), as some have defined it, but also a ratio identitatis (ground of identity).",
"blomberg-s07-008": "A mark is called in general a nota.",
"blomberg-s07-009": "All marks, however, are in general either",
"blomberg-s07-010": "1 internal",
"blomberg-s07-011": "2 external",
"blomberg-s07-012": "Sunt vel externae, vel internae omnes Notae. (All marks are either external or internal.)",
"blomberg-s07-013": "1: The marks that I cognize of a thing are internal when I consider the thing solely in itself.",
"blomberg-s07-014": "2: The marks that I have of a thing are external when I compare the thing with other things. And such marks are called characteres, grounds of distinction, tokens in the proper sense.",
"blomberg-s07-015": "The use of all marks will therefore be able to be, likewise,",
"blomberg-s07-016": "1: internal",
"blomberg-s07-017": "2 external",
"blomberg-s07-018": "If I cognize the internal marks which, taken together, make up the whole concept, then these marks are complete and sufficient to cognize in a thing everything that can be cognized in it absolutely.",
"blomberg-s07-019": "Absolute cognitions of things, however, are far harder than the cognitions one acquires through comparisons. For the latter are far easier and more natural to us, because it lies, as it were, already in our nature that in comparison with others we far more easily become aware of, and are able to notice, agreement or difference in objects. Thus, e.g., ugliness en comparaison with a beauty, and a true beauty, strikes the eye when she is among many ugly persons, and the human being himself, when he makes an assessment of himself, mostly asks not how good he is and how much he still lacks, but rather whether he is not as good, or perhaps even better, than others. Hence it comes about that Herr von Wolff, and our author himself, take marks and tokens to be one and the same.",
"blomberg-s07-020": "In the beginning it is mostly the case that most things are held to be, if not wholly the same, yet only very little different; with time the differences are gradually discovered, and the more one attends to things, the more and more marks are also found. But for this gradual search for the marks of a thing there is also needed much fineness of understanding, just as there is for being able to find, among things where the greatest differences seem to be, or really are, nevertheless a certain similarity, a sameness, and agreement.",
"blomberg-s07-021": "In this paragraph the author speaks of mediate and immediate marks. If a thing can be cognized through a certain mark without the mediation of a mark that is different from this, then this is an immediate mark. A mediate mark, however, is on the contrary a mark of a mark: e.g. perishability is a mark of the human being, but an immediate one, for perishability is a mark of a mortal being, i.e. of a body, but the human being is mortal and has a body.",
"blomberg-s07-022": "There are therefore degrees of remote marks, and so there are degrees of mediate marks. The first degree is: when a thing can be cognized only through one nota intermedia. But among all marks, of which one is the mark of another, a relation of subordination is always to be found. But there is also a relation of coordination, and this takes place with immediate marks, where each particular mark is a new ground of cognition of the thing.",
"blomberg-s07-023": "Through experience we can become aware of, and cognize, no other marks of a thing than coordinated, i.e. juxtaposed, ones. Reason, however, is able to supply us only with subordinated marks of a thing, i.e. to present to us series of marks.",
"blomberg-s07-024": "In the subordination of marks I have nothing more than a single immediate mark, and the remaining notae are marks of marks. In the coordinatio notarum, however, all marks are immediate.",
"blomberg-s07-025": "In the series of the subordinated, i.e. marks subordinated to one another, there is always a first, i.e. the concepts are limited.",
"blomberg-s07-026": "In coordination, however, there is no limitation.",
"blomberg-s07-027": "There are countless marks that belong immediately to a thing, and our understanding is not able to see all properties and to determine them exactly.",
"blomberg-s07-028": "In subordination the immediate mark is called nota infima, and the last mark of all marks is called the nota summa.",
"blomberg-s07-029": "Through the subordination of marks I obtain a deep, i.e. intensive magnitude; through the coordination of marks, notarum, however, one obtains an extensive magnitude, i.e. a cognition worked out according to its ground and extended.",
"blomberg-s07-030": "The series of marks coordinated with one another is, as it were, a line without boundaries, which is infinite; for it is still always possible, indeed to be presumed, that posterity will with time still be able to find out ever more immediate marks of a thing.",
"blomberg-s07-031": "The series of marks subordinated to one another, however, is bounded, and has its limits, and indeed often very soon, when no mark of a mark can be given in it any more.",
"blomberg-s07-032": "Metaphysica is the science by means of which we seek out the terminus a priori of the subordinated marks. Through it we obtain simple and unanalyzable concepts.",
"blomberg-s07-033": "Too much beauty also harms, and really arouses suspicion. Beautiful simplicity is often found through nature. The greatest art, however, consists in so concealing the art that is really applied somewhere, that it is not noticeable at all, but seems to be sheer nature.",
"blomberg-s07-034": "Profundity is in a certain way opposed to beauty; cognition becomes dry the more abstraction is made in it. Merely abstract cognitions in general cannot be fully understood unless I present and see a case in concreto. The dry, however, often also falls into the droll, and for this art is often needed: thus, e.g., in witty remarks in company. And one is accustomed to call this simplicity.",
"blomberg-s07-035": "In all description one seeks more to understand (verstehen) the thing than to have insight into (einsehen) it. In all explanation, on the contrary, one seeks more to have insight into the thing than to understand it.",
"blomberg-s07-036": "The multitude of marks coordinated with one another increases experience.",
"blomberg-s07-037": "The extensive magnitude of a cognition is, as it were, its volumen, the intensive magnitude, however, is its density. In certain cases the extensive magnitude of cognition is more useful, in others the intensive. In morals, e.g., the intensive magnitude of a cognition is more necessary, in physics, however, and medicine, the extensive magnitude of cognition is very often more indispensable.",
"blomberg-s07-038": "Dry cognitions are not at all suited to sensibility, and therefore not to beauty either. Thorough, deep, solid cognitions, however, can and must be dry without reproach. This dryness, however, one then compensates for by considering the abstract in concreto in a certain case. (Analysis goes ascendendo, synthesis, however, descendendo.)",
"blomberg-s07-039": "Through negative marks of a thing I think something that is not really to be found in the thing. Through affirmative marks, however, I think what is actually present in the thing. Negative marks, however, are possible only by first representing to oneself the marks opposed to them, which affirm. Thus, e.g., I cannot know what darkness is unless I have beforehand a clear idea of light.",
"blomberg-s07-040": "Negative marks, however, serve only to avoid errors. E.g. animals have no rational soul. Affirmative marks, however, serve to obtain an extended, unelaborated cognition of a thing. E.g. God is omniscient etc. Gold loses nothing of its massa and weight in fire, is ductile.",
"blomberg-s07-041": "Besides ignorance there is another, still more dangerous evil in the understanding, and this is error. Through affirmative marks we avoid ignorance, through negative marks, however, error. This is the purpose of both kinds of marks. Through negative marks one admittedly obtains no new cognition at all; nonetheless they are by all means very necessary and important, for one thereby guards oneself against erroneous cognitions.",
"blomberg-s07-042": "The fewer means we have at hand for arriving at true cognitions, the more negative marks we will need in order not to err in the cognition of the thing. Thus, e.g., in no science is there a greater multitude of negative marks than in rational theology (vernünftige Gottes-Gelahrtheit), in natural theologia. For in it we have no means at all at hand for arriving at extended cognitions. Thus, e.g., we are at every moment inclined to err in the concept, idea, and representation of the divine being and of God himself in general, because we very gladly like to compare him sensibly with the things we perceive on earth, but these can be no measuring-rod of God at all. God cannot be cognized by us through the senses, and consequently our whole knowledge of him can only be very much restricted.",
"blomberg-s07-043": "If, therefore, we consider God in theologia naturali and remove all negative marks through which we speak of him, nothing will remain for us but the idea of the all-sufficient and most perfect being.",
"blomberg-s07-044": "Negative judgments are really often foolish, because what is denied is often already impossible in itself. E.g.: the human being has reason, therefore he is no stone. It is true, that is an established truth which no one will doubt; but because it is also manifest that no one will take it into his head seriously to hold a human being for a stone, this judgment is in vain.",
"blomberg-s07-045": "Every mark is a ground of cognition, which is estimated by its consequences. Now according as the consequences are of worth with regard to reason or to our perfection and happiness, so too the marks as grounds of cognition will be of worth.",
"blomberg-s07-046": "From the former consequences the logical worth, from the others, however, the practical worth of marks is to be derived.",
"blomberg-s07-047": "If many consequences are to be derived from one ground, then it is fruitful, or it has an extensive magnitude, e.g. from the sun flow many consequences with regard to the earth. Thus the rules of morals are very fruitful, in that they have an influence on our life, i.e. on our conduct, which is very great.",
"blomberg-s07-048": "A mark is extensively great if I can cognize many consequences of a thing. A mark is intensively great, however, when I can cognize from a thing not so much many as great and important consequences. The former mark is called fruitful, the latter, however, correct (richtig).",
"blomberg-s07-049": "Empty speculations have a logical worth. They are often of many consequences with regard to reason, although they indeed have no influence on the happiness of human beings, e.g. that the moon is inhabited etc. This proposition has no influence on the will, on the state etc. No one becomes more perfect, happier, or better with regard to his external understanding through this cognition, although with regard to his reason he becomes somewhat more perfect.",
"blomberg-s07-050": "Other cognitions are aesthetically correct because they stimulate our taste and give pleasure, e.g. poetry delights because it accords with our taste. All importance of cognition, however, exists only when it is considered by itself, without comparison with other cognitions.",
"blomberg-s07-051": "Marks either suffice for our purposes, and then they are comparative sufficientes, or for distinguishing a thing from all others, thus for all logical purposes, and then they are notae absolute sufficientes",
"blomberg-s07-052": "All experiences have only a relative sufficientia. E.g. when I consider a pear tree closely, I can easily distinguish its leaves from the leaves of all other trees in the forest. This cognition is therefore sufficient in relation to other cognitions.",
"blomberg-s07-053": "The sufficientia relativa, or comparativa, suffices to distinguish a thing from such things as we merely know so far, but not from all things that are possible, that can ever exist. E.g. gold is distinguished from all other metals by its weight, but one has already found a metal, called platina, which is of white color but has the same weight as gold. Consequently this mark, and ground of distinction, of gold from all other metals is not sufficient enough",
"blomberg-s07-054": "Things that fall under our senses can be cognized and distinguished only through marks that are sufficient relatively, not however by means of such marks as suffice absolutely.",
"blomberg-s07-055": "The sufficientia notarum, however, is",
"blomberg-s07-056": "1: externa",
"blomberg-s07-057": "2. interna",
"blomberg-s07-058": "The former consists in this, that the marks of a thing are sufficient to distinguish it from other things by comparison. The latter, however, consists in this, that one mark of a thing suffices to derive from it all the remaining determinations of it. From this sufficientia arises the division of the grounds of cognition into",
"blomberg-s07-059": "A: internal and B: external",
"blomberg-s07-060": "From the latter nothing can be derived, e.g. gold has a yellow color, the human being is an animal that laughs. The former, however, are fruitful, so that much flows from them.",
"blomberg-s07-061": "External grounds of cognition can often be very important in themselves, but with regard to internal sufficiency be unfruitful and useless.",
"blomberg-s07-062": "Necessary marks are such as pertain ad essentiam (to the essence), without which the thing cannot be thought at all. Pertinent autem ad Essentiam 1: vel Solum ut Causae aut 2: ut Causata (They pertain to the essence either 1: solely as causes or 2: as caused)",
"blomberg-s07-063": "Omnia autem ad Essentiam pertinentia (But all things pertaining to the essence) A: Essentialia, e.g. the",
"blomberg-s07-064": "extension B. Attributa. E.g. the divisibility of bodies. Contingent marks, however, are extra essentialia, without which the thing can nevertheless be thought, e.g. learnedness (Gelahrtsamkeit) in a human being.",
"blomberg-s07-065": "If the essentialia are marks, then they are coordinated, not subordinated, to one another; the attributa, however, are not coordinated with the essentialia but subordinated, for they follow from one another.",
"blomberg-s07-066": "The extra essentialia marks are either",
"blomberg-s07-067": "A. internal or B. external.",
"blomberg-s07-068": "The latter are also called relations (Verhältniße). The internal ones, however, are in turn",
"blomberg-s07-069": "1: necessary",
"blomberg-s07-070": "2: contingent",
"blomberg-s07-071": "The former are in turn",
"blomberg-s07-072": "1. complete, or marks that suffice to derive from them everything contained in the thing, or",
"blomberg-s07-073": "2: incomplete. When the marks do not suffice to derive and cognize from them everything that belongs to the thing.",
"blomberg-s07-074": "The internal contingent relations are called modi. The modification of the external relations does not modify the human being himself, although among the things of nature a change of relation also seems for the most part to be connected at the same time with a change of modification.",
"blomberg-s07-075": "Summer and winter are properly not changes in the sun itself, but only altered relationes, and relations of the sun to our earth.",
"blomberg-s07-076": "Modi are nothing other than internal determinations and changes that can be thought in a thing.",
"blomberg-s07-077": "Relationes, however, are: changes occurring in a thing in relation to others, and in reference to other things.",
"blomberg-s07-078": "But we now put the question whether, in the completudo notarum, necessaria interna, a praecisio (precision) cannot also take place, so that superfluous marks are found in it which are no longer necessary to distinguish the thing from all others and to derive from it all the proper determinations, so that everything is left out which, even if it is lacking, is nevertheless so constituted that the concept would still be complete even without it?",
"blomberg-s07-079": "Responsio (Answer): Yes. It often works. The notae rei necessariae internae sufficientes make up the praecisio.",
"blomberg-s07-080": "If the marks contain no less than is necessary for the distinction of the thing, then this is a praecisio.",
"blomberg-s07-081": "If, finally, the marks of a thing are wholly adequate to it, they are notae adaequatae.",
"blomberg-s07-082": "The adequate concept of a thing, insofar as it can no longer be derived from any other, is the concept of the essence, essentia.",
"blomberg-s07-083": "The essence of a thing is the first sufficient fundamental concept, which suffices to derive everything that belongs to the former.",
"blomberg-s07-084": "The notae necessariae sunt (Necessary marks are)",
"blomberg-s07-085": "A: primitivae or B: derivativae",
"blomberg-s07-086": "The former make up the essentialia, the latter the attributa. E.g. in the triangle the 3 angles are just as necessary and indispensable as the 3 sides. But the former one derives in turn from the latter, and they are therefore attributa; the sides themselves, however, are fundamental concepts, essentialia.",
"blomberg-s07-087": "To cognize the essential of a thing the following is necessary: to cognize the attributa I must cognize the internal necessary marks, which are, however, accompanied by others. But to cognize the essentialia I must see that the marks can no longer be derived from any other; to cognize the essentia itself I must know that the marks and essentialia are complete and, taken together, sufficient for the cognition of the whole.",
"blomberg-s07-088": "The coordination of the marks, which is complete, constitutes the completudo.",
"blomberg-s07-089": "The fundamental concept of all that is necessary to a thing is its essence. To investigate the essence of things, this is the business and the purpose of philosophy.",
"blomberg-s07-090": "The marks which, taken together, make up the essence of a thing are the essentialia. The consequences, the rationata sive consectaria, from the essence, these are the attributa. Modi sunt notae internae, quarum possibilitas tantum per essentiam determinatur. (Modi are internal marks whose possibility alone is determined through the essence.)",
"blomberg-s07-091": "The modi are therefore not rationata essentiae with regard to their truth, but only with regard to their possibility. Modi are therefore nothing other than contingent marks.",
"blomberg-s07-092": "Modi as well as relationes do not belong to the essence at all, ad essentiam non pertinent (do not pertain to the essence), neither as constitutiva nor as attributa.",
"blomberg-s07-093": "All marks of a thing belong either to the essence of a thing, essentialia, or not essentialia: ad essentiam pertinentia sunt vel essentialia, vel attributa (those pertaining to the essence are either essentialia or attributa). Extraessentialia autem sunt vel notae, sive notae internae, vel relationes, sive notae externae, quarum possibilitas tantum per essentiam determinatur. (But the extraessentialia are either notae, or internal notae, or relationes, or external notae, whose possibility alone is determined through the essence.)",
"blomberg-s07-094": "The essence is divided",
"blomberg-s07-095": "1stly into the logical",
"blomberg-s07-096": "2ndly into the real essence.",
"blomberg-s07-097": "The complete fundamental concept of a thing is in general its essence.",
"blomberg-s07-098": "The first ground of all that is thought by me in the concept of the thing is the logical essence. The first fundamental concept, however, of all that really and in fact belongs to the thing is the real essence",
"blomberg-s07-099": "From this it is naturally evident that the logical and the real essence must necessarily be very different from one another.",
"blomberg-s07-100": "If I pronounce words and connect with them a certain concept, then what I think in connection with this word and expression is the logical essence. E.g. when I pronounce the word matter, then everything that is inseparable from the concept that I connect with the expression matter is the logical essence of matter. The logical essence can always be represented and thought by itself alone. Thus, e.g., in the case of matter I always think an extension, an impenetrability, a certain constant inertia and lifelessness, so that it is not able to change its place or move of itself, but only through the addition of another, foreign force.",
"blomberg-s07-101": "All this are the essentialia of the word matter, and consequently, taken together, make up its logical essence.",
"blomberg-s07-102": "The subjective fundamental concept is that which is contained in an inner concept of the thing. This, however, does not yet prove that still more marks can be present in the object; rather, I connect with the thing only what I represent to myself. Hence, e.g., with the word matter still several other properties can take place.",
"blomberg-s07-103": "The logical essence is the subjective fundamental concept. This concept, however, holds only for me, and not for others. Another can represent to himself more or fewer marks with the thing.",
"blomberg-s07-104": "The essence of our concept is not always, indeed seldom, the essence of the thing itself.",
"blomberg-s07-105": "The logical essence is far smaller than the real essence. The logical essence is a concentrated fundamental concept of a thing. It comprehends in itself only what we represent to ourselves in the thing.",
"blomberg-s07-106": "This contracting, combining of concepts is really very suited to the human understanding, and quite necessary. It must deal sparingly with its powers; such a compendium is nevertheless just as good as an extended concept of the thing.",
"blomberg-s07-107": "The real essence comprehends in itself in general everything that can ever really belong to a thing. This eduction is the only means of making our concepts really distinct.",
"blomberg-s07-108": "Distinctness, however, is one of the most essential logical perfections. Through this my cognition becomes far more fit for use; through the real essence, however, it becomes more extended.",
"blomberg-s07-109": "The logical essence, however, must not be mixed up with the real essence. For in my concept that I have of the thing I do not yet think everything that lies in the thing and belongs to it, or can lie in it and belong to it. E.g. if we take the concept of matter, more recent philosophi have discovered that the force of attraction also lies in the essence of matter; this property I would certainly never have thought immediately with the word matter, and there may belong to matter perhaps far more marks that have not yet been discovered and which only a philosopher will cognize.",
"blomberg-s07-110": "From this it is evident that infinitely more is contained in the real essence than in the logical essence.",
"blomberg-s07-111": "Of no single object of experience, or of the senses either, can we properly cognize the real or objective essence. At most it is possible for us to see the logical or subjective essence of it rightly.",
"blomberg-s07-112": "What we think with the word about a thing is very small and slight, indeed often very little; but all that really, revera, belongs to a thing is often immeasurable and cannot be determined at all.",
"blomberg-s07-113": "To seek out the real essence of concepts of experience and objects is therefore a wholly vain endeavor; but one can well represent the logical essence of concepts of reason, and elucidate it properly for oneself.",
"blomberg-s07-114": "Our concepts of experience are very changeable, because our experience is extended more and more daily. The logical essence is therefore also very variable and changeable, because in the concept of a thing one with time always thinks more and more determinations",
"blomberg-s07-115": "to oneself. But all moral concepts are pure concepts of the understanding.",
"blomberg-s07-116": "There are, namely, such pure concepts of the understanding as have their origin not from experience but solely from pure reason. Whoever wants to find the real essence must know all the marks that constantly belong to the thing. Then he must further seek out their ground and strive to investigate it, and this is then the real essence. E.g. a body that grows through internal unfolding belongs to the logical essence of a plant. The fundamental concept, however, of its constant determination is the real essence, to which may also belong that plants beget their like.",
"blomberg-s07-117": "The concept that water is a fluid element, without smell and taste, 14 times lighter than quicksilver, etc., is the logical essence of water, for if I am in some measure at home in physical knowledge, then, as soon as I name the word water, I think all this. From this I can by no means yet derive all the remaining properties that are determined for water, belong or can belong to it, and perhaps are not yet all discovered, although we do not always think them along with it; consequently it is not yet the real essence.",
"blomberg-s07-118": "Of individual things we can never give the real essence, but certainly of the genera, e.g. of body in general.",
"blomberg-s07-119": "Nevertheless we do not know all concepts and determinations of all things that can constantly belong to them, and then we are also not always in a position to give the true ground of all these determinations immediately; consequently we can indicate their real essence only very seldom.",
"blomberg-s07-120": "Herein clear and obscure cognition is spoken of. The author first dealt with confused and distinct concepts in the first section in the logica, without thinking first of clear and obscure cognition. How does it come, then, that he now begins to speak of it?",
"blomberg-s07-121": "Responsio: the concept of clear and obscure cognition is, at the beginning of logic, something that can be assumed without proof, and thus must be presupposed and laid down as a foundation. But here one can already derive it in turn from what has been treated beforehand.",
"blomberg-s07-122": "It is thus here just the reverse. A representation, however, is obscure (to return to our topic) of which one is not immediately conscious. Mediately, however, one can well become conscious of this cognition by means of reason, and so there remains a way to make an otherwise obscure cognition nevertheless [329 (339)] clear and distinct to oneself, and thus its insight easier. E.g. with the naked eye, when I behold the Milky Way, I see in it nothing other than a white streak; but if I take the tube (telescope) to help, I at once become aware of its individual parts as individual stars, and then judge at once by means of reason that these must be the very same stars that I saw with the naked eyes only as a mere white streak. Of this representation I am therefore mediately conscious, but not immediately; so it is at first only obscure, but afterwards mediate distinct, or clear.",
"blomberg-s07-123": "A cognition is either obscure in and for itself, so that, despite all the effort applied, it can never become clear; or it is relatively obscure, so that in certain circumstances it can become clear, but in others it cannot.",
"blomberg-s07-124": "The former, in itself obscure cognition, however, is according to its description wholly impossible, for everything can become clear, however hard it may seem to us; and what seems to us most obscure is, when we investigate it, always only relatively obscure.",
"blomberg-s07-125": "Logic prescribes to us the rules which we are to apply in order to know how we are to proceed with cognitions of which we are conscious that we really possess them.",
"blomberg-s07-126": "All clarity is the right way to arrive at distinctness. Clarity itself, however, is not yet a logical perfection, which distinctness is. Logic therefore already presupposes clarity, it supposes it and does not itself produce it; it deals with cognitions and representations already made clear.",
"blomberg-s07-127": "Distinctness is therefore also not the proper object of their occupation. Distinctness, Disjunctio, is to be well distinguished",
"blomberg-s07-128": "from evidentness, Dispicuitaet. Insofar as clarity of marks obtains in a cognition, to that extent the cognition is distinct.",
"blomberg-s07-129": "A cognition is brought into Perspicuum, however, only when it is so bright and clear that even the slightest understanding grasps it; on the other hand a cognition is imperspicua, or non perspicua, when it is too great for the whole horizon of the whole human race, when it can be called incomprehensible. Perspicuity, by contrast, means nothing other than comprehensibility.",
"blomberg-s07-130": "often a cognition ceases to be comprehensible in the very degree in which it ought to become truly distinct. This, however, cannot be avoided at all, unless one is not to speak of the matter at all.",
"blomberg-s07-131": "If we now go back to the distinction between clear and obscure cognition, we have further to remark: all obscure cognitions, however, can be",
"blomberg-s07-132": "a) Objectively",
"blomberg-s07-133": "B) Subjectively obscure; if we are to say it properly, the former does not obtain, but only the latter.",
"blomberg-s07-134": "Yet when the relation of a thing to the limits of human cognition is such that it cannot be cognized clearly enough, but always remains obscure, then for the most part one ascribes all the blame not to him who does not cognize the thing clearly enough, but to the thing itself or to him who presents it, although the fault often lies sooner with the former than with the latter. One then calls this cognition, which that person does not grasp and cannot grasp, objectively obscure. A cognition is subjectively obscure, however, when the blame for the thing's remaining obscure despite all the effort applied lies not in the thing and the cognition itself, but in him who wants to cognize something, namely because he either possesses great ignorance and therefore naturally is not in a position to cognize the thing, or also because he does not apply to it the diligence required for it.",
"blomberg-s07-135": "This latter obscurity, however, is not really grounded at all in the nature of the cognition itself, or indeed, as some hold, in that of all human beings and their understanding. For if that were so, the cognition could not be cognized clearly by a single human being; rather it lies merely either in the incapacity and ignorance, or in the negligence of this or that individual subject.",
"blomberg-s07-136": "Hence it comes about that the same cognition which is clear to one subject can be obscure to another, and conversely the very cognition that is obscure to one subject can be clear to another.",
"blomberg-s07-137": "Few human beings have even a proper concept of what it means to have a proper clear concept of a thing, or cognition.",
"blomberg-s07-138": "No human being can, so to speak, any longer understand himself properly; he always judges that everything he says is fully clear to him, and for just that reason it comes about that he is also deterred from investigating properly whether he understands himself properly or not.",
"blomberg-s07-139": "Metaphysica and morals, these two pure sciences of philosophy, are of such a kind and so constituted that a human being is never in a position to represent to himself distinctly the slightest concept in them unless he can first acquire for himself a complete and full concept of the whole, that is, of the whole science.",
"blomberg-s07-140": "Either someone who wants to study metaphysica has a quite pure, quite distinct concept of it, or he knows absolutely nothing of it. There are only these two ways; there is no middle course between them at all.",
"blomberg-s07-141": "On the other hand someone can be very comprehensible, especially if he as yet knows nothing, or at least no errors, and he who hears him goes away again as clever as he came to him; this is very easy, and such a presentation, which does no harm but also does no good, can also be very comprehensible and graspable.",
"blomberg-s07-142": "It is therefore not possible in some cases to combine easy comprehensibility with complete distinctness, and those certainly judge wrongly who hold the easiest presentation to be the best, and who esteem a somewhat harder, less childish presentation arranged to prompt reflection as obscure, unintelligible, indeed exaggerated.",
"blomberg-s07-143": "To make the obscure clear is so difficult that no human being has yet invented a generally valid means for it.",
"blomberg-s07-144": "Extensiveness (Ausführlichkeit) is the sufficiency of a cognition for a certain aim. The consequence, if it serves to pursue and to judge the aim, is called the end (Zweck).",
"blomberg-s07-145": "In all Completudo, then, we compare the cognition with a certain aim. This Completudo, however, is",
"blomberg-s07-146": "a) Externa, which appears in the comparison of the cognition with others.",
"blomberg-s07-147": "B) interna, which obtains in itself and lies in the cognition itself.",
"blomberg-s07-148": "The Completudo Comparativa, however, can often be so very useful to us that we have no need at all of the completudo absoluta or interna and can do without it. Extensiveness, however, rests solely and entirely on the multitude of marks that are coordinated with one another, or set beside one another.",
"blomberg-s07-149": "Philosophia has the aim of instructing us how we are to learn to understand ourselves ever better.",
"blomberg-s07-150": "All new cognitions that deviate greatly from the usual way of judging are at first wholly obscure and are not understood by the best head; and this often happens not because the cognitions are obscure in themselves, but because it is commonly very difficult to deviate from the usual way of judging and to observe this or that matter from another side. Whoever, e.g., has learned to dance under a poor master and then is to begin learning under a skilled one finds it really very hard to unlearn the old steps and shifts of the feet once habituated and to follow the newer, better method. It is just so with cognition: everything is hard to understand and obscure that departs from the old, once-established kind of cognition, of presentation, of manner of writing, etc.",
"blomberg-s07-151": "All inventors of new methods in all sciences are therefore obscure at the beginning; indeed, for this very reason, because one loves the old far too much and does not want to depart from it, they are often even despised, until in time one has accommodated oneself to this new method, whereupon one finds that it is often far better than the old.",
"blomberg-s07-152": "Locke and Newton have experienced this fate, that their writings were at the beginning exceedingly obscure and unintelligible, or at least seemed to be so, until afterwards one considered them more closely and discovered their merits.",
"blomberg-s07-153": "The doctrine of obscure cognitions is not logical at all but only metaphysical. Logic is not a science of the nature of the subject, of the human soul, in order to cognize what is actually hidden in it; rather it already presupposes clear concepts and deals with the use of our understanding and of our reason.",
"blomberg-s07-154": "In our soul all cognitions already lie actually hidden, and nothing more is really needed than to develop these cognitions and bring them into a brighter light.",
"blomberg-s07-155": "Rational philosophy teaches us nothing new; rather it seeks only to make distinct to us what we already know and to bring about a consciousness of it in us.",
"blomberg-s07-156": "Empirical philosophy, by contrast, is occupied with bringing us new cognitions that we have not known until now.",
"blomberg-s07-157": "In this paragraph the author speaks of extensive clarity.",
"blomberg-s07-158": "The completudo, however, is",
"blomberg-s07-159": "1) Externa",
"blomberg-s07-160": "2) interna.",
"blomberg-s07-161": "The former consists in this, that the marks attributed to a thing suffice to distinguish it from all other things.",
"blomberg-s07-162": "The latter, however, in this, that the marks of a thing are sufficient to derive from them all its other possible determinations.",
"blomberg-s07-163": "The Completudo externa always presupposes the Completudo interna. Where the latter is not, the former certainly is not either, and conversely the former, namely the Completudo externa, can be attained by means of the completudo interna. I cannot know immediately that a thing and its marks are sufficient to distinguish the thing from all other things; rather this must happen through the completudo interna.",
"blomberg-s07-164": "Inner Completudo is therefore not only a means to outer extensiveness, but the latter also cannot be attained and acquired without the former.",
"blomberg-s07-165": "No empirical concept can therefore become internally complete. Inner extensiveness thus does not obtain in objects of experience. From this it follows at the same time that outer extensiveness too is not to be found in objects of experience. For the inner is always the ground of outer extensiveness. E.g. with metals, herbs, plants, etc., I cannot cognize the thing according to all its determinations, and consequently cannot always distinguish it from all other things either.",
"blomberg-s07-166": "In many books that deal with physics one finds definitiones of objects of experience. But it is a certain proposition that these never are, nor can be, correct and sufficient. For every definitio must be complete.",
"blomberg-s07-167": "No one can therefore obtain a complete concept of objects of experience such as would serve to distinguish them from all other merely possible and actual things.",
"blomberg-s07-168": "Hence Locke took pains to show that no one, however learned, can be in a position to give a correct definition of the human being. From this, however, it is evident that all our concepts of objects of experience can never be absolutely complete, though a Completudo comparativa can obtain with them, namely when the marks of a thing suffice to distinguish the thing from everything that we have cognized in experience up to date.",
"blomberg-s07-169": "Pure concepts of reason, however, can be complete both internally and externally.",
"blomberg-s07-170": "With empirical concepts there are things outside us; the judgments are the exemplaria, and our concepts are the exemplata. With pure concepts, however, the concepts themselves are the exemplaria, and that of which we have the concepts are the exemplata, as e.g. the concept of virtue, of right and wrong, of goodness, of the rightfulness and wrongfulness of actions, of the simple and the composite, and of the contingent and the necessary.",
"blomberg-s07-171": "These concepts do not arise from objects at all; hence I cannot represent their determination to myself merely in part, but they are arbitrary.",
"blomberg-s07-172": "Reason is the creator of these concepts and consequently the thing also has no other determination than that reason has attributed to it.",
"blomberg-s07-173": "Mathematica is of this kind; it has nothing but pure concepts of reason, which can therefore also be completely complete, internally and externally. E.g. mathematicians think a conus, a cone, or a right-angled triangle which turns around its cathetus, or one of its sides. Here he thinks everything that suffices to distinguish the thing from all others, for the cone is not a thing outside him, which he cognizes in part according to certain determinations, but a thing in his pure reason, which he arbitrarily thinks for himself and to which he attributes certain determinations accordingly, whereby he wants the thing to be distinguishable from all other things.",
"blomberg-s07-174": "Only pure concepts of reason are therefore extensive (ausführlich), but not objecta of experience, which is clearly evident from what has been adduced so far.",
"blomberg-s07-175": "No one must therefore venture to define empirical concepts, but one can well have correct definitiones of pure concepts of reason.",
"blomberg-s07-176": "Here the author speaks of cognition that is wholly or in part clear, wholly or in part distinct.",
"blomberg-s07-177": "Representations are wholly clear when the thing I think is composed of no more determinations than those I represent to myself in it. Representations are in part clear, however, when I think not all and every determination of the thing, but only some of its determinations.",
"blomberg-s07-178": "Some of those ancient philosophers therefore distinguished concepts into Rationes Archetypas and ἔκτυπα, Ectypos. The former are to be regarded as archetypes and Originaria, the latter as copies and Exemplata.",
"blomberg-s07-179": "To the former belong the pure concepts of reason, to the latter, on the other hand, the concepts of experience. The former can be wholly clear, the latter, by contrast, are never wholly but always only in part clear. If, e.g., I see a house before me and cannot walk around it, the house is only in part clear to me, namely with regard to the side I have seen; the rest is wholly obscure.",
"blomberg-s07-180": "With concepts of experience I always cognize only those determinations of the thing which touch our senses, and indeed in such a way that we are conscious of them.",
"blomberg-s07-181": "Liveliness and strength of cognition are not opposita, but both of them are bound up with one another. Liveliness is an aesthetic perfection. Distinctness, however, the Rationabilitas, or rather deep distinctness, is a logical perfection. The deeper the distinctness of a cognition, the more the liveliness in it vanishes; and conversely, the livelier a cognition is, the shallower it is.",
"blomberg-s07-182": "Liveliness in a cognition, however, is attained with the help of a great connection. Distinctness in a cognition, on the other hand, one attains when one seeks to bring it forth by means of a great separation.",
"blomberg-s07-183": "Through coordinated marks we make a cognition lively, through subordinated marks, however, deeply distinct. Extended distinctness is bound up with the liveliness of a cognition, deep distinctness of a cognition, on the other hand, with its dryness. E.g. in describing spring I represent it vividly through a multitude of marks coordinated with one another. So the poet does it: he shows, e.g., the sprouting flowers, the young green of the woods, the leaping herds, the renewed rays of the sun, the fair, pleasant air, the reviving of the whole of nature.",
"blomberg-s07-184": "In all eloquence and poetry one seeks to adduce the marks coordinated with one another that one becomes immediately aware of in the thing to be described, in order to make the concept of the thing lively.",
"blomberg-s07-185": "Hereby one attains aesthetic perfection in a cognition. The philosopher, however, who represents a thing to himself through deep distinctness, brings forward only one mark of the thing and leaves out the rest, but seeks out marks, and the grounds of the marks, which he thinks of the thing. His reason is therefore chiefly occupied here.",
"blomberg-s07-186": "The form of cognition is therefore wholly different when one represents the thing through subordinated marks than when one represents it through coordinated marks. Aesthetic is occupied only with painting the thing and making it distinct through coordinated marks. Reason, however, ascends from one mark to the other and so leaves out many coordinated marks, but the cognition thereby becomes dry.",
"blomberg-s07-187": "The poet and the orator cognize much in few things. The philosopher, on the other hand, considers many objects and cognizes little in many objects. His cognitions are therefore universal; they do not go merely to single objecta but to whole genera of them. He represents to himself only a few marks, but through a deep distinctness.",
"blomberg-s07-188": "Distinctness of reason is a distinctness a priori, which I cognize through marks a priori.",
"blomberg-s07-189": "Through extended distinctness we cognize objecta without needing the use of reason, without reasoning subtly (vernünfteln). For deep distinctness, however, reason is necessarily required.",
"blomberg-s07-190": "For lively cognition sensibility is required. Sensibility is the perfection of cognition whereby we represent a thing to ourselves as objects of the senses.",
"blomberg-s07-191": "Rationabilitas belongs to things insofar as we think them through general concepts. Sensibility, on the other hand, insofar as we represent a thing through single concepts.",
"blomberg-s07-192": "All sensibility brings liveliness about. Rationality, however, produces dryness. Our concepts of reason acquire a sensibility when we represent general judgments in Concreto. This happens, for example, through examples and Similia.",
"blomberg-s07-193": "The sensibility of a cognition is not an imperfection of it at all. We thereby think the general, which we have already grasped in the abstract cognitions, in those cases in concreto in which it actually occurs.",
"blomberg-s07-194": "Extended clarity of cognition combined with sensibility brings forth liveliness.",
"blomberg-s07-195": "Our understanding here, as it were, always wavers between two rocks. If we seek to cognize something aesthetically perfectly, we soon lack the right grounds, the solid in cognition, and we fall into the shallow. But if in turn we cognize something through a logical perfection, we very easily lack the aesthetic liveliness, etc., and we lapse into the dry. Indeed it is really very hard to hit the right mean between these two dangerous wrong paths.",
"blomberg-s07-196": "Nevertheless it is also possible to unite both perfections with one another in a high degree.",
"blomberg-s07-197": "The clarity of cognition is just like its completeness, completudo",
"blomberg-s07-198": "A:) Extensiva",
"blomberg-s07-199": "B ) intensiva",
"blomberg-s07-200": "Claritas extensiva rests on a multitude of coordinated marks that are cognized immediately in a thing.",
"blomberg-s07-201": "It is the right way to liveliness, in that it brings much sensibility with it, for this consists precisely in the coordinatio of the marks.",
"blomberg-s07-202": "The completion of the perfections of all our cognitions is: to give them sensibility in the end, so that one represents the general in particular circumstances and cases and thinks the abstractum in Concreto in a single individual sensible case. E.g. when I think friendship, true love, and the readiness to serve one another flowing from it in Damon and Phintias. Here, then, I think the general in single cases. Hereby, however, my cognition becomes lively, e.g. when I think patriotism in Cato.",
"blomberg-s07-203": "Without sensibility our pure cognition of reason is very poor indeed.",
"blomberg-s07-204": "For extended clarity and liveliness only understanding is required, because with coordinated marks I do nothing but judge.",
"blomberg-s07-205": "Claritas intensiva, however, rests on subordinated marks, and for this, reason is necessarily required, because we thereby infer and derive one mark from the other.",
"blomberg-s07-206": "Claritas extensiva serves for the description of an object. Claritas intensiva, however, investigates the grounds of the thing. From one mark belonging to the thing it seeks to show that another mark must also belong to the thing. It seeks, then, to find out the cause of things and always asks Why? why this, why that too, indeed why is it not otherwise?",
"blomberg-s07-207": "The senses give us a lively but not fully distinct cognition.",
"blomberg-s07-208": "He who can narrate completely and in order everything he has ever seen possesses much liveliness of cognition; indeed through this narration he can often bring about a lively cognition in others too.",
"blomberg-s07-209": "Sensible clarity is nothing other than liveliness; in intuition the clarity is sensible clarity. Clarity through general concepts is a logical clarity.",
"blomberg-s07-210": "Mathematics is the only science that is in a position to judge according to its concepts.",
"blomberg-s07-211": "He who is in a position to combine in his cognition both liveliness and deep distinctness has in fact already attained the highest degree of perfection of human cognition; this, however, is so hard that it seems almost impossible to anyone.",
"blomberg-s07-212": "Through sensibility the perfection of reason is not lost in the least; rather it only obtains a greater liveliness.",
"blomberg-s07-213": "From this, then, we see quite clearly that sensibility does not conflict at all with the perfection of reason but rather promotes it in certain cases, indeed often brings about a greater correctness in it, in that in abstracto we often omit, and must omit, such marks of things as really belong to the nature of the thing, which can then be restored when one considers the thing in concreto. Orators and poets can therefore often be very helpful to the philosopher.",
"blomberg-s07-214": "It is a most general rule of a good, and especially learned, presentation: connect with a presentation, and give your instruction, sensibility.",
"blomberg-s07-215": "Logical clarity, however, rests on the subordination; sensible clarity rests in turn on the coordination of the marks.",
"blomberg-s07-216": "Just so we can also have A: a sensible distinctness according to aesthetic laws.",
"blomberg-s07-217": "and B: a logical distinctness according to logical laws",
"blomberg-s07-218": "We must often abstract, but this too is really very hard for us.",
"blomberg-s07-219": "1stly: Sensible distinctness according to the laws of aesthetic is nothing other than the distinctness of intuition.",
"blomberg-s07-220": "2ndly: Logical distinctness, by contrast, according to laws of the understanding and of reason, or of logic, is nothing other than the distinctness of reflection",
"blomberg-s07-221": "Something can be very lively but all the less distinct. Again something can be skilful, colourful, and lively, yet nevertheless little distinct and confused.",
"blomberg-s07-222": "That our cognition, as the author maintains, becomes distinct only through analysis (Zergliederung) is false.",
"blomberg-s07-223": "Our cognition can be made distinct in two ways",
"blomberg-s07-224": "a) per Synthesin",
"blomberg-s07-225": "B) per Analysin",
"blomberg-s07-226": "Here, however, we must distinguish well between the science of making a distinct cognition and the science of making distinct a cognition that was previously obscure. For we either make a distinct concept, and this happens per Synthesin, or we make distinct a concept that was previously confused, and this happens per Analysin.",
"blomberg-s07-227": "In synthesis we generate and, as it were, create a concept that formerly was not there at all, wholly new both quoad materiam and quoad formam, and at the same time make it distinct.",
"blomberg-s07-228": "All concepts of the mathematicians are of this kind, e.g. the concepts of the triangle, square, circle, etc.",
"blomberg-s07-229": "All concepts feigned by reason are at the same time distinct, but only per Synthesin. If a concept is to be made distinct per Analysin, it must already be given; one is therefore occupied with making the confused and obscure in this given concept clear and distinct, with developing it, setting it apart, and so illuminating it. To this belong, e.g., each and every concept of metaphysic.",
"blomberg-s07-230": "With these one is merely and solely to become conscious of the marks that belong to the thing. E.g. with the concepts of virtue, of vice; and we therefore need nothing more than to analyse, to set apart, and to dissect, to make the confused concepts distinct.",
"blomberg-s07-231": "The matter, then, is already there; we are only to give the thing a form. By means of analytic distinctness we cognize in a thing no more than we had already thought in it beforehand; rather we cognize only what we had actually known already, better, that is more distinctly, more clearly, and with more consciousness. E.g. with the concept of perfection I will first lead someone to the cases in which he makes use of the expression perfection, in order thereby to instruct him what he actually understands by perfection, what concept of it he forms for himself, and what he thinks when he pronounces the word perfection and ascribes it to a thing. Here he will naturally find that he calls much perfect that is really very imperfect or even vicious. E.g. the voluptuary holds his Vagam Libidinem to be the greatest possible perfection. For if this were not so, he could not possibly cling to it so strongly and find in it so very great a pleasure, obscuring everything else for him. Analysis therefore really aims at the making distinct of the confused. The composition of cognition, however, or synthesis, is serviceable and useful solely for producing something new and at the same time making it distinct all at once.",
"blomberg-s07-232": "In general all concepts of the human understanding are either",
"blomberg-s07-233": "1stly: conceptus dati or 2ndly: Conceptus facti",
"blomberg-s07-234": "A conceptus datus, a given concept, is one that is produced either by the nature of our understanding or also by experience.",
"blomberg-s07-235": "Conceptus Facti, made concepts, are those that are arbitrarily generated or invented by us without having been given beforehand.",
"blomberg-s07-236": "All these invented concepts are brought forth together with distinctness.",
"blomberg-s07-237": "One there feigns something arbitrarily, and indeed at the same time with consciousness of it.",
"blomberg-s07-238": "There are many such Conceptus facti in Aesthetic and in Mathematic. In Philosophia, by contrast, there are Conceptus dati",
"blomberg-s07-239": "A cognition does not become distinct through analysis alone, for the action by which a cognition becomes distinct is one and the very same as that by which its marks become clear.",
"blomberg-s07-240": "A cognition can obtain a distinctness of reason per analysin, but also a distinctness of understanding per Analysin.",
"blomberg-s07-241": "Through analysis I merely bring the marks of a cognition under more general marks that I had already grasped beforehand per Analysin a priori. Per Synthesin, however, I learn new marks of a cognition in addition, a posteriori, e.g. with the concept of the essence of gold.",
"blomberg-s07-242": "This is empirical distinctness; the former, however, is the distinctness of understanding, which has its origin in my reason and takes place by means of analysis. E.g. morals is nothing more than the setting apart of our moral representations of virtue, of the good, and of the evil. Socrates said: I am the midwife of the thoughts of my hearers.",
"blomberg-s07-243": "To make a cognition means to invent (dichten)",
"blomberg-s07-244": "We now want to indicate briefly the degrees of the representations of all cognitions.",
"blomberg-s07-245": "1stly: “to represent something in general is the most general and most usual, also easiest, cognition of a thing. To represent something to oneself, however, with which a consciousness is still bound up, is different from representing in general, in which we often are not even conscious to what these representations actually relate.”",
"blomberg-s07-246": "Sibi aliquid repraesentare id est cognoscere, Repraesentatio.",
"blomberg-s07-247": "If, however, with such a representation there is also bound up the capacity to bring what I represent to myself under a general concept, and so to be able to know to what my representation actually relates, then this is the",
"blomberg-s07-248": "2nd degree, namely to know (kennen): “I then know (kenne) what I represent to myself.”",
"blomberg-s07-249": "3.) “the next degree is to understand (verstehen) something, that is, to know it through the understanding, or to know something distinctly through a distinct concept.”",
"blomberg-s07-250": "Nature hands us many things that we are indeed able to know, but are for that reason not actually able to understand. Further",
"blomberg-s07-251": "4: To have insight (Einsehen). much “that is found in nature we do indeed understand, but we do not yet have insight into it, e.g. the growth of plants.”",
"blomberg-s07-252": "To have insight means to cognize something through and by means of reason, that is, a priori. “Here the thing need not even be given to me. Human beings often believe they have insight into something as soon as they are merely in a position to give an explanation of it.”",
"blomberg-s07-253": "To make a cognition, facere, means to invent it, Fingere",
"blomberg-s07-254": "A Conceptus Factitius is therefore at the same time a Conceptus Fictitius. To make a cognition arbitrarily and with consciousness thus means to make a distinct cognition.",
"blomberg-s07-255": "All mathematical definitiones are, e.g., merely invented, and are therefore nothing other than made, arbitrary, distinct concepts of the thing.",
"blomberg-s07-256": "5: “To comprehend (begreifen) something is finally the last and highest degree, namely: it is to have insight into something through reason, yet in such a way that it is sufficient for a certain aim; that is comparative insight.”",
"blomberg-s07-257": "If, however, I have insight into something sufficiently for every and any aim, this is absolute, simply comprehending.",
"blomberg-s07-258": "Much that I really understand, know, and have insight into I do not for that reason yet comprehend. And conversely much that I comprehend Comparative I do not for that reason yet comprehend Absolute.",
"blomberg-s07-259": "Absolute, that is sufficient for every aim, we have no insight at all into anything in the whole of nature.",
"blomberg-s07-260": "The Sufficientia Notarum rei est Completudo, et est",
"blomberg-s07-261": "a:) externa, i.e. when the marks I have of a thing are complete and sufficient enough to distinguish this thing from all others. The whole concept of it must therefore be known.",
"blomberg-s07-262": "An externally sufficient concept is therefore always also internally sufficient. Further, conversely, an internally sufficient concept is always also externally sufficient; and a concept that is not internally sufficient is not externally sufficient either, and one that is not so externally is again not so internally",
"blomberg-s07-263": "Philosophia est vel pura, vel applicata",
"blomberg-s07-264": "In Philosophia pura each and every concept is given through the understanding and through reason, e.g. the concepts of the possible and again of the impossible, of the necessary and of the contingent.",
"blomberg-s07-265": "Omnes conceptus sunt vel",
"blomberg-s07-266": "1: pure rationales",
"blomberg-s07-267": "2 Empirici",
"blomberg-s07-268": "All concepts are made distinct through analysis, that is per analysin, when they were previously obscure and confused. For through it one learns to have insight into the partial concepts of a whole concept.",
"blomberg-s07-269": "A distinct concept, however, arises per Synthesin.",
"blomberg-s07-270": "In Analysis the concept becomes either completely distinct or not.",
"blomberg-s07-271": "All my concepts of reason can become completely distinct, but empirical concepts are excluded from this and always remain incompletely distinct",
"blomberg-s07-272": "Here, in this paragraph, the author speaks of the comprehensible and the incomprehensible. In order to get a better Idea of it, let us once more first present the degrees of human cognition. This cannot be described further, nor defined.",
"blomberg-s07-273": "The 1st degree is to know (wißen) something, that is, to represent something to oneself with consciousness.",
"blomberg-s07-274": "The 2nd degree is to know (kennen) something, i.e. to be able to cognize something in distinction from other things through comparison with them.",
"blomberg-s07-275": "The 3rd degree is to understand (verstehen) something, i.e. to cognize it distinctly through the understanding.",
"blomberg-s07-276": "The 4th degree, finally, is to have insight into (einsehen) something, or to cognize through reason.",
"blomberg-s07-277": "When I have insight into something, I cognize it through mediate marks; I therefore infer, and seek thereby Notam notae, a mark of the mark.",
"blomberg-s07-278": "If I am to understand something, the understanding must be occupied with it; I must cognize a thing through immediate marks, but also judge in doing so.",
"blomberg-s07-279": "To understand something is far easier than to have insight into something, for in the first case it is only necessary that I coordinate the marks of a thing, in the other case, however, I must subordinate them. To understand what gold is, I need nothing more than to know the properties of this metal, that it is, e.g., ductile, yellow, heavier than others, etc., that it does not rust. But to have insight into what gold is, I must investigate one mark of it in particular and separate out the ground of it, e.g. why it does not rust, why it is ductile, heavier than others.",
"blomberg-s07-280": "The fifth and highest degree of our cognition, finally, is called to comprehend (begreifen) something, that is, to have insight into something sufficiently, or in the degree to which it is necessary for a certain insight.",
"blomberg-s07-281": "One can comprehend something either Absolute, or Totaliter, wholly, or also partialiter, i.e. relative.",
"blomberg-s07-282": "I comprehend something Absolute when I have insight into it as far as is required for this or that end.",
"blomberg-s07-283": "We never comprehend anything totaliter, or absolute. Even the most learned among us cannot boast that he has insight into something so that it would suffice for all aims. The common man, with regard to religion, if he only has a sound understanding, always comprehends just as much as the greatest Theologus as is necessary for him to know in order to live uprightly and to order all his actions according to the laws of God.",
"blomberg-s07-284": "This, however, will nevertheless never be sufficient for all his ends.",
"blomberg-s07-285": "With regard to the practical perfection of cognition we all have equal insights, although we are distinguished by our insights with regard to logical perfection, whereby, however, he too who has greater insights and more elaborated cognitions comprehends nothing absolute.",
"blomberg-s07-286": "The author falsely translates the word begreifen by concipere, whereas concipere really means nothing other than einsehen (to have insight).",
"blomberg-s07-287": "The 6 degrees of our cognition, of which we have already spoken more fully above, are called rather",
"blomberg-s07-288": "1. repraesentare.",
"blomberg-s07-289": "to represent (vorstellen)",
"blomberg-s07-290": "2. Scire.",
"blomberg-s07-291": "to know (wißen)",
"blomberg-s07-292": "3. Noscere, to know (kennen).",
"blomberg-s07-293": "4. intelligere, to understand (verstehen)",
"blomberg-s07-294": "5. Concipere, to have insight (einsehen)",
"blomberg-s07-295": "6. Comprehendere, to comprehend (begreifen)",
"blomberg-s07-296": "To one human being something is often comprehensible that is in turn incomprehensible to another.",
"blomberg-s07-297": "Something is not comprehensible to us at the beginning that in the course of time can be comprehended by us very easily. Yet one can often err in this, that one believes and fully holds that others will comprehend very well what one does not comprehend oneself, et vice versa, when one holds that what one comprehends well is incomprehensible to others.",
"blomberg-s07-298": "If I am to make the concepts of a thing distinct to myself, my representation of it must first of all be clear. Then I must attend to the various marks of the thing; thereupon I must take many marks together in one thing, or Compare them, hold them against one another and compare them with one another. In this Comparation there takes place not merely a mere collection or gathering of the marks but rather also a setting beside one another, a coordination, of them.",
"blomberg-s07-299": "Finally, however, there is added the Abstractio Notarum, or that action in which I disregard all such marks of a thing as could hinder and disturb me in making the representation distinct, or at least are of no use to me and are therefore superfluous for it.",
"blomberg-s07-300": "In this § the author shows the difference of distinct cognition into Cognitionem totaliter and partialiter distinctam.",
"blomberg-s07-301": "One often cognizes an object distinctly in one part, but indistinctly in the other.",
"blomberg-s07-302": "Much is very hidden from us in experience, whereas in logic more distinctness is already to be found.",
"blomberg-s07-303": "Indistinctness actually arises from a lack of attention to the parts of a whole concept.",
"blomberg-s07-304": "The blame for this indistinctness one is commonly accustomed to ascribe either to the thing itself of which one has a distinct cognition, or to the person and subject in whom the indistinctness of the cognition is found. If a thing has far too many marks, so that one is not in a position to survey them all at once, the blame for its indistinctness necessarily lies in the thing itself.",
"blomberg-s07-305": "With empirical concepts there always reigns more confusion than with concepts of reason.",
"blomberg-s07-306": "The concepts of experience arise, namely, through the touching of our senses, but these cannot have insight into all determinations of things; rather many of them remain hidden and unknown to us.",
"blomberg-s07-307": "Our cognitions are either",
"blomberg-s07-308": "1: adequate, i.e. complete, or",
"blomberg-s07-309": "2) incomplete and not adequate.",
"blomberg-s07-310": "The author in this paragraph, following Baumgarten, and Baumgarten in turn following Wolff, has called a certain kind of distinct cognitions complete.",
"blomberg-s07-311": "When the marks of a thing are cognized fully distinctly, this is the intensive clarity of cognition of the first degree; but if I cognize the immediate marks of a thing distinctly, yet derive from them other, mediate marks, then this distinctness of cognition is the intensive clarity of the 2nd degree, and, according to Wolff's expressions, the completeness (Vollständigkeit) of cognition, e.g. when I say: vice is a skill (Fertigkeit) for sinning.",
"blomberg-s07-312": "A skill, however, is a facility for acting. To sin, however, means nothing other than to live contrary to the moral rules and laws. Vice is therefore a skill for performing (ediren) actions that are contrary to the moral laws.",
"blomberg-s07-313": "This 2nd degree of intensive distinctness of cognition, however, cannot possibly be completeness, for otherwise the third degree of distinctness would have to be still more complete, and this would go on ever further. Here all Comparation falls away, and if something is already absolute complete, nothing can be found that would be still more complete.",
"blomberg-s07-314": "The author, however, has called a complete cognition, in an improper way, adaequatam Cognitionem, and in turn incomplete cognitions, cognitiones inadaequatae.",
"blomberg-s07-315": "Completeness itself, however, consists in nothing other than in Completudo and in Praecision.",
"blomberg-s07-316": "A cognition is therefore complete (cognitio rei adaequata) when the representation of a thing is rightly conformable and adequate to it, so that it contains nothing more, but also nothing less, than actually lies in the thing: Si Repraesentatio Rei de re quadam nec Supra, nec infra rem est.",
"blomberg-s07-317": "All distinctness through subordination of the marks is of various degrees, and therefore one cannot call a cognition complete until its marks are no longer subordinated to any other concept.",
"blomberg-s07-318": "Completudo, or extensiveness, we have already considered above, and of Praecision we shall speak at greater length hereafter.",
"blomberg-s07-319": "Completudo consists in nothing other than in the sufficiency, or adequacy, of a distinct concept of a thing for deriving from it all the other marks of the thing.",
"blomberg-s07-320": "Omnis Cognitio Sufficiens est",
"blomberg-s07-321": "a) Extensiva, that is extensiveness (Ausführlichkeit)",
"blomberg-s07-322": "B: Intensiva, that is thoroughness (Gründlichkeit).",
"blomberg-s07-323": "For thoroughness, then, profundity is required; to this belongs proving, and it is attained by ascending to the highest mark of many marks subordinated to one another.",
"blomberg-s07-324": "Some complete thoroughness is demanded with regard to morals.",
"blomberg-s07-325": "Nevertheless one very often errs here too. So it is, e.g., with a certain casus necessitatis such as an emergency lie, a Christian deception, etc.",
"blomberg-s07-326": "Extensiveness consists really in nothing other than that nothing is lacking, that the concept can be whole.",
"blomberg-s07-327": "Yet even with this extensiveness a fault can occur, namely if my concept contains more than it ought to contain in order to be complete.",
"blomberg-s07-328": "A further perfection is therefore added, which amounts to this, that neither more nor less than is necessary is contained in it; and this perfection is called conformity to measure (Abgemeßenheit), Praecision.",
"blomberg-s07-329": "A cognition is therefore measured (abgemeßen) when it has neither too many nor too few marks.",
"blomberg-s07-330": "Cognitio est Praecisa, quando non abundat notis.",
"blomberg-s07-331": "Praecision, then, is the determinate degree of the marks according to which they contain no more than is necessary to derive from them all the other determinations of the thing.",
"blomberg-s07-332": "It requires more effort to make a cognition measured than to bring it to an abundant extensiveness, i.e. to make it more than complete.",
"blomberg-s07-333": "Praecision is really a great and difficult art; one sees this more easily in all cases, even by means of experience; so the mathematician, otherwise so precise, nevertheless finds no Praecision, e.g., in the quadrature of the circle. The calculations that have been made of it so far are all of such a kind that the number that came out has been either too great or too small, and the right figure has not yet been hit.",
"blomberg-s07-334": "To be in a position to observe such Praecision with regard to philosophical concepts, however, certainly requires far more art and skill. A complete and precise concept taken together, however, is adequate to the thing, and such a concept is then called a Conceptus adaequatus, Cognitio rei adaequata.",
"blomberg-s07-335": "It is really the same with marks as with a measure. A measure is adequate to the thing if it contains neither more nor less than is necessary for the magnitude to be measured. A mark too is adequate to the thing of which it is to be a mark if it contains neither more nor less than lies in the concept of the thing.",
"blomberg-s07-336": "With completudo I take care that nothing is too little in the cognition, with Praecision, however, we see to it that nothing in it is too much. Completudo is, as is easily seen, always far more necessary than Praecisio, for superfluity in cognition, although it too is a fault which one must strive to avoid as much as possible, can nevertheless be tolerated far sooner and more easily than lack in cognition.",
"blomberg-s07-337": "Praecision is really only relatively a perfection, because it serves only so that one deals sparingly with one's knowledge and does not apply one's powers to a thing all too superfluously, and so, as it were, squander them where it is not necessary, so that afterwards one has no capacity for other, more useful cognitions. Praecision is therefore nothing other than a rule of thrift, which for just that reason also has a certain inner beauty: it is to be found especially in Mathematic, in geometry, and in mechanic.",
"blomberg-s07-338": "The superfluous, the Abundans in a concept, comes merely from this, when one concept already lies in the other, and this superfluity is always a consequence of the lack of Praecisio.",
"blomberg-s07-339": "One says in many words, and more than are required for it, what one could have expressed and said in a few words. Just so when in Arithmetica one expresses a fraction in very large and prolix figures which one would be just as well able to express correctly with few and small numbers",
"blomberg-s07-340": "There is also a certain kind of Praecision in proofs according to which not a single proposition is too many or too few for what serves conviction. Such excellent Praecision in proofs is likewise found in Mathematica. This beautiful science is thus, as we see, a model in many respects, and one has always sought from of old to introduce these its properties, and among them especially Praecision, into philosophy too.",
"blomberg-s07-341": "In systematic presentation and cognition Praecision is a chief perfection.",
"blomberg-s07-342": "With beautiful, i.e. aesthetic, cognitions, by contrast, Praecision is in turn a dispensable perfection.",
"blomberg-s07-343": "In eloquence and poetry one is not sparing in expressions but often indeed all too prodigal. One says a thing quite often more than once, and nevertheless only with other expressions and under other images. Praecision is therefore merely a rule of thrift and of prudence, but not at all of taste and of Aesthetic. Praecision is only a rule of prudence; it is therefore also hard to attain, and that Praecision by which one is so measured and sparing in one's expressions that another thereby attains a determinate degree of clarity of the concept is a particular talent, and therefore also a work of genius, and through mere art only very hard, indeed almost impossible, to attain. Each and every maxim is of this kind; all proverbs have a certain kind of Praecision, or adequacy. By them one says a whole wisdom in few expressions. Praecision therefore belongs merely to logical perfection.",
"blomberg-s07-344": "In the 151st paragraph the author calls the Praecision of cognition a determinate (bestimmte) cognition. This expression, however, is wholly false.",
"blomberg-s07-345": "Indeterminate marks are those that contain too little, and our author says in turn that indeterminate marks are those marks that contain too much, or more. Something can be complete without having any Praecision in itself. Now we want to say a little about profound cognitions. Intensive distinctness in a cognition, as we have already heard above, has various degrees.",
"blomberg-s07-346": "A greater degree of distinctness of cognition through subordinated marks is profound distinctness. A higher degree of distinctness of cognition, on the other hand, through coordinated marks is extensive distinctness. I can attain to depth of cognition in no other way than by going from one mark to another, and in this way cognizing mediately one mark of the thing through the other.",
"blomberg-s07-347": "Profundity is nothing other than the faculty for profound distinctness, or for a great series of marks by means of, or mediately through, ascent. Here I go up from the experiences that I have already had to the first grounds, or from the first grounds down to the first experiences.",
"blomberg-s07-348": "The first, with which I begin here, can indeed be either the first or, if I end with it, the last.",
"blomberg-s07-349": "__________________________________",
"blomberg-s07-350": "In this paragraph the author teaches us nothing further than a mere name. He does not show us here how one can and should make concepts bright and clear, but only teaches us to know a name for a head that has clear concepts.",
"blomberg-s07-351": "Some people are of such a kind that they foolishly imagine that it is bright in their head alone, whereas other people have nothing but obscure and confused concepts. “In their head alone there is a Goshen, whereas on the contrary everything else is covered with an Egyptian night.”",
"blomberg-s07-352": "A learned person, however, must exert himself most especially to attain clear and distinct concepts of the things that are, or can be, objects of his contemplation.",
"blomberg-s07-353": "__________________________________",
"blomberg-s07-354": "________________________",
"blomberg-s08-001": "Certainty is nothing other than subjective necessity in the quality of judgments. The opposite of judgments is the negation of what is affirmed in the judgments, or also the affirmation of what is denied in the judgment.",
"blomberg-s08-002": "Many judgments are thus constituted that their opposite seems to me to have to be wholly impossible, and thereby it is subjectively necessary.",
"blomberg-s08-003": "Everything that is true is, by that very fact, at the same time subjectively certain.",
"blomberg-s08-004": "Objective necessity is thus properly truth.",
"blomberg-s08-005": "In a multitude of cases we find that we judge much to be true that we nevertheless, subjectively, in certain cases always find uncertain, indeed even find its opposite possible.",
"blomberg-s08-006": "It is therefore clear that the uncertainty of a cognition rests merely on its objective falsity, while the certainty of a cognition, on the other hand, rests in turn on its objective truth.",
"blomberg-s08-007": "If something is true, it is also at the same time certain; if something is false, it is likewise always at the same time, without doubt, uncertain.",
"blomberg-s08-008": "Objectively the true cannot be false; that is, regarding the constitution of a thing, or of a true property, it cannot be uncertain whether it is true or false.",
"blomberg-s08-009": "All uncertainty is therefore grounded in the subject and is subjective, i.e. it is grounded in him who is not in a position to see and cognize something either as true or as false.",
"blomberg-s08-010": "Every ground for truth is a ground of holding-to-be-true; every ground of cognition, supposing even that it were insufficient, is nevertheless a ground of holding-to-be-true.",
"blomberg-s08-011": "All holding-to-be-true from grounds as to which we do not investigate whether a great or a small degree of truth is in them is plausibility (Scheinbarkeit). If the degree of truth is greater than the degree of the grounds of the opposite, the cognition is probable. In that case, therefore, not in the least any established truth takes place.",
"blomberg-s08-012": "In probability a sufficient ground is indeed present, but this ground for the truth is greater than the grounds of the opposite; it outweighs them.",
"blomberg-s08-013": "Holding-to-be-true for the sake of the semblance of cognition is called persuasion (Überredung).",
"blomberg-s08-014": "Here, then, one assumes any degree of truth whatever in order to be able to assent to a cognition, without investigating whether the grounds of the opposite have a greater degree of truth or not. Judgment according to the measure of probability is not persuasion but judgment from mere plausibility. Persuasion is in fact a kind of blinding;",
"blomberg-s08-015": "for one always considers only the one side, without reflecting in the least on the opposite side, which is nevertheless highly harmful.",
"blomberg-s08-016": "The consciousness of a cognition through the sufficient ground of truth, however, is called conviction (Überzeugung). This arises merely from the consciousness through reason of the necessity of the cognition. Where there is still a contingent cognition, which can be so but also otherwise than it actually is, there is no conviction present. One can quite properly represent conviction as composed of many moments or elements, i.e. the small degrees of persuasion and of holding-to-be-true, and assume all moments or degrees of holding-to-be-true and of persuasion to be of the same kind.",
"blomberg-s08-017": "If the moments of holding-to-be-true make up just half of the sufficient ground, a cognition is uncertain, doubtful, ambigua ejus modi cognitio (a cognition of this kind is ambiguous). The mind thus always remains undecided, it hovers as it were between fear and hope. On the one side there are just as many grounds as on the other, which hold each other in equilibrium.",
"blomberg-s08-018": "But if there is even one degree more of truth on the side of the insufficient ground than on the side of the opposite, the cognition is no longer ambigua but already probable.",
"blomberg-s08-019": "If, however, conversely, there is one degree of truth less on the side of the insufficient ground than on the side of the opposite, the cognition is not only uncertain, and not probable, but even improbable. Yet the cognition still always remains plausible. It cannot on that account be immediately rejected, for only the slightest degree of truth is required for plausibility.",
"blomberg-s08-020": "All certainty is to be regarded as a unity and as a complete whole, and is therefore the measure of all our other holding-to-be-true, and of each and every degree of it.",
"blomberg-s08-021": "Certainty, however, arises from nothing other than the relation of equality between the grounds of cognition that I have and the entire sufficient ground itself.",
"blomberg-s08-022": "If the grounds of cognition that I have are not equal to the entire sufficient ground, my cognition is uncertain. It is true that there is a certain heterogeneity in the objects and concepts; nevertheless concepts can become homogeneous when one brings the manifold under a general concept.",
"blomberg-s08-023": "This production of homogeneity arises merely from comparison. One compares, or holds the grounds of truth against the grounds of falsity; one then sees whether the grounds of truth have a greater degree in themselves than the grounds of the opposite, and then, after maturer consideration, passes a more correct and more apt judgment on the probability and improbability of a cognition.",
"blomberg-s08-024": "If we had no cognition of the sufficient ground of certainty, we should be able to pass no judgment at all on the probability or improbability of a thing. For all of these arise from the relation of our grounds of cognition to certainty. But one is always accustomed to assent first of all to that of which one has, and sees before oneself, several and convincing grounds of truth.",
"blomberg-s08-025": "The mere sufficient ground is objective, the insufficient ground, however, is subjective. The former determines in the thing what is to be held of it; the latter, on the other hand, is very doubtful, for it is surely easy to see that one may today have more, tomorrow again fewer grounds of truth for a cognition.",
"blomberg-s08-026": "The sufficient ground, however, is that whose opposite cannot possibly be thought and represented as true.",
"blomberg-s08-027": "It therefore naturally contains really everything that is to be found in the thing.",
"blomberg-s08-028": "The insufficient grounds, on the other hand, merely enumerate what we think and represent of the thing, and cognize of it.",
"blomberg-s08-029": "Certainty, therefore, as we have just said, and then the sufficient ground, are the measuring-rod. All judgments are not probable but plausible if no relation of the grounds of cognition to certainty takes place in them.",
"blomberg-s08-030": "This plausibility, however, can change very much.",
"blomberg-s08-031": "Probability and improbability are objective grounds. Plausibility, however, is a subjective ground, that is: probability and improbability lie in the object itself, in the thing that is to be cognized; plausibility, on the other hand, lies in him who has no cognition of a thing.",
"blomberg-s08-032": "In a subject the state is the same whether the cognition it has is mere persuasion, or a certain truth, a conviction, or not; it is thereby not altered outwardly in the least.",
"blomberg-s08-033": "It is, however, not possible at all to specify an infallible, secure and universally valid mark of conviction by which to distinguish it from persuasion.",
"blomberg-s08-034": "Since it is now impossible to specify properly and securely which grounds of judgment and of persuasion lie in the object and which in the subject, we make here the following important general remark: namely “all error properly arises only from our confounding the causes of assent that lie in the object itself with [445a] the grounds that lie in the subject.”",
"blomberg-s08-035": "But in order to investigate whether something we accept is properly a mere plausible and invalid persuasion or a real, secure conviction, one first of all weighs the grounds of the opposite separately.",
"blomberg-s08-036": "If I now find that I encounter for the opposite of my cognition just as many great and weighty grounds as for that to which I have actually assented, I readily see from this that my whole cognition cannot have been in the least a conviction, but merely a persuasion, a phenomenon of conviction.",
"blomberg-s08-037": "All our representations together are phenomena of this sort; e.g. with the rainbow it seems as if it rested on the earth; it is thus a mere appearance; on closer investigation, however, we see that this is not so.",
"blomberg-s08-038": "Experience teaches us that the strength of persuasion about a thing can often be, and is, just as great as the conviction of its opposite ever is afterwards.",
"blomberg-s08-039": "A means, then, of perceiving what is properly necessary and required for true conviction is this: to study diligently those sciences that pave the surest way to it, i.e. which really convince us of certain truths, e.g. mathematics. Further, one should apply oneself carefully to those sciences that confirm the grounds of reason through experience, such as experimental physics.",
"blomberg-s08-040": "Since we therefore lack a universally valid mark by which to distinguish rightly and infallibly the subjective grounds of holding-to-be-true from the objective, the phenomenon",
"blomberg-s08-041": "of conviction from true certainty, and conviction itself, we must be content that in individual cases we endeavor to convince ourselves of the certainty of this or that thing, so that we may at least always be in a position to distinguish the false from the true. But in order to be able to avoid error carefully in this or that cognition, one must heed and observe the following well:",
"blomberg-s08-042": "1mo “One must compare one's cognition of this or that object with the cognitions of other people about one and the same object, about one and the same Gegenstand, and hold them carefully against one another, since it is surely not to be presumed that every human being should have one and the same motives for holding one and the same thing to be true, but rather the greatest probability is that what the one considers from one side, another, who thinks differently and forms different concepts of the whole matter, turns toward here from the other side.”",
"blomberg-s08-043": "2do “One must accept and suppose the opposite of the cognition that one has, put oneself as it were in the place of one's opponent and challenger of the truth of my cognition, and then see whether it can be thought as true, or even only as probable. If this takes place even to some extent, one has in every case reason to place some mistrust in the truth of the cognition one had. But if this does not take place in the least, and I can see impartially, and also maintain, that the opposite of the cognition I have is absurd, impossible, and, if one wished to accept it, would be foolish, then it is evident from this that my cognition must be true and correct.”",
"blomberg-s08-044": "etc. etc. etc. In respect of certainty, however, there are three different kinds, or degrees, of holding-to-be-true:",
"blomberg-s08-045": "1.) Knowing (Wissen)",
"blomberg-s08-046": "2) Opining (Meinen)",
"blomberg-s08-047": "3) Believing (Glauben)",
"blomberg-s08-048": "1mo Knowing means to judge something with certainty and to hold it to be true. Much is therefore required in order to know something. I can never say: I know that there are inhabitants on the moon; I have not been there, I have not seen them.",
"blomberg-s08-049": "2do Opining means to judge something incompletely, and occurs when the grounds we have for holding something to be true are insufficient and do not have the preponderance over the opposite, and that is then an opinion.",
"blomberg-s08-050": "In these opinions, however, the grounds, and thereby also the degrees of holding-to-be-true, can grow and increase; but it is also to be noted that in opining one always remains undecided.",
"blomberg-s08-051": "But to opine something practically, there is required",
"blomberg-s08-052": "3.) Believing. Here I am always decided, without however finding it necessary in myself to be decided.",
"blomberg-s08-053": "Opinions are therefore nothing other than subjectively incomplete grounds of holding-to-be-true.",
"blomberg-s08-054": "The human being opines at all times.",
"blomberg-s08-055": "To accept something without a subjective necessity according to logical concepts is called believing.",
"blomberg-s08-056": "A logical necessity, however, is nothing other than the necessity of holding-to-be-true according to logical laws of the understanding and of reason.",
"blomberg-s08-057": "The necessity of accepting something according to practical laws, however, is always subjective. Believing therefore means nothing other than accepting something of which I am not yet logically certain.",
"blomberg-s08-058": "Belief is, moreover, a practically sufficient holding-to-be-true.",
"blomberg-s08-059": "Certainty, on the other hand, is a holding-to-be-true that is also theoretically sufficient. [454a]",
"blomberg-s08-060": "In belief I am not even inclined to listen to the grounds of my opponent. For my grounds of holding-to-be-true are in that case always practically sufficient. In belief there also occurs a certain direction of our judgment through the will.",
"blomberg-s08-061": "If I accept something, I do not on that account yet hold it to be certain; and likewise, if I concede something, I do not on that account by a long way hold it to be true.",
"blomberg-s08-062": "A practical certainty would be firm belief, and this will be, both in its conviction and in its power to dispel all doubts, as strong as belief according to logical laws can ever be. This belief is often stronger than some apodictic certainty of a cognition. The latter, however, takes place only in the general cognitions",
"blomberg-s08-063": "a) of reason",
"blomberg-s08-064": "B) of experience",
"blomberg-s08-065": "We can also believe something without thereby attaining a practical certainty of it, but merely, at most, a greater degree of holding-to-be-true.",
"blomberg-s08-066": "There is also a practical holding-to-be-true, and a certain believing, the removal of which would at the same time cancel and annihilate all general and necessary laws of the practical will, of the whole of morals, ethics, theology, religion, etc., and whatever sublime thing can be thought of; and this is then all a firm belief.",
"blomberg-s08-067": "Of such propositions we have two:",
"blomberg-s08-068": "1:) There is a God.",
"blomberg-s08-069": "2) There is another world.",
"blomberg-s08-070": "These two propositions are the sole ground of all religion, morality, etc. These two propositions are the true conditio sine qua non.",
"blomberg-s08-071": "The belief in these two propositions is not historical but rational, derived from practical rules of reason.",
"blomberg-s08-072": "This belief is no opinion but a certainty, which however is of such a constitution that, although it is not actually apodictic and, according to the laws of logic, irrefutable, it nevertheless outweighs by far all holding-to-be-true from logical grounds, and retains precedence over them.",
"blomberg-s08-073": "Apodictic certainty, which comes and arises merely from the understanding, does not, when it comes to the decision of all our welfare, our good fortune and misfortune, however convincing it otherwise is, really have the inner force to compel the subject always to adhere to it.",
"blomberg-s08-074": "It is so even with mathematical propositions and demonstrations, which otherwise have the very greatest and surest certainty. As certain as it is that a circle has 360°, and as apodictic as the Platonic theorems in geometry are, yet no one would want to die for their infallibility and irrefutability.",
"blomberg-s08-075": "Belief according to practical, but especially moral laws, however, has the full power to strengthen the subject that has it with a complete confidence, and to bring it about that, in spite of all dangers and misfortune, even when it concerns the decision of everything that touches its weal or woe, it nevertheless always adheres to it.",
"blomberg-s08-076": "One has indeed seen people die for moral propositions.",
"blomberg-s08-077": "The resolve, therefore, not to be turned aside by anything at all is more a resolve of practical and moral belief than an effect of apodictic certainty, and it is far more unshakable; indeed it carries with it in fact always a certain kind of merit.",
"blomberg-s08-078": "A cognition is objectively certain when it contains those grounds according to which all human beings must agree in regard to it. A cognition is subjectively certain, however, when it contains the manner in which human beings actually agree in regard to it.",
"blomberg-s08-079": "Evidentness (Augenscheinlichkeit) is the intuitive, the intuiting element in certainty: evidence. Thus, e.g., mathematical propositions are evident. But we are certain of many things without nevertheless having evidence of them.",
"blomberg-s08-080": "We have two methods of the grounds of holding-to-be-true. The assent of other human beings to our opinions, and the testing of our thoughts against the sentiments of other human beings, is properly a very excellent logical experiment of our understanding upon the understanding of others. The human being has great need of this communication of his cognitions in order to be able to judge them properly.",
"blomberg-s08-081": "Human beings have a natural inclination to communicate to others the judgments their understanding has made, and from this alone the writing of books originates, the cause of which other censurers of the human race, who like to interpret everything in the worst way, also place in vanity and ambition.",
"blomberg-s08-082": "People who separate themselves from all human society find in the end, when they begin to examine their condition and the causes of their misanthropy, necessarily that they themselves do not have means enough to distinguish the true from the false.",
"blomberg-s08-083": "The freedom of communicating one's thoughts, judgments, and knowledge, however, is certainly the sole and surest means of properly testing and verifying one's knowledge. And whoever takes away this freedom is to be regarded as the worst enemy of the spread of human cognitions, indeed of human beings themselves. For by that very act he takes from human beings the one true means that they still possess of ever uncovering, becoming aware of, and correcting the frequent deceit of their own understanding and its missteps.",
"blomberg-s08-084": "The deprivation of the freedom to think unconstrained, and to bring these thoughts to light, is really a deprivation of the first rights, of the greatest prerogatives of the human race, and especially of the human understanding.",
"blomberg-s08-085": "Human beings are as it were called to use their reason in common, and to make use of it. Just so the temporal goods of this life.",
"blomberg-s08-086": "From this it follows quite naturally that everyone who has the principle of self-conceit thinks the judgments of others, for the use of his own reason and for the cognition of truth, wholly dispensable to him, very poor, and worthy of blame.",
"blomberg-s08-087": "This, however, is properly logical egoism, which then indeed also would not need, and would not have to, communicate its own judgments to others. This so-called logical egoism consists therefore in nothing other than in the supposed but often false self-sufficiency of our wholly self-subsisting, and so to speak isolated, understanding, in which one believes oneself alone to know enough, and all one's judgments to be infallibly correct and incapable of improvement. And one readily sees that this conceited manner of thinking is not only wholly ridiculous but even highly contrary to real humanity.",
"blomberg-s08-088": "Sensible certainty is to be well distinguished by us from the certainty of the senses.",
"blomberg-s08-089": "Through observations, experiments, and frequent and often repeated experience one attains a certainty of the senses.",
"blomberg-s08-090": "The sensible certainty of a cognition, however, arises not properly through the actual intuition of the objects by means of our senses, not through the immediate sensible representation of the objects, but merely thereby that one makes the cognitions capable of being seen distinctly, convincingly, and with more or less effort, through images and examples.",
"blomberg-s08-091": "A cognition can properly be called rational in a twofold way, namely",
"blomberg-s08-092": "1mo “insofar as it can be seen through reason and also agrees with it; of this kind are all true, well-grounded, demonstrable cognitions;” or",
"blomberg-s08-093": "2do “insofar as it springs merely from reason; of this kind are for the most part all the logical cognitions, which can therefore also be called praecise and in the strictest sense cognitions of reason (Vernunft-Erkenntnisse).”",
"blomberg-s08-094": "In the certainty of a cognition one can look either to logical and rational certainty or to aesthetic and sensible certainty.",
"blomberg-s08-095": "The latter cognition is indeed subject to very many errors, yet on the other hand it is also very easy to see into.",
"blomberg-s08-096": "A cognition that is perfect according to the rules of coordinated concepts is called, in common parlance, complete. Yet one could more readily and more correctly call it an extensive cognition, because the coordinated concepts are all set out at the same time, and are thus, so to speak, spread out over the object.",
"blomberg-s08-097": "Those grounds to which others are subordinated are properly not at all the complements of these latter toward the sufficient ground, but rather their origin. In the series of grounds coordinated with one another, however, one concept is indeed often a complementum of another.",
"blomberg-s08-098": "In the highest degree of rational certainty I look to the series of marks subordinated to one another, in order to arrive at the first ground. In this way I seek to find the intensive clarity of conviction. In the highest degree of sensible certainty, however, I always look merely to the series of marks coordinated with one another, in order thereby to be able to produce a certain strict and accurate completeness according to the rules of extensive clarity.",
"blomberg-s08-099": "A cognition, however, can always be certain",
"blomberg-s08-100": "1.) Mathematically but also 2) Philosophically.",
"blomberg-s08-101": "The peculiarity of mathematical certainty is this, that although it arises from reason just as the philosophical does, one nevertheless proceeds in it synthetically, that is, one ascends in the series of grounds subordinated to one another, by composition, from the lowest and simplest up to the highest mark.",
"blomberg-s08-102": "In philosophical certainty, on the contrary, one indeed likewise runs through the series of concepts subordinated to one another, i.e. ranked beneath one another, but this happens not synthetically but analytically, i.e. by means of dissection. And one descends from the composite and highest to the simplest and lowest concept.",
"blomberg-s08-103": "The mathematical certainty of cognition is always very distinct and intuitive, so that one can see it very easily. This, however, arises from the fact that all mathematical concepts are synthetic and arise through arbitrary composition. For one can be conscious most easily of that which one has oneself invented. In philosophy, on the other hand, all concepts are analytic; they are not arbitrary, as in mathematics, so that one could assume and establish something at one's own pleasure, but are already given confusedly, and are to be made distinct and easy [473/474] only by philosophy.",
"blomberg-s08-104": "Since the concepts in philosophy are therefore already given, the philosopher cannot so easily be certain that he has touched upon all the marks that belong to a thing, and sees them all entirely completely. Rather, many notae may still belong to the thing of which he knows nothing at all.",
"blomberg-s08-105": "But no marks of a thing can flee or slip away from the mathematician; nothing can escape his keen eye. For he has himself arbitrarily, if not invented them, at any rate assumed them and arbitrarily ascribed them to it; consequently he must surely also be perfectly conscious of them all.",
"blomberg-s08-106": "The author speaks in this paragraph of shallow cognitions. In our language, and in our expressions, we very often borrow analogies.",
"blomberg-s08-107": "We make use, namely, of certain images in order, with their help, to give a sensibility to cognitions that are in themselves abstract.",
"blomberg-s08-108": "In just the same way we also here in logic call certain cognitions (as elsewhere kinds of understanding, lectures, etc. etc.) shallow, ad Analogiam (by analogy) with water.",
"blomberg-s08-109": "A water is then, according to physical principles, as is well known, called shallow when its bottom is to be met with not far below the surface.",
"blomberg-s08-110": "A cognition, however, is shallow ad Analogiam when it is grounded on mere experiences and on no investigation.",
"blomberg-s08-111": "To the shallowness of cognition is opposed, or contra distinguished, its thoroughness; this latter is namely the terminus of certainty.",
"blomberg-s08-112": "Superficial, on the other hand, is a cognition when one is already content with mere semblance, i.e. when one lets that cognition be enough and rests with it which one could attain at the mere first glance at it. In a superficial cognition one therefore cognizes nothing more than what strikes one's eyes immediately. Thus one has, e.g., a superficial cognition of geography if one knows nothing further than how to recite readily the names of countries, cities, rivers, etc. Thus one has a superficial cognition of history if one knows some conspicuous histories of the states without looking to their origin.",
"blomberg-s08-113": "By and by means of a superficial cognition one sees, so to speak, the straws floating on the surface of the water, but the pearls that lie at the bottom of the sea one does not become aware of.",
"blomberg-s08-114": "Superficial cognitions, which concern themselves only with the surface of a thing and its cognitions, are most nearly related to shallowness.",
"blomberg-s08-115": "Thorough, on the other hand, is a cognition when one goes to the ground in it, i.e. when one goes as far as one can possibly go in order to arrive at complete certainty.",
"blomberg-s08-116": "A cognition must be deep in order to become thorough. When the series of grounds subordinated to one another is very long until one comes to complete certainty, the cognition is profound.",
"blomberg-s08-117": "All cognitions, the empirical as well as the rational, can become thorough.",
"blomberg-s08-118": "Thorough cognitions of experience, empirical cognitions, however, are not of the kind that they can become profound, but only the rational ones. The ground of this is that in empirical cognitions no series of subordinated grounds, but only of coordinated ones, takes place.",
"blomberg-s08-119": "Empirical cognitions are thorough, however, when they have in themselves as many coordinated concepts as are necessary for distinctness.",
"blomberg-s08-120": "One has a thorough historical cognition, e.g., when one goes back to the authors who first wrote about a matter.",
"blomberg-s08-121": "A cognition can be thorough even though it is very restricted in respect of extent and spread. Thorough cognitions, however, must always be preferred to extensive and wide-ranging cognition.",
"blomberg-s08-122": "Extent without thoroughness is namely nothing but an empty shadow; it resembles a null, or zero, a cipher, in which there is no real being.",
"blomberg-s08-123": "Herein the author speaks of three different kinds of actions that we must undertake in order to be certain of a cognition or to reject it. These 3 actions are namely nothing other than our different conduct with regard to the assent which we believe we owe or do not owe to the cognitions, as there are:",
"blomberg-s08-124": "1mo the giving of assent",
"blomberg-s08-125": "2do the withdrawal of assent, and",
"blomberg-s08-126": "3tio the withholding of assent.",
"blomberg-s08-127": "The first is a positive, the second a privative, the third, however, no action at all. In the first case, namely, I take something to be true;",
"blomberg-s08-128": "in the second case I accept something as false; in the third case, however, I do not judge at all, but I suspend my judicium (judgment).",
"blomberg-s08-129": "In any cognition whatever we can think either a",
"blomberg-s08-130": "Plus or a Minus, or a",
"blomberg-s08-131": "Zero thinking.",
"blomberg-s08-132": "Affirmative judgments increase our cognitions, and are therefore a Plus.",
"blomberg-s08-133": "Negative judgments do not increase our cognition, and are therefore a Minus. For they seek only to diminish the false in our cognitions, to put us in a position to accept nothing but what is true; in short, they serve only to avoid error.",
"blomberg-s08-134": "But if finally in every cognition we neither affirm nor deny anything, this is the state of Zero.",
"blomberg-s08-135": "Just so, in respect of our outer humor, we are in the state of Plus in pleasure, in the state of Minus in sadness, but in the state of Zero in complete indifference and insensibility.",
"blomberg-s08-136": "The withdrawal of assent indeed brings no increase to our cognitions, but nevertheless keeps us from error.",
"blomberg-s08-137": "But if we give our assent to a cognition, our knowing receives an accrementum (increase).",
"blomberg-s08-138": "The withholding of assent, however, brings about ignorance, although in some cases it can be quite useful. Here, however, we must necessarily discuss the following question as well: whether, namely, our free choice has an influence on our giving a cognition our assent, or withdrawing it from it, or perhaps even withholding it?",
"blomberg-s08-139": "Answer: In most cases this procedure of giving our assent, or withdrawing it, or withholding it, rests not at all on our free choice but is rather properly necessitated through and by the laws of our understanding and of our reason.",
"blomberg-s08-140": "So it goes, for example, in mathematics. A merchant, e.g., who sees well from his accounts that he owes much, and more than he has to hope for and possesses, will surely not be able to withdraw his assent and consent from this cognition, which is so evident, however gladly he might wish to; even supposing he does not much esteem it positively, he will nevertheless not be able negatively to regard the sum of the debt as still-available existing capital, since he is far too much, and evidently, convinced of the correctness of arithmetic in this respect, and the debt-account has far too great an evidence in itself.",
"blomberg-s08-141": "This much is certain: if we were in a position, and it pleased us, to give assent, to accept something arbitrary at one moment and to reject it at the next, we should certainly thereby be put in a position to promote, produce and increase very greatly our pleasure, our comfort, indeed even our happiness.",
"blomberg-s08-142": "On the other hand, we should to the same degree also be able always to remove at will everything that could bring about sadness in us and cause vexation. But in the case of such arbitrary cognitions there would then have to be either no sufficient grounds present for maintaining the opposite of them, or, supposing they were even present, we should have no need at all to reflect on them. In this way the debtor could easily take himself for a creditor, the man fallen from favor for a favorite of his prince, etc., he who receives the most distressing news for a fortunate man, or at least remain in suspenso (although, considered from another side, the hovering between fear and hope is far more burdensome and anxious, indeed insufferable, than the consciousness of one's misfortune), and then, even when a complete ruin of his station, honor, etc. and of his whole fortune was inevitably and shortly impending, still be able, with a contented soul and undisturbed or stirred mind, to sleep well and quietly, and also to eat and drink well.",
"blomberg-s08-143": "This free arbitrium in respect of assent might perhaps belong among the felicities of the old poets. It falls away entirely, and in the presence of certain degrees of grounds it is always very difficult, indeed wholly impossible, to withhold one's assent.",
"blomberg-s08-144": "In very many cases one can therefore not only count on the assent that someone must give to another, but even insist on it (pochen), that is: one can extort and wring it from him.",
"blomberg-s08-145": "The mathematician, whose propositions are resplendent with sufficient grounds and are proved, can always speak imperiously; he can rightly demand from me assent to his propositions, and he who, notwithstanding this, still wished to remain in suspenso and to doubt whether he should accept these propositions, etc., or reject them on the other side, would always make himself ridiculous and show that he does not even know what mathematics and its properties are.",
"blomberg-s08-146": "Notwithstanding this, although assent does not depend immediately on the choice (Willkühr) of human beings, it nevertheless often depends on it indirecte, mediately, mediate, since one seeks out at one's free pleasure those grounds which could in some way or other bring about an assent for this or that cognition, which cannot insist on the consensum aliorum debitum (the assent of others owed to it).",
"blomberg-s08-147": "If assent does not arise immediately through the nature of the human understanding and human reason, it still requires a closer direction of choice, of pleasure, or in general of our free will toward the grounds of proof.",
"blomberg-s08-148": "It is certainly true that in the human soul there takes place an arbitrary direction of the powers of the mind toward that which we gladly see and wish for.",
"blomberg-s08-149": "We give our assent at once to a cognition that is very pleasing and agreeable to us, without even taking the trouble to investigate more closely the actual and true grounds of holding-to-be-true.",
"blomberg-s08-150": "When something is very significant for us and of very great importance, so that a great, indeed the greatest, part of our peace of mind and of our outward well-being and happiness depends on it, the mind is already not free enough to consider the matter indifferently and impartially from both sides, to weigh the grounds on one",
"blomberg-s08-151": "side and the grounds of the opposite on the other, to hold the weight of all these two kinds of grounds properly against one another, and to pronounce the right verdict of preference; rather the mind, indeed often even our understanding, is as it were fettered and restricted, so that we readily approve at once that which is advantageous to us and give our assent to it, and on the other hand soon reject and disapprove what could redound to our harm or mortification.",
"blomberg-s08-152": "In suspension e Judicii (of judgment) finally, that is, in the withholding of our assent, one performs, as we have already remarked above, properly no action at all.",
"blomberg-s08-153": "A judgment is dogmatic when one either actually accepts or affirms a cognition put forward, or on the other side rejects and denies it. But if, on the contrary, one does not pass one's judgment but holds it back, and thus neither affirms nor denies anything, this judgment is skeptical.",
"blomberg-s08-154": "The skeptical in judging, where one makes no decision at all, settles nothing but leaves everything unsettled, and harbors doubt about all that of which one can maintain nothing with certainty, until [496/7] one has proper grounds of proof for either accepting or rejecting a cognition. This cautious and prudent procedure in judging, where one takes great care to affirm outright nothing of which one is not wholly reliably convinced, and to deny nothing whose impossibility, or non-being, one does not see quite distinctly, this procedure is really most conformable to distinct truth and most advantageous, provided only that one does not have the constant resolve never to distinguish anything, to doubt everything forever, and to leave everything undecided. Hence it comes about that a true philosopher is much inclined not to squander his assent needlessly everywhere, but always to hold it back until he has sufficiently and fully sufficient grounds of the truth. But as soon as he does have adequate grounds for the truth of a cognition and sees its validity himself, he no longer asks; rather it is then his custom to accede to the cognition at once, and to cognize it as true, indeed to maintain it.",
"blomberg-s08-155": "The insatiable desire, ruling in many a so-called philosopher or dogmatist, to decide each and every cognition at once at first sight without the least investigation, that is, either to accept or to reject it, has seduced many a one, and is always the surest way to endless errors.",
"blomberg-s08-156": "One of the chief causes, however, which very often lead human beings to pass a false judgment, or also into an error, are, further, the affects.",
"blomberg-s08-157": "These perverters of the human being, which cause so much disorder in common life, are no less able to confuse the human understanding and lead it to errors. These affects of the human being now turn the grounds of certainty about a thing merely to the one side, and do not permit him to consider the other side of it as well. Hence we can indeed blame someone who has given assent to a false cognition, if namely the fault really lies with him himself, in that he rejects those grounds which could have convinced him about the object of the cognition he has, and so freed him from his error.",
"blomberg-s08-158": "Mathematics, and pure and immediate experience, are alone of the kind that they leave us no grounds for the opposite.",
"blomberg-s08-159": "All and any other cognitions, however, are of the kind that they very often indicate to us just as great and weighty grounds of proof for maintaining the opposite of a thing as they offer us proofs for accepting the thing. It is therefore certainly a real prudence to know how to hold back one's assent in most cases until one has sufficient grounds for the matter.",
"blomberg-s08-160": "Deceit is almost universal in all matters; therefore this is in fact a really secure, indeed in many cases infallible way of avoiding many illusions of reason, and of doing without error. Nevertheless, this withholding of assent must certainly not be so constituted that one is at the same time inclined never to let oneself be convinced of the matter, and always to refuse it one's assent; for in this way we never settle anything at all, and so remain ignorant in all things.",
"blomberg-s08-161": "The withholding of assent, namely with the named inclination never to settle anything, is really nothing other than a lazy doubt, a lazy doubting-mania, or at least the way to it.",
"blomberg-s08-162": "The suspensio is only to serve to accept nothing until one has sufficient grounds, but not on that account immediately to reject entirely each and every hope of ever being able to attain to the complete certainty of a thing, or of a cognition.",
"blomberg-s08-163": "Consideration (Überlegen) is distinguished from investigating (Untersuchen) and investigation.",
"blomberg-s08-164": "To consider means to compare something with the laws of the understanding. To investigate, however, properly means to consider mediately. Of many things we can well cognize without investigation what is true and what is false. Consideration, however, is always necessary for every judgment, and for the distinction of the true from the false, whether in general or in a cognition, etc.; it is indispensable in all individual cases.",
"blomberg-s08-165": "The word praejudicium has a double, or twofold, meaning, namely",
"blomberg-s08-166": "1mo it signifies a provisional judgment, which however is as yet no real prejudice.",
"blomberg-s08-167": "2do It signifies a real prejudice. E.g. when one values the writings of one man, before one has even seen them, far higher than the writings of another, this is as yet no proper prejudice but only a provisional judgment, in which reason infers something from certain grounds.",
"blomberg-s08-168": "It therefore properly decides nothing at all here, but judges merely from the few grounds that it has of the thing; according to these grounds it thinks and represents something to itself.",
"blomberg-s08-169": "This judgment is therefore by no means dogmatic, but is merely always connected with a certain postponement.",
"blomberg-s08-170": "This provisional judgment, namely, is no ground for holding something already to be certain, not in the least. Meanwhile, however, this much is certain, that in the present state there are more grounds for accepting the matter than for rejecting it, and for maintaining the opposite of this matter. One therefore readily praises a book which is said to come from a learned, famous, and well-known man, even before one has first read it, merely because one represents to oneself that a quite famous man must write nothing but things worthy of his fame, and that this book too will accordingly be well written.",
"blomberg-s08-171": "There has never been an inventor in the world, and none has existed, who invented something and did not at the same time form a provisional judgment of his invention and of the thing invented. He was not certain of the matter, but the judgment paved the way for him to try and to experiment. E.g. miners judge from the strata in the earth where they are to find the metals, and conjecture ever so long until they have obtained certain knowledge of the metal-mines, which no longer deceives them, and in accordance with which they attain their end.",
"blomberg-s08-172": "Cognitions, however, arise in us either through the cognition of the object, or through and by means of the properties that belong to the subject; these are subjective grounds of cognition.",
"blomberg-s08-173": "A subjective ground of holding-to-be-true prior to all consideration, as the necessary and exceedingly useful action of the understanding, can be called the prejudice (VorUrtheil).",
"blomberg-s08-174": "Inclination is a disposition of the mind to love something and to be taken with it.",
"blomberg-s08-175": "Inclinations are very seductive; to overcome them steadfastly is certainly the greatest art, indeed almost impossible, and so it is also in logic, both with the Argumentis ab utili (arguments from utility) and with the Argumentis ab odio (arguments from hatred). And this takes place especially when this or that matter becomes entangled in an interest of human beings, whether near or remote, general or particular, and has a particular share in it.",
"blomberg-s08-176": "Those prejudices that arise from inclination are of many kinds.",
"blomberg-s08-177": "A further subjective ground of holding-to-be-true is also imitation, to which man is accordingly by nature so very much inclined, and the judgments arising from it extend to almost the whole life of human beings.",
"blomberg-s08-178": "The spirit of imitation is, of everything that can only harm and oppose the philosophical spirit, always really the worst. Imitation is the shaping of one's understanding, one's will, indeed one's choice (Willkühr) after the example and model of others; namely, when one is not capable of thinking for oneself, one takes refuge in them and copies them quite faithfully, as the painter copies the original, except that this copy often turns out very unsuitable, indeed ridiculous.",
"blomberg-s08-179": "One thing, then, is very often opposed to the spirit of the holding-to-be-true of cognitions that does not wish to err: habit, which very often conflicts with the understanding, but which, once it has taken root, is hard to eradicate.",
"blomberg-s08-180": "Those people, however, are very rash who blindly confuse a provisional judgment with a decisive judgment. Hence a certain Suspensio Judicii (suspension of judgment) is very necessary and useful here, so that one does not rush ahead. This teaches us merely how we are to regard grounds that are plausible and still far from sufficient, which we still do not at all hold to be very convincing grounds; and it consequently keeps us in many cases from many wrong paths and errors in cognitions.",
"blomberg-s08-181": "The enemy of men, so dangerous, of whom we have already spoken above, is one of the greatest causes of overheating. A calm person, who is not thrown into disorder, confused, inflamed, etc. by any violent movement of the mind, will not rush ahead so quickly and so easily as one who is in the grip of affect and is carried away irresistibly by it.",
"blomberg-s08-182": "Reflection, however, is an important, very great and secure way, if not to extinguish the affects entirely, then still to quiet them, to prevent their dangerous consequences, and thus to avoid errors. One lets, and takes, namely, the time to examine and inquire well whether something is true or false, or not, whether one can assume the opposite of it, or not. And by this way, in which one reflects calmly on the matter, one will thus very soon be convinced of the truth of a thing, or cognition.",
"blomberg-s08-183": "Philosophy likewise strives greatly to tame and eradicate the affects as far as possible. Hence truly learned people and philosophers can keep their affects well in check, so that they do not easily let themselves be taken in by a thing without first having sufficient grounds.",
"blomberg-s08-184": "They rather weigh everything that they choose as the object of their considerations with cold blood, that is, with a calm mind; they further consider carefully, in every matter, the one side as well as the other, and in this way come best, most easily and most securely to the right cognition of the truth.",
"blomberg-s08-185": "Through provisional judgments we seek to help ourselves to a closer conviction; we judge, however, already before we have considered the matter more closely and cognized it.",
"blomberg-s08-186": "One does not come upon the truth without seeking it; but even in order to seek it, one must already make judgments about the way on which one thinks to come upon it.",
"blomberg-s08-187": "We attain complete certainty never otherwise than by means of investigation; but before all investigation a provisional judgment must still precede.",
"blomberg-s08-188": "Between a provisional judgment and a prejudice (Vorurtheil) there seems to be only a small difference, and really to be, in that both judgments are judgments of the kind that always precede the investigation of a matter or of a cognition.",
"blomberg-s08-189": "The provisional judgment, however, as a means to investigation, joined with the consciousness and the intention of thereby finding the ways to investigation, is not only supremely fitting for our understanding, but is also a secure, indeed infallible way of arriving at the truth of a cognition.",
"blomberg-s08-190": "Without it, truly no important invention, none of any consequence, has been made.",
"blomberg-s08-191": "But if one judges and assumes something before the investigation, with the intention of making no closer investigation at all of the whole matter, but of letting it simply rest there entirely, then this is in fact a culpable prejudice, which is not only not conducive to the cognition of truth, but even obstructive and harmful.",
"blomberg-s08-192": "The former judgment, namely a cautious, provisional judgment, yet with the intention of investigating the cognition more closely in order nevertheless to arrive at the truth, is therefore truly useful to the truth, and afterwards also directs the investigation that one then really undertakes of the cognition. The essential thing about all prejudices, however, is that they are general rules for judging.",
"blomberg-s08-193": "It is not at all a prejudice, after all, when one assumes a proposition without extensive investigation; rather, one assumes this proposition through a prejudice. One has accordingly the two states and particular properties of the human understanding:",
"blomberg-s08-194": "1st To have a prejudice or to be occupied with it, and then",
"blomberg-s08-195": "2nd Not to have a prejudice, but to assume something through it.",
"blomberg-s08-196": "Almost everyone, whoever he may be, regards the fashions and customs of his country, or fatherland, as the best and most fitting. With us, e.g., we bare the head, among the Turks, however, not; we therefore hold these to be coarse, although it is not yet settled whether it is a coarseness to cover the head or to bare it. The Turks perhaps in turn hold us to be impolite. This, however, is truly not itself a prejudice, but only something that is assumed through a prejudice.",
"blomberg-s08-197": "All prejudices are not grounded on reflection. This is lacking in them, for otherwise they would be real judgments of the understanding.",
"blomberg-s08-198": "Reflection, of which we have already spoken above, is nothing other than the comparison of a cognition with the laws of the understanding and of reason. We are, however, directed not merely by the laws of the understanding and of reason, but also very often by inclinations, or by our taste. In the case of inclinations no reflection at all takes place.",
"blomberg-s08-199": "Prejudices, however, have very many and various sources.",
"blomberg-s08-200": "Habit is likewise one of the causes and grounds for judging without reflection. Even without having an inclination toward something, one nevertheless very often assumes it as true from mere habit. Hence if one wants to cleanse someone of his errors, one must not only convince him of the opposite of what he believes, but also accustom him to it slowly and gradually over time, and give him time to accept the truth. With the most convincing, most evident and most certain proofs, one who has long hovered in error and been possessed by doubt will nevertheless not be brought round all at once, and thus convinced and truly overcome by the irrefutability of the proofs, so that he would at once abandon all doubt and resolve and be disposed to assent to the truth immediately and without hesitation; rather, a long time is very often required for this.",
"blomberg-s08-201": "The following 3 sources of prejudices, however, there are, which are the most usual and the greatest",
"blomberg-s08-202": "1) Imitation",
"blomberg-s08-203": "2) Habit",
"blomberg-s08-204": "3) Inclination",
"blomberg-s08-205": "In youth one has as yet no skill in judging, and so one lets oneself easily be swept along by imitation, and soon assumes as certain and unquestioned what others assert, either those in whom we place a particular trust, or who stand in particular esteem with us, or also what the general vote of many prescribes to us.",
"blomberg-s08-206": "If now habit is added to this imitation, of judging thus and not otherwise, then this prejudice becomes all the stronger and ever more gains the upper hand.",
"blomberg-s08-207": "A child, e.g., certainly always takes the first impressions made on its mind, for the most part, very easily, and as the greatest and most important truths; indeed it also retains them longest of all.",
"blomberg-s08-208": "According to the given rules of the laws of imagination, such prejudices are indeed not easily to be brought out of the soul once they have taken root in it.",
"blomberg-s08-209": "Here, then, properly no reflection at all takes place, but one assumes straightaway what others before us have asserted, without reflecting oneself whether it is reasonable to assume it or not.",
"blomberg-s08-210": "Hence it comes about that when false concepts, images, representations etc. are instilled in children in their tender youth, these can afterwards not be eradicated at all from their imagination, or power of imagination, and one therefore has great cause to proceed very cautiously in this, and to take care not to spoil the children's understanding before its time, and to make it in a certain way unusable.",
"blomberg-s08-211": "Habit is, further, as it were a very fertile source of many pernicious prejudices. If one has often undertaken an action, has, so to speak, fared well by it, and has thus once grown accustomed to it, then [526a] there finally arises, in the course of time, a certain kind of necessity of acting so, and of never proceeding otherwise.",
"blomberg-s08-212": "To dress fashionably (yet according to one's station) is praiseworthy and good, for it is always still more tolerable, and better, to be a fool within the fashion than one outside it. But to judge, to infer, to think, to write, as it were, in turn according to fashion is always silly, and a proof of no reflection at all. Ceremonies, kinds of dress, compliments, indeed, but not the understanding, can be brought under a fashion.",
"blomberg-s08-213": "Further, inclination is also a cause and ground of many judgments, and indeed one judges thereby, and infers that something is good, agreeable and perfect, because it attracts us, because it is pleasant to us and suited to our taste.",
"blomberg-s08-214": "This inclination moreover brings it about that we always conduct examinations and investigations only from one side, and indeed only from the side where we wish that it were so and not otherwise, leaving the other side, which might perhaps furnish us with grounds for the opposite, entirely uninvestigated. We even seek thereby to find grounds by whose help we can demonstrate the opposite of what we wish. Here too the laws of the understanding and of reason are as it were absent, and not at home; there is no reflection of one's own at all, but only certain sensations take place.",
"blomberg-s08-215": "All prejudices arise from imitation, habit, or inclination, and without these three sources no prejudices would ever occur in the world, which are nevertheless so very often an obstacle to the truth.",
"blomberg-s08-216": "Everything that is immediately certain requires no investigation at all. All cognitions must be reflected upon, that is, they must be compared with the laws of the understanding and of reason, and held against one another; but not each and every cognition may be investigated, for to investigate means nothing other than to compare something mediately with the laws of the understanding and of reason.",
"blomberg-s08-217": "All cognitions whose agreement with the laws of our understanding and of reason takes place immediately are indemonstrable; these can be assumed without investigation, they may not be proved, indeed one cannot even undertake an investigation of them.",
"blomberg-s08-218": "One is, in the proper sense, not in a position to do it. A prejudice, however, is nothing other than a certain general ground of judging without any reflection.",
"blomberg-s08-219": "A cognition, however, that is assumed merely by means of a prejudice is not yet itself a prejudice at all; if we want to speak strictly, we must understand it so: there are properly",
"blomberg-s08-220": "really only few prejudices, but on the other hand infinitely many errors arising from these existing prejudices.",
"blomberg-s08-221": "A prejudice always arises when one judges without reflection, i.e. when one already judges about a thing or its cognition without having first compared this cognition with the laws of the understanding or of reason. Prejudices, therefore, cannot properly lie at all in the deficiencies or faults of our understanding and our reason, but must arise from other powers.",
"blomberg-s08-222": "The understanding and reason themselves, left to themselves alone, never err; this much is beyond doubt established and certain, for this would be an obvious contradictio in adjecto (contradiction in the adjective), as we have already remarked above; they would then have to conflict with their own powers, and no power can conflict with itself; rather it comes about only because other, foreign powers mix themselves with the powers and laws of our understanding and of reason, from which then naturally a mixed effect results, and from this again an error arises.",
"blomberg-s08-223": "First of all, prejudice cannot possibly arise from the laws of the understanding and correct itself, for it is, after all, a judgment without reflection. But that which is assumed by means of a prejudice is on that account not yet always false as to its matter.",
"blomberg-s08-224": "Prejudices, further, are not exactly quite necessary sources of errors, but this much is certain, that each and every cognition that is assumed through a prejudice is, as to form, already in itself always false.",
"blomberg-s08-225": "Through a cognition a certain intended end can often be attained very well, but the method of attaining this end can very often not be the genuine and right one. And so it also goes very often with the actions of human beings.",
"blomberg-s08-226": "Any human being can, if he only wants to, very well take revenge (revanchiren) on the one who has offended him, or has demanded too much of him, that is, on his enemy, and let him pay for the wrong done to him; but the manner and way of taking revenge, and the form of avenging oneself, is wrong and unlawful: one should not be one's own judge. He on whom one takes revenge cannot complain in the least; no wrong at all has been done to him, but one has merely paid him in the same coin.",
"blomberg-s08-227": "But it is not at all the proper manner of avenging oneself on someone. Such great disorder would arise from it that it would finally become almost incurable. Rather, divine and human laws prescribe to us the lawful ways on which we can arrive at compensation for our damage, honor, etc., without having to be ashamed of the way in which we arrived at it.",
"blomberg-s08-228": "Exactly so it goes with prejudice too. With regard to the object the judgment and the cognition can very often be very true, but not with regard to the form.",
"blomberg-s08-229": "One must therefore take great care not to reject each and every prejudice straightaway, but one must first examine them and investigate well whether something good may not perhaps still be found in them. One can really find again a kind of prejudice against prejudices themselves, namely when one straightaway rejects everything that has arisen through prejudices.",
"blomberg-s08-230": "In this way one can very often reject the very greatest and most important truths, and thereby innocently deprive of them those who are not capable of, and equal to, the investigation and inquiry into them. E.g., if one assumes what all people say, and always holds this to be quite certain, this is a prejudice. But it is really so constituted that very many truths rest merely on the say-so of human beings and have, or can have, no other ground, which nevertheless on closer investigation are really certain truths.",
"blomberg-s08-231": "If one now wanted to reject all these truths straightaway, many people would thereby be robbed of quite good cognitions, who do not possess sufficient capacity and strength of mind enough to investigate everything according to the laws of the understanding and of reason and to examine it with proper reflection.",
"blomberg-s08-232": "There are some people of such a kind that their mind is hostile to the true, and that such prejudices therefore take root in them first and most easily: people who seek to make suspect every truth that is asserted by others with assurance, firmly and earnestly.",
"blomberg-s08-233": "Without any doubt very many useless prejudices are to be found; but whether our procedure is therefore already fair and lawful, merely on that account, and with the intention, because to another a prejudice is perhaps in a certain case useful, beneficial, or at least not harmful, that therefore in him the prejudice is not only not to be suppressed but even to be generated, produced, maintained, increased: this is another and quite different question. This is truly a kind of deceit and a breach of faith and honesty, to generate and sustain in others prejudices on the ground that prejudices are useful in this or that case.",
"blomberg-s08-234": "A prejudice considered in itself can indeed in itself produce some use, but notwithstanding, such methods as one makes use of in order to procure and produce use by means of them are not right, not permitted.",
"blomberg-s08-235": "Nevertheless it also always remains true that it would be an obvious impertinence if one wanted to deprive someone who has a prejudice that is either not wholly harmful to him, or even in a certain respect useful and beneficial, of it straightaway by a deceitful way, and thus to put him in unrest and confusion.",
"blomberg-s08-236": "But to strengthen a prejudice in someone is just as reprehensible; so, e.g., if one proceeds irresponsibly in the instruction of morals, it is just so constituted when, namely, in order to keep a person from one or another vice, one makes use of an often superstitious delusion, in order to hold him back from it and frighten him off. That is the so-called Fraus pia (pious fraud).",
"blomberg-s08-237": "Everything true, however, that is brought forth by means of one or another prejudice is formaliter (formally) false, although it can be, and is, materialiter (materially) correct. How wretched is the education that seeks to ground true moral rules in young people through prejudices and through errors.",
"blomberg-s08-238": "Many prejudices are therefore so constituted, and of such a kind, that if one were to venture to attack them, to want to eradicate and clear them away, they would nevertheless make the human mind all the more embittered, indeed would stun the same even to hearing and attending to true teachings and important dogmata (doctrines), which have great consequences for them.",
"blomberg-s08-239": "There really are quite comfortable (commode) prejudices, in which a human being finds himself quite well, and it would therefore be a great folly to tear from another such prejudices, which would be able to contribute so very much to his well-being, and often really do.",
"blomberg-s08-240": "It is otherwise, however, with harmful prejudices, which can have an influence not only on a single case or person, but quite often even on the welfare of a whole Republique (commonwealth). These one can, and indeed must, always endeavor to eradicate with the greatest diligence as far as possible, because one thereby is harmful to him who has this prejudice, and also serves others, indeed a great multitude of people, and is useful to them.",
"blomberg-s08-241": "We must therefore also not despise other human beings if they are perhaps, whether with or without their fault, very much filled with prejudices.",
"blomberg-s08-242": "The common man has and harbors very many prejudices, to which he also very often clings very stubbornly; but it is just as certain that the learned man, even the greatest, always has just as many of them, and still far more dangerous ones. He too sometimes holds to his Canones (canons) as much as anyone else, and does not depart from them at all, any more than the common man from his old proverbs, inherited from his forefathers and confirmed by long experience. Only the difference holds that the prejudices of a learned man are hidden under the name of canons, but those of the common man under the name of proverbs.",
"blomberg-s08-243": "The author here distinguishes the prejudices from one another. He calls some prejudices logical, just as if he had in mind to indicate thereby that there are other kinds of prejudices that are not logical.",
"blomberg-s08-244": "Otherwise the aesthetic is commonly contra distinguiret (distinguished in contrast) from the logical, and hence the prejudices will be, and can be divided,",
"blomberg-s08-245": "1.) into logical, and",
"blomberg-s08-246": "2.) into aesthetic prejudices.",
"blomberg-s08-247": "The former consist in this, and take place when our cognitions are contrary to the rules of the understanding and of reason; the latter, on the other hand, consist in this, that our cognitions are contrary to the rules of taste.",
"blomberg-s08-248": "Fashion is properly nothing other than a prejudice of taste, according to which one holds something to be beautiful or ugly through a prejudice. Fashion is therefore the source of aesthetic prejudices. Fashions bring it about that one represents to oneself that some inner beauty lies in this or that kind of dress or costume; one thus thinks, and also judges, according to the rules of taste.",
"blomberg-s08-249": "Imitation and habit are the greatest sources of aesthetic prejudices. A frequent approval of one or another object makes in the soul as it were an archetype, with which one is quite unable to compare others that look different from this once-established original, which stands as a pattern incapable of improvement.",
"blomberg-s08-250": "If everyone were to dress according to his own liking and taste, we would finally have the most excellent kinds of dress; but now the right taste in dress is truly lacking, and indeed because fashion rules therein, which everyone follows incessantly and constantly.",
"blomberg-s08-251": "On the whole, all fashion is more harmful than useful, for it is opposed to the rules of pure reason as well as even to the rules of taste. And he who is, or wants to be, fashionable in his judgments of taste, or even of reason, certainly has no reason at all. He shows thereby his very poor talents and his complete lack of all taste. Yet it is still far more tolerable to be fashionable in taste than to be fashionable in thinking, in judging, and in Sentiments (sentiments); to be fashionable in clothes is tolerable and often good. But to want to be fashionable in sentiments, that is the real logical prejudice. The prejudice of taste, however, rules not only in dress but even in style.",
"blomberg-s08-252": "Almost every century has a style, or a form of its own",
"blomberg-s08-253": "At one time a great pomp of inflated words rules in the manner of writing; at another, anxious contortions of wit; at another again, a quite shallow manner of writing, which one is pleased to call natural, because it is easy, costs little or no effort, and can be produced without art. At one time one loves again in all things such a manner of writing as one was properly accustomed to use in all letters. At another one loves again above all a heavy, dark, riddling and aenigmatic manner of writing, which one prefers to the light, complete and distinct, and which is supposed to betray a certain profundity and a deep-thinking learnedness in the authors, because one must guess for a long time and has as it were a riddle to solve, until one comes to guess what the author meant by it, to be able to discover the author's proper sense and understanding, and to find what the author thought thereby.",
"blomberg-s08-254": "All taste is very much corrupted by imitation, a fertile source of all prejudices, in that one borrows everything, and holds nothing to be a beauty that one might perhaps invent oneself and hit upon, except what others have already thought out and previously cognized, and have declared to be a beauty.",
"blomberg-s08-255": "If, therefore, everyone would endeavor not so much always to imitate, but rather to be an original himself, we would certainly soon behold the greatest geniuses, who would be sublime and great in judgments of taste.",
"blomberg-s08-256": "Just so there are again different kinds of prejudices in the various fine arts and sciences (Wißenschaften), e.g. in music, in poetry, in painting, in sculpture etc. Everywhere, in every taste, prejudices always rule, indeed even in moral taste.",
"blomberg-s08-257": "Habit and examples bring it about, and are therefore the causes why so many prejudices, so many fashions, rule in them.",
"blomberg-s08-258": "It has been observed that the English nation has far more, and greater, originals than the French and other nations. This, however, really comes from the freer and less constrained form of government. For there almost everyone is his own master, at least of his own genius. He thinks, judges and acts therefore naturally for the most part without being moved in any way by examples, or at least without conforming to them, following them, imitating them. Everyone there thinks and judges according to his own taste, and hence there are so few copies there.",
"blomberg-s08-259": "Whereas, on the other hand, countries and states in which a monarchical form of government has been introduced and rules are naturally unable to bring forth any such originals, or at least not so many for long, for here one conforms very much to the examples, which often become the norm in thinking, in acting and in judging; and indeed, as is usual, the magnates of the realm, the great ones of the realm, and at court for the most part the originals, are the patterns after which one is accustomed to seek to form oneself, and to imitate them.",
"blomberg-s08-260": "The author here further divides prejudices into the prejudices 1.) of too great trust, and 2.) of too great mistrust; but both kinds of prejudices are in fact one and the same, and inseparably bound and united with one another.",
"blomberg-s08-261": "The cause of the prejudices of too great trust, however, is properly nothing other than the desire to extend one's cognitions, but without acuteness and true judging of that with which one wants to enrich them; and diligent observation teaches that this takes place for the most part especially in youth, whereas old age, which already has more experience and cognitions than youth, has also already suffered more delusions, often reflects more and looks to see whether it is really worth it and worth the trouble to take pains to attain this or that cognition, and whether it is really correct or not. And by means of this thinking back and forth and often repeated doubt it often falls finally into the prejudice of too great mistrust, and for the most part it turns out so: the older we gradually become, the less credulous and the more mistrustful we then become.",
"blomberg-s08-262": "In the prejudice of too great mistrust, however, we grant certain persons a certain precedence over us; we must therefore ascribe to them more prudence, more learnedness (Gelahrtsamkeit) etc. than to ourselves, and so we choose them as originals after which we endeavor to form ourselves.",
"blomberg-s08-263": "Distinguished people are for the most part of such a kind that one is taken up with very many prejudices toward them, and with regard to their skills and qualities. One always holds them to be prudent, learned, exalted, noble-minded etc.; even clothing often makes one learned and prudent; a person in gold braid even awakens respect, and seems at once to possess more understanding than others, although very often nevertheless he sits in the greatest shadow of darkness.",
"blomberg-s08-264": "What a distinguished man says is often noticed far sooner than what a common or poor man brings forth, though the latter is very often wiser than the former. There is further a prejudice of too great a multitude, in which one assumes, and persuades oneself, that many persons are in a position to judge something more, and better, than few. This is the common and lowest prejudice of authority (Ansehen).",
"blomberg-s08-265": "Hence many a one is frightened when he is to give a public speech, address etc. for the first time before a large assembly. To speak before 2 or 3 persons he would perhaps not be frightened, but before some 1000 one is afraid. One judges, namely, that what finds approval with all must be true, and must really lie in the object itself, though this is often very false.",
"blomberg-s08-266": "The prejudice of too great a multitude arises for the most part from the prejudice of imitation. A great number of people, who all pay attention to me alone and carefully observe me alone, must necessarily have something venerable for me.",
"blomberg-s08-267": "With regard to morality, however, the prejudice of the multitude becomes very harmful, detrimental, indeed shameful; hence it also comes about that man, even in his actions that have an influence on morals, so gladly follows the great crowd; he hopes, when he does something evil, to hide as it were among it, and thus unseen to escape the eye of the eternal heavenly judge.",
"blomberg-s08-268": "From a great multitude an equal judgment can easily be produced; none has the spirit of imitation so much as a great multitude.",
"blomberg-s08-269": "If something is so constituted that it receives the general approval of the people all at once, as soon as it appears and at first sight, it is very often to be feared that it is not worth much; and a reasonable man ought, when a general and universal approval is bestowed on him all at once, not only not to be pleased, but to be much humbled. Books that, as soon as they have come to light from the press, are certainly for the most part very shallow.",
"blomberg-s08-270": "That which is good and in fact excellent, which brings forth correct but new truths, commonly always meets at the beginning with very great contradiction, and this lasts until, after closer examination and investigation, after inquiry into and discovery of its proper properties and of its difference from other cognitions, one also discovers in it its beauties and what is especially preeminent over other writings, and begins to esteem it highly.",
"blomberg-s08-271": "All prejudice of too great authority, however, can be suitably divided into three kinds. – – –",
"blomberg-s08-272": "namely 1st) the judgment of too great authority in which others stand with us.",
"blomberg-s08-273": "2nd) the prejudice of too great authority that we place in ourselves.",
"blomberg-s08-274": "3.) the prejudice of too great authority that a cognition has in us, because of some perfections that it carries with it, and from which one infers that still more perfections must be found in it.",
"blomberg-s08-275": "As for the first kind of prejudices, we are for the most part inclined to ascribe and entrust more to others than to ourselves. We believe that another knows better something that we do not know. This prejudice arises not only from the consciousness of our own weakness, for this is no ground for attributing more to others than to ourselves, but from a certain regard that we have for the whole human race, in that we judge that what we lack will nevertheless be found in others.",
"blomberg-s08-276": "Another prejudice arises from a certain laziness.",
"blomberg-s08-277": "Nothing is more convenient than to imitate and to draw one's cognitions from others. From this prejudice arise the various and manifold citations in books, in which one adduces the judgments of others and their opinions on this or that matter, and presents these citations as proofs.",
"blomberg-s08-278": "There is truly in this a certain kind of laboriousness, an industriousness, but no difficulty. One must work long, though without a certain exertion of the powers. This prejudice of too great authority that others have with us flatters, therefore, very much our comfort. One need not think for oneself at all, but can rely entirely on the genius, on the understanding of the other, and merely imitate others.",
"blomberg-s08-279": "Yet another kind of the prejudices of authority toward others arises from vanity. One thereby really subjects oneself, as it were, to others; one makes oneself thereby the slave of a great man, and of the understanding, the power of judgment, of another. This, however, often happens also only in order to be able to command others much, to have a great dominion over others who are still lower than we.",
"blomberg-s08-280": "Nowhere, however, does a greater bombast, nowhere a greater pride take place than in polyhistory. One exalts in it, namely, out of ambition and vanity, other great learned men, in order under this cloak to be exalted oneself, to be able to exalt oneself, and thus as it were to raise oneself far above the so-called learned rabble. One sets before the eye of the learned world, namely, a great and glorious learned man, and seeks to persuade all others firmly that despite all the diligence, labor and trouble they can possibly apply, they would still always act in vain, since they could never be able to equal this great man or come near him; indeed, since one regards the pronouncements of this great archetype as irrefutable and unimprovable oracles, one rejects outright all their opinions, merely because they contradict, or seem to contradict, the judgment of this great man. One frightens, or seeks as it were to frighten all others, never to want to strive to become equal to this learned man, as if it would be a vain undertaking to strive after it.",
"blomberg-s08-281": "Thus it comes about, e.g., that most proud people, who do not like to be subject to several and to obey, or who envy their fellow citizens some power or other, would most like to have in a state the unlimited form of government of a monarch, and also help to promote it, namely in order then to be equal to one another and not to have to fear any of their fellow citizens; and this is in fact the true origin of all monarchies. But there is really nothing more harmful to the human race than always to set up others as an unattainable example and, as it were, to take them as a pattern for imitation. Thereby one copies more the faults than the good qualities of the proposed original, because everything in the world is imperfect, and so these patterns also cannot be wholly excluded from it. Holy Scripture itself says: we are never to choose anyone else as a pattern than God, this being most perfect in all respects. But to conform and form oneself constantly after other human beings, who are just like us supremely imperfect, this brings about more harm than use.",
"blomberg-s08-282": "Such prejudices of authority and of imitation, however, are greatly grounded by a bad education and unskilled instruction, namely when, for the confirmation of one's judgments of the understanding, one adduces, cites, alleges not one's own reflection or experience but the judgments and opinions of others. And although this method, whether in an oral or a written presentation, is very pleasing and a witness to great reading, it nevertheless often grounds only the prejudice of authority.",
"blomberg-s08-283": "Of the prejudice of authority that one places in a great multitude of people we have already spoken above. It is to be presumed that what all people assume is universally valid. There lies in our nature already a particular inclination to communicate our opinion to others, as we have already indicated above, so that if someone were in a desert and had to stay there without any society of human beings, all his judgments would seem to him in vain and futile.",
"blomberg-s08-284": "This inclination does not arise at all from vanity, but from a particular and preeminent communicative constitution of human reason. When, namely, one judges alone and for oneself, one is never quite sure whether the judgment does not arise from a certain illusion that might impose something on us. Hence one is eager to test and examine it on the understanding of other human beings. One makes here as it were an experiment, and tries whether what we think is universally valid, whether others accept it, or whether it does not agree with reason. One whets, so to speak, as it were, those judgments that one has, i.e. whose inventor one is, on the sure touchstone of the understanding and the insights of other human beings.",
"blomberg-s08-285": "To this inclination of human reason to communicate its judgments to others, logical egoism is opposed, of which we have also already spoken something above, in that one believes, and persuades oneself entirely, that one needs in a judgment of the understanding absolutely no help from the judgment of others. It is indeed true that a stranger's judgment judges nothing in matters of the understanding; but it is not on that account superfluous, nor dispensable. The understanding of man is from instinct communicatio (communicative). If it is therefore communicative, it must also rightly be participatory, and concern itself also with what others judge of it.",
"blomberg-s08-286": "The prejudice of authority in which other people stand with us is, further, the prejudice of antiquity, or the prejudice of novelty, on which the liveliness of wit depends. The prejudice of antiquity is grounded on the high esteem for the old. What survives from the old down to our times always already has about it the appearance that it is good, for one infers: it would hardly have survived and come down to us if it were not good and worth nothing. Just as we also hold those buildings to be good which, when they are old, nevertheless still stand without having fallen into ruin, whereas others have fallen into ruin through the length of time.",
"blomberg-s08-287": "No judgment is more general than that the world gradually grows old. Everything in the world deteriorates: animals, horses etc. become worse when they have lived long, bodies, e.g. watches, become worse with time. And just this judgment one passes on the whole world, on the whole of nature in general: it deteriorates more and more, it ages gradually. Hence one admires old and yet healthy, robust people, and says: where does one find such a one today among us? Just so it goes with books, which remain over from among thousands of others that have been lost; one holds them for that reason, and not without all ground, to be preeminently good, because despite the usual decadence that tends to befall such writings they have nevertheless maintained themselves in esteem for so long a time.",
"blomberg-s08-288": "Young people incline for the most part to the prejudice of novelty, old age, however, to the prejudice of antiquity. “Just as youth for the most part tends to be generous, but old age miserly.” Yet it is indeed also true that the cause of these prejudices can often be the change of temperament.",
"blomberg-s08-289": "To the old belong diligence and industriousness.",
"blomberg-s08-290": "To the new, however, genius, liveliness, and wit without steadiness. We do indeed see the individuals of nature fall into ruin and perish, all works of art perish. Rome itself falls in, rocks split etc. – Man perishes, and dies at every age. Up to the age of 15 years half of mankind dies, in the 16½th none, in the 50th one among 100. Further, one holds that the perfections of the world itself in all things gradually decrease. Horace says: “our posterity will produce a still worse brood than we are.”",
"blomberg-s08-291": "but this prejudice is entirely incorrect. Only in more recent times has one reflected on this matter more and better than in ancient times.",
"blomberg-s08-292": "What nature brings forth has such a great constancy that nothing, no time, no age can alter it. If one takes out the time before the general Flood, then after it everything in the world is equally good, equally strong, procreation and the power of procreation just as good, just as possible, although indeed a single nation may have some advantage over another in this, to which the particular circumstances of climate, of means of life etc. must indeed contribute something.",
"blomberg-s08-293": "Of many books one can tell at the first moment, as they appear before the world, that they will not be valued and highly esteemed for long. These books are like the Papillons (butterflies), which appear with all splendor, are admired by everyone, but last no longer than over the summer, and soon after are forgotten. Other writings, however, which contain much thoroughness, are of such a kind that they are propagated from one age to the next and constantly remain in their authority.",
"blomberg-s08-294": "From this it naturally follows that one harbors a kind of high esteem toward them, and holds them to be valuable.",
"blomberg-s08-295": "When one considers how time sifts out everything bad in the sciences (Wißenschaften), it may well be said: how will it one day go with our present learnedness (Gelahrtsamkeit)?",
"blomberg-s08-296": "A living language always changes, a dead one never; all authors are inexhaustible sources of it. The German poet of today will certainly appear bad after 100 years. The dead languages have the advantage that they remain eternally beautiful and independent.",
"blomberg-s08-297": "Our present languages would perhaps never become dead, for only Holy Scripture and religion make a language dead. Thus the theologians are the true preservers of learnedness (Gelahrtsamkeit), and with them many languages would cease entirely.",
"blomberg-s08-298": "The corpus Juris is certainly the greatest and surest proof of human profundity. The discovery of the Pandects at Naples in the 11th Saeculo (century), however, is the best find that human beings could ever have obtained among books. On the whole, however, the ancients are always inimitable models in the manner of writing.",
"blomberg-s08-299": "If one considers the old world as a whole, and all individual creatures upon it, one easily finds that the longer the series of copies subordinated to one another, the greater again is the deviation from the original.",
"blomberg-s08-300": "The first creatures were very perfect, but through generation and propagation they have become more and more dissimilar to one another at least. Notwithstanding this, however, one finds that no change at all has taken place in the species and genera of creatures, but that everything is still constituted as it was at the creation of the world and of all things. It is therefore always a prejudice when one esteems the old so exceedingly.",
"blomberg-s08-301": "Moreover, the high esteem that we have for the old also arises from a certain kind of illusion of our understanding and of our reason.",
"blomberg-s08-302": "We always esteem learnedness (Gelahrtsamkeit) highly; even in ourselves we always feel a certain high regard, an Estime (esteem) toward ourselves, when we are learned.",
"blomberg-s08-303": "To the learning of the old belongs, and is required, learnedness (Gelahrtsamkeit). This is a further ground of the prejudice of antiquity. To the cognition of the old a great learnedness often belongs; hence it comes about that we hold the old in high regard, and indeed because we are able to see into it only through learnedness. This high esteem of the old, however, also often falls on old authors, because they have written something for the understanding of which learnedness is required. This is a kind of inner deceit. One aestimates (esteems), namely, the old and per consequentiam (by consequence) also the ancients.",
"blomberg-s08-304": "The language of the ancients itself is already a learnedness; from this it follows that what is written in such a language acquires already a certain kind of high esteem, because for the science (Wißenschaft) of the language alone a certain learnedness",
"blomberg-s08-305": "is required, without which one is not in a position to understand it.",
"blomberg-s08-306": "Hence the ancients are favoured in so many ways; one sometimes does not even notice their errors, indeed one is not even in a position to recognize whether there are errors in an old writing or not. Yet it seems altogether fair to esteem the ancients highly, full of gratitude, since we are after all only their pupils. We ourselves hope, after all, that one day we too shall be called the dear ancients by our descendants, and then promise ourselves from them the same esteem and the same thanks that we now pay to the ancients. The ancients are for us the original itself, and hence those who are exalted above all judging and above all criticism. What a prejudice, when an author is so constituted that his language is no longer criticized or judged at all, but is accepted as already perfectly beautiful in itself and as incapable of improvement.",
"blomberg-s08-307": "When one comes across twisted turns of phrase in a Cicero, Horatius etc. and other such authores classici (classical authors) once accepted as incapable of improvement, one will not say: Cicero etc. should not have spoken so, he could have avoided an error if he had expressed it somewhat differently etc., but rather one approves it, and we are permitted to speak in the same way, merely because Cicero etc. did it so perversely. Likewise the old poets have the Licentiam poeticam (poetic licence) on their side despite all their errors. Here one sees what prejudices arise from the authority which one has toward antiquity. Hence there are so many champions of antiquity who, as soon as something is invented in more recent times, show at once that the ancients already knew all of that as well; so that it is really nothing new, but only something sought out from the writings of the ancients and brought somewhat to light.",
"blomberg-s08-308": "Here, however, we must ask whether we do not have reason to pass a certain provisional judgment in favour of antiquity and of the ancients regarding their cognitions and insights.",
"blomberg-s08-309": "Answer. Certainly; the ancients definitely had the advantage over us that they at least had no models set before them, no originalia (originals), which they could have followed and were to copy. Hence they had to furnish originals themselves, and became so, whether they wanted to or not, since they had no other guidance than nature and their reason.",
"blomberg-s08-310": "Nowadays, however, through the far too great multitude of originalia, nothing but copies are produced, and many perhaps new originalia are stifled.",
"blomberg-s08-311": "The ancients had another advantage with regard to their thinking in that their realms were for the most part divided into very many small territories and republics, all of which were very free and did not have to sigh under the pressure of a monarchical and strict rule (for freedom in thinking originates from the free form of government, as we have already had occasion to remark above); this free condition, so very advantageous to the expansion of the arts and sciences, was found pre-eminently in the land of the ancient Greeks. Here one knew of no monarchy, but everything followed its own inclination and governed itself.",
"blomberg-s08-312": "This way of life, this form of state and of government naturally brought it about that among them there were found such great, inimitable and excellent geniuses and minds in the especially beautiful sciences, and in particular in painting, an Apelles etc., in eloquence a Demosthenes, in poetry, and showed themselves to the world.",
"blomberg-s08-313": "Even today we find that in those countries where the spirit of freedom reigns, much more, far more is invented, many more originalia arise, far fewer copies appear, than where a despotic monarchical form of government has been introduced.",
"blomberg-s08-314": "England furnishes us an example of this; here one still finds today the most and the best archetypes and models. Everyone there thinks for himself, speaks and writes for himself etc., without choosing a model and imitating it, wanting to follow it everywhere.",
"blomberg-s08-315": "Countries, on the other hand, where monarchs and individual princes rule despotically, and where one has to command over all, produce nothing but copies, and seldom originalia. The court is here for the most part the original, and everyone takes pains to imitate what goes on at court. The style of writing of the court is the style of writing of everyone else (just as one gladly wears the clothes that are usually worn at court). One sees this today, e.g., in France; there one seldom meets with an original, but for the most part nothing but copies. Everything there is fashionable, in clothes as well as in judgments and writings.",
"blomberg-s08-316": "The style of writing of the English authors is therefore also very easily distinguished from all others. One always says something other than what the other has said. The French, on the other hand, always write in one and the same style; one makes use of almost the same turns of phrase as the other. Hence it also comes about that one French author is not easily distinguished from another, and one very seldom meets among them with anything special, outstanding, new and unusual.",
"blomberg-s08-317": "Now we come to the prejudice of novelty. This is founded partly on the provisional judgment which we for the most part pass in favour of and to the advantage of the new. One has reason often to pass favourable judgments for the old, but also for the new.",
"blomberg-s08-318": "All those sciences which presuppose an extensive, historical and correct cognition become ever more perfect in more recent times, for it is well known that the more recent learned men take more and more pains to extend historical cognitions. In empirical cognitions the new always has an indisputable advantage over the old. In sciences, on the other hand, for which sustained diligence and perpetual effort are required, the new likewise has a more favourable judgment than the old, and not because it contradicts the old, but because it often adds still more to it. But if one esteems the new because it contradicts the old, this is truly a very harmful prejudice. Partly, however, the prejudice of novelty is also founded on a certain illusion of our understanding, our reason and our imagination. The old man loves the old, a young person the new. Age makes a person very inclined to defend antiquity, youth in turn to speak in favour of novelty. If youth owes its cognition and all science to the ancients, and has merely learned it from them but not itself discovered it, then it has no merit of its own, and is therefore also very much inclined to invent new sciences and to add more to the ancients.",
"blomberg-s08-319": "Whole nations are often very much inclined toward the old, and others again toward the new. The Spaniard and the Portuguese love the old, the Frenchman on the other hand the new. Sluggish, slow, melancholic and reflective temperaments love the old very much; lively and cheerful ones, on the other hand, love the new. The former are enemies, the latter friends of new fashions. The Frenchman is fashionable in clothes, in systems, in medicaments etc. The Frenchman is the first author and founder of all and every fashion, and also the only one who will always remain so. He alone has the necessary and requisite flightiness and lightness for it. The French are therefore too much given to the prejudice of novelty, which arises",
"blomberg-s08-320": "1.) from the fact that the new contradicts the old, or 2ndly from the fact that one loves the new because it, first, retains the old, but also, second, adds something new to it.",
"blomberg-s08-321": "Inclination is also a great source of favourable prejudice. To this belongs the insinuation, e.g., of outward bearing, of the mien, of the name, e.g. in books the title. “A good title sells its book”, and indeed, according to the present fashion, a French, light, légère, impertinent title, e.g. when Beaumelle brings out a book with the title: Mes Pensées etc. Should we not esteem the ancient inhabitants of Greece highly, since in this favourable climate all learnedness flourished, as we have said above?",
"blomberg-s08-322": "A kind of recommendation, however, which drives one toward esteem for someone, also originates from this: when we have despised someone, and then discover and become aware of something unexpectedly great, a fine insight, a genius that exceeds all our expectation, then we esteem him all the higher for that, we admire him all the more, because we had despised him beforehand. So it is, e.g., when we hear a woman, of whose insights and skill we have only very poor concepts, speak with great insight, wit etc.; we admire her all the more, and our contempt or disdain is soon turned into reverence. Thus, e.g., we learn to esteem and revere very highly the great sage Epicurus, of whom we perhaps at first formed poor concepts, when we examine his conduct, his speeches, his judgments, his knowledge more closely. etc. Whoever is already eager to regard everything from its worst side, and takes pains to seek out everywhere faults rather than good qualities, has certainly a very malicious heart.",
"blomberg-s08-323": "One always reads with vexation and aversion such books in which the fame and honour, together with the good qualities, of the human race is undermined. It is already proper to human nature, uncorrupted by any evil heart, always to assume and presume the best in every writing, in every action, and one actually always profits by it rather than suffers harm from it.",
"blomberg-s08-324": "Some time ago a dispute arose among some French learned men; some raised up and defended the old, others in turn the new. The former held the old to be an original, and the new only for often corrupted copies. The latter, among whom Fontenelle also distinguished himself, maintained that the new can often surpass the old very much. The ancients may often be in a position to bring forth products superior to those of the moderns, but often the new can also surpass the old by far.",
"blomberg-s08-325": "In eloquence, poetry, sculpture, painting, in all the fine arts and sciences, then, the ancients have surpassed us. Whether more recent times could not also bring forth better products than the ancients brought forth, however, is a quite different question. Eloquence, e.g., was so cultivated among the ancients for this reason, that everyone was permitted to step forward before a people which was free, and indeed often did step forward and speak.",
"blomberg-s08-326": "Here, therefore, everyone who was minded to deliver a public address before such a public assembly had of necessity to apply all diligence and effort in order to attain his end, to move the people, and to win the approval of his hearers.",
"blomberg-s08-327": "Question. Would it ever be possible to destroy and root out all and every prejudice in man?",
"blomberg-s08-328": "Answer. It seems almost impossible. Man is already accustomed to having springs of action in his soul which he cannot resist, and which run ahead of the judgments of the understanding, and in this way form a prejudice.",
"blomberg-s08-329": "True, man can learn to distinguish what arises from springs of action and what from the understanding; that is really already a great deal, and he can then of course no longer be deceived and cheated as much, nor as often, as hitherto, although the propensity to it is of course still there every moment; but to rid himself of it entirely and completely, to get free of it, this seems impossible, and would never work. Nevertheless, every and any pious fraud, in common life in morals as well as here in logic in the application of the powers of the understanding, is quite unpardonable, i.e. the intention and the effort to want to bring forth something true with the help of prejudices; this philosophy always pursues.",
"blomberg-s08-330": "A proposition is no prejudice, be it as false as it may. For a prejudice is nothing other than the mere desire to want to judge, yet without the requisite acuteness or reflection.",
"blomberg-s08-331": "How, then, do our prejudices arise?",
"blomberg-s08-332": "The propensity of the mind to judge according to authority, according to inclination, according to custom, according to fashion, that is prejudice. Proverbs are for the most part false, for many of them are of such a kind that one can use them indifferently.",
"blomberg-s08-333": "Children learn Latin at school for the most part with great difficulty. Whence does this come? From the fact that one demands of them, and wants them to be able to speak pure Latin when they have hardly mastered the first principles of this language and the first grammatical rules. All the prejudices of which we have hitherto spoken thus arise either",
"blomberg-s08-334": "1.) from a bad upbringing, or",
"blomberg-s08-335": "2.) from examples and their imitation.",
"blomberg-s08-336": "The prejudice of too great a confidence which one places in oneself is nothing other than egoism (Egoisterey). Egoism, however, is vel cosmologicus (either cosmological), when one holds that there is no other thinking being, no world at all outside me, vel Logicus (or logical), when one holds that one alone judges rationally, but that no other is in a position to judge anything or to see it better. This latter, namely logical egoism, seems at the outset really to be permitted. One certainly has no need then to consult other judgments, when one cognizes something correctly oneself; one need not seek to bring about an agreement of others with the cognition which one has oneself. But on closer examination one finds that one cannot be certain whether one has judged rightly or not if one does not compare one's judgments with the judgments of others, and test them against the understanding of others. For a cognition is not correct when it agrees with my private understanding, but when it agrees with the universal laws of the understanding of all human beings. Hence every human being, as we have already shown above, has an inclination to communicate his judgments to others",
"blomberg-s08-337": "and to share them. This is the true friendship of sentiment. The logical egoist therefore deprives himself of a great and exceedingly important advantage, also very conducive and necessary to the enrichment and improvement of his understanding, namely of being able to distinguish the true from the false correctly per Communicationem judiciorum, et mutuam dijudicationem eorum invicem (through the communication of judgments and their mutual appraisal of one another). Logical egoism, however, also sometimes arises from a certain kind of humility and faintheartedness, when one trusts oneself and one's insights far too little, and believes that one's judgments are not worthy of becoming known to others in the whole learned world.",
"blomberg-s08-338": "Opposed to logical egoism, however, is yet another prejudice: namely when one builds all one's judgments on the reason of others, judges nothing oneself, but merely imitates, in that one raises others above oneself and trusts oneself in nothing. These are, to put it briefly, the prejudices of the lazy.",
"blomberg-s08-339": "There are nevertheless certain arts and sciences which are of such a kind that they can, indeed must, be learned through mere imitation. To these belong, e.g., each and every handicraft, likewise mathematics, in which one need only present certain propositions to someone and duly demonstrate and prove them, which he grasps into memory, and thereby is in fact able to become a real mathematicus. Other sciences, on the other hand, are again of such a kind that they cannot be learned by imitation at all, but genius is required for learning them. To these belongs, e.g., the whole of philosophy. One may be quite sure that one will never be able to become a true philosopher [619a] as long as one does nothing more than imitate the philosophers of former times, leaf through their writings one time or another or read them through, accept as true what they have said, and, so to speak, quite forget oneself in the process; if one therefore does not trust oneself in the least, nor pass any judgments oneself, but swears in verba magistri (by the words of the master).",
"blomberg-s08-340": "Nothing is more harmful than to imitate in philosophy. Nothing is more wretched and ruinous for the understanding than, e.g., to take up a Wolff or a Crusius or others, to learn and impress on oneself their definitiones strictissime (most strictly) and word for word by heart, and to esteem them as stars of the first magnitude and oneself as nothing; rather one must learn to think for oneself, to judge for oneself, to reflect on objects for oneself, and in order to be able to become and be a philosopher, to learn to philosophize. Worldly wisdom, however, cannot be learned in the least through books, but only through one's own reflection and one's own meditation.",
"blomberg-s08-341": "We have, however, two kinds of prejudices which relate to learnedness: namely a judgment in favour of, and again a prejudice against learnedness, and in favour of common sound understanding.",
"blomberg-s08-342": "Learned men, and also other common people, have a prejudice in favour of learnedness. They often accept everything that has been said by learned people and also taught. The moralists, on the other hand, have a prejudice in favour of common and sound understanding, which praejudicium (prejudice) is also peculiar to many common people. This latter prejudice comes from the fact that nobody denies himself sound understanding. For there has never yet been a human being who believed that he had no Sens commun, Sensum communem (common sense), common sound understanding. That one has a poor, deficient memory, one readily and willingly admits. Another admits in turn that he has no dexterity in comprehending, no wit, no witty ideas, no genius for this or that single science, no taste for poetry. But everyone believes that he possesses the Sensum communem. This no human being will so easily deny himself.",
"blomberg-s08-343": "Since, now, the fewest of those who have a sound understanding are learned people, and it is nevertheless a well-known but true proverb: ut ars non habeat osorem, nisi imperitum (that art have no hater save the ignorant), it is certainly not at all to be wondered at if it very easily results from this that learnedness is by very many, whether from ignorance or from envy, if not despised, yet held in low esteem, and common sound understanding, on the other hand, is infinitely exalted.",
"blomberg-s08-344": "The author here adduces further the prejudice of the accepted system. In almost all parts of learnedness the prejudice of unity takes place.",
"blomberg-s08-345": "A system is distinguished by nothing other than that there is a unity in it. The human understanding, however, is so constituted that it approves everything in which a unity can be found. And from this there arises, of course, the prejudice in favour of unity.",
"blomberg-s08-346": "We always believe that we have made everything perfect, or at least as perfect as possible, if we have been able to bring it about that we have reduced it to a unity.",
"blomberg-s08-347": "Likewise unity also has, in a certain way, something ingratiating and pleasing in that it seems to contain in itself an economy of the powers of our understanding.",
"blomberg-s08-348": "Furthermore, where unity is to be met with, there one also necessarily finds order.",
"blomberg-s08-349": "Many beginners also have a certain prejudice in favour of the excellence and pre-eminence of a thing which they have learned with much diligence and labour, and which has cost them much effort and many a bitter sweat. (So to speak, e.g., someone had terribly much labour in learning the French language and had to apply effort. But he was in earnest about learning it, and finally also attained his end; so he has a great prejudice in favour of the inner worth of this thing.)",
"blomberg-s08-350": "The prejudice of lazy confidence arises from a certain kind of gross ignorance and sluggishness in attaining to this or that cognition, and from a contempt for this cognition arising from it, but very ill applied and worthy of contempt, merely because one cannot become a partaker of it. So, e.g., like the fox in the fable, who despises the grapes, which he cannot snatch despite all his leaps and effort, and calls them sour.",
"blomberg-s08-351": "In almost all parts of learnedness the prejudice of unity takes place.",
"blomberg-s08-352": "When very many properties and determinations of a thing are to be derived from one ground, one is commonly accustomed for the most part to pass the provisional judgment and to have the presumption that all the remaining determinations of the same thing will probably be derivable from it as well, and that this one ground which one has of this thing will therefore be nearly the true and fully sufficient ground of this whole thing. One namely sometimes assumes a ground, and then infers from the consequences that this is the only true ground, although this is very seldom fully certain.",
"blomberg-s08-353": "E.g., when one entertains a presumption or two that this or that person is a thief, one often infers merely from the consequences whether this presumption may well be true or not, but false. If one finds, e.g., that this or that person connected with us at first had and possessed nothing at all, but afterwards suddenly squandered a great deal of money, and that this happens at the same time as this or that money has gone missing from me and has presumably been stolen, then one believes with certainty that he must be the thief of our money, although this presumption can very often be false and incorrect, indeed even insulting and sinful. From this provisional judgment arises the prejudice of unity, namely when one finds a ground of a thing which has very many consequences and determinations, one derives all the remaining consequences and determinations from it. So it went, to give an example of this, e.g., quite at the beginning with electricity. For at that time one explained each and every thing at all wondrous from it; indeed there were even some fine spirits who went so far that they even wanted to assert that electricity had an influence on religion, in that they wanted, e.g., to endeavour to explain even the healing of the woman with the issue of blood by Christ from the powers of electricity.",
"blomberg-s08-354": "In general, whoever finds, or at least believes he finds, in one ground the source of all wisdom and cognition is always very inclined to ascribe still more to it than is really to be found in it. Another believes he has sound understanding and yet also wants to amuse and delight himself; but he is far more inclined to satisfy his inclinations as much as possible, far rather than to take the path of reflection and labour, and in this way one often falsely takes an understanding which is neither exercised nor cultivated for the merely common understanding. For this understanding one can with right, in a good sense, call a plain understanding (schlechten Verstand). For although one now connects with this expression a bad concept and a harmful interpretation, it is nevertheless well known that the dear ancients had the proverb: schlecht und recht (plain and right), and thereby good, and took the word schlecht here in a very good sense.",
"blomberg-s08-355": "All and every such prejudice, of which has been spoken hitherto, can however be divided",
"blomberg-s08-356": "“1) into prejudices of pride",
"blomberg-s08-357": "“2) into prejudices of humility, and of too great",
"blomberg-s08-358": "“timidity.",
"blomberg-s08-359": "The former, the prejudices of pride, arise when one ascribes far too much to one's insights, cognitions and judgments.",
"blomberg-s08-360": "The prejudices of humility and of timidity, however, arise from the fact that one ascribes far too little to one's judgments in general, or to the powers of the understanding.",
"blomberg-s08-361": "Often there arises in us even a prejudice against a whole science as well, and indeed from the fact that it has perhaps often erred, and we have exactly noticed and taken note of its errors. So it has gone, e.g., with metaphysica, and if it goes so with many sciences, that they thus often err, then there arises from this a kind of scepticism, doubt-mania, which could often spread over all sciences. This, however, comes from a weak understanding, which does not want to be laughed at, although it is not itself in a position to judge the cognitions and sciences. Such people commonly believe little or nothing at all. And this prejudice of mistrust, which originates from experience, arises, alas, only too often among human beings.",
"blomberg-s08-362": "Fashions are nothing other than excitations of vanity or recommendations of the use of a certain vanity, and arise therefore through the choice of eminent, great and distinguished men. So it goes, e.g., for the most part with style, with music, with architecture.",
"blomberg-s08-363": "A matter of vanity is that which rests not on true honour but merely on the effort to please, i.e. on fashion.",
"blomberg-s08-364": "Much, e.g., which is now really counted as permanently beautiful in our manner of writing is nevertheless nothing other than the fashion of our time. Very many learned men and fine-thinking spirits are, on the whole, really often more harmful than useful to the learned world.",
"blomberg-s08-365": "A Young, Klopstock, Gleim etc., e.g., have really corrupted a multitude of weak minds.",
"blomberg-s08-366": "The ignorant man for the most part imitates the faults, but not the beauties, of the original he has before him, in order only to become like a person who is capable of causing so much stir through this or that book, poem etc. So it would be, e.g., if some man or woman seeks to imitate the lisping of a beautiful person, and thereby becomes ridiculous.",
"blomberg-s08-367": "Sometimes a fault helps the wit, the talents etc. of a person, which are otherwise not very considerable, to shine forth and shimmer nonetheless.",
"blomberg-s08-368": "Fashion introduces clothes, manners, indeed often even morals (Sitten), and in a certain way it is almost good to follow fashion, if only its customs do not conflict with the eternal and unchangeable laws of morality, for by its help human beings are, as it were, in a certain way better united and made to agree.",
"blomberg-s08-369": "The prejudice of shallowness, further, is the same prejudice of which we have already spoken above, namely the prejudice in favour of sound understanding. For this is shallow in the object of learnedness.",
"blomberg-s08-370": "Voltaire is certainly one of the greatest defenders of shallowness. He seeks namely to derive almost everything merely from sound reason, and his judgment also sometimes has no sufficient ground.",
"blomberg-s08-371": "Sound reason is not so much to be rejected as one thinks; it is often really the source of many cognitions.",
"blomberg-s08-372": "Hence it is, on the contrary, again a prejudice if one rejects the Sensum Communem.",
"blomberg-s08-373": "There is, moreover, a certain prejudice against sound human reason, and also a prejudice in favour of sound human reason. In the former prejudice namely one believes nothing at all, in the other case, however, that one can see everything by means of human reason. In the former case, then, one trusts sound human reason too little, in the other case, however, one trusts it and ascribes to sound reason too much.",
"blomberg-s08-374": "One must by no means despise the human race, hold all its prejudices worthy of nothing and want to despise them, but leave to it its due advantages. It is true that one cannot see much through reason (namely through common sound human reason); nevertheless it is not on that account to be rejected, but always has its worth.",
"blomberg-s08-375": "At the end of this paragraph the author further speaks of the prejudice against antiquity, and also of the prejudice against novelty; but both of these prejudices have already been spoken of above.",
"blomberg-s08-376": "The prejudice against the old, namely, is always connected with the prejudice in favour of the new, and again every prejudice against the new is, on the contrary, always connected and inseparably united with a prejudice in favour of the old; and the one kind of prejudices cannot be thought without the other. For whoever loves the old very much cannot esteem the new highly et vice versa (and vice versa).",
"blomberg-s08-377": "“Every cognition from merely insufficient grounds is uncertain.” If a cognition is cognized from merely insufficient grounds of truth, so that I see far more grounds for than against the thing, then it is plausible (scheinbar). If, however, there are finally more grounds for than against the cognitions, or at least more than half of the grounds are for the cognitions (whether I now see these grounds or not), then it is probable.",
"blomberg-s08-378": "Plausibility (Scheinbarkeit) rests merely on the subject.",
"blomberg-s08-379": "Probability on the object, so that through it I really cognize more grounds for the thing than grounds for the assertion of the opposite of the thing are possible. In plausibility an error is really to be met with, and indeed in the form that one is not conscious of the insufficient grounds. The first rule is therefore: in all cognitions, even in true ones, seek first of all to become conscious of the uncertainty that arises, and could ever arise, from the insufficient grounds; one should therefore place oneself, as it were, in the situation of the opponent, the doubter, who believes he has sufficient grounds to assert the opposite of these cognitions; for otherwise one will really almost always, for the most part, err and deceive oneself.",
"blomberg-s08-380": "One can never know whether one cognizes more grounds for than against the thing, and cannot learn to see this before one has a cognition of the sufficient ground.",
"blomberg-s08-381": "Therefore in all cognitions one must first make oneself acquainted with the sufficient ground, or want to know and cognize how many grounds are actually required for the complete certainty of a thing, so that one can duly distinguish whether something is plausible or probable.",
"blomberg-s08-382": "If one does not cognize the sufficient ground of a thing, one also cannot determine how many grounds for the truth are necessary even to make the cognition probable at first.",
"blomberg-s08-383": "If I subtract the grounds of holding-to-be-true from all possible ones that are necessary and required for certainty, and hold this properly against one another and weigh it, then the remaining ones are the grounds of the opposite. Hence I can quite easily see which grounds, whether the grounds for or the grounds against the cognition, are preponderant. Plausibility can change very much, for its weight is grounded in the subject, probability, however, always remains unchanged the same, and indeed merely because it is grounded in the object.",
"blomberg-s08-384": "The manner of writing (Schreib Art) is naturally very changeable, and especially because one can cognize today more grounds for and tomorrow again more grounds against this thing, according as one directs one's thoughts variously toward this thing, and investigates and reflects more on this or that, and takes pains to seek it out. The ground of assent is always at least the beginning of the sufficient ground.",
"blomberg-s08-385": "It is nevertheless a ground, even if it is not complete. An insufficient ground is quite wrongly, indeed even erroneously, held to be no ground at all; it is nevertheless material for further investigation and inquiry. A half-ounce is always a ground for lifting a pound, even though it is not sufficient, for several half-ounces taken together make up a pound.",
"blomberg-s08-386": "All our conviction and assurance of any cognition must of necessity pass through all such elements. First we merely presume that something is true. Incompleteness is after all the beginning in each and every thing, whatever they may be. Through inquiry and untiring attention we make something complete.",
"blomberg-s08-387": "True sagacitas (sagacity) is always, as it were, a scouting, a scent of the truth already in advance. It is one of the foremost talents of good minds and of reflection. So it goes, e.g., with the discoveries of the world.",
"blomberg-s08-388": "Grounds of presumption have often been in these cases causes of inquiry and causes of discoveries. Thus the new world etc. was discovered, and thus much more can still be discovered. If we have before us some such insufficient grounds of truth, then we have either a plausibility (and this is only the lowest degree), the Probabilitas (probability), or probability (Wahrscheinlichkeit), the Verisimilitudo (verisimilitude), as a greater degree. The sufficient ground of holding-to-be-true, the unity, is that through which a thing is posited.",
"blomberg-s08-389": "No ground can be more than sufficient, for otherwise it is no ground at all, just as nothing can be more than true, more than false, more than good, more than evil, for otherwise it is nothing.",
"blomberg-s08-390": "The sufficient ground already comprehends everything in itself, the concept of everything, however, also contains at the same time a unity. But more than everything I can never say of a thing. Nothing is more than true. Nothing is all-too-true. Nothing is too certain. Nothing is more than sufficient, as one nevertheless often falsely expresses oneself in common life. The insufficient ground of holding-to-be-true produces plausibility.",
"blomberg-s08-391": "Probability is a fraction in which the sufficient ground of truth is the denominator, but the insufficient grounds of holding-to-be-true which I have are the numerator.",
"blomberg-s08-392": "One can really calculate mathematically the degree of probability or the degree of improbability of one or another empirical matter; so, e.g., in each and every game, lotteries, the dying of human beings according to the number of years, and many other augmenta (increases), such as changes of the world.",
"blomberg-s08-393": "With our philosophical truths we cognize nothing other than a plausibility of this or that thing.",
"blomberg-s08-394": "We have certain grounds for the truth of the thing, and cognize fewer for its opposite, since there is nobody who is in a position to adduce to us more grounds against than for the truth of the thing; our cognition is therefore nothing more than plausible.",
"blomberg-s08-395": "Formerly one held the moon to be only a small luminous body which existed solely and alone for the sake of our earth. Afterwards, however, one gradually began to measure the size of the moon, and one soon found that it was somewhat smaller than our earth, that mountains and valleys are to be found in it. Again, some astronomers even conjectured that it has inhabitants, others, however, that it has none.",
"blomberg-s08-396": "This cognition of the moon was therefore very changeable, and for that very reason not even probable, but only plausible, for what is otherwise probable cannot be changeable at all, but always remains one and the same, seeing that all probability is not subjective but objective.",
"blomberg-s08-397": "All plausible judgments, on the other hand, change daily, according as one cognizes more or fewer grounds for and against the thing. But what is probable is probable for all eternity, so to speak. For the grounds of probability are indeed still insufficient, but nevertheless already greater than possible grounds of the opposite.",
"blomberg-s08-398": "In order, however, to know whether the grounds for the truth of the thing are greater than the grounds of the opposite, or whether the grounds for the cognition make up more than half of the sufficient ground, one must first of all always take pains to obtain a solid and honest cognition of the sufficient ground, as has already been remarked above. Mathematics alone is that science which is of such a kind that in it we can cognize the sufficient ground of a thing. In philosophy, however, this is not feasible at all; hence it comes about that all our cognitions in philosophy which are not indubitably certain can never be probable, but only ever plausible. For in probability we judge not what is actually the case, but only what is possible under certain conditions.",
"blomberg-s08-399": "This possibility, however, remains always unchangeable under one and the same conditions.",
"blomberg-s08-400": "By means of probability, then, we properly cognize the degrees of possibility; if these degrees are great, a great probability takes place, if they are small, however, a small probability takes place.",
"blomberg-s08-401": "A cognition is doubtful, taken subjectively, when the grounds for the thing which I cognize are equal to the grounds against the thing, i.e. to the grounds of the opposite. Objectively, however, something is again doubtful when the grounds for the possibility of a thing, or of a matter, which I cognize, are quite equal to the grounds of the opposite of this matter, and thus there are as many grounds for as against the matter.",
"blomberg-s08-402": "Something can be subjectively practically certain, although objectively it contributes only very little to the sufficient ground.",
"blomberg-s08-403": "As regards certainty, there are three particular and quite different kinds of it, namely",
"blomberg-s08-404": "1stly) the logical",
"blomberg-s08-405": "2ndly) the practical",
"blomberg-s08-406": "3) the aesthetic certainty.",
"blomberg-s08-407": "Logical certainty is the certainty according to the rules of the understanding and of reason, and thus an objective certainty, which is grounded in the thing itself.",
"blomberg-s08-408": "Aesthetic certainty is a certainty according to the rules of taste.",
"blomberg-s08-409": "Practical certainty, finally, is a certainty according to the rules of the free will.",
"blomberg-s08-410": "Someone is practically certain when the grounds of holding-to-be-true of a cognition which he has are sufficient to determine him to actions; but if he nevertheless does not want to act, then these grounds are still far from sufficient to make him logically certain. For practical certainty really very little cognition is needed in many cases, in many cases, however, far more is again required for moral or practical certainty than for logical. E.g. when it comes to condemning someone, to sentencing him to death etc.",
"blomberg-s08-411": "One can be aesthetically certain when one holds the opposite of a thing or cognition which one has to be impossible, merely because it does not please him. So, e.g., when his cognition is to believe in a divine being, eternal government, a future world, reward of good and punishment of evil actions etc. The certainty rests here merely on feeling; according as something brings about pleasure or displeasure in someone, so he accepts something or rejects it.",
"blomberg-s08-412": "The human mind is in this way really subject to very many illusions and deceptions, merely because, often, since something pleases us we hold it to be certain, and merely because something displeases or vexes us we hold it to be uncertain. This certainty, or uncertainty, however, is not objective, but rather subjective. It can even often keep us from honestly seeking out or investigating the opposite of a thing and the grounds for it",
"blomberg-s08-413": "We often find more comfort in holding something to be true, since otherwise we would have to inconvenience ourselves if we wanted to take the trouble of investigating the contrarium (opposite) of the thing. We hold the opposite to be impossible merely because we have no desire to want to investigate it.",
"blomberg-s08-414": "Practical certainty, finally, is a certainty according to the rules of prudence and of morality. The latter, however, is a moral certainty. There have been some who believed that the moral is far less than the practical. So, e.g., one says: it is morally certain that there are inhabitants in the moon etc.; but that does not connect in the least with my actions. If something is certainly already practically certain, it is on that account often still far from morally certain, and if something is morally certain, it is nevertheless often not yet logically certain. The possibility of a thing can produce in us a practical certainty, e.g. when someone merely believes that it is possible there is a God, this thought must nevertheless necessarily already have an influence on his life, just as if he were fully assured and convinced of this cognition.",
"blomberg-s08-415": "The plausibility of a thing can likewise produce a greater degree of certainty.",
"blomberg-s08-416": "In the practical sense something can be fully certain, even if in the logical sense it is only plausible, or at most probable.",
"blomberg-s08-417": "The poet can become certain of one or another thing only morally. Histories are of such a kind that they are only practically certain. Logical certainty is very often lacking, and one can easily take pains to find grounds for the opposite and to refute historical accounts.",
"blomberg-s08-418": "One can easily prove that the historian has not actually narrated true histories, but only fables and fictiones (fictions). There are also really many such people who busy themselves with weakening the complete certainty of history, which is also no art, but easily done, because that which is necessary and sufficient for practical certainty is not sufficient for speculation and logical certainty. A practical certainty is that which, with regard to certain actions, brings about just the same as a complete certainty. E.g., if something of value vanishes in my house, and my servant happens to stay away at just that time, then my practical certainty is there that this missing servant has purloined what is missing to me, although there is as yet no logical certainty at all, and the servant may have gone away, gotten away, for quite different reasons and in another way.",
"blomberg-s08-419": "Practical certainty, however, is twofold",
"blomberg-s08-420": "1.) the pragmatic and 2.) the moral certainty.",
"blomberg-s08-421": "If a cognition, probable in itself, is just as secure a ground of actions according to the rules of prudence as if it were fully certain, then this is a pragmatic certainty.",
"blomberg-s08-422": "If, however, a cognition, probable in itself, is just as secure a ground of actions according to the rules of morality as if it were fully certain, then this is a moral certainty (Weisheit [sic]).",
"blomberg-s08-423": "Even what is logically improbable can nevertheless be morally certain, e.g. when I demand money from someone, and I do not know whether I have already received it or not, and the other, my supposed debtor, just as little knows whether he has already paid it to me or not: then according to morals I can demand nothing more from him, although it is logically uncertain whether something has already been paid or not. A logical certainty of the existence of God can also be attained, although this is very difficult. To moral certainty, however, all human beings can attain even without great logical speculationes (speculations). If one only sharpens the moral judgments in someone, one can thereby easily bring him to conviction of the existence of God.",
"blomberg-s08-424": "A logical certainty of a future life is very difficult to attain, but one can indeed attain a moral certainty of it, if one considers that here on earth happiness is not always a consequence of good conduct, and that therefore another world is to be hoped for, in which this will take place.",
"blomberg-s08-425": "Of moral certainty, or uncertainty, there must be spoken and dealt with at greater length in morals itself.",
"blomberg-s08-426": "Most, and almost all, authores do not know moral certainty at all, but hold it to be any probability whatever, e.g. It is uncertain whether there are inhabitants in the moon, but it is nevertheless always somewhat probable. Some accept this as a moral judgment, but this does not hold, for such a judicium (judgment) has no influence at all on conduct. It is a logical probability, and mere speculation.",
"blomberg-s08-427": "Nothing is a moral judgment except what has a relation to my actions. If someone, therefore, is to and must act, and the grounds are merely probable, then the grounds which have the greatest probability constitute the practical certainty.",
"blomberg-s08-428": "Every ground of the opposite of a thing, of which one is conscious, is called a doubt. Every hindrance of holding-to-be-true is a logical difficulty, and this is",
"blomberg-s08-429": "1st objective, i.e. doubt.",
"blomberg-s08-430": "2nd subjective, i.e. scruple.",
"blomberg-s08-431": "The latter, that is, the subjective difficulty, is properly the incomprehensibility, where I do not reject a cognition but notice that I cannot come to terms with it. This subjective hindrance of the understanding is a fine task for a philosopher who wants to study human beings. The study of scruples is of great importance.",
"blomberg-s08-432": "The study of doubts is somewhat easier; to meet with scruples in ourselves, before we can say that we cannot comprehend a cognition, is very difficult. Scruples hinder a human being in holding-to-be-true, and can be resolved neither by the person who has the scruple nor by another.",
"blomberg-s08-433": "But scruples can become doubts [672a] when one becomes aware of them, and this too is highly necessary.",
"blomberg-s08-434": "One transforms a scruple into a doubt by considering the cognition that is accompanied by the scruple in all sorts of different relations and under different circumstances, whereupon one will at once, without any trouble, find out and notice which is the side on which we cannot yet be fully decided, and which on the other hand is the side where no scruple remains any more.",
"blomberg-s08-435": "Scruples in themselves, then, are not at all to be despised, or directly censured and rejected.",
"blomberg-s08-436": "But there are subjective scruples, which lie in the person who has one cognition or another, and these one must not heed. The objective scruples, however, which lie in the cognition itself, one must seek to make clear. If someone presents to me nothing but plausible propositions, I have a scruple and do not yet give him my assent at once. E.g. I consider the proposition that all moral judgments are supposed to lie in a special feeling and sensation; I give it my assent, yet a scruple still remains for me; I cannot refute the proposition, and nevertheless I cannot trust the cognition.",
"blomberg-s08-437": "If I consider the scruple more closely and think the matter over better, a doubt arises from it. I see that if I accept this proposition as true, it follows necessarily that whoever does not have this feeling and this sensation must be unable to pass moral judgments, which yet seldom or even never occurs.",
"blomberg-s08-438": "But from this doubt something else must necessarily arise in turn, namely that I will then make various objections against the truth of the proposition; and these objections are finally followed by a complete refutation from certain grounds, which is otherwise also called merely an answer.",
"blomberg-s08-439": "If I refute the doubt from grounds that only one person holds and accepts as correct, sufficient and adequate, then these grounds, like this answer, hold only for a single subject; this is then a so-called Responsio, et Refutatio ad hominem (answer and refutation addressed to the person).",
"blomberg-s08-440": "This already happens with a kind of reluctance, and happens merely in order to get rid of one's opponent as soon as possible. But if, on the contrary, I refute the doubt from grounds that hold for everyone, hence are universally valid, and also so convincing and assuring that no one is any longer able to object anything against them, then this is a real categorical refutation, Refutatio Categorica, and this occurs to the end of really bringing the truth to light and removing false cognitions out of the way as far as possible.",
"blomberg-s08-441": "Scruples often remain forever in a cognition and cannot be removed.",
"blomberg-s08-442": "The development of scruples is a very fine science. Through it a scruple is drawn, as it were, out of the dark, and then, after it has been properly set in the light, it must be formally refuted as a significant objection.",
"blomberg-s08-443": "But it is really impossible to think of a more wretched condition of the human being than the condition that leaves us undecided. The condition of indecision, especially when it concerns our interest. This condition of indecision is far more troublesome than the condition of speculation.",
"blomberg-s08-444": "Everything that holds us back and makes us inactive, puts us into a certain kind of inaction, is very much opposed to the essential determinations of the soul. The human being would rather know a future misfortune in advance than remain uncertain in the expectation of it, hovering between fear and hope.",
"blomberg-s08-445": "Physicians are often themselves to blame for the timidity of their patients; they mostly feed them constantly with empty words, and conditions, imaginings.",
"blomberg-s08-446": "Young people especially are for the most part of such a kind that they very much dislike being in suspenso (in suspense), for it is their nature: they always want to get further. Yet in all this uncertainty a certain kind of certainty can still take place, namely in this, that all our cognition must be very limited and bounded, and consequently very imperfect and defective.",
"blomberg-s08-447": "For we can always cognize the grounds of uncertainty with certainty and reliability; that is a certainty of subjective philosophy.",
"blomberg-s08-448": "Settled truths are, again, those truths against which no further objection can be made.",
"blomberg-s08-449": "To these belong, e.g., the Res judicatae (matters adjudged), that is, matters settled in foro (in court) by a judgment with the force of law; e.g. after a completed process it is a settled matter that, when the judge has pronounced a verdict, this or that person must pay to this or that other person, e.g., a certain assigned sum of money.",
"blomberg-s08-450": "All and every new truth always finds at the beginning very many opponents, very much resistance; a certain time must be necessary and required to settle it, after one has examined it better. So, e.g., it went at the beginning with the writings of Cartesius (Descartes). At the beginning they met with very much contradiction, and 30 and more years passed without their truth being settled.",
"blomberg-s08-451": "Through this settling of the truth really no new cognition is added to it; rather, nothing else happens than that all other human beings cease to judge about it and agree with it. So long, then, as a matter is still disputed, so long as disputes are still exchanged on this side and on the other, it is not settled at all.",
"blomberg-s08-452": "We can often have complete certainty about this or that matter, but the grounds of the certainty are often of such a kind that in the manner of inferring one still notices a great deal of uncertainty. E.g. the soul is immortal: this is completely certain. But the philosophical grounds for it are not so evident that one should be completely convinced by them. The philosophical form of it is still very uncertain.",
"blomberg-s08-453": "Every uncertainty is a ground of a rightful doubt, since I still find it possible to seek out grounds for the opposite of the cognition.",
"blomberg-s08-454": "The consciousness of the insufficiency of a ground for truth is at least useful for assuming it as still possible that there are still grounds for the opposite of the matter. Doubt in the proper sense does not yet take place with the uncertainty of a cognition.",
"blomberg-s08-455": "The uncertainty of one matter or another, however, makes doubt entirely possible, in that one has grounds for the opposite. Often there really arises from the fact that one has seen and noticed that reason is erring, very often a kind of hatred of reason, misology, roughly in the way that misanthropy is hatred of human beings, which springs from a suspicion against the whole human race, which one draws from the observation of how little justly human beings act.",
"blomberg-s08-456": "“That author and writer deserves without doubt the greatest thanks who teaches in his writings what will hold for perhaps a hundred years,” says Terrasson. The world then still moves by its prejudices, which that author wanted to root out, until it finally begins to think rightly about them,",
"blomberg-s08-457": "and with the course [685/6] of time gradually also calms itself and consents to this.",
"blomberg-s08-458": "One also cannot always investigate the grounds of the conflict of speaking. With people who have themselves written great books and works on this or that matter, it is not good or advisable to dispute this matter further; for one can well infer for oneself that they cannot so easily be brought to esteem lightly, to value little, or to refute themselves in their own labour of many years, etc.",
"blomberg-s08-459": "In an inner proof one is immediately conscious of the uncertainty in a cognition. An outer proof, however, that is, the consciousness of the uncertainty of a cognition, and indeed not immediately but mediately, according to the judgments of others. In many a Quaestion of reason a plausibility is to be met with and found such that I cannot withhold my assent from it at all; but if I present it to others, they often hold directly the opposite of it, and I then first become aware that, according to the minds and insights of others, my cognition is still not entirely and altogether certain beyond doubt.",
"blomberg-s08-460": "In common usage the word doubt signifies every uncertainty, and in this respect and in this sense doubt is either dogmatic or sceptical. The former is a doubt of decision, the latter a doubt of retardation, of postponement. From the former certainty arises, from the latter closer investigation and inquiry, in order thereby to arrive at last at the right and undoubted certainty of the cognition.",
"blomberg-s08-461": "With dogmatic doubt we reject all inquiry, and do not accept something against which we have a well-founded doubt, or believe we have one. We then decide briefly, and say: in this matter it is not possible at all to attain any certainty. Dogmatic doubt, then, regards very many cognitions as if absolutely nothing could be established and settled about them. A Dogmaticus is accordingly that philosopher who passes the judgment on universal truths of reason that they can be brought to complete certainty and reliability, but does not engage at all in the consideration of such truths of reason as are or can be exposed to one doubt or another. The mathematician, e.g., is so constituted; all his proofs are at once decisive; he presents a proposition positively, proves it straightway decisive, so that it is quite impossible that the judgment should not hold.",
"blomberg-s08-462": "The Methodus Dogmatica Philosophiae (dogmatic method of philosophy) will therefore consist in this, that in all judgments we attain complete, secure certainty, are not content with indecision, and leave nothing undecided. But through this dogmatic method one will never completely settle the real certainty of a matter; one will not teach truths thereby, but rather very often help to propagate errors, in that one sets oneself, as it were, upon the judgment-seat of human reason and pronounces that no certainty whatever is to be attained of this or that cognition, so that all inquiry is applied only in vain. So, and in this way, one sets, as it were, bounds and barriers to the human understanding.",
"blomberg-s08-463": "All reflection, all efforts to procure a true certainty about something [691a] and to attain conviction, are thereby wholly removed, and what else can arise from this than ignorance, indeed even error?",
"blomberg-s08-464": "The dogmatic spirit of philosophy is therefore the proud language of the ignorant, who like to decide everything and have no wish to investigate anything; whereas properly our understanding is very much inclined to test everything beforehand and to investigate it exactly before it accepts and asserts it, and also to look about well beforehand, without blindly rejecting anything that comes before us. The Dogmatici could be set over against the Dialectici.",
"blomberg-s08-465": "The cognitions are firstly those whose certainty can be cognized apodictically. These are cognitions a priori, which are seen through mere reason. In testimonies an apodictic certainty can never be attained. Apodictic judgments are judgments a priori, and cognitions of this sort are Dogmata; mathematics, e.g., is of this sort.",
"blomberg-s08-466": "In cognitions of experience, however, no apodictic certainty at all can take place. It is no pure impossibility that all those who preach to us much about practice in thinking, acting, judging, inferring, should nevertheless deceive us, without needing on that account to be ill-disposed. Everything that is apodictically certain is capable of a dogmatic, but everything else properly only of a dialectical kind of cognition.",
"blomberg-s08-467": "The ancients divided their philosophers into Dogmatici and Sceptici, into seekers, inquirers, speculatores of truth, which they always sought but could never find.",
"blomberg-s08-468": "Further there were intellectual philosophers, among whom Plato said: “Everything good that we want to cognize and rightly see must happen a priori through reason alone. The senses alone contain nothing but deception.”",
"blomberg-s08-469": "The sensual philosophers, on the other hand, among whom one reckoned Epicurus as the foremost, said in turn:",
"blomberg-s08-470": "“Everything that we are able to cognize a priori through reason is nothing but chimerical. The senses alone give the right, true certainty.”",
"blomberg-s08-471": "Thereupon, however, a third came and made peace between these 2 heated, quarrelling parties, by exclaiming: “I believe both equally little, or not at all",
"blomberg-s08-472": "Neither the intellectual nor the sensitive has certainty. Everything in the human understanding is nothing but confusion.” And from this arose formal scepticism, which seems to originate from so famous and highly esteemed a man as Socrates is. When, namely, the Dogmatici either quarrelled among themselves or asserted one proposition or another with all gravity and certainty, he at first listened to them quite humbly, but soon began, as a learning-eager listener and pupil of the heated and pathetic Dogmatici, to ask them for advice while they disputed with one another, and thereby he could then catch them with their own speeches and knew how to confuse them so, that in the end they quite contradicted themselves and so often had to become ridiculous to whole assemblies. He did this so often, and at the right time, that the enemies of the proud Dogmatici already rejoiced beforehand when Socrates visited their lecture-halls, and these feared him already when he began to ask, since they had to answer him, as he knew how to present himself to them as a pupil eager for knowledge. From this arose finally a pernicious inclination in his followers to doubt entirely all and every dogmatic cognitions and their certainty. Pyrrho, however, can rightly be regarded as the real founder and propagator of the sceptical sect. His Non Liquet (it is not clear), which he indifferently called out against the proud assertions of the Dogmatici, and thereby at least often made them very startled, as also his careful and untiring efforts to examine carefully all propositions of the Dogmatici, are known everywhere.",
"blomberg-s08-473": "The Sceptici, however, if we consider it rightly, have nevertheless in a certain way brought philosophy far more benefit than the proud Dogmatici, although it is indeed also true that the former degenerated at last into bitter mockeries through abuse. But scepticism, or the method of sceptical doubt, whereby one places a mistrust in oneself, considers the grounds for and against the cognition one has, and in this way endeavours to come to complete certainty of it, this is the Katharkticon (purgative), the best means of purification of reason. It prevents errors as far as possible, leads the human being to more inquiry, and is the way to arrive at the truth of the matter (though indeed not at once and suddenly, but slowly and gradually through a further and closer testing).",
"blomberg-s08-474": "The doubt of postponement is therefore really a surer mark of the maturity of reason, and of experience in the truth of cognition.",
"blomberg-s08-475": "But if we wanted to ask here which method of philosophizing will be the most suitable and the best at Academies, and please most, the dogmatic or the sceptical?",
"blomberg-s08-476": "Then we must nevertheless necessarily answer: the dogmatic.",
"blomberg-s08-477": "When a learned man steps up here and establishes something dogmatically about this or that cognition, nothing can be easier for the listener; he need not test anything, investigate anything, but only impress upon his memory the little that the teacher tells and presents to him. He remains in this way in complete rest and comfort, he need only memorize; whereas doubt about cognitions is by far not so commodious, but rather far more disquieting, and requires one's own reflection and inquiry.",
"blomberg-s08-478": "Doubt can therefore be divided",
"blomberg-s08-479": "a) into the academic",
"blomberg-s08-480": "B) into the sceptical.",
"blomberg-s08-481": "The school of Plato, the great Dogmaticus, was called the Academy. The academic doubt is very much distinguished from the sceptical.",
"blomberg-s08-482": "1.) “The academic doubter proved and demonstrated per definitionem (by definition) that properly nothing could be proved, nothing defined, which was yet a Contradictio in adjecto (contradiction in the adjunct), and this was the dogmatic-seeming doubt.”",
"blomberg-s08-483": "2nd The Pyrrhonian, i.e. the sceptical doubter, on the other hand, said: against all and every, or at the least against most of our judgments, another judgment can always be set and opposed, which asserts exactly the opposite of what is contained in the former judgment; this was therefore really to be regarded more as a kind of very fine and excellent observation than as a reprehensible doubt. And it would really be desirable that this observation had been made correctly and carefully; very advantageous consequences would surely have sprung from it for the whole of human cognition.",
"blomberg-s08-484": "But how? Is it not a contradictio to establish something in the case of the error, and to be a dogmatic doubter?",
"blomberg-s08-485": "Answer. Both can very well subsist together. Dogmatic doubt is always bound up with persuasion of the certainty that nothing at all is to be established or asserted about this or that cognition.",
"blomberg-s08-486": "Dogmatic doubt, then, consists in nothing other than that one judges that in a cognition one can never attain complete certainty of it, and that all inquiry is therefore henceforth always applied in vain and for nothing.",
"blomberg-s08-487": "Sceptical doubt, on the other hand, consists in this, that one is conscious of the uncertainty in a cognition and is therefore driven to inquire into it more and more, so that one may finally nevertheless attain certainty with the help of careful investigations. The former, that of the Dogmatici, rejects certainty wholly at once. The latter, that of the Scepticers, seeks it gradually. The origin of the expression scepticism is properly as follows: σκέπτεσθαι. This word means in Greek: to inquire into, Scrutari, investigare, indagare. The Scepticus therefore inquires constantly, he tests and investigates, he places a mistrust in everything, but never without ground. He resembles in this a judge who weighs the grounds for as well as against the matter, and hears the plaintiff as well as the defendant before he decides the matter and passes a judgment. He postpones his final judgment very long, until he ventures to settle something completely. These were the old and pure characteristics of scepticism and of an unadulterated Scepticus.",
"blomberg-s08-488": "At the beginning scepticism was really very reasonable; only the pupils spoiled it and brought it into such ill repute.",
"blomberg-s08-489": "These latter were so subtle that they even went so far as to say: everything is uncertain, even this is uncertain, that everything is uncertain. That was really a kind of purgative for human reason, of such a constitution that, after it has wholly cleansed our understanding of all impurities, i.e. of all false delusion, prejudices, incorrect judgments, it carried itself off again.",
"blomberg-s08-490": "But nothing is in fact more harmful to the learned world than a general rest and agreement and peace; at least this always hinders an improvement and increase of learnedness. It is with this roughly almost the same as with the English, who, as soon as the court stands in proper agreement with the subjects and they are completely at one with each other, already apprehend danger and believe they have lost all their freedom.",
"blomberg-s08-491": "A general resolve to doubt everything is of no use at all; it is wholly absurd, but there are also few, or we might even say no human beings, who would be inclined to so childish and harmful a mania for doubting.",
"blomberg-s08-492": "True scepticism, however, is certainly of great usefulness. And as such it is nothing other than an exact, careful investigation of all Dogmata alleged to be apodictic, which, in so far as they really are so and hence hold water, shine forth and strike the eye in their whole Valeur (worth), in their whole strength, only after this testing.",
"blomberg-s08-493": "All sceptical method is a true investigation of truth through postponement, in that one names or rejects nothing at once, but first lets it be disputed. By our nature we are properly not at all inclined to postponement. In our times one calls Scepticers partly such people as do not in the least deserve the name of philosophers (e.g. a Voltaire), partly such people as are not real Academici but merely have a sceptical method about them and, as it were, affect it, e.g. a Hume. Among the new Scepticers, however, one can reckon Bayle etc.",
"blomberg-s08-494": "With us now, then, the name of a Scepticer is for the most part regarded as something hateful; one thinks, namely, of such a one who does nothing, applies himself to nothing else than to endeavouring to overthrow the most undeniable and most certain truths; one therefore holds him really for a dogmatic doubter, who believes he is unable to judge about anything, and that one can attain from no matter a complete certainty which nothing can overthrow. Formerly, however, an upright Scepticer meant nothing other than one who merely postpones his judgment until he has had occasion and time to take this or that matter into closer consideration, and only then ventures to infer whether a cognition is to be taken as true or rather rejected as false. The sceptical method is directly opposed to the dogmatic.",
"blomberg-s08-495": "The method of judging, however, in which one doubts more than one decides, in which one therefore believes that our cognitions can be false rather than true, that is the Suspensio, or that kind of mistrust against the cognition we have, in which we likewise really settle nothing, but nevertheless judge, and first investigate whether the grounds for or against the cognition retain the upper hand.",
"blomberg-s08-496": "With this method we collect grounds of assent or of rejection, but we settle nothing at all, we do not yet decide at all. This kind of suspension is therefore directly opposed to dogmatic pride. The Dogmaticus asks: is something true or not? He therefore decides at once, but he who suspends his judgment trusts himself not very much; he inquires, tests and investigates everything well beforehand, precisely in order thereby to find out the way to truth and to attain genuine, true insight.",
"blomberg-s08-497": "Human nature is really far more inclined to decide than always to test and settle, than always to inquire. For we are not at all content when we must leave something, and something particular, unfinished in our cognition, but we want gladly to settle everything, so that we can have recourse to it as to a completely certain and reliable cognition in an emergency that may arise.",
"blomberg-s08-498": "Through the decision our understanding is really more satisfied; one believes, namely, that one has thereby already acquired something with which one could settle further cognitions.",
"blomberg-s08-499": "The dogmatic way of philosophizing is therefore directly opposed to, and contradistinguished from, the problematic way of philosophizing.",
"blomberg-s08-500": "With the former one settles everything, with the latter one does not decide at all, but postpones one's judgments until one has duly sought out the grounds and counter-grounds, and can then pass a judgment.",
"blomberg-s08-501": "In physics one must philosophize preeminently problematically, and every cautious learned man will without doubt make use of this way of philosophizing. But the doubt in the problematic way of philosophizing is either a sceptical one, an inquiring, investigating, testing one, or a dogmatic, settling and deciding doubt.",
"blomberg-s08-502": "The history of philosophy (Weltweisheit), when it comes to this point, namely to the point of the origin of the sect of the Scepticers, always appeals for the most part first to Socrates. The Sophists, who in earlier times, from vain ambition and imagined science, believed they had the right to decide everything and defended a matter from whatever side it pleased them, were hateful to Socrates, in that he held them merely for pseudo-sages (which they also properly were) and declared so publicly.",
"blomberg-s08-503": "He judged that it was not necessary for us to know many things, whence he also had the motto which sufficiently attested his wisdom: “quod supra nos, nihil ad nos” (what is above us is nothing to us). He therefore established that our best cognition was to learn to know ourselves, that the human being was the proper object of all philosophy. Hence he accepted nothing except what he had tested. He gave no cognition his assent blindly, but inquired and investigated.",
"blomberg-s08-504": "If, therefore, someone was inclined to decide something at once, he showed him the counter-grounds and difficulties which would compel him to suspend his judgment. This inclination to decide nothing without testing did not proceed in him from any pride but from a high degree of modesty, in that he did not trust himself too much but really placed a kind of mistrust in his cognitions. And this modesty he also impressed preeminently on all his pupils, yet it did not remain long with them, for his immediate pupil Plato already began to become somewhat more dogmatic, and from him arose and originated the sect of the Academici, which was opposed to the sect of the Scepticers.",
"blomberg-s08-505": "These endeavoured to philosophize problematically, but those, the Dogmatici, decided everything; their whole philosophy consisted in deciding.",
"blomberg-s08-506": "Pyrrho was the first who brought the sect of the Scepticers into vogue, and his adherents were therefore also called Pyrrhonists.",
"blomberg-s08-507": "This sect did not properly doubt all truths, but prolonged the investigation and postponed the decision. Hence it proved through the doubts that the grounds for decision were not yet complete, and so led the Dogmatici, who wanted to decide everything, onto the path of caution.",
"blomberg-s08-508": "The Scepticers, however, are also called Zetetici, seekers and inquirers, and indeed for this reason, because they sought and inquired. They will therefore, with all propositions, neither at all times doubt them decisively, nor hold something to be true and accept it decisively.",
"blomberg-s08-509": "The Zeteticus is therefore not he who has the maxim of rejecting everything and each thing, and of deciding positively on everything and each thing without distinction, or of asserting blindly, but he who reflects on cognitions and tests them.",
"blomberg-s08-510": "Pyrrho had a certain motto of his sect: non liquet.",
"blomberg-s08-511": "To everyone who asserted his propositions with the greatest and most settled gravity he called out non liquet, it is not yet settled, and then began to present his counter-speech.",
"blomberg-s08-512": "A skilful and learned way of humbling many a proud Dogmaticus and showing his weak side. This is a sure motto for every pure reason. One must not accept everything at once, but test, and investigate and consider the objects. This sect of the Pyrrhonists, however, departed so far from its originator that it was afterwards assumed that Pyrrho himself had denied altogether the certainty of all and every Dogmata.",
"blomberg-s08-513": "That Pyrrho denied many Dogmata and placed a just and well-considered mistrust in them, further that in particular he rejected many rational judgments, is indisputable and not to be denied; but that he denied all and every Dogmata, that is utterly false. He who accepts no Dogmata at all can teach no morality. There are certainly certain, so to speak, eternal fundamental propositions of our reason which cannot be contested at all. But Pyrrho was moreover also blamed for having doubted the truth of all empirical judgments and not having trusted them. This, however, is nothing but a fiction which has no ground at all. Pyrrho was a very wise man, but he would surely have been a fool if he had wanted to reject all empirical judgments, whatever they might be; a Pyrrhonist therefore at the beginning did nothing but moderate his judgment, but afterwards the Scepticers indeed sought to put everything into uncertainty, they endeavoured to show that in all our cognitions no certainty at all can take place. And just hence arose the Scepticismus problematicus, et Dogmaticus. The former doubted indeed, but nevertheless investigated beforehand, and wanted by means of this investigation to attain certainty. The latter, by contrast, decided, and assumed that in no matter could complete certainty be attained.",
"blomberg-s08-514": "This inclination to hold nothing at all for certain springs really from nothing other than a desire for [724a] completion. He who saw that in very many things one cannot attain certainty, who doubted the truth of many things and dogmata and had not accepted them until a testing had preceded, saw it as better to doubt everything and to contest the truth of all and every principle by adducing the counter-grounds.",
"blomberg-s08-515": "Of Pyrrho himself we properly have no writings left, except that Diogenes Laertius cites something of them. Meanwhile this much is certain, that one imputes to him, roughly as to Epicurus, far more, and pins on him more, than he really did. One says, e.g., of him that he denied the certainty of all experiences, that he therefore went straight into all swamps and marshes, that he feared neither the running of horses nor deep abysses and ditches, and yet adds to these reports so unfavourable to him that he reached 90 years of age, as a manifest proof that all such accusations as had been devised about him must be false, for with such rashness he would surely have lost his life much sooner.",
"blomberg-s08-516": "Carneades, the originator of the last academic school, departed very much from the teachings of Pyrrho, and really asserted that in no matter could one attain a right certainty and come to certain truth.",
"blomberg-s08-517": "Scepticism, however, is either Logicus, the logical. To this belongs, e.g., the Diallele (circle): truth is supposed to consist in the agreement of a cognition with itself.",
"blomberg-s08-518": "Many of the Argumenta of the Scepticers had and received their names often from the Terminus medius (middle term) of the Syllogismus. So, e.g., one of their arguments is called the Liar, which ran thus: if you say you are lying, and in saying this speak the truth, then you speak the truth in lying; atqui (but) you say you are lying, and in saying this you therefore speak the truth in lying. The right Conclusio, however, should be: Ergo you speak the truth in saying you are lying.",
"blomberg-s08-519": "Or scepticism is also physicus, the physical, in which the certainty of all physical laws was called into doubt, e.g. the possibility of motion. So the old Sceptici had a certain argument which they also, on account of the medius Terminus, called the Achilles. They inferred, namely, thus: if the running Achilles were to run a race with a tortoise but give a mile in advance, and Achilles were also able to run a hundred times faster than the tortoise, he would nevertheless never be able to catch up with the tortoise.",
"blomberg-s08-520": "The grounds which he made use of in defence of this argument were: that all numbers are divisible in infinitum, so that, however swiftly Achilles ran, there would still always be a, though very small, spatium (space) between him and the tortoise.",
"blomberg-s08-521": "They inferred here, then, merely through reason without having recourse to experience, for otherwise they would have found that Achilles must necessarily catch up with the tortoise in 1¼ miles, provided only that he could run 1000 times faster than the tortoise. Scepticism was further vel moralis (or moral), and this consisted in this, that one passed off all rules of virtue as fashions or customs and usages in the actions which human beings undertake. So, e.g., close marriages in Egypt were not forbidden at all, as they now are with us; among some Negroes, e.g., the greatest, most excellent and last service of love that children who tenderly love their parents can render them is to strangle them when they reach advanced age. Among the Spartans, e.g., further, theft, so far as it could be done without being publicly noticed, was not punished at all, and it was even permitted to steal, provided it was done in secret.",
"blomberg-s08-522": "Scepticism was further vel Historicus (or historical), the historical, which consisted in this, that one believes all and every histories and reports, also narratives of ancient times, to be uncertain, invented and fabulous.",
"blomberg-s08-523": "Yet another kind of Scepticer had the habit of always talking and conversing very much about the uncertainty of the senses. They flatly denied the certainty of all and every experiences. So, e.g., there was a Scepticer who, after he had often asserted with many grounds that one could not trust the senses at all, wanted to convince his friend of this quite surely and undeniably, by saying to him: I have daily a great store in my pantry, but when I go into it, count over the store, and come again the next day, this store is nevertheless smaller than it was yesterday, a sure and irrefutable proof that the senses must deceive. His friend, who well noticed what must be behind it, advised him to guard the key to the pantry as carefully as possible. But the Scepticer's servant, who was very sly, soon knew how to get hold of the key, and under this cover diminished his master's store as before, until his master once caught him in the act and let him feel the just punishment of his theft, but also had to learn in this respect that one could well trust the senses.",
"blomberg-s08-524": "Such a scepticism could well be called dogmaticus, for the Sceptici themselves were Dogmatici, in that, though they doubted things etc., they nevertheless believed they had a right to demand that every human being should give assent to their proofs and accept their doubts. They contradicted themselves, then, for they said: everything is uncertain without distinction, and yet they asserted their propositions and attributed to them an infallible certainty. They had, e.g., the proposition: everything is uncertain, and indeed in such a way that it is even uncertain that everything is uncertain –",
"blomberg-s08-525": "As for modern times with regard to scepticism, the dogmatic method was very usual at the beginning of the Reformation. At that time one held philosophy, and regarded it, as a necessary support of religion; one therefore also made not much doubt and counter-grounds against the certainty of philosophical propositions, but taught and established. But after a free way of thinking had afterwards crept in among many nations, one began with greater and larger steadfastness and certainty to doubt the truth of many previously unchallenged propositions, and not to believe and accept everything straightway at once. One therefore soon also took pains to resist the proud Dogmatici somewhat and to show the groundlessness of many of their propositions. In the most recent times David Hume is especially known as a Scepticus, who however had a predominant, indeed somewhat extravagant inclination to doubting. His writings, which appear before the learned world under the title Philosophical Inquiries (Philosophische Untersuchungen) and are also otherwise called miscellaneous writings, contain political articles, treatises on literature, moral and also metaphysical articles, but all of them run out into scepticism. But in these writings of Hume there is found a gentle, composed, prejudice-free testing. He considers, namely, first of all one side of a matter; he seeks out all possible grounds for it and presents them in the best orator's style. Then he takes up the other side, likewise presents it quite impartially for judgment, again presents all the counter-grounds with the same eloquence, but at the end and in the conclusion he appears in his true shape, as a proper Scepticer; he complains about the uncertainty of all our cognitions in general, shows how little they are to be trusted, and in the end doubts, instead of inferring and settling which cognition of these two is true and which false. He would surely be one of the best and most readable authors, if only he did not have the predominant tendency to doubt everything, but sought by means of the testing and investigation of cognitions to attain true certainty.",
"blomberg-s08-526": "Cardanus in his book de vanitate Scientiarum (on the vanity of the sciences) also expresses scepticism, and likewise asserts that everything is uncertain.",
"blomberg-s08-527": "Voltaire is the Scepticus of the newer Scepticers of our times. But his scepticism is far more harmful than it is useful. He brings forward neither grounds for nor against the matter; he inquires and tests nothing at all, but doubts without any proof that a cognition is to be trusted at all. His grounds are therefore also nothing other than sham grounds, which can indeed deceive a simple man but never a sharp-witted and reflective learned man. And just hence Voltaire is very dangerous for the great multitude, and especially for the common man. For he Suppeditirt (furnishes) to him wholly false grounds for doubting the truth of this or that matter. But if one wants closer instruction on the scepticism of more recent times, one can read with great profit on that account the work which Haller published under the title: Examination of the Sect that Doubts Everything (Prüfung der Secte, die an allem zweifelet). The above-mentioned writings of Hume too are in a certain way really to be recommended.",
"blomberg-s08-528": "In this paragraph the author speaks of an opinion in general. It is, however, contradistinguished from persuasion. In both there are uncertain cognitions, but in persuasion one holds them to be certain. In opinion, on the contrary, one does not hold them to be certain. We must therefore endeavour preeminently to distinguish persuasion well from conviction. The very same person who holds something as an opinion cannot yet hold it for a persuasion, and really errs as soon as he does so. Persuasion is therefore always blameworthy, in that one thereby holds something for certain from insufficient grounds.",
"blomberg-s08-529": "The difference of persuasion from conviction, however, is rather difficult, and so is very seldom rightly determined. This comes from the fact that perhaps one has never yet tasted a true conviction but only a sham conviction.",
"blomberg-s08-530": "Opinion, however, is properly no error, but only an imperfect cognition, a lack, in that something in our judgment has no sufficient grounds.",
"blomberg-s08-531": "We can always put forward many opinions, if only we do not hold them for persuasions, without erring thereby. The rabble does this very often. Even if a cognition is wholly false, the opinion of it can nevertheless be quite certain and agree with the rules of the understanding and of reason. E.g. when someone in common life chooses a servant who is of good parents and also has a good upbringing, he thinks he is good, that one can trust him. Here all reason is present and applied, although the opposite of it very often takes place and my cognition can be false. Wolff has a quite reasonable opinion that there would be inhabitants in the moon, for at that time the discoveries were not yet as frequent as now, when one has seen that the moon has no atmosphere.",
"blomberg-s08-532": "An opinion can often be false according to the matter although it is true according to the form; and conversely it can be true according to the matter but false according to the form; in the first case it is false, in the other, however, always true.",
"blomberg-s08-533": "If my cognition agrees with the constitution of the matter itself, it is true. But if it is contrary to the constitution of the matter, it is false. An opinion, however, can also be very true, reasonable and well-grounded according to the form, although according to the matter it does not agree with the constitution of the matter.",
"blomberg-s08-534": "An opinion is never a certainty; the opposite of it can always take place; meanwhile it can very often be reasonable and right.",
"blomberg-s08-535": "One gets an opinion about something from certain grounds which one cognizes. The outcome of the matter, however, teaches us whether our opinion has been true or false according to the matter. But whether the form of the opinion has been right, this the Eventus (outcome) of the matter cannot properly show us.",
"blomberg-s08-536": "The chief error of our cognitions consists in the formal, and not in the material of the cognitions. One can hold something for true according to all the form of reason and have a well-founded opinion about something, when the data one now has for the matter are more weighty than the grounds of the opposite, although the outcome of the matter first properly teaches one whether the opinion has been true according to the matter, and the Eventus has been other than one had opined.",
"blomberg-s08-537": "All and every opinions are very changeable. Today one can have more grounds for, tomorrow against, the opinion, whereupon one must at once change one's opinion and often adopt another opposed to that one, which can also in fact be in no way disadvantageous to a true philosopher.",
"blomberg-s08-538": "Hypotheses are a quite special kind of philosophical opinions. But not all philosophical opinions are hypotheses, only some. They serve not to explain the appearances of the world, but only to explain something in general, whether it spring from reason, or from experience, or from appearance.",
"blomberg-s08-539": "An opinion of the truth of a ground from its sufficiency for the consequence is a hypothesis. If, namely, I hold something true a priori on account of its grounds, this is nothing other than an opinion. But if, on the contrary, I cognize the ground from the sufficiency of the consequences for a ground, this too is an opinion, but not a priori but a posteriori, and thus a hypothesis. A hypothesis is, as it were, a presupposition. So a physician makes Hypothesin (a hypothesis) when he cures the patient; he must subsume everything under hypotheses and see whether the consequences arise from it which he now sees before his eyes.",
"blomberg-s08-540": "If all the consequences that follow from an assumed hypothesis are true and agree with what is given, then it is no longer an opinion but a certainty.",
"blomberg-s08-541": "It is, moreover, a demonstrated truth; but since we are not at all, or only very rarely, in a position to draw all and every consequence from it, they are for the most part only pure opinions, and so we must say: if all the consequences we have been able to draw from the hypothesis are true, I have great cause to infer that the hypothesis itself is certain and true. If a hypothesis, besides its sufficiency for its consequences, is also strengthened by other assurances, then it is confirmed. A hypothesis subsidiaria, however, is one that presupposes a new hypothesis. The more I must assume in it, the less steadfast it is. The more unity there is in the hypothesis, the better it is, for otherwise fabrication for the most part is added as well, and in this way a philosophical romance arises. A romance of reason, such as that of Descartes, etc. etc. A world-edifice of pure figments of the brain. But note the rule: I may indeed invent in order to philosophize, but not while philosophizing itself.",
"blomberg-s08-542": "Every presupposition, however, has infinite consequences. In relation, however, something is certain when the grounds of assent exceed all the grounds of the opposite; that is then a comparative certainty.",
"blomberg-s08-543": "Absolutely, however, something is certain whose opposite is impossible or absurd. Two presuppositions, if they are so constituted that one and the same consequences flow from the one as from the other, are one and the same representation, and not at all different.",
"blomberg-s08-544": "Thus the Copernican system is a hypothesis. One can still always represent the opposite as possible, however difficult that is; and however easy this system is, and in accordance with reason, and in agreement with all the phenomena of the heavens. –",
"blomberg-s08-545": "In the case of all hypotheses one is compelled, in order to procure them admission and certainty, to see that they can be confirmed and derived not merely a posteriori through the relation to their consequences, but also through the nexus a priori, that is, through the relation to their grounds.",
"blomberg-s08-546": "From the sufficiency of a ground for a given consequence it can never be inferred with complete truth that this is the true ground of the consequence: for this is only an opinion. There is a ground of possibility there, but not of certainty, for a consequence can often spring from several grounds and originate from more than one ground. The more sufficient a ground is, however, the more one will infer that it is the ground of the consequence. For all hypotheses are posited arbitrarily. I assume something, namely, and see whether it suffices to derive a certain consequence from it, or not.",
"blomberg-s08-547": "If a ground suffices for all the determinations that are contained in a certain given consequence, no more and no fewer, that is, if it is adequate, so that neither fewer determinations are to be inferred from it, and it is therefore complete, nor more, and it is therefore precise, then it is the true ground, since it is constituted as it ought to be.",
"blomberg-s08-548": "More grounds than those that are to be quite adequate to a consequence are impossible. Grounds that are different from one another cannot agree with one another in all cases. But grounds that agree in all consequences are not different grounds but one and the very same ground.",
"blomberg-s08-549": "If a ground suffices for something that is contained in the consequence, then according to logical rules an opinion obtains that the ground is the true one. If the ground suffices for several determinations of the consequence, the opinion is so much the more probable.",
"blomberg-s08-550": "Until finally, when the ground suffices for all the determinations, but not for more determinations than are contained in the consequence, a true ground is there, and then the hypothesis ceases. The ground becomes a theory. A certainty.",
"blomberg-s08-551": "If a consequence is given to me and I am to find the ground for it, this is a hypothesis. But if a ground is given to me and I am to derive the consequences from it, this is indeed an opinion, but no hypothesis. In the hypothesis the consequences are given, but the ground is fabricated.",
"blomberg-s08-552": "One of the most famous hypotheses is the Copernican. Copernicus assumed that the sun stands still and the planets move around it, and saw whether all the appearances of the heavenly bodies could be explained from this; but this hypothesis of his afterwards becomes a true opinion and a complete system, which deserves approval everywhere. If it is asked: why do all bodies have just this inclination to fall toward the earth? then the consequence is here, and the ground is to be found.",
"blomberg-s08-553": "Descartes assumed that bodies made certain vortices; still others assumed that these vortices intersected, and so they sought to explain from this the fall of bodies, and indeed always toward the center of the earth.",
"blomberg-s08-554": "In metaphysics there are many hypotheses. The proof of the existence of God is at first a hypothesis. One assumes a being that is the most perfect among all others, and then investigates whether explanatory grounds of it can be derived from this, of which nature, experience, and the creatures that are on the earth instruct us.",
"blomberg-s08-555": "Plato and Malebranche assumed it as a hypothesis that human beings stand with the deity in an entirely special connection, and saw whether they would be able to explain from this the consequences they had insight into.",
"blomberg-s08-556": "Hypotheses, however, really have a very great utility, and can never be wholly banished from human cognition. All judges make hypotheses, which they often reject again on better investigation. Hypotheses are the way to theory, and often to a truth entirely opposed to it.",
"blomberg-s08-557": "Through hypotheses one does not always find what one wants, but often something else; one tries, one tests, assumes something, and investigates whether one can explain the known consequences from it or not; if the first obtains, one accepts the hypothesis; if the latter obtains, one rejects it.",
"blomberg-s08-558": "Since from the sufficiency of a ground for the consequence the truth of the ground can be inferred, and this ground is fabricated, it follows from this: the more there is of the fabricated in a ground, the more follows licite (permissibly) from it, and the newer it is. And the more hypotheses must be assumed in order to explain something, the more fabrication takes place in it.",
"blomberg-s08-559": "In some inferences one must fabricate many hypotheses subsidiarias. If we have consequences and fabricate the ground for them, it can often happen that we have still more consequences than the ground suffices for.",
"blomberg-s08-560": "We must therefore fabricate new grounds, and these new grounds are nothing other than the hypotheses subsidiariae.",
"blomberg-s08-561": "These, however, are really opposed to true philosophy, and show effrenis fingendi licentia (unbridled license of inventing). If I want to fabricate everything, then I want to explain everything. Unity is therefore a chief property of hypotheses, namely that one assumes a ground that suffices to explain from it all the determinations and consequences of the thing that we cognize, without, however, having to help oneself further by hypotheses subsidiarias and the assumption of them.",
"blomberg-s08-562": "The unity of hypotheses is a great reason that the ground is the true one and that no other can obtain from which one could explain just as many consequences.",
"blomberg-s08-563": "A chief ground of the truth of the hypothesis is also this: if one shows that the ground one has devised for the sake of the sufficiency for the consequences also deserves to be assumed for other reasons. Here, namely, one shows from other grounds that the fabricated must be assumed; one thus confirms the truth of the hypothesis.",
"blomberg-s08-564": "All holding-to-be-true arises from a twofold connection, namely either because the ground is sufficient to derive all consequences from it, or because it also agrees with other grounds that have a similarity to it and is, so to speak, aequivoce (equivocal). In all hypotheses we shall therefore first show the sufficiency of the fictio historica (historical fiction) for the consequences, and also the agreement of the fiction with other grounds, so that there are still other grounds from which some consequences can be derived. Hypotheses must first be probable as inferences, but at the same time also be capable of being confirmed by other grounds. E.g., whoever wants to explain thunderstorms assumes in the air an electrical kind of matter, and afterwards shows from other grounds that a sulphurous and electrical matter must really be found in the air. Hypotheses have on one side something very detrimental to philosophy (Weltweisheit), insofar as they give permission to invent. On the other side, however, they are again very advantageous, namely when the fictions are heuristic, or have as their aim to discover something.",
"blomberg-s08-565": "The hypothesis is thus in philosophy a method of our reason and our understanding that is really entirely indispensable; all hypotheses must absolutely be assumed problematically; consequently one must not be directly obstinate about any hypothesis and hold fast to it, so that one could not soon let it go as soon as one sees its falsity or uncertainty. Often many attempts at fabrication are entirely in vain, since one discovers no truth by them. Hypotheses therefore also have the harm that, when they have been artfully devised and one has noticed in them the agreement with many consequences, they take deep root in our understanding, and when one afterwards sees their falsity, one nevertheless does not let them go so easily. Thus, e.g., if this or that hypothesis were sufficient for new and correct consequences, and there is only one, e.g. the hundredth, that contradicts the ground, then one rejects this one, although it is evidently true, rather than let go of that hypothesis which seems so good. It is therefore very sad when in an age nothing but hypotheses rule that are false, and thus do not suffice to find out the true ground of the matter. Such hypotheses do more harm than good, for they do not suffice to explain everything, and if one assumes them in addition as true, one gets, instead of true, nothing but false cognitions and representations. He who will not let go of false hypotheses because he has hatched them and they seem to him so probable is like a person who has raised a child with much trouble and care, and afterwards does not want to cast it away so as not to lose all his labor, trouble and expense.",
"blomberg-s08-566": "Nevertheless such hypotheses, in which nothing but false hypotheses are forged together, serve to the end that in the following ages",
"blomberg-s08-567": "true hypotheses are discovered, for it is impossible that he who knows all possible false ways should not finally find the right way. The remaining propositions that the author",
"blomberg-s08-568": "adduces are for the most part tautological, and therefore deserve no closer investigation and explanation (the author often proceeds so). When he says in § 183, in the 7th rule: if one has not invented far too many opinions, we can remark thereby: much can be a perfection according to quality, but not a perfection according to quantity, namely when it exceeds a certain degree that constituted the perfection.",
"blomberg-s08-569": "This degree, however, is difficult, indeed almost impossible, to determine; e.g. to say: One should not be too open-hearted is no art, but the art consists in determining quite exactly the degree of open-heartedness and of reticence that one must not exceed, if one does not want to be too open-hearted and too reticent.",
"blomberg-s08-570": "There are countless dazzling shows in morals; one gives rules that say nothing more than what one has long known; it is thus nothing new at all for our understanding.",
"blomberg-s08-571": "Certainty is either",
"blomberg-s08-572": "a) comparative or also B) apodictic.",
"blomberg-s08-573": "Comparative certainty is the relation of the grounds of holding-to-be-true to the grounds of the opposite.",
"blomberg-s08-574": "Apodictic certainty, however, is absolute, and consists in the relation of the grounds of holding-to-be-true to the sufficient ground.",
"blomberg-s08-575": "History is merely comparatively certain, and never apodictic. Morals, however, and among them especially the jus naturale (natural right), contains many apodictic certainties within itself.",
"blomberg-s08-576": "Different from apodictic certainty is still the mathematical certainty in intuition, which is evident. Mathematics presents its propositions",
"blomberg-s08-577": "1: Discursively",
"blomberg-s08-578": "2: intuitively",
"blomberg-s08-579": "Moral, physical, indeed not even metaphysical propositions can possibly be mathematically certain.",
"blomberg-s08-580": "Evidence is certainly always lacking, although they are apodictically certain. The designation arbitrary truths amounts to nothing, for can a truth then also be false? What a contradictory expression!",
"blomberg-s08-581": "A proposition, however, is 1: true",
"blomberg-s08-582": "2 false",
"blomberg-s08-583": "3 arbitrary, and then it is not settled whether it is this or that, that is: it can be true, but also false. I cannot form the object arbitrarily, but I can dispose the subject so, arbitrarily, as is most convenient to me, to cognize the object, and make the object comprehensible to myself in the most commodious and convenient way.",
"blomberg-s08-584": "Everything that is mediately certain is demonstrable. What is immediately certain is indemonstrably certain, i.e. it is so undeniable that it needs no means of certainty or of proof.",
"blomberg-s08-585": "Proof, as a means of apodictic certainty, is demonstration. Proof, as a means of comparative certainty, is probation.",
"blomberg-s08-586": "A mathematical demonstration, however, is moreover intuitive, evident. It speaks of the universal in individuo (in the individual). Who can comprehend that? Just as little as how we can see everything with our eyes, though they are so small. We continually see objects that strike our eyes, without in the least thinking how this comes about.",
"blomberg-s08-587": "In this paragraph the author says: one should prevent all and every false cognition, one should avoid a seeming conviction (Scheinüberzeugung) or seeming persuasion (Scheinüberredung).",
"blomberg-s08-588": "But everyone already knows this; he would, however, have said something excellent if he had given the marks by which persuasion can be distinguished from conviction.",
"blomberg-s08-589": "Persuasion does not consist in material falsity but in a false form, in that one holds an opinion for complete certainty. The matter itself can always be true; only this is not the manner, the way, of arriving at truth.",
"blomberg-s08-590": "In persuasion one does not err as to the object, but one errs as to the form, in that one takes the grounds for opinion, for holding-to-be-true from insufficient grounds, for grounds for certainty from sufficient grounds.",
"blomberg-s08-591": "But we have in fact always only a theory of morals, a speculation about the moral, ethical conduct of human beings, and no actual practical morals. Hence no one can demand that one should give the notae (marks) by which false persuasion differs from conviction in the true sense. This is still to be discovered. We likewise have only a theoretical and quite and entirely no practical logic.",
"blomberg-s08-592": "Some persuasions arise from logical grounds, others from aesthetic grounds, which do not come from the understanding and from reason but arise from a certain inclination. We are often not indifferent to finding a thing true or false, and then there is a great ground that we shall be persuaded of a cognition. The human being is in general subject to very many illusions; they play with him as with a ball. He, however, can most easily be persuaded who is very easily inclined to be put into one or another affect.",
"blomberg-s08-593": "The great crowd, the rabble, the common man is therefore for the most part very easy, and easiest, to persuade by someone who understands the art of winning over. But he who can easily be persuaded can also easily be put into affect. Both are therefore almost inseparably bound together.",
"blomberg-s08-594": "Science (Wißenschaft) (which really does not belong here at all, and whose consideration the author has introduced here wrongly and in a certain way unsuitably) can be set against conjecture and also against opinions. To believe or to opine something is very much different from knowing. For knowing means to hold something to be true with sufficient certainty and grounds that no doubt remains, nor can remain.",
"blomberg-s08-595": "As for the degrees of holding-to-be-true, they are the following:",
"blomberg-s08-596": "1: Opining, or holding-to-be-true from any insufficient ground which nevertheless has more weight than the ground of the opposite that I cognize and have insight into.",
"blomberg-s08-597": "2: Believing, or holding something to be true to the degree that it is sufficient for acting and for resolving myself to it.",
"blomberg-s08-598": "3: Knowing, or cognizing the truth sufficiently.",
"blomberg-s08-599": "The word wißen (to know) is the origin, or the root-word, of certainty (Gewisheit). We have already explained a certainty above. A science (Wißenschaft), however, is nothing other than a cognition through which we know something. The word science is contradistinguished from art; hence one also commonly says in common life, and conversely, that is no science but an art. E.g. in riddles this is the case; one then exclaims, that is no science but an art (a great difference).",
"blomberg-s08-600": "There are actions for which a certain art, but on the other hand again certain actions for which a science is required and necessary. Something, however (in order to set these concepts apart), is called an art for which a specific perfection of praxis is required. Something is called a science, however, for which a specific perfection of theory is necessary. Every art involves a practical skill in acting. The practical skill that one has in art, however, cannot be obtained merely by rules and certain particular precepts, but one must also have a particular talent for it. Further, science is opposed to critique. All theoretical cognition is either a doctrine or a critique. Critique, however, is in fact nothing other than a species of theoretical (hence not practical) cognition of any product that exists, which shows us the rules according to which this product is executed. Doctrine, however, is that theoretical cognition in which one finds the grounds how a thing can be executed, or finds the rules according to which a good product can be brought forth.",
"blomberg-s08-601": "A critique is really no science, for otherwise we would be able straightway to bring forth good products ourselves. But this happens with critique only after a long time, when one gradually learns to apply the rules cognized to oneself. In common usage, however, the word science is often falsified, and one involves with it other concepts than this word really contains.",
"blomberg-s08-602": "In certainty, what is necessary is not the truth itself of the thing that we cognize, but the holding-to-be-true of it. The greater the probability of a thing, the more the understanding is compelled to hold it to be true. This necessity to accept the thing, however, lies not in the object itself but in the subject, because the grounds of the opposite by no means come up to those that I cognize for the truth of the thing. This subjective necessity to accept something is very deceptive and changeable, because it is not grounded in the object.",
"blomberg-s08-603": "The author also speaks here of mathematical certainty, of the first and second rank. The former rests on reason, the latter on experience: and both certainties therefore differ in the data. To all mathematical certainty there belongs a sufficient ground, a precision, and a certain intuiting of the cognition, i.e. the intuitive. Mathematical certainty carries a certain sensibility with it, for this occurs always only in quantities, never in qualities.",
"blomberg-s08-604": "In philosophy there are no intuitive cognitions, but only in mathematics, which has to do with magnitudes. In mathematical certainty there is found, just as in philosophical certainty distinctness and sufficiency, but in the former only precision and evidence alone are to be met with. Historical certainty can never possibly become apodictic. The grounds of the truth of a historical certainty, however, are",
"blomberg-s08-605": "1: The purity of insight.",
"blomberg-s08-606": "2, the correctness of the power of judgment.",
"blomberg-s08-607": "3 truthfulness, further",
"blomberg-s08-608": "4 the inclination to truth or to speak the truth, and finally",
"blomberg-s08-609": "5: uprightness. For the last two grounds are the causes of the third ground, namely the truthfulness of him who asserts it. Now, however, it cannot be inferred apodictically from consequences at all, because there can be far more than one consequence of one and the very same thing. Certain historical cognitions will have such grounds of holding-to-be-true that they are sufficient for practical, though to be sure not for theoretical certainty. Apodictic certainty is consequently not really distinguished from practical certainty in gradu (in degree), but only in specie (in kind), even if some would maintain the former.",
"blomberg-s08-610": "The author in this paragraph calls hypotheses arbitrary truths. An arbitrary proposition makes something true, only it must not contradict itself. Such an arbitrary proposition is called positio. Nothing that is objective can ever be assumed arbitrarily, but only the subjective is firmly posited by choice (Willkühr). All arbitrary propositions concern what one ought to do, the practical, and thus never teach what the thing is. A positio occurs only with regard to praxis, and through this it is distinguished from the hypothesis. Arbitrary positiones that concern actions occur chiefly in mathematics, e.g. in the circle, that one divides it into 360 degrees, in logarithms, the relation of the arithmetical to the geometrical number, proportions, progressions.",
"blomberg-s08-611": "The word hypothesis therefore means no arbitrary proposition at all, but an opinion from certain grounds a posteriori that are sufficient for these or those consequences.",
"blomberg-s08-612": "In positiones I really posit nothing firmly that lies in the object, but that which I arbitrarily wish and want to have in it. A mathematical magnitude rests on the arbitrary and optional repetition of the unit. What holds here of a particular kind of triangles, circles, etc. holds at the same time of all triangles, circles, etc. of this kind. A triangle, circle, quadrangle etc. takes the place of all the other possible ones. Every triangle contains, e.g., 180 degrees; this holds of all other possible triangles. Every square has 4 equal sides and 4 right angles; this holds of all and every possible square, etc. etc. Here, then, the universal is always seen and considered in a single case.",
"blomberg-s08-613": "Every certain proposition is either indemonstrable, or immediate, or mediate, or demonstrably certain. That cognition which contains in itself the ground of the truth of another cognition is the proof-material (Beweisthum), and the connection of the cognitions",
"blomberg-s08-614": "with their truth is the proof. There are thus really 3 pieces to be met with here, namely",
"blomberg-s08-615": "1: the proof-material (Beweisthum) or the matter",
"blomberg-s08-616": "2 the proof, or the consequentia (consequence), or the form",
"blomberg-s08-617": "3 and finally the conclusion, which follows from the consequens.",
"blomberg-s08-618": "In every proof one really does not investigate the proposition to be proved, but the ratio probandi (ground of proving). The matter of the proof is the medius terminus (middle term), and the form the consequentia. Some proofs are empirical, other proofs are rational; and these are either mathematical, and then they are called demonstrationes, or they are philosophical, and incomplete probationes. If the convincing proofs lack evidence, the intuitive, and precision, exactness, then they are not yet demonstrationes.",
"blomberg-s08-619": "All rational proofs, supposing that they were even sufficient, are therefore not yet demonstrationes, still less are the empirical proofs demonstrationes. Empirical proofs can often be very sufficient and evident; nevertheless they are not demonstrationes, for they are not rational. The word indemonstrable (unerweislich) means here, as we have already mentioned above, such certain cognitions of whose certainty and truth one need not and cannot doubt at all.",
"blomberg-s08-620": "All demonstrable cognition is not clear through itself, but is held to be true only by means of other cognitions as a consequence. If one therefore holds a demonstrable cognition to be indemonstrably certain, one errs in the form. The proofs of the existence of God, of his properties, of his effects are not indemonstrable but demonstrable. In indemonstrably certain cognitions I postulate, as it were demand, assent. In demonstrably certain cognitions, however, I as it were beg and ask the concept from others. All immediate experiences are indemonstrably certain, and also cannot be proved at all. E.g. there is a sun in the sky, the barometer has fallen or risen; all this is immediately certain and therefore indemonstrable.",
"blomberg-s08-621": "But if I establish general judgments from experiences, or propositions of experience, they are always demonstrable. All rational and indemonstrable cognitions are axiomata, and they are either synthetic or analytic. In every judgment one conceives the subject as a mark of a thing that may otherwise be unknown to me. One thus knows merely the mark; this is known to one. E.g. the proposition: A body is divisible is analytic, for to the subject to which a body belongs, divisibility also belongs, and to that to which divisibility belongs, to the subject a body also belongs. A body, however, has an attractive force; this proposition is synthetic, for attractive force, separated from all experience, does not lie in the concept of a body.",
"blomberg-s08-622": "All analytic propositions, where the predicate lies in the notio (notion) of the subject, belong under the law of identity. The synthetic propositions, however, whose predicate lies not in the notion of the subject but in a mark that is different from the subject, do not belong under the principium identitatis and contradictionis (principle of identity and contradiction). This principle relates only to the analytic propositions; in synthetic propositions a predicate is added to the notion of the subject that is not contained in it. And all the propositions that have this constitution can only be cognized intuitively, not through reason. Through reason one can cognize no coordination at all, for reason only subordinates. All synthetic propositions are either immediate propositions of experience, or intuitive propositions that are produced through the understanding and do not lie in the notion of the subject. The analytic propositions, however, can be drawn out per analysin (by analysis) from the concept of the subject. For they are contained in the notion of the subject and subordinated to it. Here reason can busy itself and dissect and lay apart the subordinate concepts. E.g. gold does not rust. This is a synthetic proposition; it is evident merely from immediate experience, and can really not be derived at all from the notion of gold per analysin. If something is to be postulated, it must be so constituted that it convinces absolutely everyone. In a proof the falsity as to matter is only accidental, but as to form essential. The false matter can be improved, but the false form, on the other hand, is quite incorrigibile. Further, the falsity of the form is also far more widespread and general than the falsity of the matter. The proton pseudos (first falsehood), when someone infers to an absurdity or also assumes false propositions, likewise lies in the form, for it is remarkable that the form is subject not only to many but even to incorrigible faults.",
"blomberg-s08-623": "To prove something directe (directly) means to prove something in connection with the grounds. That is an ostensive proof, whereby one shows that what is the opposite is false, thus an apagogic proof.",
"blomberg-s08-624": "To prove something indirecte (indirectly), on the other hand, means to prove something in connection with its consequences. This latter, however, can never happen otherwise than negative, by way of negation. But we never cognize all the consequences of a thing, but many of them are still unknown to us. Hence it comes about that the indirect proof can never become a demonstration, but certainly a philosophical probation.",
"blomberg-s08-625": "If we want to prove that a cognition is false, this can really happen [thus:] The opposite of such a false cognition, drawn out, already proves the partial falsity of the cognition; hence it comes that the apagogic inference is so much liked, because it is the only one that can give apodictic certainty, whereas the direct inference is able to give nothing other than a comparative certainty. The apagogic proof, however, is also the one that comes nearest to evidence. It is also therefore far easier than the direct proof, since it brings the opposite ad absurdum, and it is settled that the absurdity always strikes the eye far more strongly than any and every truth, just as the greatest pleasure that has ceased is by far not so sensible to me as the opposite pain, and a pleasure is heightened through the pain that has gone before. Truth, e.g., has by far not the striking, the frappant quality about it that the representation of the absurdity of the opposite has. The fault of this proof, however, is: it can indeed prove that the opposite of the proposition is false, but it cannot prove it from itself. In this respect the direct, the genetic proof is far better than that; the latter discovers, namely, not only the truth but at the same time the genesis, the source of generation, of it.",
"blomberg-s08-626": "The apagogic proof, then, however liked it is, is used only so ad interim (provisionally) etc. The right true insight, however, one obtains really only through the direct genetic proof. A cognition is proved directe when its truth can be shown from its relation to the grounds. Indirecte, on the other hand, when its truth flows from the relation to the consequences. Direct proofs are really genetic, and are conducted per rationes essendi, non vero cognoscendi (through grounds of being, not of cognizing). Indirect proofs, however, are conducted per rationes cognoscendi, non essendi (through grounds of cognizing, not of being). To infer affirmatively from the consequences to the grounds is not possible at all, but this must always happen negatively.",
"blomberg-s08-627": "In order for me to say that something is demonstrated, the proof must be so constituted that it convinces everyone, that is, the proof must hold not for me alone but also for others. For demonstration evidence is required, so that the cognition and the proof of it is so constituted that one cannot possibly postpone one's assent. From this it is evident, then, that one must use the word demonstration very sparingly. Much can be a philosophical probation but no demonstration. The mathematical proofs, however, are of such a kind that they are all demonstrationes. Something therefore cannot be passed off as a demonstration until experience has taught that it is impossible to withhold one's assent to it and to oppose it, or until other human beings also agree in it that it is not possible to doubt the cognition any further.",
"blomberg-s08-628": "Demonstrationes are therefore to be met with only very rarely in philosophy. No aesthetic proof can therefore be a demonstration, for an aesthetic probation arises merely from the agreement of the cognition with our feeling and our taste; it is thus nothing other than a persuasion.",
"blomberg-s08-629": "Aesthetically I can prove something to someone as soon as I know his passions and his feeling and am in a position to move it. This probation, however, is not universally valid, and is therefore wrongly called a demonstration by our author.",
"blomberg-s08-630": "Everyone surely has an inclination to demonstrate; evidence pleases everyone; only it is a pity that it is so difficult and so rarely to be attained.",
"blomberg-s08-631": "The all too great inclination to demonstrate, says our author, is the Demonstrations-Sucht (mania for demonstration). Charlatans really have no mania for demonstrating themselves, but rather deceive those who are sick with the mania for demonstrating. The mania for demonstrating from pedantry, however, is the true mania for demonstration, and it really consists in this, that one has an inclination either to demonstrate everything or to assume nothing that is not demonstrated and shown beyond doubt. Pedantry in demonstrating thus believes with certainty that in the whole of human cognition one cannot get on at all without demonstration. This pedantry can nevertheless very often be combined with a very sharp critique of cognition. It is thus really no ignorance, nor an error, but one perhaps does not demonstrate at all because something cannot be demonstrated, and prefers not to assume at all such cognitions as cannot be demonstrated. One can nourish this mania for demonstrating very much if one knows how to conceal the weak side of demonstrations. One puts it off, however, when one knows and has insight that in many things nothing at all can be demonstrated; and when one thus recognizes that much is recognized as a demonstration that is not proved at all, or is only a probation.",
"blomberg-s08-632": "In our present age, however, no mania for demonstration rules at all, but a certain shallowness, a kind of gallantry even in learned cognitions (Gelahrten Erkenntnißen). The latter, however, is still always far more tolerable than the former. For shallowness requires only an addition in order to become thorough; it is thus entirely capable of improvement. The mania for demonstrating, on the other hand, is incorrigible; one very often assumes false cognitions as true, and is moreover proud of this very falsity, and not at all eager to let oneself be instructed and rightly convinced of the opposite of it.",
"blomberg-s08-633": "Sensation (Empfindung) is the representation of our present state, insofar as it stems from the presence of a certain object. Sometimes representations arise in us of which we ourselves are the authors, but whose presence we derive from the existence of a thing. And then it happens that we often confuse fictions with sensations, and in this way we commit an error. We can often fabricate something for ourselves, but this is no sensation at all. Thus, e.g., the fear of an impending pain is different from the pain that is present. Even the fear of pain, namely, is a fiction; the pain itself, on the other hand, is a sensation. Nothing further can be said or raisoniren (reasoned), i.e. vernünftelen (subtilized), about sensations, for they are the principium materiale (material principle) of all our representations.",
"blomberg-s08-634": "A representation of sensation cannot be fabricated by us at all, but must really have been given to us by means of the senses. Experience and sensation are distinguished in form and in degree. In matter both are one and the same, but in experience there is in addition a form, reason. Experience is nothing other than a reflected sensation, or a sensation that is expressed through a judgment.",
"blomberg-s08-635": "Experiences, namely, are not mere concepts and representations but also judgments; e.g. the representations of warmth or of cold are concepts of experience. They are general constitutions of things, but these can be understood not merely through the senses but really through judgments. Unreasoning animals therefore have no experience but only sensations. Everyone who can describe the objects of his experience has experience, for description requires not merely sensation but also a judgment.",
"blomberg-s08-636": "Mere sensations, without judging about them, cannot be described. Every experience is really immediate, and mediate experiences are, according to our author, contradictiones in adjecto (contradictions in the adjunct). The judgment that is passed immediately on the occasion of sensations is the judgment of experience (Erfahrungs-Urtheil).",
"blomberg-s08-637": "All judgment is either empirical or rational. Some can be empirical as to matter but rational as to form. Some, however, can be rational both as to matter and as to form. The form of all our experiences is rational. All experiences have the form of reason, and without it they would be no experiences.",
"blomberg-s08-638": "In experiences we must be active, through reason; in sensations, however, we are merely passive. The proofs of certain cognitions are as to form always rational, but as to matter they can arise either from experience or from reason. A proof without the form of reason is no proof at all; if we want to prove something, we must after all infer from certain data, supposing that the matter also arises from the senses or is a sensation, and therefore every proof is rational. Nevertheless it is called empirical if its matter is a sensation, or if the ground of proof stems from experience. If one has an empirical proof, the ground of proof is immediately a sensation, and so always stems, as to experience, from sensation. Immediate sensations cannot be proved.",
"blomberg-s08-639": "We must learn well to distinguish from one another the inferences from experiences and the judicia discursiva (discursive judgments) about experiences.",
"blomberg-s08-640": "Nothing, however, is more deceptive than that one infers secretly from certain examples without knowing that one infers, and therefore passes off an inference from experience as an immediate experience. Immediate experience shows us only what is in our sense, without our inferring thereby.",
"blomberg-s08-641": "The judgment that expresses the appearance is an immediate judgment of experience. If we now mix up the judgment that expresses the appearance with those judgments that express the causes of the appearance, we confuse the judicium discursivum with the judicium intuitivum. The judicium discursivum is thus that which [expresses] the cause of the appearance, the intuitivum that which expresses the appearance itself.",
"blomberg-s08-642": "The care not to mix the judicia discursiva with the judicia intuitiva is of particularly great importance, and it is indispensably necessary to apply them properly, so that one does not fall into various errors. All proofs happen from reason, and the demonstration of a cognition from an immediate experience is not a proof but a fact, as it were an inspectio ocularis (inspection by eye).",
"blomberg-s08-643": "A proof from reason, however, of which the author speaks in the 204th paragraph, is such a proof as arises from reason both with regard to the ground of proof, or the matter, and with regard to the formal, and so needs no experience. The proofs from reason have indeed as ground of proof such propositions as arise from reason, but in part these propositions arise, as to their first origin, from experience. A proof from reason is thus not always a proof of pure reason without application of experience. A proof through pure reason, however, can be conducted only of such cognitions as are not objects of the senses at all. If cognitions are only in part objects of the senses, they must already, as to their first principia, be derived from experience. Proofs of pure reason therefore also find a place only in mathematics and metaphysics. That which can be demonstrated through pure reason not only needs no proof from experience but does not permit it at all. There have been some who have tried to invent an experimental geometry, but all their efforts have become nothing but child's play.",
"blomberg-s08-644": "Experiences permit no general judgments at all, except only of possibility. Experience cannot teach me with apodictic certainty that all human beings must die, e.g., but only that all human beings who have lived up to now have died. A proof from pure reason, however, proves general propositions, and can therefore no longer be supported at all by proofs from experience, for these prove no general propositions. A demonstration from pure reason is so different from the proof from experience that if both were to be combined and joined together, a bastard would necessarily arise from it. The proofs from pure reason are like a light against which all proofs from experience vanish, just as the light of the moon is wholly dispensable and vanishes when the rays of the sun suddenly make the day. Everything that can be proved through pure reason receives through experience no new proof but only a mere illustration and elucidation. To have the idea of a thing is very difficult.",
"blomberg-s08-645": "One can have much utility from a science, indeed have solid cognitions of it, without nevertheless having an idea of it. So it evidently goes, e.g., with logic, with jurisprudence.",
"blomberg-s08-646": "Logic is a science that states the actions that arise as to form. All conceptus (concepts) are 1: empirici, which arise from experience; 2: intellectuales, which arise from the understanding; 3: arbitrarii, i.e. concepts arising from choice (Willkühr). There are intuitions in the concepts, i.e. perceptiones communes (common perceptions), generally valid or common representations. All perceptiones, however, are",
"blomberg-s08-647": "1) intuitive, intuiting",
"blomberg-s08-648": "2 do. (ditto) reflexive, requiring reflection.",
"blomberg-s08-649": "The reflexive perceptiones, however, are again",
"blomberg-s08-650": "A) Communes",
"blomberg-s08-651": "B) Singulares",
"blomberg-s08-652": "In logic, however, all perceptiones must be communes. If they are applied separately, they receive the names of particular concepts. Through logical abstraction no cognition at all can arise. Through a conceptus communis I can consider an object",
"blomberg-s08-653": "a) in abstracto or also B) in concreto.",
"blomberg-s08-654": "The word conceptus abstractus, however, is a bad expression. For what can the art of the human understanding be able to form generally valid concepts, conceptus communes? They serve to assign each concept its place, and they are as it were the representatives of a multitude of things; they thus serve especially for elucidation. The common concept contains many under itself; these concepts, however, which it contains under itself, contain it in themselves; it is contained in them.",
"blomberg-s08-655": "A wonderful counter-relation, e.g.: humanity goes to all human beings, and in all human beings humanity nevertheless lies. The multitude of things, however, that I can think under the conceptus communis make up the sphaera conceptus (sphere of the concept). The more things a mark belongs to, the greater a concept is according to its extent, i.e. according to its capacity, the less it contains in itself in return.",
"blomberg-s08-656": "The concept of something holds of everything and contains nothing in itself, and so it always is. Every conceptus communis, however, can be considered as a conceptus superior, a higher concept, yet it is called so especially only insofar as it in turn contains other conceptus communes in itself, e.g. the human being the man. There is thus a subordination of our concepts. A conceptus communis is called, with regard to the concepts that are contained under it, the genus, the kind (Gattung); with regard to those concepts, however, in which it is contained, the species. Thus, e.g., the human being is the genus with regard to men etc., but with regard to a rational being the human being is again the species. The limit of the concepts subordinated to one another, however, is the conceptus summus, i.e. that conceptus which is indeed a genus but no species. The conceptus infimus, on the other hand, will be a species that has no genus any more. A conceptus singularis, however, has no sphere. The genus summum, then, and the individuum are the two limits of the logically subordinated concepts. Every conceptus has an extent of the concepts to which it goes, the sphaera notionis (sphere of the notion), and the proposition is known: Quo major est Sphaera Conceptus, eo minus in illo est Contentum, et vice versa (The greater the sphere of a concept, the less is contained in it, and vice versa). That conceptus whose sphaera is only a part of the sphaera of another conceptus is called a conceptus angustior (narrower concept).",
"blomberg-s08-657": "A conceptus singularis (singular concept) has no extension at all, and therefore also no sphaera (sphere).",
"blomberg-s08-658": "Cognitio a priori is a cognition, and probatio a priori is a proof from above downwards. Cognitio a posteriori, however, is a cognition, and probatio a posteriori a proof from below upwards. These expressions fit far better than the author's expressions “from behind” and “from the front.” Proofs a posteriori are proofs from experience; proofs a priori, on the other hand, are proofs from reason.",
"blomberg-s08-659": "If one proves something from pure reason, no experience takes place. But if one demonstrates something from experience, the proofs from pure reason drop out; nevertheless some proofs can be conducted partim a priori, partim a posteriori (partly a priori, partly a posteriori), when experience is the origin of the proofs of reason. Reason and experience, however, are connected either through coordination or through subordination. In subordination I lay an experience down as the ground and infer from it by means of reason.",
"blomberg-s08-660": "In coordination, however, I first prove entirely from reason alone, and then entirely from experience alone, so that the proof from experience serves the proof from reason ad evidentiam (for evidence). In this way the connubium rationis et experientiae (marriage of reason and experience) is brought forth. Experience has no need at all of the proof from reason, and conversely the proof from reason has no need of the proof from experience. Neither is subordinate to the other; the two do not make up one proof but two proofs. For in these proofs there is merely a coordination. One can, however, be very much deceived in this. If the proof from reason is a demonstration, or a complete proof, then the proof from experience serves it for no greater strengthening, but only for illustration; and if the proof from experience is a complete proof, the proof from reason helps it not at all either. Proofs from experience and from reason make no coordination at all, and still less, once they are coordinated, a distinctness. Many mutually coordinated insufficient proofs never yield a demonstration; they are mere probationes (probable proofs) and can bring about no apodictic certainty at all. Proofs from experience and from reason can therefore not possibly possess their evidence unless they are subordinated and subordinate to one another. Where we already find very many proofs of a thing, we can already infer that we shall never attain apodictic certainty; a single proof from sufficient grounds is far better than 1000 insufficient proofs that are subordinated to one another, because one is still not in a position to attain full certainty through them. It does happen often, to be sure, that in mathematics as in the other sciences more than one sufficient proof of one and the same thing is given; but this happens for the most part more in order to show one's skill in demonstrating than because one requires still more proofs for the evidence of the thing. But if several mutually coordinated insufficient proofs are nevertheless adduced, they serve for no greater distinctness and certainty, but the matter remains as before, undecided.",
"blomberg-s08-661": "One can opine something without believing, namely when one has more grounds for than against the cognition. Here I hold something to be true without its having any influence on our actions. To know something, however, means nothing other than to cognize something with certainty. In belief, however, there is a great degree of holding-to-be-true, so that one would be willing to wager that something is true.",
"blomberg-s08-662": "Belief is either a belief in things or in a person. One can hold a thing to be true immediately, merely because one has grounds for the thing, and then it is called believing a thing. Conversely, one can also believe a thing immediately because a person declares it true, which one would otherwise not have held true in itself, and then one believes in a person. This belief, however, which relates to a certain kind of person, is twofold. One sometimes believes anyone at all, whoever he may be, merely because he says something, without attending to his person, or his caractère (character) or station. If someone says, for example, that it has snowed in his region, or that many ships have arrived, one believes this simply, without investigating much; one thus does not judge at all about the credibility of the witnesses. But in certain matters one does not believe just anyone, rather a particular credibility is required from us in the one who declares something true, in order that one accept it and hold it true. Belief toward a person, however, is either moral or historical. The former consists in trust in the other's uprightness, even though he has made no statement of a narrative. The latter consists in holding true what the other asserts, merely because he affirms it. For historical belief no particular morality needs to be in the other; rather one holds something true because he relates something that has happened. Moral belief, however, requires that one have a particular trust toward a person, that she will be kind and upright. E.g. when a faithful servant, in his master's absence, in an arising great danger, abandons his own property and saves his master's, in the hope that he will reward these services well, this is a moral belief. Historical belief alone is required in theology, that one hold something true on account of a statement. This belief, however, anyone can have, even the most abandoned. Without fidelity and belief no republic, no commonwealth could subsist, and no pacta (agreements) could take place either. Fidelity is always required in regard to him who promises something, that he also keep what was promised; belief, however, in regard to him to whom something is promised, that he namely assume as true that the other will keep his promise. Both must be joined together. For even if everyone kept faith but no one believed, no agreements etc. could take place, and just as little if all believed but no one wished to keep faith. Belief is therefore very necessary in common life; if no one wished to give credit, things would indeed often look bad, for one cannot always pay cash at once. It also indicates a very bad way of thinking if one trusts no one anything but wants everything that is promised and pledged to one to be immediately present and fulfilled. The Greeks were of such a kind; they believed no one, but wanted everything paid out in cash at once. Hence the proverb arose: graeca fide mercari (to trade on Greek faith).",
"blomberg-s08-663": "Theological belief trusts someone merely on his statement and holds it true. In historical belief the object of belief is always a thing that has happened, an experience of the other, but not a judgment of reason; hence, e.g., if someone said to me with confidence: “Believe me, there are inhabitants in the moon,” I will naturally be able to answer him: “Have you been there? Have you seen them? What you have produced merely through reason, I cannot believe you.” Consequently all judgments in which the other reasons by subtle argument (vernünftelt) are no objects of historical belief. Theological belief is therefore merely historical. Religious belief, however, is not merely historical but moral, in that one holds something true on account of a promise, and therefore undertakes certain actions, in the hope of one day reaping good consequences from them in the future. Through this belief one must hope that there will be a future world, and future rewards or punishments for these actions. The natural, moral belief is in all virtuous people, in that one believes that good actions will be rewarded of themselves, although one also sees no particular promise for oneself in this. Such a virtuous person is to be compared to a creditor; he hopes for the future good, supposing he has as yet no trace of it. Here, however, we do not speak at all of this moral belief, although it is the most worthy of consideration and the most excellent, in that one receives credit, and trusts not merely the present but the future, in that one does not want everything paid in the world, but some things also in a future life.",
"blomberg-s08-664": "All dogmata permit no other ground of holding-to-be-true than either one's own experience, or that one conducts observations of reason about them, or reasons by subtle argument. The experience of others therefore takes place in it. It follows, then, that they also cannot be cognized through belief. [841a]",
"blomberg-s08-665": "The subject, however, that asserts something, and whom I am to believe, must chiefly have three properties: 1mo) A capacity to make an experience; it must therefore have rational reflection. 2do) it must have a capacity to retain faithfully the experience made, and to preserve it; or a good memory. 3tio) it must also be able to declare clearly the experiences made, to set them forth, and to make them known to everyone. The moral constitution of the subject, however, is that it also actually has the disposition of mind, seu ut animum habeat (or that it have the intention), to represent and declare the experiences faithfully, just as they were made by it, and not otherwise; and the pragmatic principle for believing the subject is finally: that it has no grounds or advantages to expect if it asserts the opposite of the experience that it has made.",
"blomberg-s08-666": "What can we therefore accept on belief? On belief one cannot accept dogmatic cognitions that the other asserts, but merely experience. General cognitions of reason one cannot accept on belief for this reason, that one can very easily err in them. Assertions of dogmata are therefore no testimonies, but assertions of empirical cognitions; of experiences there are testimonies. A witness is therefore properly he who asserts an experience. But he who asserts general judgments of reason as true is no witness, and the best historical belief therefore also does not go to dogmata (insofar as they are divine dogmata), but merely to experiences. The conditions, however, under which a subject who has made experiences is to be believed are grounded partly in his own properties, partly in his condition. The properties of the subject are partly logical, in that it is able to have experiences, to make them, to know, and to assert. Partly moral, in that it also has the will to assert the truth, or to declare the experiences as they were. In regard to credibility with respect to the condition, however, one must also divide the condition",
"blomberg-s08-667": "A:) into the logical condition and B) into the practical condition: the most upright man can sometimes come into such circumstances that his uprightness succumbs. E.g. when he is kept by threats or promises from telling the truth; therefore one must also pay attention to his condition.",
"blomberg-s08-668": "A large part of our cognitions arises through belief, and without belief we would have to do without very many historical cognitions. We would have no greater cognitions than at most of the place where we live and the time in which we live.",
"blomberg-s08-669": "A great aid of historical cognitions is therefore to adopt the experiences of others; through this we can learn what happened 1000 years before us and 1000 miles away from us.",
"blomberg-s08-670": "If a very great deception of the senses took place, we could not trust the experiences that others have made; if, further, those who made experiences had so defective a memory that they could not remember them, and the imagination presented other images to them; if finally our language had such faults that we could not express ourselves properly and communicate our experiences, then the communication of others' experiences would be quite impossible. But if the opposite is presupposed, as it is indeed actually true that 1:)mo we always have the capacity to make experiences, and then 2:) our memory is also faithful enough to retain and preserve the experiences had, without being disturbed by the imagination, 3) we are also in our language able to make all experiences made known to others, then for the credibility of this or that person who has made experiences it is still required that they be honest and have an inclination to truth. This inclination to truth, however, often arises more from love of honor than from honesty, namely because the whole commercium (intercourse) of human beings would be abolished if no one asserted the truth, since then no one would trust another; because lying too is something harmful, and the assertion of truth is the surest way to escape contempt. E.g. geography, physics, history, and other sciences always presuppose the experiences of others. In common life one must not believe the common man in regard to such cognitions as do not concern the interest of all human beings but are indifferent. For in such cases it is all one to him whether something is so or otherwise constituted, since he does not see the importance of the cognition. The learned man, however, already has much more credibility in this kind of cognitions, for to him even the slightest thing in every cognition will be of importance, and out of love of honor he will also be more inclined to assert everything as exactly as he has experienced it, since otherwise he would lose his credit.",
"blomberg-s08-671": "As for the credibility and honesty of witnesses who assert experiences made, everyone is held honest and upright until the contrary, namely that he departs from the truth etc., has been proven. According to the well-known rule of equity:",
"blomberg-s08-672": "Quilibet prosumitur bonus, (Everyone is presumed good,)",
"blomberg-s08-673": "Donec probetur contrarium. (Until the contrary is proved.)",
"blomberg-s08-674": "But in order to arrive at the right cognition of the truth, it is certainly required that one also investigate whether someone speaks the truth or lies.",
"blomberg-s08-675": "An eyewitness is one who has himself made experiences or seen something, and is thus fully convinced of a piece of knowledge.",
"blomberg-s08-676": "A hearsay witness, however, is one who has heard something from an eyewitness, i.e. has received it, or who is a witness of the testimony of the eyewitness. It is one of the greatest human faults that they are so very much inclined, when they are hearsay witnesses of a thing, to want to be eyewitnesses. They relate a thing with such certainty and confidence as if they had seen it themselves, though they have only received it from others. But what is the difference in credibility between eyewitnesses and hearsay witnesses? The hearsay witness has, as it were, only half as much credibility as the eyewitness, for in his case I must first examine his own credibility, and then also that of him who saw the thing.",
"blomberg-s08-677": "In the series of mutually subordinated witnesses credibility diminishes. In the series of mutually coordinated witnesses, however, the probability and credibility of the testimony always increases. E.g. if someone describes the history of a learned man or of a king, but his successor does not hand down the history itself but clothes it in his own style, and this goes on thus continuo (continuously) ever further, then credibility thereby becomes ever smaller and smaller; but if many at the same time assert one and the same thing, the credibility is only so much the greater, especially if these are people who love honor.",
"blomberg-s08-678": "The series of mutually coordinated witnesses is called public rumor, sive rumor (or rumor). The series of mutually subordinated assertions is called an oral transmission, traditio oralis. But if in the series of mutually coordinated as well as subordinated witnesses the first originator, i.e. the eyewitness, is unknown, then this is a mere common saying, sive rumor sine capite (or headless rumor); and such an assertion is little or not at all to be trusted, little belief is to be attached to it. There are, however, certain assertions in which one has no need at all to examine the credibility of the witness, but which one rejects before any examination.",
"blomberg-s08-679": "This seems, to be sure, at first sight to be very harsh, but it is nevertheless certain. Cognitions that are wholly and entirely and manifestly false need no examination at all, and deserve a complete contempt. Of this kind are the assertions about the divining rod; these deserve no examination, one can reject them at once, they deserve no refutation, for they are false in and for themselves.",
"blomberg-s08-680": "There are thus grounds for rejecting a testimony that are not historical or taken from the constitution of the witness, but are rational and lie in the constitution of the thing itself. Yet one must be very cautious in this, and not reject a cognition at once until it is evident that the thing is manifestly false. Much seems quite false at first sight, but on closer examination one finds the opposite. All empirical judgments about the possibility or impossibility of an eventus (event) do not yet entitle us to reject the cognition straightaway without examining the credibility of the witness. Someone testifies to something that is very improbable and contradicts the ordinary occurrences in nature; he cannot yet demand that one believe him at once on his statement, but if he wants this, he must furnish the proof. So, e.g., when Pontoppidan writes that in certain seas there is an exceedingly great fish that looks like a piece of land, and which he calls Crac (the kraken), he must prove this, since such an animal has never been seen. But if someone testifies to something that agrees evidently and well with experience and reason, one believes him without proof. But if the credibility of the witness cannot be shaken at all, while the narrative or testimony he gives contradicts reason and experience in the highest degree, the question arises: must one in this case give assent to his statement and as it were deny one's reason, or not?",
"blomberg-s08-681": "Answer: The best then is neither to reject the cognition nor to accept it as true, but to postpone one's assent until one has more grounds for or against it. There are sometimes persons who assert something, whose credibility one cannot doubt in the least, of whom one also cannot assume that they had the animus (intention), or purpose, of deceiving others, whom one nevertheless may not trust. The fault of their assertion, however, then lies for the most part only in the experience not having been made, or in a vitium (defect) of memory, which does not retain it faithfully enough. Hence it also happens that people who have put forward something very probably and asserted it as true, when one asks them whether they could swear to this statement of theirs, hesitate and must resort to all sorts of evasions. They then commonly say they might well have forgotten something since that time. The imagination can also certainly reproduce to them other images than the experiences were that they had.",
"blomberg-s08-682": "Belief can be taken in the moral sense, when one lacks all trust in the moral constitution of a witness. He who has such a moral unbelief holds all human beings to be liars, he trusts no human being, he believes no promises, but always doubts the sincerity of other people. A moral unbelief is opposed to the moral foundation of all human society. Without fidelity and belief, as has already been indicated above, no state can subsist. It is, however, commonly to be found in those who themselves possess a moral pravitas (depravity), so that one cannot attach much belief to them.",
"blomberg-s08-683": "These people also judge other people by themselves. Moral belief, on the other hand, trusts everyone, even without any promises. Of this kind is the belief in a future world. Incredulity, however, is distinguished from unbelief. It consists in one's being unwilling to give assent to the logical grounds of a testimony. This incredulity can also be called logical belief (sic), and is thus very different from moral unbelief.",
"blomberg-s08-684": "This logical unbelief lies not in a certain lack of the heart but of cognition, in a certain inexperience, in that one does not know the grounds of others and does not see their importance. It is therefore chiefly found in those who do not have a proper acquaintance with literature; to these everything appears paradoxical and ridiculous that is otherwise true and credible. One must therefore be very careful to avoid having dealings with such people, since one is not at all in a position to convince them.",
"blomberg-s08-685": "A seeing belief is that which is joined with an examination of the witness and rests on it. A blind belief, however, is that which accepts a testimony without examination and investigation of its credibility. The former leads to truth, the other, however, is the way to error, hence harmful, the former, however, useful.",
"blomberg-s08-686": "The union of all these three sources of cognition rests on this, that they have not one but many proofs as ground. Belief always requires a connubium (marriage) of other sources of cognition, namely of reason and of experience, so that it become evident and convincing. If a source of cognition is not in a position to give a sufficient proof of the truth of a thing, one must take the remaining fontes cognitionis (sources of cognition) to help, and seek to bring forth from them a greater evidence. But if one source of cognition is adequate to give a complete proof of a thing, one has no need of the others. If something can be demonstrated completely and evidently through reason, one does not need experience to make the proof still more complete. If experience is sufficient to give a complete proof of a thing, one need not consult reason.",
"blomberg-s09-001": "If a cognition contains within itself motives (Bewegungsgründe) for actions, it is practical. All practical cognition is either a cognition of ends or of means; the latter must be distinguished from the former. Practical geometry teaches no ends, no motives for actions, but only means to attain certain ends. It shows the conditions under which an end can be achieved with regard to measuring.",
"blomberg-s09-002": "All doctrine of skill is a practical cognition of means; all doctrine of morality, however, is a practical cognition of ends, as is also the doctrine of prudence. Our author speaks in this whole section of cognition as it relates to the free will; in logic, however, the relation of cognition to the will is not considered at all, but this belongs in morals. The relation of the free will is not an objectum domesticum (proper object) of logic. The practical science of skill gives us no motives at all for certain actions. For any science that shows only means and no ends contains no motives; all motives, namely, must be derived from the end. A cognition is called practical insofar as it is contradistinguished from the theoretical",
"blomberg-s09-003": "cognition, or from mere contemplation and speculation. A cognition, however, is practical vel explicite, vel implicite (either explicitly or implicitly). Explicite when it shows the rules for actions themselves; implicite when it offers the motives or represents such truths from which certain practical rules for actions can be derived. E.g. the proposition: you shall not lie, you shall be faithful and honest, is explicite practical. The proposition, however: there is a God, consequently one is obligated also to arrange one's actions in such a way as is in conformity with the holy will of this divine being, is implicite practical. In metaphysics there are for the most part pure speculationes (speculations), not practical but theoretical, which therefore have no influence on the free will. Such speculations are neither harmful nor useful. For they contain no grounds for actions at all. Many subtleties and errors devised by human beings are nothing other than pure speculations; we therefore pass over this section, because it is introduced inappropriately here and belongs more in morals than in logic, and no advantage is to be hoped for in logic from its treatment, for logic likewise occupies itself with speculative cognitions.",
"blomberg-s10-001": "Not every representation is a concept. A representation through the senses is, e.g., a sensation. A representation through the understanding is an appearance. A representation through reason is a concept. The senses sense, the understanding coordinates, reason, on the other hand, subordinates. General representations are concepts, and concepts are general representations. Sensations, on the other hand, are only singular representations, appearances likewise only so; concepts, however, are general and arise through reason. So long as I cannot yet subordinate a thing to a general representation, so long I have as yet no concept of it. Of many things we have a learned cognition, but yet no proper concept.",
"blomberg-s10-002": "In learned and unlearned concepts there is no specific difference to be noticed. The difference between common and learned concepts is better. A concept is not exactly a simple representation. The concept, the result of many representations, can be very rich, pregnant, beautiful, and learned, and lively. A concept that possesses much liveliness is an aesthetic concept. An aesthetic concept arises through a multitude and combination of many coordinated representations. A proper concept of reason, however, arises through a multitude of subordinated representations. The matter of an aesthetic concept consists in its relation to our taste and feeling, the form, however, in a multitude of mutually coordinated representations, in order thereby to bring forth the proper liveliness. Such aesthetic concepts are used especially by the poets and the orators, when, e.g., they want to describe summer or spring: they do not show its causes, but only depict the manifold changes that go on in these seasons in the field, among the animals, in the woods, in the air, in the country, and in the city. Of many things we have sensations, to be sure, but no concepts. Concepts we meet with only in certain cases, where reason subordinates the representations to one another.",
"blomberg-s10-003": "This division in this § is in no way correct. Through abstraction we obtain no representations; rather representations must already be given before abstraction, and are only made clear through it. All our cognitions are either given, or made and invented. Omnes conceptus sunt vel dati, vel facti, sive ficti (All concepts are either given, or made, or feigned).",
"blomberg-s10-004": "Through experience many cognitions are given to us, and the matter of all cognitions arises through sensation, through the senses. Often, however, one invents cognitions for oneself through the imagination; we form for ourselves, as it were, our own world.",
"blomberg-s10-005": "Conceptus dati (given concepts) are further dati a priori (given a priori) through pure reason, vel dati a posteriori (or given a posteriori) through experiences. All conceptus a posteriori dati are drawn off ab experientia (from experience). Others, however, do not arise through experiences, but are drawn off from the laws of pure reason, e.g. the concept of the possible and impossible. A conceptus abstractus (abstract concept), however, signifies especially a representation that is drawn off from experience, and not a representation drawn off from reason.",
"blomberg-s10-006": "The conceptus dati are therefore either abstracti (abstracted) from experience, or rationales (rational).",
"blomberg-s10-007": "The concept of all kinds and genera of things that fall under our senses is a conceptus abstractus, e.g. of horses, sheep etc.: when I say a horse, this is a concept given per experientiam (through experience). The generality of the representations, however, has arisen through abstraction. In all concepts through experience, the generality arises through abstraction from single concepts and images, the representation itself, however, arises through experience. The matter itself thus lies here in experience, the form of generality, however, lies in abstraction. Not the least cognition arises through abstraction, but the generality arises through it. The first origin of our cognition is therefore experience. Concepts of experience are therefore those that are given through experience and have become general through abstraction. Hence all concepts of experience are abstracted concepts. The pure concepts of reason, however, are not given through experience by means of separation, but through pure reason, and by this they are distinguished from the concepts of experience. The concept of right and wrong is in this way a pure concept of reason. These concepts remain constant, supposing that no experience at all took place. If the world were ever so godless, everyone would still have the concept of right and wrong. The concepts of experience, however, do not take place at all without experience. If there were no horse, no one would be in a position to feign a horse.",
"blomberg-s10-008": "Invented concepts, however, are those that were made, insofar as they are not given. These invented concepts are called idea prototypa (prototype ideas). The abstracted concepts, however, are ἔκτυπα (ectypa, copies). The former are the originals; these concepts, however, are the copies. He who made the fine fairy tales made nothing but prototypa, for such a thing does not exist at all.",
"blomberg-s10-009": "The Stoic sage, on the other hand, is such a Πρωτότυπον ἀρχέτυπον (prototype, archetype). All definitions of the mathematicians contain nothing but ideas prototypae, e.g. the concept of the octagon is not borrowed from experience, but the mathematician thinks it for himself through his pure reason. He represents to himself a figure that has 8 lines and is enclosed by them.",
"blomberg-s10-010": "Every inventor must therefore invent and feign, and all inventions are fictions, although not all fictions are inventions. Fiction brings forth archetypes, abstraction ectypes, through arbitrary combination or through arbitrary separation.",
"blomberg-s10-011": "Through omitting and adding one makes fictiones (fictions). All fiction is, further, arbitrary and involuntary. Involuntary fiction is a chimera.",
"blomberg-s10-012": "Hypochondriacal persons are very much subject to involuntary fictions. Here, however, is properly the place to speak of arbitrary fictions, which are connected with consciousness and with knowing that one has them. If the human being can subject everything to his arbitrary will, he is the most perfect among all beings, and therefore arbitrary fictions will often be very useful to him.",
"blomberg-s10-013": "All concepts that do not arise through attention, through the senses, that are also not given but arbitrarily invented, are pure concepts of reason. Of this kind are the concepts of the necessary and the contingent, of causes and effects. Concepts, however, that arise through the senses and are drawn off from experiences are concepts of experience.",
"blomberg-s10-014": "We must chiefly take pains to distinguish concepts of experience from concepts of reason. What does not fall under the senses does not belong to experience. From the mixing of concepts of experience and of reason arises the vitium subreptionis (fault of subreption), of which there is talk in the Metaphysica. Right and wrong are no concepts of experience, but pure concepts of reason; they would be concepts of experience, however, if wrong were what is not customary, what human beings do not do.",
"blomberg-s10-015": "But this never takes place. Much is right that no one does, and wrong that all do. In the concept of cause and effect many commit the vitium subreptionis, in that they want to derive it from experience, though it is a pure concept of reason. Concepts of experience all relate to space and time; concepts of reason, however, to the foundation of cause and effect. Of possibility and impossibility we have no experience, but only of actuality. We also have experiences only of affirmative, not of negative representations; what cannot be cannot become sensation. E.g. when we have read a book, we cannot immediately experience that this or that passage is not contained in it, but we must work this out through inferences.",
"blomberg-s10-016": "Here we have nothing in particular to note; metaphysics, namely, speaks of it at greater length. The only question that remains for us is: how does one make concepts out of many experiences? This is not so easy as it perhaps seems at first sight. One can have made many experiences and yet have drawn off no concepts of experience from them.",
"blomberg-s10-017": "To obtain concepts of experience, instruction is required. Anyone who has good organa sensoria (sense organs) can experience; but to draw off from single experiences general concepts of experience, under which the experientiae singulae (singular experiences) can be subordinated, requires a skill in seeing what is common to many experiences.",
"blomberg-s10-018": "One cannot make money by depriving someone of it, and just so one cannot make concepts through separation either. Through separation our representations are only made general, as has already been indicated above. If we have no representations at all of things, then no abstraction will be able to make concepts for us. In logical separation we compare many concepts with one another, we see what they have in common or in what they agree, and thereby our representations become concepts.",
"blomberg-s10-019": "If many concepts have a mark in common, this mark is contained in them. The things are contained under the concept that is separated from them. This concept is a common ground of cognizing; it is contained in all things, and all things from which it is abstracted are under it. A concept can be abstracted from experience, and also from the laws of pure reason. The latter are not separated from things, but are called rational.",
"blomberg-s10-020": "Under separated concepts, however, concepts of experience are always understood.",
"blomberg-s10-021": "We shall first speak something of invented concepts. Every concept that has been given neither through experience, i.e. the sensation of things through our senses, nor through reason, is an invented concept. This concept, however, is either made so that, in regard to the matter, an experience yet lies at the ground, or so that not even the matter is taken from experience, but both the matter and the form are invented. The latter, however, is impossible. All invention, however, arises either through arbitrary combination or through separation. Through combination, when I tie together what in experience has never been combined and united with one another. Through separation, however, when I separate from a thing that which in experience has never been separated from it. For the most part, in invented concepts arbitrary combination and arbitrary separation are combined with one another. The novelist ascribes to his hero all laudable properties that he still lacks, in order to present a really perfect person, and if he notices only a few faults in him, he at once abstracts from them, he separates them all from him at once, or if this often cannot be done with difficulty, or at all [889/890] he is at least concerned to make him praiseworthy even in his transgressions, and to be able to give all his vices such a varnish or semblance that they either lose what is peculiar to them and striking to the eye as ugly, or even seem to be so many virtues.",
"blomberg-s10-022": "The Arcadian pastoral life, so famous among the ancients and so highly praised by the poets of that time, is of this kind; it is one of the most agreeable representations that one can ever have or imagine. For everything beautiful, pleasant, and charming is thought of it, the greatest abundance of everything that can charm is joined with it, and conversely all and every adversity, displeasure, all want, which yet very often has been and is wont to be found in pastoral life, is separated from it and removed, so that thereby a wholly perfect ideal of happiness might be sketched.",
"blomberg-s10-023": "All conceptus, however, are either",
"blomberg-s10-024": "a) conceptus singulares (singular concepts) or b) conceptus communes (common concepts).",
"blomberg-s10-025": "In the former I think to myself only one thing; through the latter, however, I go further, namely I think to myself there already that which is common to many things.",
"blomberg-s10-026": "The former concepts therefore consider something individual, so e.g. Roma, Bucephalus etc. This is a conceptus singularis. The latter, on the other hand, go to a complexus (aggregate) of many individual things, so a city in general, e.g., a 4-footed animal etc. A human being, that is a conceptus communis. The representations of immediate experience are all conceptus singulares, for they represent single things. Mediate concepts of experience, however, which are abstracted from many experiences, are conceptus communes, as are also all our concepts of reason. The concept of God applies, to be sure, to only one being, to which alone it is to be ascribed; yet one can, without deciding whether more than one God is possible, or only one, form more concepts of God. Afterwards, however, a proof must be added that the concept of God is a conceptus singularis. Every conceptus communis contains under itself all the concepts to which it is common. E.g. the concept of mortality holds under itself all human beings and animals, because it is common to them. Yet such a conceptus communis is contained in all those things that it contains under itself. The conceptus communis is the ground of cognition, or the mark, of all those that are under it; but it is at the same time also a partial concept of the things it contains under itself, for it is abstracted from the things that are contained under it.",
"blomberg-s10-027": "The marks are always coordinated to a thing, and as parts together make up the whole concept of the thing, the thing, however, is subordinated to the marks that are given of it, and contained in them. The sphaera notionis (sphere of a notion) properly signifies the multitude of things that are comprehended under a concept as a nota communis (common mark). Through a nota communis I can therefore think to myself many things. The sphaera conceptus communis (sphere of a common concept), however, can be large and can be small: the very same concept, under which many things are comprehended, is contained in the things that are under it.",
"blomberg-s10-028": "The greater the sphaera a conceptus communis has, and the more it holds under itself, the less is contained in it. A concept that contains much under itself arises through abstracting what is common to many things. It is therefore a mark of a mark, thus has much under itself but little in itself. For a mark of a mark contains still much less than the first mark of which it is a mark. If I think something of many things, what I think of these many things is very little. The less, however, a concept contains under itself, the more it holds in itself, and a single concept contains the most in itself, for here I cognize only one object, but very much in it. The more I think in my concept, the less I think through the concept. A conceptus communis arises only by considering what is common to many objects. What they have as different, however, is left out and not considered. This conceptus contains little in itself, but is a concept of a large sphaera, e.g. the concept of body has a large sphaera, but contains little in itself. A concept can never become of greater extension, so that it contains more under itself, except by containing less in itself. This consideration teaches us especially to estimate the value of our cognition. Through reason we cognize nothing but notae communes, hence little of many things; much is under, but little in, the cognitions of reason. Through experience we cognize few objects, but very much in them. The concepts of experience are minores sphaerae (lesser spheres), but they contain much in themselves. A mark of a mark always has a greater sphaera than a nearer mark, or always contains more under itself than a nota proprior (nearer mark). The nota of a thing in general is the nota remotissima (most remote mark) of a thing. Then comes the nota remotior, remota, and only then come the nearer marks. The nota remotissima, however, contains the most under itself. When several concepts stand in such a relation that one is contained under the other, then that which contains the other under itself is a conceptus superior, and the concept that is contained under the other, the conceptus inferior. One and the very same concept, however, can be now a conceptus superior, now a conceptus inferior according to different relations, namely according as it contains other concepts under itself and also stands under another. E.g. I say human beings. Then I compare the human being with the irrational animals, and say animals; so the concept of the human being already contains very much under itself, e.g. white human beings, and Negroes, and all sorts of nations etc., and is therefore in this respect a conceptus superior. It is, however, a conceptus inferior insofar as it is contained under the concept animals. The conceptus nullo inferior (concept inferior to none), however, is the conceptus summus (highest concept). To this belongs the concept of a thing, or of a possible. This conceptus summus is not merely relatively but absolutely superior. The conceptus infimus (lowest concept), however, is that which is not only relatively but absolutely inferior. The conceptus singulares are infimi. The conceptus universales, however, are summi, and between these conceptus infimi and summi there is a longa series (long series) of conceptus inferiores and superiores.",
"blomberg-s10-029": "Every mark is a ground of cognition of things; we cognize all things through reason only by means of marks. The further a concept is removed from the immediate mark of a thing, the higher it is, and the more it contains under itself. The very same conceptus communis is called, in regard to the conceptus communes that it contains under itself, relatively a genus, but in regard to the conceptus communes under which it is contained, it is called relatively a species. E.g. the unlearned are a genus in regard to the particular kinds of the learned, the philosophers, physicians, etc., jurists; but they are a species in regard to the concept of a human being in general. A conceptus communis is called a species for this reason, that 1mo it is contained under another conceptus communis superior, but also 2: because it is itself a conceptus communis. For a conceptus singularis, even if it is subordinated to another conceptus communis, is not a species; e.g. if I think of Herr von Wolff among the learned, this is no longer a species but an individuum (individual). Every species is infima insofar as under it no conceptus communes but merely singulares are contained. Species infima est, quae tantum individua sub se continet (The lowest species is that which contains only individuals under itself). Otherwise one can always think of a species again as a genus, and subordinate other concepts to it. E.g. a philosopher is a species of the learned; this, however, becomes a genus if I think under it especially the Wolffians, Crusians etc. All genera that are contained under higher genera are, in regard to these genera, to be regarded as species. The genus summum, however, is that which can no longer be a species. The species infima, however, can never more be a genus. In the former case it is subordinate to another, in the second case, however, others are subordinated to it. Thus the same concept can be both genus and species in different respects. The subordination of concepts, however, can take place both logice (logically) and realiter (really). Logical subordination consists in this, that I take what is common to many concepts with one another, and thereby form for myself a general concept, under which I can subordinate the single representations. In this way I make for myself different genera, and subordinate to them the species and individua. Real subordination, however, consists in this, that I actually connect the concepts with one another, so that not only is one contained under the other, but they also cohere as causes and effects.",
"blomberg-s10-030": "Now we come to the Sphaeram Notionum (sphere of concepts). The more abstracted a concept is, the greater its sphere. The greater the sphere of a concept, the more things I can judge of, but the fewer things I can cognize in. The senses cognize much in few things, reason, however, little in many things. Reason and experience must therefore be united as far as possible, so that what is lacking in the one ground of cognition can be made good again by the other ground of cognition.",
"blomberg-s10-031": "The concept that contains another under itself is a Conceptus Latior (wider concept); the concept, however, that is contained under another is a Conceptus angustior (narrower concept). Thus the concept of the human being is a Conceptus Latior with regard to the learned; the concept of the jurist, etc., of the philosopher, however, a Conceptus angustior with regard to the concept of the learned person. The first concept is therefore that which contains only a single thing under itself, and through which I can cognize only a single one.",
"blomberg-s10-032": "The Conceptus Latissimus, however, is that through which I can cognize the most things, or to which the most things are subordinated and contained under it.",
"blomberg-s10-033": "A reciprocal concept is one that contains just as much under and in itself as another concept, which can therefore properly be exchanged with it, and of which one can take the place of the other completely. A general or common concept is that under which many are contained. Every Conceptus Communis is also at the same time, with regard to those concepts that are contained under it, a Conceptus universalis.",
"blomberg-s10-034": "A concept can be contained only in part under another concept. Quare Conceptus inferior Semper est particularis, ratione conceptus Superioris, quare Conceptus Superior vero Semper est universalis, ratione conceptus inferioris (Hence the inferior concept is always particular in relation to the superior concept, and the superior concept is always universal in relation to the inferior concept). All abstract cognition, namely with regard to the concepts that are contained under it, is the most general cognition, that is, that which contains the conceptus Summos (highest concepts) under itself and descends from them to all Conceptus inferiores that are contained under them. Metaphysics is a science that contains such Conceptus Summos under itself; it therefore also cognizes the most general constitution of things.",
"blomberg-s10-035": "We can properly pass over this paragraph, for here the discussion is not of inferences but only of learned concepts. The place for speaking of this will be in the doctrine of inferences, quare hoc non tractare volumus anticipando (wherefore we do not wish to treat this by anticipation).",
"blomberg-s10-036": "Here the author speaks of the use of abstract cognition. It is very great, yet it is not very necessary to cite and go through it in particular, because the greatest part of all our cognitions rests on it.",
"blomberg-s10-037": "The author now speaks here of the concepts through arbitrary combination. All conceptus ficti (fictitious concepts), however, are made vel per Combinationem, vel per Separationem arbitrariam (either by combination or by arbitrary separation). Yet it is to be noted generally that in all our invented concepts only the form is, and is being, invented.",
"blomberg-s10-038": "The matter can never be invented; whoever has no sight cannot invent a color for himself, whoever has no hearing cannot invent a tone for himself. All form, however, is invented according to the relation of space and time, and according to the general nature of our reason. All concepts are either empirical or transcendental concepts of reason. To the latter concepts belong the representations of the possible and impossible, of the necessary and contingent; these do not arise from experience at all, and are not abstracted from it, but are produced through pure reason. Concepts are therefore either given or made. They are given either through experience or through pure reason. They are made when they have been given neither through experience nor through pure reason. These made concepts, however, are always invented only as to form. Through abstraction (Absonderung) I think of a part of the concept, but through separation (Trennung) I deny something of my concept. Absonderung, abstraction, Trennung, separation are therefore distinct from one another. Through separation alone invented concepts arise, through abstraction never. To a Fictio Separando facta (fiction made by separating) belongs a removal of that which otherwise is wont to be combined with a concept. One invents much Separando; all novels arise through this kind of fiction. In just this way one must also feign the idea of God, by abstracting all imperfection from it and thus arriving at the concept of the highest being. Through combination, however, one feigns something when one puts together much that is never connected with one another in experience. In this way, as already indicated above, novels arise. The Stoic sage arises through this fiction; one gives him more power than is proper to the human being. Inventions are either Fictiones aestheticae or heuristicae. The former serve for pleasure, the latter for discovery.",
"blomberg-s10-039": "Mathematics and physics have many Fictiones heuristicas, of which kind are the hypotheses. Fictions occur, further, either according to quality or according to quantity. The Fictiones Aestheticae according to quality are the best, those according to quantity, however, are not exactly the best. For no great art is required for them, except merely in the description, in order thereby to get a lively concept. If I only have an object, I can soon attribute to it a monstrous size, which can indeed cause admiration but no emotion. But there are also involuntary inventions, or involuntary",
"blomberg-s10-040": "invented concepts of reason, e.g. of spirits. These concepts have not arisen or been given through experience, nor through reason. One arrives at these involuntary concepts through an unnoticed invention and through secret inferences of which one is often not aware at all.",
"blomberg-s10-041": "The distinctness of a concept is a property of a logical perfection. The making-distinct happens through analysis. But if I make distinct a concept that contains, e.g., many marks, I must try to avoid tautology among these marks; I shall therefore promote the precision of the marks, i.e. I shall limit the quantity of the marks without, however, being detrimental to their quality. Also the totality of the distinct representation of a thing is asked for, and this totality I often need very much. In all distinctness of concepts I can be content with partiality, but if my marks suffice for the whole concept of the thing, then I have a totality. But if precision is also asked for, then I have a complete, precise concept of a thing, and that is a definition. The definition, and the action of our soul therein, can be compared perfectly with the reduction of fractions ad minimos Terminos (to lowest terms) occurring in arithmetic, e.g. 4/2 3/6 2/3 1/3 = 4/12. And this is also the aim of defining. We want 1) a distinct, 2) a complete, and then 3) a precise concept of a thing. In order, therefore, to seek whether a definition is correct, we always ask 1) whether this alleged definition is true as a proposition, and thus we can use many definitiones as true propositions, in which many otherwise imperceptible marks occur to us. False definitiones can sometimes even be useful to us as true propositions; indeed one can often even dispense with the usability of such a true proposition of the whole definition. Supposing it does not fulfil what it promised, it can still be so useful, it can always bring about benefit, even though it does not attain its aim.",
"blomberg-s10-042": "2:) One asks whether the definition is distinct as a concept, whether I can cognize it clearly. For every distinctness, however, it is required that a mark be a clear ground of cognition of the thing. All tautology in definitions is therefore a lack of distinctness, and such a definition is not only no definition, but also no distinct concept. All empty propositions are tautological. The soul, e.g., is in an animal a ruling monad; this proposition is not empty, but in it a comparative obscurum (comparatively obscure) is explained per aeque obscurum (through something equally obscure). The uselessness of this proposition, however, can still be remedied here. With tautology, however, this does not work at all. 3:) One asks whether the definition as a distinct concept is exhaustive, that is: distinct. nam definire est fines conceptus alicuius ponere (for to define is to set the limits of some concept). I.e. the definition is a determination of everything that belongs to the thing only.",
"blomberg-s10-043": "4thly it is asked whether this distinct and exhaustive concept is also measured off, that is, precise, namely that the marks of the definition do not flow out of one another, or are subordinated to one another, but are coordinated with one another. The marks must stand together as compartes (co-parts) of a whole, but not alongside one another as members in a series. If everything were omitted without which a concept nevertheless remains distinct and complete, such a one is called: conceptus rem adaequans (a concept adequate to the thing). The completeness, then, and the precision of a concept together make up its adequation. An Adaequatum, therefore, admits no Comparativum. Wolff says: A concept is adequate when the marks of a concept, and these marks again in turn the marks of the marks, are distinct. Definitio autem adaequat Conceptum (But the definition adequates the concept).",
"blomberg-s10-044": "Finally there belongs to this the precision; this consists namely in this, that one mark in the definition is not already contained in another, or, which is the same, that no repetition is to take place therein. E.g. if I were to say: The body is an extended divisible matter, this would be acting against precision, for the mark of divisibility already lies in the mark of matter, and consequently the mark of divisibility is merely repeated, for it already lies in the one mark of the body, namely in the concept of matter. In short, the most correct and best definition of precision is this:",
"blomberg-s10-045": "Precision is a reduction of a definition ad minimos Terminos (to lowest terms), just as one is wont, e.g., in arithmetic to bring fractions ad minimos Terminos. Accordingly the definition is nothing other than the relation of equality of two concepts, namely so that one can always be substituted for the other.",
"blomberg-s10-046": "But now we ask: is the concept in the definition then wholly equal to the concept of the Definitum? And here we must note: materialiter id est quoad objectum (materially, that is, as to the object) these concepts are indeed always equal to one another; but as regards the form, they are not, and also must not be, wholly equal to one another. As regards the matter, I indeed always think the same object, but not in the same way, rather in another way: what I previously represented of the Definitum confusedly, that I represent in the definition distinctly, and accordingly every definition must be distinct. For distinctness in general, however, and so also for the distinctness of definitions, marks are required; these marks, however, must first be clear concepts, they must also be even clearer than the concept of the Definitum, and from this flows this canon: ne definiatur obscurum per aeque obscurum (let the obscure not be defined through the equally obscure). E.g. if I were to say to someone who does not know what a monas is: the soul is a monad thinking in the body, this would again offend against this rule. Second, however, these marks must also be clear grounds of cognition. For as a ground of cognition it should after all be distinguished from the consequence, and consequently it must be thought otherwise, and far more clearly, than in the Definitum, for he names me no ground at all who says merely the same thing. And from this flows then this rule: ne Notae in definitione Sint Tavtologicae (let the marks in the definition not be tautological). Otherwise this is also called defining per circulum (through a circle). The Circulus vitiosus (vicious circle) in defining is either implicitus (hidden) or Explicitus, namely when the marks are the same even according to the expressions. An example of the first: e.g. the definition, the ground is that wherefore something is cognized; here the wherefore means just the same as quâ ratione (by what ground), and consequently a Tavtologia implicita is hidden herein, and hence the form in the definition must always be different from the form of the Definitum. This was, then, the first requisitum of a good definition, namely that it be distinct.",
"blomberg-s10-047": "The 2nd requisitum of a proper definition now is this, that it be adequate, or that it comprehend neither more nor less than is contained in the Definitum. One can also properly call it the Completudo, or exhaustiveness and sufficiency. To the Completudo of a definition now belongs that 1. the marks be sufficient to distinguish the Definitum from all others, 2.) also sufficient for the cognition of its identity with others, or, said briefly, that the definition be neither Latior nor angustior. But what does this mean? In comparison of the thing with others every ground of cognition is to be used in a twofold way.",
"blomberg-s10-048": "1mo (firstly) to distinguish therefrom the identity or",
"blomberg-s10-049": "2do (secondly) the diversity of the same from all others. The definition is therefore complete when the marks are sufficient",
"blomberg-s10-050": "1mo (firstly) for [cognizing] the difference of the concept from all others that do not stand under its general concept, and",
"blomberg-s10-051": "2.) also for cognizing therefrom the sameness of it with other concepts that stand together under a general concept; the former is the sufficiency for distinction, the latter, however, the sufficiency for recognition (Agnition).",
"blomberg-s10-052": "Now we can note: The more marks a concept has in itself, the less it is the same as others; so if there are too many marks in the definition, this can indeed contribute to the distinction, but not at all to the recognition of the concept. We can therefore also say: the Definitio is angustior when it does not suffice for the cognition of identity, and Latior when it does not suffice for the cognition of diversity; or if too few marks are present, the definition is latior, but if too many marks are present, the definition is angustior. It is certainly curious: the means to cognize identity are exactly opposed to those I use to see its diversity from others. For the more I want to cognize the difference, the more I must pile up the marks; the more I want to see the identity, the more I must reduce the number of marks. Now we can also remark this: Quo major conceptus determinatus est, eo angustior est quoque definitio (The greater the determinate concept is, the narrower also is the definition). Besides, as concerns persons, we can say: people who have much wit commonly make Definitiones Latiores. They will, e.g., quickly say: living beings are those that grow and generate. But, e.g., one experience also generates another, yet it does not on that account generate a living being. This definition is therefore Latior. It does not serve for the investigation of all things and the distinction of the thing from others, but rather for the cognition of the sameness of the thing with others, and from this we see, too, that the latitude of a definition belongs to the cognition of identity. But as regards the brooder (Grübler), he often makes definitiones angustiores. He seeks to find differences where none are to be met with, and that is the so-called Argutatio inanis (empty subtlety).",
"blomberg-s10-053": "From all this that has been said there now follows the logical rule, and the 3rd canon: ne Definitio nec latior nec angustior suo Definito sit (let the definition be neither wider nor narrower than its Definitum), or in one expression: Definitio et Definitum sint conceptus reciproci (let definition and Definitum be reciprocal concepts).",
"blomberg-s10-054": "That is the 3rd Requisitum of a good definition, and it seems as if with this demand upon definition all further precautions come to an end. But because in different expressions a like mark, which has already occurred once in the definition, can still occur once more in it, a certain precision in the definition is still necessary to prevent this, and according to this it is required that one concept or one mark must not already be found in another.",
"blomberg-s10-055": "As concerns tautology, we have already above grasped it as a great fault, for in it the marks given are no grounds of cognition at all, and the definition is mingled in it with the conceptus Definiti. But as regards this: when the Notae (marks) in a definition are the same and differ from one another merely according to the expression, this is no such great fault, and distinctness can still take place with it. The fault lies only in this, that one expresses one's own thoughts all too luxuriantly. To remove this in the definition, a certain precision is very necessary. This serves, however, as said, more for neatness than for the essence of the definition itself, yet one always gladly desires it in the definition. It is just like a garment: it does me the same service when it is wide as when it is made even, but for the sake of neatness we reject the first and choose the other.",
"blomberg-s10-056": "As concerns precision, then, the well-known and customary definition of the circle line offends against it. For one says: The circle line is a curved line whose individual points are equally far from the center. But the words curved line are here wholly unnecessary and already lie in the concept of what follows. For every line whose parts are equally far from the center must necessarily be curved. One must therefore not coordinate in a definition that which is already contained once under the same concepts.",
"blomberg-s10-057": "From the definition the description is now distinguished, for this is a distinct concept which, however, is only comparative complete. Its exhaustiveness is sufficient merely for my purpose, although it can be wholly contrary to the purposes of all others. The description is therefore not, like the definition, absolutely complete, i.e. it does not serve for every aim but only for my private aim, yet on that account is by no means to be rejected.",
"blomberg-s10-058": "Now we can note: [934a] All Definitiones are either nominales or reales Definitiones. Nominal definitions are those that contain in themselves everything that is equal to the whole concept which we make of the thing, but real definitions are those that contain in themselves everything that belongs to the thing in and for itself.",
"blomberg-s10-059": "Or",
"blomberg-s10-060": "Nominal are those definitions whose marks taken together are adequate to the whole concept that we think with the expression of the Definitum, e.g. when I think and say: Salt is that which is dissolved in water. I do indeed think this with the concept of salt, but these are not all possible characteristics of salt itself. [934b] Real definitions, however, are those whose marks make up the whole similar concept of the thing. Now",
"blomberg-s10-061": "1): all definitions of arbitrary concepts,",
"blomberg-s10-062": "2.) all definitions of concepts of reason, and 3:) all definitions of arbitrary inventions are real definitions. But why, we ask, are the definitions of arbitrary concepts always real definitions? Because it lies solely and alone with me to invent the concept and fix it as it pleases me, and the whole concept therefore has no other reality than merely as my invention wills it; consequently I can always posit all the parts in a thing that I name, and these must then also make up the whole [934c] possible concept of the thing, for the whole thing is actual only through my will.",
"blomberg-s10-063": "Secondly, however, the definitions of such things as are arbitrary inventions, and consequently also the definitions of all works of art, are real definitions, for they are always first fixed through an arbitrary concept.",
"blomberg-s10-064": "Now we want to remark this: That all mathematical definitions are definitions of arbitrary concepts. The mathematicians also have some given concepts, which they cannot define, and the mathematician must not do this either, for otherwise he philosophizes, and that is then a gross fault for a mathematician, a fault into which Wolffius himself has fallen. The mathematician does not concern himself at all with what others think under the concept, but says: this I will call so, and so it shall be called. As soon, therefore, as the mathematician represents the concept in the definition to himself as given, he philosophizes, and only then can one dispute with him, but not before; and then he encroaches on the philosopher, who can then lay claim to him, and for this reason a mathematician can define no place, no direction, no straight line, etc., for these are all already given concepts.",
"blomberg-s10-065": "Besides, as concerns this further, [936-939] there are indeed many arbitrary concepts, and also heuristic definitions, to be met with in philosophy, but they are not philosophical definitions at all.",
"blomberg-s10-066": "Now we can therefore say: All definitions of arbitrary concepts are",
"blomberg-s10-067": "1.) real, 2) each of them is without any error, 3): each of them is a complete definition. Arbitrary definitions cannot be false at all, for in that he says: I will that this shall be called so, it is also called so, and he can commit no error, even if he wanted to commit it by force. Only with regard to precision can he fail there, but this is no error, only a mere fault. And from this we see again what a great advantage a mathematician has. All his concepts, namely, are true, and he can fail only with regard to precision; otherwise one can well say that he could offend against linguistic usage, e.g. when he calls the circumference of a sphere, or of a polygon, periphery, whereas it should be called perimeter. Further, the names of the things that occur in art are also always true; the inventor of them is always right, for they are arbitrary concepts. But if we want to define the works of art of others, we must hear what the others want to understand by them. These definitions of arbitrary concepts are all synthetic, and arise only per Synthesin (through synthesis), for no concept can arise per Analysin (through analysis), but only distinctness can thereby be given to it. But with arbitrary concepts the concept is to arise, and hence all arbitrary definitions are made only per Synthesin. Per analysin a definition arises only insofar as the Definitum is given to us confused",
"blomberg-s10-068": "and there I can then make it distinct per analysin. Per Synthesin, however, I make the concept together with the distinctness. We can therefore also note, with regard to given concepts: all given concepts are given either through reason or through experience. Those, now, that are given through reason are made per analysin, and those that are given through experience are made realiter per Synthesin. Now all concepts of reason can be defined through definitiones reales. For since the concept is already given to me through reason, I need only think in it what reason shows me in the thing, and in such a way I then have a real definition, and hence we can also say that all metaphysical and moral definitions can all be real, and occur only per analysin, for the concept is already given through reason and I am only to make it distinct, which however happens only per analysin. Thus, e.g., the concept of honesty is constituted, etc. But how is it then 2ndly with the concepts of experience with regard to their definition? Here we must note: The definitions of concepts of experience are never other than nominal, and then only per analysin; secondly, the concepts of experience are never real except only per Synthesin. With an empirical concept I always ask what is given? And there I can then make a definition of it in two ways, nempe vel definio conceptum datum, vel definiendum est definitum ipsum (namely, either I define the given concept, or the definitum itself is to be defined); the first is always, with all concepts, a nominal definition, and the other a real definition. For my concept contains only what I think in the thing, but not what belongs to it according to nature; if I want to attain the latter, I must make a real definition, and with empirical concepts these happen only per Synthesin, while on the other side I again obtain the nominal definition of the same in no other way than merely per analysin.",
"blomberg-s10-069": "Now we ask: Is a definition possible with empirical concepts? O no, with all empirical concepts only a description takes place. At most it seems only that nominal definitions would be possible. For to define realiter means to indicate all marks that taken together make up the whole, and a real definition is thus the completudo of those marks that belong only to the thing itself. But with empirical concepts I do not define the object, but only the concept that one thinks with the thing. The marks of experience are therefore variable, and serve merely for nominal definition.",
"blomberg-s10-070": "Since, then, the concept is already given through experience, and I am to make it distinct, I need only enumerate all the marks that I think with the expression of the Definitum, and so it seems in fact as if a nominal definition could still take place with it. But even this is not possible with it. For in the naming of empirical concepts themselves we do not even all have the same opinions; for the one has a wholly different experience of the thing than the other, and hence only a description can be thought with empirical concepts, which I can consider only in relation to my aim, but not to every aim.",
"blomberg-s10-071": "But what aim, we ask, do we have herewith?",
"blomberg-s10-072": "The aim therein is not to obtain the cognition of the Definitum through its analysis, but merely to give a meaning to the Definitum. The end-purpose therein is therefore only the preliminary signification, so that this namely does not go wholly lost from the language. For, e.g., in the Harz mountains one already calls the firs pines, and again the pines firs. I define an empirical concept in order afterwards always to add something per Synthesin. But never do I define a concept of experience in order that I may get to know the object through analysis. I define no concept of experience, e.g. gold, in order to infer something from it and to draw consequences out of this definition, but merely in order thereby to fix the meaning of the word of the Definitum. Here we note this: the definitions of morals are analytic, and serve so that I may draw from this concept all possible consequences. The definitions of morals are all real, and I therefore need only analyze them; so it is really curious that one can infer from the use of definitions to their particular kind. Empirical definitions go only to the understanding, but not at all to reason (for only through the understanding do I see the meaning), so they also have no philosophical use at all. From this it follows that definitions of empirical concepts are wholly unnecessary, for even if there could really be some, they would be merely nominal definitions. For I do not say of them: all this taken together makes up the whole concept of the thing, but only: this is all that I think with the concept of the thing. All empirical concepts are capable only of description, all arbitrary concepts are synthetic, and their definition real. Finally, all concepts of reason are, because they are given, analytic, and their definitiones likewise real. Now we make these remarks: With all given concepts of reason the definition is not the first, but rather the confused concept of the Definitum. For with the given concepts of reason I am through the definition only to make the confused concept distinct, and so the confused concept of the Definitum must already precede all definition; only with arbitrary concepts does the definition always go first, for it is this that gives the concepts their esse (being). Now all distinctness of definitions can be attained only through reason per analysin, and the concept of reason becomes complete only successively, namely when I weigh one clear mark after another; and since every cognition, when it has clear marks, is a judgment, all definitions are at the same time judgments. Before all analytic definitions many Judicia Elementaria (elementary judgments) go ahead, which expound the marks that contain the concept of the Definitum in themselves. It is sometimes acceptable for my aim to prove something where merely one mark is necessary and the whole definition is often superfluous, e.g. when I say: the soul of the human being is incorporeal, I do not need the whole definition, but take out only the one mark, namely divisibility, and say: every body is divisible, but the soul is simple, consequently it cannot be corporeal.",
"blomberg-s10-073": "One must never conclude the definition too early in philosophy, unless I can cognize the whole thing from one mark, for this hinders the way to the cognition of the thing. Before I conclude the definition, I must analyze the concept for a long time yet, and postpone my judgment, so that in the meantime I may perhaps attain a more exhaustive cognition of the object.",
"blomberg-s10-074": "As concerns Wolff's fault, it consists in this, that Wolff wanted to bring the mathematical procedure into philosophy; all concepts in mathesis are arbitrary concepts, and must not be given at all.",
"blomberg-s10-075": "From the general the particular is cognized. With general concepts I have",
"blomberg-s10-076": "1:) to look to the lower concepts that are contained in the general,",
"blomberg-s10-077": "2:) but also to the difference of these lower concepts, and this all happens in logical division. This synthetic method consists namely just in this, when I compare the sphere of the concept with all its lower concepts, yet so that I also note their proper difference.",
"blomberg-s10-078": "To a correct division now belongs",
"blomberg-s10-079": "1: that it be complete, i.e. that the membra dividentia (dividing members; these are just the lower concepts) taken together make up exactly the general concept.",
"blomberg-s10-080": "2:) but the membra dividentia must also be orderly opposed, so that I can say with truth either this or that. There must therefore also always be a real difference, e.g. I shall not divide thus: all human beings are either virtuous, or vicious, or philosophers. This concept does indeed lie in the human being, but it is not represented in oppositione (in opposition) at all. The division can be as well a codivision as a subdivision. A subdivision, however, is that where the membrum dividens is again subordinated.",
"blomberg-s10-081": "The rules that the author cites herein serve us for nothing at all; I learn thereby only to express in formulas what I already know, to represent it to myself in abstracto, which, if I only follow my sound reason, I already actually practice in concreto.",
"blomberg-s11-001": "Properly we already know judgments from the previous section, because we have dealt with distinct concepts, which, however, can arise only through a judgment, for to cognize distinctly means to cognize everything through a clear mark. To cognize something through a clear mark, however, is just also to judge. We can therefore also say: distinct concepts are those that are cognized through a judgment. Now: the 2 concepts that are compared with one another, and indeed so that the one contains the marks and the other the clarity, make up the matter of the judgment. That concept, now, which is to be made distinct through comparison with its mark is called the subject; the concept, on the other hand, which is added to the mark as a ground of distinctness, is called the predicate.",
"blomberg-s11-002": "The comparison is twofold, for either I can compare 2 concepts through the relation of connection, or through the relation of conflict with one another. A judgment, now, in which the predicate is thought in connection with the subject is called an affirmative judgment. A judgment, however, where the predicate is thought in conflict with the subject is called a negative judgment. The form of the judgment is the relation. The sign that indicates this relation or the form is the copula est (is). And since every relation is either affirmative or negative, the copula est, when it is simply affirmative, is accompanied with yes (id est simply with est), but when it is negative, with an id est non (that is, not). The negation no, or the non, does not affect the matter at all, but only the form of the judgment; it must therefore be placed not with the subject or with the predicate, but with the copula, e.g. anima non est mortalis (the soul is not mortal), but not anima est non mortalis (the soul is non-mortal), for otherwise the negation of the judgment of reason affects not the form but the matter of the judgment, and consequently it is then also no negative judgment but a so-called Judicium infinitum (infinite judgment).",
"blomberg-s11-003": "In every judgment there occurs",
"blomberg-s11-004": "1): the matter of the judgment, that is, subject and predicate. The matter of the judgment, now, is called the Termini of the judgment, and indeed, for reasons that we shall hear later, we call the subject the Terminus minor, and the predicate is called by us Terminus major, and is always regarded as a general concept under which the Conceptus Subjecti is contained. All judgments have the aim of distinctness, but the predicate one always represents as a ground of the distinctness of the subject in a judgment. Since, however, in a mark there is also always the ground of distinctness, we see that the subject must always be contained under the predicate as under a mark. The",
"blomberg-s11-005": "2nd piece of every judgment is its form. This is always expressed by the Copula est. This copula is posited Simpliciter (simply) when it indicates the relation of two concepts in their connection; but if it expresses the relation in their conflict, it is also accompanied with the word non. This non now expresses the negation of the judgment, but NB: only then, when it affects the form of the judgment, i.e. the copula.",
"blomberg-s11-006": "Where the author says thus: all true judgments have a ground, the ground of truth, now, is the condition of judgments; all unprovable judgments have no particular ground of cognition, but are the grounds of cognition themselves, and consequently can contain no grounds of truth at all. As the author nevertheless means here, the judgment is, e.g., so constituted: Every body is extended, for this judgment can be derived from nowhere further. But if the condition of truth is merely added to a judgment, then the judgment is determinate. Every judgment is now determinate, and I cannot represent to myself at all how there should still be such people who say: certain judgments are indeterminate, among whom our author also belongs; properly one should say unrestricted, for everything that is not determinate is also never a judgment.",
"blomberg-s11-007": "All judgments are either universal or particular. It is universal when the Nota (mark) of the subject is either wholly contained in the Sphaera of the predicate or not contained at all, and indeed in the first case it is a universal affirmative, in the other case, however, namely when the Nota of the subject is found wholly outside the Sphaera of the predicate, a universal negative judgment. A particular judgment, however, is one where the Nota of the subject is contained under the Sphaera of the predicate, or also not contained, only in part; in the former case it is then a particular affirmative, and in the other a particular negative judgment.",
"blomberg-s11-008": "There are no more judgments quoad quantitatem (as to quantity), for as concerns singular judgments (Judicia Singularia), or those where the subject is an individual, these already belong to the universal judgments. Yet one can note this further about it: a singular judgment must be either wholly affirmative or wholly negative. More does not take place there, for it belongs to the universal",
"blomberg-s11-009": "judgment, and so all singular judgments are universal. This was now the division of judgments quoad Quantitatem, namely into universal judgments and into particular. With regard to quality, now, all judgments are either affirmative or negative, and indeed either universal affirmative or universal negative or particular affirmative or particular negative. The logicians express the first by A, the second by E, the 3rd by I and the 4th by 0. This whole designation (the Barbara Celarent) is mere pedantry, and the whole syllogistic is also otherwise mere pedantry, and I shall show afterwards that every inference of reason outside the first figure is not simple, as one nevertheless falsely holds it to be, but is composed of the Consequentia immediata (immediate inference) and a Ratiocinium. Although syllogistic is now unnecessary, one must nevertheless know it.",
"blomberg-s11-010": "The following remark is well to be noted: Omnia Judicia sunt vel problematica, vel assertoria (decisive) (All judgments are either problematic or assertoric). A problematic judgment is one in which I merely consider the relation of two concepts indeterminately but do not posit it; I weigh something in it as given, for investigation, i.e. I fix no relation in it, but see only what, were it actual, would follow from it. They have, notwithstanding this, a great use. For through the relation of two such judgments one can nevertheless learn to use a certain caution in judging and to make our judgments advantageous.",
"blomberg-s11-011": "All relation in judgments is either a relation of connection or a relation of conflict. That judgment, now, through which the relation of connection of two judgments with one another is indicated is called a hypothetical judgment. That judgment, however, in which the relation of conflict of two judgments is indicated is called a disjunctive judgment. For one calls those judgments in which one thinks the relation of two judgments with one another relation judgments (relative); these judgments now consider the relation of one judgment to another, either according to connection, and then they are hypothetical judgments, or according to conflict, and they are then disjunctive judgments. In the former one always finds the relation of the ground to the consequences. In conditioned judgments, now, that which contains the ground is called antecedens or also prius. That, however, which contains the consequences is called in them consequens or posterius. But as concerns disjunctive judgments, the relation of conflict in them is again of two kinds: either it is a relation of two or more judgments that contradict one another; but we shall show that a true disjunctive judgment takes place only with two, and not at all with more judgments, for no true and pure contradiction can take place otherwise than merely with two concepts that stand exactly opposed to one another in opposition. It is then also called bimembris propositio (two-membered proposition), and since the disjunctive judgments contain the relation of a true contradiction in themselves, they can comprehend only 2 judgments. If we judge merely rationally, the Propositio disjunctiva in the judgment must be Bimembris; those disjunctive judgments, however, whose propositio disjunctiva is not bimembris have all arisen from induction, for how else do I know that I have also enumerated the cases correctly, than only from experience, and so per inductionem. A disjunctive judgment, if it is to become certain without induction, must be bimembris. A disjunctive judgment is nothing more than a representation of a logical division, or Repraesentatio Divisionis Logicae.",
"blomberg-s11-012": "An exponible judgment is one which properly has 2 judgments in a hidden way, not in such a way that it has, say, 2 subjects or predicates, but the judgments in it are twofold in quantity and also in quality, for they are affirmative and also negative. Logic now has the duty: it must express the exponible judgments. What do the questions mean: Quae? Qualis? Quanta? (Which? Of what quality? How much?) 1. The question Qualis est propositio? (Of what quality is the proposition?) aims at the quality, namely whether the judgment was affirmative or negative. 2. Quanta est propositio? contained the question whether it were a universal or a particular judgment? 3. quae est propositio? is the question whether the judgment is Judicium purum (pure) or modale (modal). Modal, however, a judgment is called which carries a condition of judgment with the Praedicatum. To these questions one must now answer according to the constitution of the judgment. If now an empty judgment occurred, one therefore used to say: Quae? qualis? Quanta? i.e. there is not even the least thing in the judgment.",
"blomberg-s11-013": "A judgment is expressed practically when it enunciates a possible-necessary action. This seems indeed to be contradictory, something is possible-necessary. But it is here quite correct, for the action is indeed always necessary, namely if I want to bring the thing about, but the case is not necessary, only possible. It is very good that with this kind of judgments one does not put the Imperativum, [965a] but only the Infinitivum, and expresses them with it, as when, etc.",
"blomberg-s11-014": "Demonstrably certain means nothing other than a mediately certain judgment. A judgment, however, is on the other side absolutely unprovable if it is immediately certain. And such judgments must also be met with in the human understanding, for even though we have ever so many consequences and can cognize these from their grounds as from their marks, we must nevertheless finally arrive at a ground that is no further consequence of another ground, but is itself the very first mark of all consequences, and this is then immediately certain, or an unprovably certain judgment. Whether there is, now, only one such absolutely first ground, or many of the kind, is dealt with in metaphysics.",
"blomberg-s11-015": "All unprovable judgments, insofar as they are the ground of all judgments, are called Grundsätze (principles) and are either theoretical or practical. A practically unprovable proposition is called a Postulatum. All these Principia are either material, insofar as they have a medium terminus (middle term), or formal, which have no medium terminus. These have no determinate predicate at all, but go merely to the relation of predicates to subjects in general. To these now belong the Principium contradictionis and the Principium Identitatis. For these there is namely no determinate subject and predicate, or no determinate case, but they can be applied to every subject and predicate. We now have in our judgments a twofold form, namely the synthetic and analytic form; the Principium contradictionis is only analytic.",
"blomberg-s11-016": "Here the author gives an instruction for demonstration. One has, just as tautological definitiones, so also tautological resolutiones, in that one answers just the same as what one asked. And so our author also does here. If a quaestio (question) is at hand, one should attend very well whether the alleged answer and resolution of the quaestio is not merely an analysis of the quaestio itself. For if so, I have not thereby taught the other to resolve the quaestio, but merely made known to him through analysis what was in it. I have therefore done nothing more than made him understand better that which he asked me. So the author also does many times. It is curious: he shows me merely that I should do that about which I question him, and shows me not at all how I should do it. In my last disputation de mundi sensibilis atque intelligibilis forma et Principiis I also had various quaestiones to resolve, but I did not merely analyze the quaestiones but in fact also indicated quite correct and complete marks of the things. E.g. pag. 30 § 25 (vid. Dict. Metaph. where this disputation is bound in): There, namely, of sensibility this infallible mark is cited: Si de conceptu quocunque intellectuali generaliter quicquam praedicatur, quod pertinet ad respectus Spatii atque Temporis: objective non est enuntiandum et non denotat nisi conditionem, sine qua conceptus datus sensitive cognoscibilis non est. (If anything pertaining to the relations of space and time is predicated generally of any intellectual concept, it is not to be asserted objectively and denotes nothing but the condition without which the given concept is not sensitively cognizable.) vid. ibid.",
"blomberg-s11-017": "Where the author speaks of intuitive judgments. An intuitive judgment is properly a singular judgment, insofar as it is cognized immediately; a judicium discursivum, however, is a concluded judgment. All immediate judgments, however, are intuitive merely because they are cognized immediately, insofar as their form is sensitive; for otherwise, if the form is rational, then the immediate judgments are nevertheless not intuitive judgments at all. Only the discursive could be contradistinguished from the intuitive judgments, and the ancients called the universal",
"blomberg-s11-018": "judgments discursiva, but the singular ones intuitiva, and so we too wish to take it here. This was now about the intuitive or empirical judgments. Whether a judgment is an empirical judgment or not, belongs to metaphysics to investigate. Logic does not consider judgments at all insofar as they are given, as metaphysics does, but insofar as it is to compare them. Logic shows only whether the judgments are universal or particular, and so it concerns itself only with opposition and identity. All propositions are either theoretical or practical. The first are those that show only the relation of concepts to other concepts, the second, however, show the relation of an action to concepts. Both kinds of propositions are, however, either demonstrable or indemonstrable. Theoretically demonstrable propositions, if they arise through a ratiocinium polysyllogisticum, are called theoremata. Every demonstrable proposition, however, that is only monosyllogistic, is called a corollarium. A practically demonstrable proposition is a problema, a practically indemonstrable proposition, however, a postulatum. In all theoretically demonstrable propositions the proposition itself is the theorema, the proof of the proposition, however, the demonstration; the action that is to be done thereby is called the quaestio; the propositions according to which it is cognized, the resolution of it. A lemma is a proposition that is indeed demonstrable, but not in this discipline, but in another one, from which it is taken. One must be very careful with this, so that one does not assume something and already give it out as proved in another science, when it has not yet been proved in that science at all; indeed one often finds something in the authors that they assume at the beginning, which they promise to prove at the end, but the proof has been forgotten. Scholia are propositions that are added to a doctrinal edifice not at all as essentialia, but only as accidentalia.",
"blomberg-s11-019": "Every inference is in general a judgment, only with this difference: if the judgment is mediate, then it is an inference of reason, or a mediate inference. But if the judgment is immediate, then the inference is only an inference of the understanding; one is namely judged from the other absque medio Termino, and then the judgment is also the conclusio immediata; or one is inferred from the other per medium Terminum, and then the inference is mediate, or also an inference of reason. We want now to consider only mediate inferences. As regards their manner of arising, it is fivefold, for either they can arise",
"blomberg-s11-020": "1) per Judicia aequipollentia, or",
"blomberg-s11-021": "2) per Judicia Subalternata, or",
"blomberg-s11-022": "3) per Judicia opposita, or",
"blomberg-s11-023": "4) per Conversionem Logicam, or",
"blomberg-s11-024": "5) per Contrapositionem. As for the consequentia per Judicia aequipollentia, since both judgments in it are wholly identical with one another, it is properly no consequence at all, and so the so-called inference per Judicia aequipollentia is no inference at all. The consequentia per Judicia Subalternata occurs without any change of the position of the termini; the quality also remains the same, only the quantity is changed therein. In the consequentia per Conversionem Logicam likewise the quality must never, but only the quantity, be changed. In the conversio Logica, however, both the position of the termini and the quality must be changed. In the oppositio Judiciorum the logicians have made the following division:",
"blomberg-s11-025": "1: Contradictorie, two judgments are set against one another when a particular negative judgment (or conversely) is opposed to an affirmative judgment, e.g. all human beings are mortal, but some are not mortal.",
"blomberg-s11-026": "2 Subcontraria are judgments when, in two particular judgments that have the same subject and predicate, the one is affirmative and the other negative.",
"blomberg-s11-027": "3 there is also a contrarietas judicii, which obtains when, of two judgments, the one is universally affirmative and the other universally negative. The judicia subcontraria contain less, or not enough, than belongs to a proper opposition; the judicia contraria contain more, and the judicia contradictoria just as much as suffices for a proper opposition. And we can therefore say: judicia subcontraria, because they do not contain enough, can 1) both be true, but they can 2) not both be false.",
"blomberg-s11-028": "Secondly, judicia contraria can both be false, for in both there is too much, and in their very excess they can also be false. For two pure oppositions this must always hold: posito uno tollitur alterum. et vice versa. In every logical conversion (see above § 346) of judgments, the judgment that is to be turned around is called the conversum, the judgment that is to arise from this turning around, the convertens. A conversio in which the quality of the judgment is not impeded is called simplex, but where it is impeded, conversio per accidens. Universally affirmative judgments can be converted per accidens, all negative judgments, however, can be converted simpliciter, and finally the particular ones can all be converted simpliciter; that is the only rule of logical conversion. The rule of contraposition is this: universally affirmative judgments can only be contraposed.",
"blomberg-s12-001": "Inferences of reason are judgments that occur mediately by means of a medius terminus. There are also, as said before, certain judgments and consequences that we have called immediatas; these were indemonstrable. We also called these followings of a judgment from other judgments that are indemonstrable not an inference of reason but an inference of the understanding. But on the other side there are still certain consequences from other mediate judgments, and these are always demonstrable. There are uncertain apparent inferences, which are not inferences of reason but only mere themata for them, and of such a kind are the so-called ratiocinia disjunctiva and hypothetica, or also speciosa. Of these inferences, which are only themata for inferences of reason, we do not wish to treat here at all, but only of the inferences of reason themselves.",
"blomberg-s12-002": "When two concepts are put together, one of which is the mark of the other, a certain relation is posited in these concepts. This relation can now be represented immediately, and then it is a judicium. But if it occurs per notam intermediam, then it is a ratiocinium. Or the inference of reason is nothing other than the comparison of a nota remota with the thing itself by means of a nota intermedia. The mediate relation of a nota remota is always remota with respect to the thing, but also nota proxima of the nota intermedia, and thus a nota rei ipsius, with the given concept; that is a judicium medium, or a ratiocinium.",
"blomberg-s12-003": "An adequate inference consists in an inference of reason whose rule is this: nota notae rei est nota rei ipsius, and this is also the basic rule of all syllogistic, and at the same time the whole difference of a judgment from an inference of reason. For a judicium requires an immediate clear mark, for a ratiocinium, however, in addition a mediate clear mark. Consequently the ratiocinium is the relation of the nota remota through a nota mediata, vel intermedia, to a certain given concept of a thing. In a ratiocinium, then, these three actions occur: 1mo the nota proxima or intermedia is compared with the nota remota, 2 the nota proxima is compared with the thing, et 3tio the nota remota is compared with the thing itself. The nota intermedia is also called the medius terminus. Since now in every judgment the predicate constitutes the major terminus, we can say: in every inference of reason there are 3 termini: 1. the proposition that I am to cognize by means of the nota intermedia, 2. the nota remota, and 3rdly the terminus medius, or the nota intermedia, which contains within itself the ground of the relation of the thing to the nota remota. And these make up the 3 termini, namely major, minor et conclusio. In every inference of reason 1. the terminus major is compared in the terminus medius, or the nota proxima with the nota remota, 2 the terminus medius with the minor, or the subject, and finally 3: the minor with the major, or the predicate.",
"blomberg-s12-004": "Thus to infer requires only the synthetic way of reason; this always requires that one go down from the universal to the particular; if one reverses this, it is again against reason, and a leap is immediately committed. And therefore the major propositio too must always come first, or the predicate of the conclusion; or first I must go per notam remotam to the nota proxima, then I must go again from the nota proxima to the thing, and finally from the thing to the nota remota. That is the synthesis of reason, and if one loses sight of this, one does not observe the order either. In every inference of reason there are thus certain termini, of which there must be no more than 3, and in every proper inference of reason no more than 3 judicia occur. The first 2 are called the premises, the 3rd judgment, however, is called the conclusion. The premises make up the matter, the conclusion, however, the form of inferences of reason; hence the correct form of an inference of reason will rest solely and alone on the correct following of the conclusion. In both inferences the medius terminus or the nota intermedia always occurs (but not at all in the consequens itself). Every inference of reason is nothing other than a representation of a mark by means of a terminus medius. To an inference of reason it belongs that one mark be subordinated to another mark of the thing as a notae nota. In an inference of reason, then, I compare the nota remota with the nota proxima, then the thing with the nota proxima, and finally also the nota remota with the thing itself. All form of inferences of reason is only twofold, and there are no more, for either the judgment is immediate or mediate",
"blomberg-s12-005": "The basic rule of all affirmative inferences is this:",
"blomberg-s12-006": "1: Nota Notae rei est nota rei ipsius",
"blomberg-s12-007": "The sole basic rule, however,",
"blomberg-s12-008": "2:) of all negative inferences will be this:",
"blomberg-s12-009": "Repugnans Nota Notae rei, repugnat rei ipsi. This is now the basic rule of all inferences of reason.",
"blomberg-s12-010": "The logicians have a certain dictum de omni et nullo, which they hold to be the very first ground of all ratiociniorum. But these basic rules just cited precede even this.",
"blomberg-s12-011": "The dictum de omni et nullo runs thus among the logicians: what belongs to a universal concept belongs also to all concepts that are contained under it.",
"blomberg-s12-012": "If in an inference of reason the relation of two judgments (not of two concepts) is weighed, that is called a ratiocinium extraordinarium; or if an inference of reason is assumed where the major propositio is not a judicium ordinarium but a judicium extraordinarium, then it is likewise called a ratiocinium extraordinarium. All ratiocinia extraordinaria are either hypothetica or disjunctiva. For either the relation of connection or the relation of conflict is indicated in them; if the first, it is called a hypothetical, in the other case, however, a disjunctive inference of reason. The judicium hypotheticum consists only of 2 propositions, and so no judicium hypotheticum can bring forth a ratiocinium. In a judicium hypotheticum only the consequens needs to be proved, for nothing is enunciated or asserted in it. But with judicia categorica not only the consequens but the assertion itself must be proved from correct grounds. The conditioned inference is no inference of reason, but only the substratum for an inference of reason, for it must first be proved per ratiocinium. It is as yet the quaestio itself, and the datum that is still to be proved; so properly nothing is yet proved thereby, but only a ground is stated of which one at the same time affirms that its consequence is correct; how it is correct, however, I do not see at all thereby; it is merely, so to speak, the ground of proof, not the proof itself; that is: thereby only the matter, but not the form of the proof is given. This analysis is certainly not at all unnecessary, and quite correct. For in a judicium conditionale no subject is connected with a predicate through a mediate mark, and in such an inference there is no major propositio at all, and so they are also no proper inferences of reason, but only substrata for them; one does not actually infer according to them, but only says there what one wants to infer. All proof goes either to the truth of the antecedens or of the consequens. Every syllogismus hypotheticus has 2 modi, namely a modus ponens, where an affirmation is inferred in the conclusion, and the modus tollens, where namely a negation is inferred. An affirmation is here inferred according to the rule: atqui verum est prius, ergo quoque posterius. The negation, however, from the falsity of the consequens to the falsity of the antecedens, and indeed according to the rule: atqui falsum est prius, ergo quoque posterius; remoto antecedente non tollitur consequens sed sublata consequentia tollitur antecedens. I can therefore here never infer a negatione antecedentis to the negationem consequentis, but certainly conversely.",
"blomberg-s12-013": "As for disjunctive inferences, these are such inferences in which the major propositio is disjunctive. A disjunctive inference is a logical division; in it I represent to myself a divided concept and all the membra that are equal to the divisum. The subject of the major propositio in it is the divisum, and the predicate is all the membra dividentia. Consequently the correctness of a disjunctive inference rests on the correctness of a logical division; namely everything must stand in true disjunction and opposition against one another, and moreover everything must also be complete.",
"blomberg-s12-014": "A dilemma is properly a ratiocinium hypotheticum whose consequence is disjunctive. One proceeds therefore in it according to the rules of the hypothetical as well as of the disjunctive kind of inference. atqui falsum est prius, ergo etiam posterius. One has a dilemma never as modus ponendi, but always as modus tollendi. By a dilemma we seek not to prove a proposition properly, but only to convince someone of falsity. The dilemma is otherwise also called cornutum, because thereby one refutes the other and at the same time cuts off all ways for him; whichever way he may go, he is caught. And therefore this dilemma is also much used in dialectic. For that reason the sceptics too were very fond of it.",
"blomberg-s12-015": "Here the author speaks of immediate consequences, but this does not fit at all, for these are not inferences of reason at all but only inferences of the understanding. For they have no medius terminus.",
"blomberg-s12-016": "Are either formal inferences of reason, or ratiocinia cryptica. Ratiocinia cryptica are those where I enunciate only 2 judgments but think the 3rd along with them. In such a syllogismus one always says less than one arbitrarily thinks, and that is the so-called dissimulating, which is very often salutary and fair. But simulating means when one speaks otherwise than one thinks, and is of course to be wholly rejected as unfair.",
"blomberg-s12-017": "Enthymema: this is such an inference in which only 2 judgments, namely one praemissa and the conclusio, are posited. To these belong also the ratiocinia contracta. These are such syllogisms where there are not 2 propositions at all, but only one proposition and the medius terminus. Further, induction also belongs here, which is especially noteworthy. The rule of reason in apodictic certainty in inferences always goes from the universal to the particular. But there are nevertheless still inferences in which we infer from the particular to the universal. The former is the ratiocinatio rigida, the latter, however, the ratiocinatio laxa. To the latter now belong 1.) the inductio, 2. the analogy. Induction is the inference where I hold that which belongs to many under a universal inference and concept to be true as if it belonged to all. I infer thus: what belongs to so many things that I have ever cognized must also belong to all that are of this kind and species. This kind of inference is indeed wholly contrary to the logical rule, but one cannot do without it at all, and with it most of our cognitions would at the same time have to be abolished.",
"blomberg-s12-018": "As to 2ndly the inference according to an analogy, it is nothing other than an induction, but only an induction with regard to the predicate. When namely 2 things have agreed in respect of all properties that I have been able to cognize in them, then they will also agree with one another in the remaining properties that I have not cognized in them, and so runs the inference with regard to analogy. Analogy and induction are merely the crutches for our understanding. For in universal propositions of truth we will indeed always be able to proceed with logical rigor, but in the use of our cognition (a posteriori) we must often content ourselves with the probable.",
"blomberg-s12-019": "There is a complete and also an incomplete induction. Complete, if its membra are equal to the given concept, or if everything can be derived from a particular concept.",
"blomberg-s12-020": "Every inference false in form is a paralogismus. Every false inference, however, that is to be a means of producing an error in another, is called a sophisma. There are various sophismata, which could be given many names, which is however not at all necessary. Otherwise there are still some sophismata that deserve a place in logic, for some of them can be truer than the others. Otherwise the author here also mentions the sophisma ignorationis elenchi. For us, however, only this is to be noted about it: he who in a dispute forgets the point in dispute commits a sophisma ignorationis elenchi.",
"blomberg-s12-021": "When inferences of reason are coordinated with one another, there arises from it a ratiocinium compositum, also polysyllogisticum. The polysyllogisticus is called a ratiocinium when the conclusio of the one inference of reason is the praemissa of the other. That syllogismus whose conclusio is the premise of another is called prosyllogismus. That syllogismus, however, whose praemissa is a conclusion of the previous one is called episyllogismus. Or more briefly: the prosyllogismus contains the ground, the episyllogismus, however, the consequence of an inference of reason. See the author § 406: § 408: There are sorites per syllogismos hypoth: and then it is also called sorites hypotheticus, or the sorites can arise per syllogismos categoricos, and then it is also called categoricus.",
"blomberg-s12-022": "The formal ratiocinia are used only in disputing, but not in books and the like, for otherwise this would become too tiresome. And as to the many inferences of reason otherwise, they make things more indistinct than distinct. They are expressed, however, only for this reason, as reason actually thinks.",
"blomberg-s12-023": "Where the author speaks of the leap in proof, of the legitimate as well as the illegitimate, we note here only this: nullus saltus in intellectu est possibilis, et non aliter saltus possibilis est, quam in enunciando. The immediate connection of a remote ground with the consequence is a leap. In thoughts such a leap is wholly impossible, but in enunciation it can certainly take place, and in this case it can sometimes certainly also be legitimus, namely when I believe the other will unfailingly know what was left out and so supply it in thought.",
"blomberg-s12-024": "The actual omission of a proposition that deserves a proof is called petitio principii. This comes from peto, I ask. For in this kind of conviction I must as it were ask the other for his assent, and from this we see that this can be called no proof at all.",
"blomberg-s12-025": "The circulus in demonstrando is that fault when one assumes the concluding proposition in a proof as its own premise. This happens",
"blomberg-s12-026": "1:) when one wants to prove that which is indemonstrably true, or immediately certain.",
"blomberg-s12-027": "2:) when one wants to prove indemonstrably false propositions; in these two cases the circle in proving is always necessary. But it arises especially",
"blomberg-s12-028": "3:) when namely one is already certain, before all proof, of the truth of a proposition that is demonstrable; then one already grants the other the proof, and therefore a circle in proving can always creep in.",
"blomberg-s12-029": "A proof proves too little if it proves only a part of the proposition. It proves too much if it proves more than can be proved. A proof that proves too little can be true, but a proof that proves too much is always false. For when it is said: the proof proves too much, that is as much as: it proves more than is true, but this is nothing other than that it is false.",
"blomberg-s12-030": "The author speaks finally somewhat of the utility of inferences of reason, which seems almost unnecessary, for who will not hold the use of reason to be useful?",
"blomberg-s13-001": "The mode of teaching (Lehrart) is nothing other than the form of a whole of cognitions, insofar as it is according to the rules of logical perfection",
"blomberg-s13-002": "arranged. Since logical perfection is twofold, namely either logical perfection according to healthy reason, or logical perfection according to learnedness and science, the mode of teaching can be divided in the same way. For the rules of healthy reason are distinct from the rules of science. To be sure, in all sciences and learnedness the method of healthy reason must prevail, but conversely not everything that is found in learnedness need always also be in healthy reason. In all sciences I do not look to how something appears in practice, but to how it can be judged before all practice. I look to how something can also be thought in abstracto; if, on the contrary, I go by the rules of healthy reason, I must always show everything in concreto. The other kind is a cognition that carries a certain life with it; the first, however, serves merely for speculation and curiosity. The methods of the learned are now distinguished:",
"blomberg-s13-003": "1.) quoad objectum, i.e. according to the matter that they comprehend under themselves, or at which they are directed. For the object can be either",
"blomberg-s13-004": "a:) historical cognition, or B) rational cognition. To the latter belong e.g. mathematics and philosophy, to the former, however, geography and the actual history of history",
"blomberg-s13-005": "2: also according to the subject. For there there is",
"blomberg-s13-006": "a) a method of instruction for women, and a separate method for men.",
"blomberg-s13-007": "B) a method for the instruction of children, and another for grown people.",
"blomberg-s13-008": "C) a wholly separate method of instruction for people destined for a lowly station, and again another for people destined for a higher station, and so on. Our mode of teaching must namely be arranged wholly differently for a peasant than if it were applied even to a person of middling station.",
"blomberg-s13-009": "3 these differences can also concern the powers of cognition. For there can be either",
"blomberg-s13-010": "1: a method of merely healthy reason, or",
"blomberg-s13-011": "2) the method can be scientific, or there can also be",
"blomberg-s13-012": "3) a difference of the mode of teaching itself in its subject,",
"blomberg-s13-013": "4) the difference, however, with regard to the 2nd method is probably this: the method can be either",
"blomberg-s13-014": "a:) analytic",
"blomberg-s13-015": "B) Synthetic",
"blomberg-s13-016": "Analysis and synthesis are either according to coordination or according to subordination",
"blomberg-s13-017": "α) the synthesis of coordination is the connection of the parts with the whole.",
"blomberg-s13-018": "β) the analysis of coordination, however, is the dissolution of the whole into its parts.",
"blomberg-s13-019": "γ) the synthesis of subordination is the connection of the grounds with the consequences.",
"blomberg-s13-020": "δ) the analysis of subordination, however, is the derivation of the consequence from its grounds.",
"blomberg-s13-021": "In all historical cognitions the connection is that of coordination, in all rational cognitions, however, only the connection of subordination takes place. In history I namely count one thing after another, whether according to space or to time. But in rational cognitions and sciences we always derive one thing from another. In analytic coordination I namely go from the whole to its parts, in synthetic coordination, however, from the parts to the whole.",
"blomberg-s13-022": "The analytic method is most advisable in historical cognitions. First I must namely form an idea of the whole, and then I make an idea and concepts of the parts contained under it. In all rational cognitions the form is that of subordination, and this is now either synthetic or analytic. In the former I go from the ground to the consequence, in the latter from the consequence to the ground.",
"blomberg-s13-023": "The artificial mode of teaching is contradistinguished from the natural, just as a distinction is to be made between a reasonable (vernünftig) cognition, i.e. one that does not contradict reason, and a rational (Vernünftig) cognition, if one understands by it one that arises from reason. Thus a cognition is also called natural in a twofold sense, namely 1: when it agrees with the nature of the object and of the subject, and then 2ndly when it springs from nature. The mode of teaching must be natural not so much subjectively as objectively, i.e. adequate to the object.",
"blomberg-s13-024": "Cf. Logik Philippi: xxviv:482.",
"blomberg-s13-025": "Methodus tabellaris, i.e. to understand and to sketch a whole science in tables, is very fine. But insight is required not to make too many, nor useless, divisions. The mode of teaching according to the dismemberment of a whole doctrinal edifice is no mode of teaching; the manner of the composition of the parts makes up the doctrinal edifice.",
"blomberg-s13-026": "Affectatio means an unnatural art; affected means everything by which one wants to seem artful. Thus it is affected when one gives not one's art but traces of an artificial investigation. The affected is commonly connected with pedantry, which shines forth especially when one applies a single method to everything, whether it suits or not.",
"blomberg-s13-027": "The Socratic mode of teaching occurs through conversations, where through well-arranged questions one brings everything out of the pupil and leads him to answers. It has great utility. It is a kind of catechetical method, but it is very tiring.",
"blomberg-s13-028": "The Platonic mode of teaching consisted in a free speech, in which one becomes aware of a system.",
"blomberg-s13-029": "In historical sciences one has 2 methods, the chronological and the geographical. Both are to be united together. The latter is preferable to the former. In all coherent cognitions one must first take into consideration the whole, then its parts, and of the parts the large before the small, the superior division before the subdivision.",
"blomberg-s13-030": "Of history the author says that it belongs to no doctrine, but it is just as good a doctrine as dogmatic truths. One must distinguish:",
"blomberg-s13-031": "a) Doctrin, i.e. a coherence of various cognitions and teachings.",
"blomberg-s13-032": "B:) Disciplin, when this coherence is brought into a method.",
"blomberg-s13-033": "C:) Science (Wißenschaft), when the cognition is completely worked out according to such a method.",
"blomberg-s13-034": "Science is a complete discipline. In the doctrine I direct my attention only to what is taught, in the discipline I look to the method. Doctrine and discipline thus take place in historical cognitions just as well as in dogmatic ones. In sciences there must at all times be doctrine.",
"blomberg-s13-035": "One thinks tumultuarily when one thinks without method.",
"blomberg-s13-036": "To meditate does not mean to recall cognitions that one has had, but to bring forth new ones that one has not yet had. Methodical meditating hinders one from procuring enough of one's materials for meditating. In the beginning one meditates tumultuarily; one must write down what occurs to us, as thoughts sometimes come into our mind that we otherwise do not have. One should first write down all thoughts as they come, without order, afterwards one should begin to coordinate, and then to subordinate. In every working-out 3 labors must precede",
"blomberg-s13-037": "1) One writes down all thoughts without order",
"blomberg-s13-038": "2) one makes a general plan.",
"blomberg-s13-039": "3) one works out all the parts",
"blomberg-s13-040": "Doubting exercises one in reflection, but so does the endeavor to see rightly everyone's opinion and to be able to put oneself into it. It is good if one alternates meditating with other sciences that are refreshing.",
"blomberg-s14-001": "Cf. Logik Philippi § 428-544, xxiv 484ff.",
"blomberg-s14-002": "The nature of words is a worthy object of the philosopher's reflections. The right use is that they designate a thought precisely.",
"blomberg-s14-003": "A terminus inanis is one whose meaning we believe we understand, but in fact do not understand. An expression is deceptive (deceptrix) where we believe something is possible, and yet it is impossible.",
"blomberg-s14-004": "A terminus familiaris is the expression whose sense has never been investigated, because it has never been noticed. Every language has such. I say, e.g., süchtig (addicted); such termini are also often deceptive on closer investigation.",
"blomberg-s14-005": "The phrase “peddling of the learned” is a deceptive trade, in which one passes off words for things; what is more usual than this expression, and deception! An expression is called intelligible that can be understood. Something can be intelligible without being distinct. When 2 expressions have one meaning, or where at least the meanings are not very different, they are called synonymous expressions. If an expression has more than one meaning, it is called terminus vagus ambiguus anceps.",
"blomberg-s15-001": "Cf. Logik Philippi § 428-544, xxiv 484ff.",
"blomberg-s15-002": "Style properly means the tool of a sculptor. Here it means the character of expressing one's thoughts. Everyone has his own particular style, just as everyone writes his own particular hand. Style is to be considered 1) insofar as it is adequate to its object, 2) to the person (subject) and his station, 3) to those for whom one writes. A particular style suits each, and not everyone can bring his thoughts into the form of another.",
"blomberg-s15-003": "The suitability of the manner of writing consists in its being so constituted that it is not too grand, but fitting for the matter. The euphony of the manner of writing is something that belongs more among the chimeras than to the true. The English say that Virgil's poems sound well, but if one hears an Englishman recite the verses, this would be intolerable to our ears. The sonorous consists in the measure of the syllables. Congruence consists in the style's being in accord with the matter. This can be learned only through experience.",
"blomberg-s16-001": "Cf. Logik Philippi § 428-544, xxiv 488ff.",
"blomberg-s16-002": "On the donum didacticum and the requisites of a teacher. In general 2 things must be found in a teacher. He must possess 1) a condescendence to the weaker, and 2) a coadscendence to the higher capacities",
"blomberg-s16-003": "1. Every teacher must know how to put himself in the place of his listeners; I can instruct another only through his own understanding, hence I have a matter to work on that already lies in the other's head. This is called condescendence; it presupposes that one knows how to put oneself in the place of those whom one instructs. Very few teachers of religion, and others, can do this.",
"blomberg-s16-004": "2): the 2nd requisite of a teacher is coadscendence, that one namely ascend to the talents that are raised above the middle rank. He who knows little certainly cannot do this. It is the best case that one choose a middle degree. But how must one conduct oneself in public lectures, where one does not know the capacity of the auditors?",
"blomberg-s16-005": "One must distinguish from one another: to impart an opinion to someone, and to refute someone's opinion. In the first case the subject is empty, in the other, however, I must empty it. Before all instruction I must first have the data in which the other errs. Every error is to be regarded as a phaenomenon that is worthy of an explanation. I must first see how he could have come to the error. There I must also track down the part of truth in his error.",
"blomberg-s16-006": "A dispute about words is when different persons believe they are contesting different opinions, and yet are at odds only in the words, but in agreement in the matter itself. Of predestination some have believed that it is more a dispute about words than a dispute about things. An argumentum ad hominem is such an argument as is indeed not true for everyone, but yet serves to bring someone to silence; if I fetch up something, e.g., ex concessis and from the particular propositions that another holds. These are good means of keeping someone off one's neck and ending the dispute, but not of finding the truth. Retorsiones and reprehensiones are personal disputes; the distinctiones serve very well to pull oneself out of a tight spot. An instantia is when something is used universally and I show that it is not universal. A defense that goes to the person is called apology; if it goes to the judgments of others, it is called refutatio.",
"blomberg-s16-007": "A formal disputation is the action in which, in the presence of learned people, one exposes some propositions in order to defend them against all objections. Disputing could bring great utility if it were not a certamen personarum, and if one did not wish to seek the truth through strife but through agreement.",
"blomberg-s17-001": "Cf. Logik Philippi § 428-544, xxiv 488ff.",
"blomberg-s17-002": "All learned writings are either historical or dogmatic. A writing can be historical both according to matter, if it is something that actually happened or cognition of the individuum, and according to form. The form is called historical that is not rational, where I do not derive through universal cognition of the grounds.",
"blomberg-s17-003": "Historical-dogmatic is a cognition called according to form when the data are dogmata but are presented historically. According to matter, however, when some is historical, other dogmatic. Everything that happens is considered in connection with space and time. If one considers what happens insofar as it is at different times, that is called history; insofar as it is at the same time, however, it belongs in the field of geography. There are various kinds of geography and history.",
"blomberg-s17-004": "The distinction that the author makes in dividing history into political, ecclesiastical, learned, and private history is determined by different objects of historical cognitions. There could also be a moral history, which considers the morals of common life, and also the moral data. Biography is the life of a single subject. All history, of whatever kind it may be, should have the aim of enlarging reason; it should furnish the material for the use of reason.",
"blomberg-s17-005": "Pragmatic is called the history that becomes useful through universal rules; this can have a relation either to speculation or to practical reason. If one merely learns the names of the rulers and knows their reigns, this gives little material for the use of reason, i.e. for speculation, or for practical rules. Meanwhile one must at the beginning already abstract from the use of reason and provide oneself, in good hope, with many historical cognitions. Learned history becomes pragmatic thereby, when one considers learnedness merely in relation to human reason, looks at its growth, or at the causes by which it has been held back. There are 2 methods of composing dogmatic writings, either tumultuarily or methodically. The latter method brings a system into being. A system is any whole whatever. Systematic writings are distinguished from tumultuary writings by their making up a coherent whole. Extracts,",
"blomberg-s17-006": "epitomes, are actual systems. In dogmatic writings the principia of the dogmatici must be presented, then drawn together and weighed in concreto; and thereupon one must scatter in something historical in order to give evidence.",
"blomberg-s17-007": "The method in which one draws a cognition not from the proper sources but takes it from those who have drawn from the source is called compiling. History is compiled if I have written it together not from the first sources but from later ones. One can also compile philosophical propositions, by reading together what others have thought through reason. One nevertheless proves skill if one gathers, with prudent choice, the most important from authors who possess great acuteness, from the most thorough histories. The plagiarius is distinguished from the compilator. The latter pretends that he has himself drawn from the first learned sources, though he exhibits only a product of another.",
"blomberg-s17-008": "The French are especially of the caractère that among them the sciences grow more extensively than intensively. To the epitomes much understanding belongs, in order to be able to choose the most important and most comprehensible.",
"blomberg-s18-001": "Cf. Logik Philippi § 428-544, xxiv 493ff.",
"blomberg-s18-002": "The character of a learned man is twofold, the character of the head and of the heart. The first goes to cognition, the latter to inclination. The character of the heart has great connection with the character of the head if learnedness is to be promoted. The constitution of the heart has a great influence on the direction of the understanding, and it is therefore not a matter of indifference even in merely speculative sciences.",
"blomberg-s18-003": "The character of the head concerns understanding, reason, and taste. The capacity of imitation is precisely opposed to the capacity of genius. Those who possess the capacity of genius sometimes lack the spirit of imitation, which, on the other hand, is native in a great degree to most who show no genius. The Russians are said to have the defect that they lack genius, and for that very reason cannot teach it. Therefore science always dies out among them. For philosophy genius is required, for the fine sciences likewise, for the useful sciences, however, the spirit of imitation. Mathematics needs only imitation. Learnedness and good taste are distinguished from one another.",
"blomberg-s18-004": "Mother wit (Mutterwitz) is the natural capacity of healthy understanding and of healthy reason. This cannot be learned; what is afterwards learned, however, is called school wit (Schulwitz). All instruction already presupposes certain cognitions, or at least the capacity for them. Thus school wit supposes mother wit. It is more tolerable to lack school wit than to have it without mother wit. What is learned is fine in a human being, but he himself is absurd in the application. Hence comes pedantry, and through school wit one becomes still more absurd than otherwise, because one then has more materials. School wit is a perfection of mother wit.",
"blomberg-s18-005": "To the particular learned exercises belongs",
"blomberg-s18-006": "1: learning. This occurs through oral lecture or through reading books. One learns by either bringing forth a cognition or reproducing cognitions one has had. To teach properly means to let cognitions be brought forth by others. The learner is in this respect purely passive, but he must also be active. He must have receptivity, i.e. capacity of the understanding, and also apply it. The oral lecture has a great advantage over the written, for the oral is not so artificial as the written. It is also more comprehensible, for it is one action of the mind fewer than in reading. In reading I must namely also hear in thought.",
"blomberg-s18-007": "2: The reading of learned writings. It depends first very much on the order in which one reads, and on the choice of the writing, where one begins. The question is: should compendia or great works be read first? One should not begin with the compendia, which contain many thoughts in little space, but with those which contain few thoughts in wide space. In mathematics and in history one must first make the universal known to oneself. One must proceed in the main first. Historical books that one chooses for reading must at first concern not so much the exact events as the general ones. One must go by a certain order, and read books of speculation in the morning and evening, historical books at midday. One must read much and, so to speak, digest what has been read, and the field must not be changed much. Every book one must read at least 2 times, once quickly, where one marks the passage that needs an explanation. Some books are of great importance and require much investigation; these one must read often. E.g. Hume, Rousseau, Locke, who can be regarded as a grammar for the understanding, and Montesquieu on the spirit of the laws",
"blomberg-s18-008": "Too frequent repetition is just as harmful as too long an interruption. § 546: Imitation in matters of style is not advisable. § 548: no rule is to be given for the discovery of new truths. § 549 Autodidactus is he who makes himself learned through meditating and reading. This is always a great genius. An oral lecture is nevertheless always to be preferred. One can make more reflexiones.",
"blomberg-s18-009": "Learned diligence means the strength of the exertion that one applies in order to learn something. The human being is by nature indolent, and laziness is itself the final end of his diligence. The future repose is the spur that sets human beings in activity. The greatness and worthiness of the objects can nevertheless exercise diligence very much. Learned diligence, when it continues, is called steady diligence. From this is distinguished vehement diligence, which consists in an exertion that is far too strong.",
"blomberg-s18-010": "The mind suffers under vehement diligence; the representations soon die away.",
"blomberg-s18-011": "A student must first weigh well the aim and the extent of his powers; it is bad that others prescribe the aim to us, and that one does not yet know oneself when one begins to study. One must never study with vehement diligence, but one must never cease to learn. One knows only when one is near the end how to judge what one has to learn. It also belongs to the character of a learned man that he be a person without title, rank and civil standing. Chiefly that he be a man of all estates, and that his cognition be generally usable, and not suit merely the form of the school. The final aim of learnedness should be to give the human race its true form, to free it from prejudices, to refine morals, and to elevate the powers of the soul; then it is a benefaction for the human race; but if it is used merely for operation, it is to be regarded only as a kind of luxury",
"blomberg-s18-012": "End of the Logic"
};
